window.TRIVIA_ARTICLES = [{"id":"kanbun-yomi-invention","icon":"📜","tags":["japan","reading","history"],"title":{"en":"How Japan Kept 訓読み: Reading Chinese Characters with Native Words","ja":"訓読みが生き残った国：漢字に大和言葉を当てた1300年","ko":"일본은 어떻게 훈독(訓読み)을 지켜냈는가","zh":"日本如何留住训读：用大和语词读汉字","yue":"日本點樣留住訓讀：用大和話讀漢字","vi":"Nhật Bản đã giữ được Kun-yomi như thế nào: Đọc chữ Hán bằng từ bản địa","th":"ญี่ปุ่นรักษาคุนโยมิไว้ได้อย่างไร: อ่านอักษรจีนด้วยคำพื้นเมือง","id":"Bagaimana Jepang Mempertahankan Kun-yomi: Membaca Aksara Tionghoa dengan Kata Asli","hi":"जापान ने कुन-योमि को कैसे बचाए रखा: चीनी वर्णों को देशी शब्दों से पढ़ना","de":"Wie Japan Kun-yomi bewahrte: Chinesische Schriftzeichen mit einheimischen Wörtern lesen","fr":"Comment le Japon a conservé le kun-yomi : lire les sinogrammes avec des mots autochtones","it":"Come il Giappone conservò il kun-yomi: leggere i caratteri cinesi con parole native","es":"Cómo Japón conservó el kun-yomi: leer caracteres chinos con palabras nativas","pt":"Como o Japão preservou o kun-yomi: lendo caracteres chineses com palavras nativas","ru":"Как Япония сохранила кун-ёми: чтение китайских иероглифов с помощью родных слов","uk":"Як Японія зберегла кун-йомі: читання китайських ієрогліфів рідними словами","ar":"كيف حافظت اليابان على كون-يومي: قراءة الأحرف الصينية بالكلمات الأصيلة","he":"כיצד יפן שימרה את קון-יומי: קריאת תווים סינים במילים מקומיות","sw":"Jinsi Japan Ilivyodumisha Kun-yomi: Kusoma Herufi za Kichina kwa Maneno ya Asili"},"summary":{"en":"By around 700 CE, Japanese scribes used 漢字 both for Chinese-derived readings and for native Yamato words. This practice established the distinction between on'yomi and kun'yomi readings.","ja":"700年頃、日本の文人たちは漢字を中国語由来の音と大和言葉の双方に対応させた。これが音読みと訓読みという二つの読みの基礎になった。","ko":"700년경 일본 서기들은 한자를 중국어계 음뿐 아니라 고유 야마토어 단어에도 대응시켰다. 이 관습이 음독과 훈독의 구분으로 이어졌다.","zh":"约公元700年，日本文人不再只用汉字记录汉语读音，而是将其对应到固有大和语词，使一个汉字同时拥有音读与训读两套系统。","yue":"大約公元700年，日本嘅文人唔單止用漢字嚟記中文讀音，仲將漢字對應到固有大和語詞，令一個漢字同時有音讀同訓讀兩套讀法。","vi":"Khoảng năm 700, các nhà chép sách Nhật Bản dùng chữ Hán không chỉ với âm gốc Hán mà còn để ghi các từ Yamato bản địa. Cách dùng này dẫn đến sự phân biệt giữa âm on và âm kun.","th":"ราวปี ค.ศ. 700 นักเขียนชาวญี่ปุ่นได้ก้าวกระโดดครั้งยิ่งใหญ่ โดยไม่เพียงใช้อักษรจีนสำหรับเสียงภาษาจีน แต่ยังเชื่อมโยงกับคำพื้นเมืองยามาโตะ ทำให้อักษรหนึ่งตัวมีสองวิธีอ่านที่แตกต่างกันโดยสิ้นเชิง","id":"Sekitar tahun 700 M, para juru tulis Jepang membuat lompatan revolusioner: daripada hanya menggunakan 漢字 untuk bunyi Cina mereka, mereka memetakannya ke kata-kata Yamato asli — memberi satu karakter dua bacaan yang benar-benar berbeda.","hi":"लगभग 700 ई. में जापानी लिपिकों ने एक क्रांतिकारी कदम उठाया: 漢字 को केवल चीनी ध्वनियों के लिए नहीं, बल्कि मूल यामातो शब्दों पर भी आरोपित किया — इस प्रकार एक चरित्र के दो बिल्कुल अलग पाठ हो गए।","de":"Um 700 n. Chr. vollzogen japanische Schreiber einen revolutionären Schritt: Sie nutzten 漢字 nicht nur für ihre chinesischen Laute, sondern ordneten sie auch einheimischen Yamato-Wörtern zu — wodurch ein Schriftzeichen zwei völlig verschiedene Lesungen erhielt.","fr":"Vers 700 apr. J.-C., les scribes japonais firent un bond révolutionnaire : au lieu d'utiliser les 漢字 uniquement pour leurs sons chinois, ils les associèrent aux mots yamato indigènes — donnant à un caractère deux lectures entièrement différentes.","it":"Intorno al 700 d.C., i copisti giapponesi fecero un balzo rivoluzionario: invece di usare i 漢字 solo per i loro suoni cinesi, li associarono alle parole native yamato, dando a un carattere due letture completamente diverse.","es":"Alrededor del año 700 d.C., los escribas japoneses dieron un salto revolucionario: en lugar de usar los 漢字 solo por sus sonidos chinos, los mapearon a palabras yamato nativas, dando a un carácter dos lecturas completamente distintas.","pt":"Por volta de 700 d.C., os escribas japoneses fizeram um salto revolucionário: em vez de usar os 漢字 apenas pelos seus sons chineses, mapearam-nos para palavras yamato nativas — dando a um caractere duas leituras completamente diferentes.","ru":"Около 700 г. н. э. японские писцы совершили революционный прорыв: вместо того чтобы использовать 漢字 только для китайских звуков, они сопоставили их с исконными словами ямато — дав одному иероглифу два совершенно разных чтения.","uk":"Приблизно 700 р. н. е. японські писарі зробили революційний стрибок: замість того щоб використовувати 漢字 лише для китайських звуків, вони зіставили їх із рідними словами ямато — надавши одному ієрогліфу два цілком різних читання.","ar":"حوالي عام 700م، قفز الكتبة اليابانيون قفزة ثورية: بدلاً من استخدام 漢字 لأصواتها الصينية فقط، ربطوها بكلمات يامتو الأصيلة — مانحين الحرف الواحد قراءتين مختلفتين تماماً.","he":"סביב שנת 700 לספירה, עשו הסופרים היפנים קפיצה מהפכנית: במקום להשתמש ב-漢字 רק לצלילים הסיניים שלהם, הם מיפו אותם למילים יפניות מקוריות (יאמאטו) — ונתנו לתו אחד שתי קריאות שונות לחלוטין.","sw":"Karibu mwaka 700 BK, waandishi wa Kijapani walifanya hatua ya mapinduzi: badala ya kutumia 漢字 kwa sauti zao za Kichina tu, walizioanisha na maneno ya asili ya Yamato — wakitoa tabia moja kusoma mbili tofauti kabisa."},"body":{"en":"<h3>A Script Adopted, Then Transformed</h3><p>When Chinese writing first reached the Japanese archipelago — probably via Korea in the 4th–5th centuries CE — it arrived as a complete package: a set of characters each bearing a fixed Chinese pronunciation and meaning. At first, writing in Japan meant writing Chinese. Characters were borrowed purely for their sounds mainly to spell Japanese names, as on the Inariyama sword inscription (usually dated 471 CE, though 531 has also been argued). This sound-only use, later developed into what scholars call <em>man'yōgana</em> (万葉仮名), soon spread to whole words and poems. The word 「あめ」 might be written 阿米 — characters meaning 'hillock' and 'rice', chosen purely for their sound, <em>a-me</em>, with no semantic intent.</p><h3>Semantic Readings</h3><p>By around 700 CE, the age of the <em>Kojiki</em> (712) and <em>Nihon Shoki</em> (720), another practice is in full view. Scribes read a character not by its Chinese sound but by its <em>meaning</em>, reaching into the native Yamato lexicon for the Japanese word that corresponded. The character 山 (Chinese: <em>shān</em>) was now also read <em>yama</em>, the native word for mountain. 水 (<em>shuǐ</em>) was also <em>mizu</em>. This is <strong>訓読み (kun-yomi)</strong>, \"semantic reading\". At first it was anything but tidy. A single character could carry many different native glosses, and the 12th-century dictionary <em>Ruiju Myōgishō</em> lists more than 30 for some characters. Only by the Muromachi period (14th–16th centuries) had most characters settled down to a few fixed kun readings.</p><div class='trivia-actions'><button type='button' class='trivia-action' data-action='setchar' data-char='山'>Show 山 on map</button><button type='button' class='trivia-action' data-action='focus' data-code='ja_kun'>Highlight Japanese kun-yomi layer</button></div><h3>Comparison with Other Sinosphere Traditions</h3><p>Korea, Vietnam, and other Sinosphere societies received Chinese characters too, and the idea of reading a character with a native word occurred to them as well. Korean scribes annotated Literary Chinese with 석독구결 (釋讀口訣) so that a Chinese text could be read out in native Korean words and Korean word order, and Korean hanja are still <em>named</em> by a native gloss bound to the Sino-Korean sound: 水 is 물 수 (<em>mul su</em>), 'water-SU'. Vietnamese chữ Nôm went further, writing words with characters picked for their meaning so routinely that a diacritic (<em>nháy</em>, 𖿱) was devised to flag the indigenous reading — 本 read the Sino-Vietnamese way is <em>bản</em>, but 本𖿱 is <em>vốn</em>. In Japan, native readings became part of ordinary writing and remain in use; comparable practices elsewhere were tied more closely to classical scholarship or to scripts that later fell out of use. Japan institutionalized the dual system: a modern kanji dictionary lists each character's <em>on-yomi</em> (音読み, Chinese-derived sound) alongside its <em>kun-yomi</em> (訓読み, native word), if it has one. Some never got a native reading because the thing itself came from China: 菊 \"chrysanthemum\" is <em>kiku</em>, which sounds native but is actually its Chinese reading. Japan also kept the matching technique for whole texts: <em>kanbun kundoku</em>, reading Classical Chinese straight off the page as Japanese, with small marks such as レ and 一・二 telling the reader which word to jump back to.</p><h3>Man'yōgana: The Bridge</h3><p>The <em>Man'yōshū</em> anthology (c. 759) is the locus classicus of pre-kana phonetic writing. Poets used characters as pure syllables: 安 (<em>ān</em>) = <em>a</em>, 以 (<em>yǐ</em>) = <em>i</em>, 宇 (<em>yǔ</em>) = <em>u</em>. Over the following centuries these phonetic uses were simplified into the two kana syllabaries: <em>hiragana</em>, from flowing cursive forms of whole characters, and <em>katakana</em>, from fragments of characters that monks squeezed between the lines of Chinese texts as reading aids, but the semantic kun-yomi layer was retained in parallel. The result: modern Japanese has four distinct scripts functioning simultaneously — kanji (with both on and kun), hiragana, katakana, and rōmaji — an orthography in which four writing systems have distinct and overlapping functions.</p><h3>Readings in Modern Dictionaries</h3><p>Today, the kanji 生 alone carries <em>on-yomi</em> セイ (<em>sei</em>) and ショウ (<em>shō</em>), plus <em>kun-yomi</em> い(きる) <em>i(kiru)</em>, うまれる <em>umareru</em>, は(える) <em>ha(eru)</em>, なま <em>nama</em> — at least a dozen valid readings. The on-yomi come from different waves of borrowing (<em>shō</em> is the older Go-on, <em>sei</em> the Tang-era Kan-on, and dictionaries even record a rare later Tō-on, <em>san</em>), while the kun-yomi preserve native Yamato vocabulary. Modern mixed-script text can therefore make several historical layers visible at once.</p><div class='trivia-actions'><button type='button' class='trivia-action' data-action='panto' data-lat='34.69' data-lng='135.50' data-zoom='6'>Pan to Nara / Osaka</button></div>"},"sources":["Seeley, Christopher. A History of Writing in Japan. Leiden: Brill, 1991.","Frellesvig, Bjarke. A History of the Japanese Language. Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 2010.","Miller, Roy Andrew. The Japanese Language. Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1967.",{"text":"Man'yōshū — Wikipedia overview","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Man%27y%C5%8Dsh%C5%AB"},{"text":"On'yomi and Kun'yomi — Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kanji#On%27yomi_and_Kun%27yomi"},"Vovin, Alexander. A Descriptive and Comparative Grammar of Western Old Japanese. Folkestone: Global Oriental, 2005."]},{"id":"kanbun-kaeriten-system","icon":"🔢","tags":["japan","literacy","writing"],"title":{"en":"返り点: How Japan Reads Chinese Texts Backwards into Japanese","ja":"返り点——漢文をひっくり返して日本語に読む技法","ko":"반점(返り点): 일본이 한문을 일본어 어순으로 읽는 방법","zh":"返り点：日本如何将汉文倒读成日语","yue":"返り點：日本點樣將漢文倒讀成日語","vi":"返り点: Cách Nhật Bản đọc ngược văn bản Hán thành tiếng Nhật","th":"返り点: วิธีที่ญี่ปุ่นอ่านข้อความจีนแบบย้อนกลับเป็นภาษาญี่ปุ่น","id":"返り点: Bagaimana Jepang Membaca Teks Cina secara Terbalik dalam Bahasa Jepang","hi":"返り点: जापान कैसे चीनी पाठ को उल्टे क्रम में जापानी में पढ़ता है","de":"返り点: Wie Japan chinesische Texte rückwärts auf Japanisch liest","fr":"返り点 : Comment le Japon lit les textes chinois à rebours en japonais","it":"返り点: Come il Giappone legge i testi cinesi al contrario in giapponese","es":"返り点: Cómo Japón lee textos chinos al revés en japonés","pt":"返り点: Como o Japão lê textos chineses de trás para frente em japonês","ru":"返り点: Как Япония читает китайские тексты в обратном порядке по-японски","uk":"返り点: Як Японія читає китайські тексти у зворотному порядку японською","ar":"返り点: كيف يقرأ اليابانيون النصوص الصينية معكوسةً باليابانية","he":"返り点: כיצד יפן קוראת טקסטים סיניים הפוך ביפנית","sw":"返り点: Jinsi Japani Inavyosoma Maandishi ya Kichina kwa Nyuma kwa Kijapani"},"summary":{"en":"Japanese scholars devised 返り点 (kaeriten) — tiny diacritical marks placed beside Chinese characters — to reorder SVO Chinese syntax into SOV Japanese without rewriting a single character.","ja":"日本の学者たちは漢字の隣に小さな記号「返り点」を付けることで、一字も書き換えずにSVO語順の漢文をSOV語順の日本語に読み直す独自技法を作り出した。","ko":"일본 학자들은 한자 옆에 작은 반점(返り点)을 붙임으로써, 한 글자도 바꾸지 않고 SVO 어순의 한문을 SOV 어순의 일본어로 읽어낼 수 있는 독자적 기법을 만들어냈다.","zh":"日本学者在汉字旁边附上小记号「返り点」，无需改动任何文字，即可将SVO语序的汉文转读为SOV语序的日语。","yue":"日本學者喺漢字旁邊加上細小嘅符號「返り點」，唔使改動任何文字，就可以將SVO語序嘅漢文轉讀為SOV語序嘅日語。","vi":"Các học giả Nhật Bản đã tạo ra 返り点 (kaeriten) — những ký hiệu nhỏ bên cạnh chữ Hán — để sắp xếp lại cú pháp SVO của tiếng Trung thành SOV tiếng Nhật mà không cần viết lại một chữ.","th":"นักวิชาการญี่ปุ่นคิดค้น 返り点 (ไคเอริเท็น) — เครื่องหมายเล็กๆ ข้างอักษรจีน — เพื่อจัดเรียงไวยากรณ์ SVO ของจีนใหม่เป็น SOV ญี่ปุ่นโดยไม่ต้องเขียนตัวอักษรใหม่แม้แต่ตัวเดียว","id":"Para cendekiawan Jepang merancang 返り点 (kaeriten) — tanda diakritik kecil di samping karakter Cina — untuk menyusun ulang sintaks SVO bahasa Cina menjadi SOV bahasa Jepang tanpa menulis ulang satu karakter pun.","hi":"जापानी विद्वानों ने 返り点 (काएरिटेन) बनाए — चीनी अक्षरों के बगल में लघु चिह्न — जो एक भी अक्षर बदले बिना SVO चीनी वाक्यक्रम को SOV जापानी में रूपांतरित करते हैं।","de":"Japanische Gelehrte entwickelten 返り点 (Kaeriten) — kleine diakritische Zeichen neben chinesischen Schriftzeichen — um die SVO-Syntax des Chinesischen in das SOV-Japanischen umzuordnen, ohne ein einziges Zeichen neu zu schreiben.","fr":"Les érudits japonais inventèrent les 返り点 (kaeriten) — de minuscules signes diacritiques placés à côté des sinogrammes — pour réordonner la syntaxe SVO du chinois en SOV japonais sans réécrire un seul caractère.","it":"I dotti giapponesi idearono i 返り点 (kaeriten) — piccoli segni diacritici accanto ai caratteri cinesi — per riordinare la sintassi SVO del cinese in SOV giapponese senza riscrivere un solo carattere.","es":"Los eruditos japoneses idearon los 返り点 (kaeriten) — pequeñas marcas diacríticas junto a los caracteres chinos — para reordenar la sintaxis SVO del chino en SOV japonés sin reescribir ni un solo carácter.","pt":"Os estudiosos japoneses criaram os 返り点 (kaeriten) — pequenas marcas diacríticas ao lado dos caracteres chineses — para reorganizar a sintaxe SVO do chinês em SOV japonês sem reescrever um único caractere.","ru":"Японские учёные разработали 返り点 (каэритэн) — маленькие диакритические знаки рядом с китайскими иероглифами — для преобразования SVO-синтаксиса китайского языка в SOV-японский без изменения ни одного знака.","uk":"Японські науковці розробили 返り点 (каерітен) — маленькі діакритичні знаки поруч із китайськими ієрогліфами — для перебудови SVO-синтаксису китайської мови в SOV-японську без переписування жодного знаку.","ar":"ابتكر العلماء اليابانيون 返り点 (كايريتن) — علامات دياكريتية صغيرة توضع بجانب الحروف الصينية — لإعادة ترتيب بنية SVO الصينية إلى SOV اليابانية دون إعادة كتابة حرف واحد.","he":"חוקרים יפנים המציאו את 返り点 (קאֶריטֶן) — סימנים דיאקריטיים קטנים ליד תווים סיניים — כדי לסדר מחדש את תחביר ה-SVO הסיני ל-SOV יפני מבלי לשכתב תו אחד.","sw":"Wasomi wa Japani waligundua 返り点 (kaeriten) — alama ndogo za diakriti pembeni ya herufi za Kichina — ili kupanga upya sintaksia ya SVO ya Kichina kuwa SOV ya Kijapani bila kuandika upya herufi moja."},"body":{"en":"<h3>The Problem: Two Languages, Opposite Word Order</h3><p>Classical Chinese (漢文, kanbun) is Subject–Verb–Object: 「我愛汝」 means 'I love you' in that order. Classical Japanese is Subject–Object–Verb: the same idea is 「我汝ヲ愛ス」. When Japanese scholars received the Chinese Confucian and Buddhist canon, they faced a dilemma: how to read it aloud in intelligible Japanese without translating it into a separate Japanese text. Their solution was a set of annotations that supplied Japanese readings and marked a different reading order.</p><h3>レ点 — The Simplest Mark</h3><p>The <strong>レ点 (re-ten)</strong> looks like the katakana レ and is placed at the lower-left corner of a character. It means: 'read the next character first, then come back here.' If 読書 ('read books') has a レ point on 読, you read 書 first, then 読 — giving the SOV reading 書ヲ読ム ('books [object] read'). A single tiny mark reverses two adjacent characters.</p><div class='trivia-actions'><button type='button' class='trivia-action' data-action='setchar' data-char='見'>Show 見 on map</button><button type='button' class='trivia-action' data-action='focus' data-code='ja'>Highlight the on'yomi layer</button></div><h3>一二点 and 上下点 — Multi-Level Reordering</h3><p>For longer inversions — where the return jump reaches back two or more characters, past at least one character in between — scholars used <strong>一二点 (ichi-ni-ten)</strong>: the numerals 一, 二, 三 written beside characters to number the return. You read straight down the line, passing over the character marked 二; when you reach the character marked 一 you read it, then jump back up to 二. In 「登二泰山一」 you read 泰, then 山, then 登 — 'climb Mount Tai' comes out as 泰山に登る. For even deeper inversions, <strong>上下点 (jō-ge-ten)</strong> and <strong>甲乙点 (kō-otsu-ten)</strong> were added. In principle, any SVO phrase — no matter how embedded — can be rerouted to SOV purely through these marks, without touching the original text.</p><h3>Regional Comparisons and Continued Use</h3><p>Chinese texts were read throughout the Sinosphere: in Korea (as 한문, hanmun), in Vietnam (as Hán văn), in Ryūkyū. Vietnamese, like Chinese, puts the verb before the object, so Vietnamese readers could simply pronounce the text in Sino-Vietnamese. Korean, verb-final like Japanese, faced the same mismatch, and Korea did the same thing on the page. Goryeo-period <strong>석독구결</strong> (釋讀口訣 <em>seokdok gugyeol</em>, also called 역독구결 'reverse-reading gugyeol'), attested from the 11th century and either written in ink or scratched into the paper with an inkless stylus (角筆), marked both the native Korean word to substitute and the order in which to jump back through the line. Similar return-reading experiments are attested for Khitan and Uyghur. Japan differs in the continuous use of the system. In Korea, seokdok gugyeol gave way to 순독구결 (順讀口訣), which reads straight through in the Chinese order; Japan's 返り点 were standardised, printed alongside the text, and taught continuously into the modern era, so that 漢文訓読 (kanbun kundoku) still lets a scholar read the <em>original unchanged Chinese text</em> aloud in natural Japanese today.</p><h3>The Pedagogical Legacy</h3><p>Until the Meiji era (1868+) and well into the 20th century, kanbun literacy was the mark of a Japanese educated person. Samurai, monks, scholars, and bureaucrats all drilled 返り点 parsing from childhood. Meiji-era reformers went further and proposed abolishing kanji altogether (Mori Arinori even floated adopting English), but the proposals never won out. Even today, kanbun appears in Japanese high school curricula, and the ability to parse a phrase like 「不亦楽乎」 (<em>mata tanoshikarazu ya</em> — 'is that not also a joy?') using 一二点 (read 亦 and 楽 first, then jump back to 不) is exactly the kind of skill the kanbun section of the national university entrance exam tests.</p><div class='trivia-actions'><button type='button' class='trivia-action' data-action='panto' data-lat='35.68' data-lng='139.69' data-zoom='5'>Pan to Japan</button></div><h3>Influence on Written Japanese</h3><p>Kanbun remains visible in the formal written register of modern Japanese. Legal Japanese and newspaper editorials still use words and set phrases that came out of 漢文訓読: 〜ごとし (\"like, as\") is a hallmark of kanbun-reading style, and 〜に於いて (\"in, at\") renders the Chinese preposition 於. When a formal speech or editorial reaches for 「努力せざるべからず」 ('one must not fail to make an effort'), the stacked double negative ざる…べからず is kanbun reading style alive in the 21st century. The conventions associated with 返り点 also influenced the syntax of modern written Japanese.</p>"},"sources":["Lurie, David B. Realms of Literacy: Early Japan and the History of Writing. Cambridge: Harvard University Asia Center, 2011.","Miner, Earl. An Introduction to Japanese Court Poetry. Stanford: Stanford University Press, 1968.","Gottlieb, Nanette. Language and Society in Japan. Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 2005.",{"text":"Kanbun — Wikipedia overview","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kanbun"},{"text":"Kaeriten — Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kaeriten"},"Whitman, John, et al. 'Toward an International Vocabulary for Research on Vernacular Readings of Chinese Texts (漢文訓読 Hanwen Xundu).' Scripta 2 (2010): 61–83."]},{"id":"tangut-3000-syllables","icon":"𗼇","tags":["tangut","xixia","sinosphere","script","history"],"title":{"en":"Tangut Script: 6,000 Characters for the Tangut Language","ja":"西夏文字：漢字に見せかけた6000字の謎","ko":"서하 문자: 한자형 구조로 설계된 약 6,000자","zh":"西夏文字：采用汉字式方块结构的六千字","yue":"西夏文字：刻意扮靚似漢字嘅六千個謎字","vi":"Chữ Tây Hạ: khoảng 6.000 ký tự với cấu trúc dạng chữ Hán","th":"อักษรเซี่ยเซี่ย: 6,000 ตัวอักษรที่จงใจดูคล้ายอักษรจีน","id":"Aksara Tangut: 6.000 Karakter yang Sengaja Mirip Huruf Tionghoa","hi":"तांगुत लिपि: 6,000 वर्ण जो जानबूझकर चीनी जैसे दिखते हैं","de":"Tangutische Schrift: 6.000 Zeichen, die chinesisch wirken – aber keines sind","fr":"L'écriture tangoute : 6 000 caractères qui imitent le chinois","it":"La scrittura Tangut: 6.000 caratteri che sembrano cinesi ma non lo sono","es":"La escritura tangut: 6.000 caracteres que parecen chinos pero no lo son","pt":"A escrita tangut: 6.000 caracteres que parecem chineses mas não são","ru":"Тангутское письмо: 6000 знаков, притворяющихся китайскими иероглифами","uk":"Тангутське письмо: 6000 знаків, що вдають із себе китайські ієрогліфи","ar":"الخط التانغوتي: 6000 حرف صُمِّمت لتبدو صينية","he":"הכתב הטנגוטי: 6,000 סימנים שמדמים כתב סיני","sw":"Maandishi ya Tangut: Herufi 6,000 Zilizoundwa Kuigiza Herufi za Kichina"},"summary":{"en":"In 1036 the Tangut state launched a script of nearly 6,000 characters that look like Chinese at a glance but were invented from scratch, for a language that is only a distant cousin of Chinese.","ja":"1036年、タングートの国家は6,000字近い文字を世に出した。一見すると漢字のようだが、すべてゼロから作られた文字で、しかも書き表す言語は中国語の遠い親戚にすぎない。","ko":"1036년 탕구트 국가는 6,000자 가까운 문자를 내놓았다. 언뜻 보면 한자 같지만 완전히 새로 만든 글자들로, 중국어와는 먼 친척뻘인 언어를 적기 위한 것이었다.","zh":"1036年，西夏推出了一套近6000字的文字。乍看像汉字，其实全是从零发明的，记录的语言与汉语只是远亲。","yue":"1036年，西夏國推出一套接近6,000個字嘅文字：乍睇似漢字，但其實係由零創造，用嚟寫一門同漢語只係遠親嘅語言。","vi":"Năm 1036, nhà nước Tây Hạ cho ra đời một hệ chữ gần 6.000 ký tự, thoạt nhìn giống chữ Hán nhưng được sáng tạo hoàn toàn từ đầu, để ghi một ngôn ngữ chỉ là họ hàng xa của tiếng Hán.","th":"ในปี ค.ศ. 1036 รัฐตังกุตเปิดตัวอักษรเกือบ 6,000 ตัว ที่แวบแรกดูเหมือนอักษรจีน แต่ประดิษฐ์ขึ้นใหม่ทั้งหมด เพื่อเขียนภาษาที่เป็นเพียงญาติห่าง ๆ ของภาษาจีน","id":"Pada 1036, negara Tangut meluncurkan aksara berisi hampir 6.000 karakter yang sekilas tampak seperti aksara Tionghoa, padahal diciptakan dari nol, untuk bahasa yang hanya sepupu jauh bahasa Tionghoa.","hi":"1036 में तांगुत राज्य ने लगभग 6,000 वर्णों की एक लिपि चलाई, जो पहली नज़र में चीनी जैसी दिखती है पर शून्य से गढ़ी गई थी — एक ऐसी भाषा के लिए जो चीनी की बस दूर की रिश्तेदार है।","de":"1036 führte der Tangutenstaat eine Schrift aus fast 6.000 Zeichen ein, die auf den ersten Blick chinesisch aussehen, aber von Grund auf neu erfunden wurden — für eine Sprache, die mit dem Chinesischen nur entfernt verwandt ist.","fr":"En 1036, l'État tangoute lança une écriture de près de 6 000 caractères qui ressemblent au chinois au premier coup d'œil mais furent inventés de toutes pièces, pour une langue qui n'est qu'une lointaine cousine du chinois.","it":"Nel 1036 lo Stato tangut varò una scrittura di quasi 6.000 caratteri che a prima vista sembrano cinesi ma furono inventati da zero, per una lingua che del cinese è solo una lontana cugina.","es":"En 1036 el Estado tangut puso en marcha una escritura de casi 6.000 caracteres que a primera vista parecen chinos pero se inventaron desde cero, para una lengua que solo es pariente lejana del chino.","pt":"Em 1036, o Estado tangut lançou uma escrita de quase 6.000 caracteres que à primeira vista parecem chineses, mas foram inventados do zero, para uma língua que é apenas prima distante do chinês.","ru":"В 1036 году тангутское государство ввело письменность почти из 6000 знаков, которые на первый взгляд похожи на китайские, но изобретены с нуля — для языка, который китайскому лишь дальний родственник.","uk":"У 1036 році тангутська держава запровадила письмо з майже 6000 знаків, які на перший погляд схожі на китайські, але винайдені з нуля — для мови, що доводиться китайській лише далекою родичкою.","ar":"في عام 1036م أطلقت دولة التانغوت نظام كتابة يضم قرابة 6000 حرف تبدو صينية للوهلة الأولى، لكنها اختُرعت من الصفر، للغة لا تمتّ إلى الصينية إلا بقرابة بعيدة.","he":"בשנת 1036 השיקה המדינה הטנגוטית כתב של כמעט 6,000 סימנים, שבמבט ראשון נראים סיניים אבל הומצאו מאפס — לשפה שהיא רק קרובה רחוקה של הסינית.","sw":"Mwaka 1036 dola ya Tangut ilizindua maandishi ya karibu herufi 6,000 zinazofanana na Kichina kwa mtazamo wa kwanza lakini zilizobuniwa kutoka mwanzo, kwa ajili ya lugha ambayo ni binamu wa mbali tu wa Kichina."},"body":{"en":"<h3>A State-Sponsored Script</h3><p>The Western Xia kingdom (<em>Xī Xià</em>, 西夏, 1038–1227) used writing as part of its state administration and cultural policy. In 1036 the Tangut ruler <strong>Li Yuanhao</strong> (元昊, later the first Xia emperor, Jingzong) introduced a national writing system for the Tangut language. The official <em>History of Song</em> says Yuanhao \"himself created the Tangut script\" and had the official <strong>Yeli Renrong</strong> (野利仁榮) work it out in full, in twelve volumes; other accounts credit Yeli Renrong as the designer. Scholars now count about <strong>5,900 characters</strong> (5,863 by a 2004 count, excluding variants). They are dense, square and multi-component like Chinese characters, but the characters themselves were newly constructed for Tangut.</p><p>The <em>History of Song</em> describes the new characters as \"square and regular, like clerical script, but with rather repetitive strokes\", and the state soon had the Confucian <em>Classic of Filial Piety</em> and the <em>Erya</em> dictionary translated into Tangut. The visual resemblance to Chinese was very likely deliberate. Located between Song China and the Liao Khitan kingdom, the Tangut state used its own script to support administration and to assert cultural parity with its neighbours. The components nevertheless carry Tangut-specific phonetic and semantic values, so knowledge of Chinese characters does not make a Tangut text readable.</p><h3>Structure Without Chinese Meaning</h3><p>Like Chinese, Tangut characters are built from recurring graphic elements, and most combine two of them. Unlike Chinese, not one Tangut character is a picture of anything. The logic of the parts is different too. In Chinese a component usually brings the same meaning or sound wherever it appears. The 12th-century Tangut dictionary <em>Sea of Characters</em> instead explains each character as borrowing parts of other whole characters: \"wild goose\" is made from part of \"bird\" plus part of \"longevity\" (which supplies the sound <em>dze</em>). The same small component can stand for \"bird\" in one character and bone, smoke, food or time in others. Pairs of characters with the same parts in a different order (AB and BA) often have related meanings, like \"finger\" and \"toe\". Only about one character in ten has a sound element, against more than nine in ten in Chinese, according to Sofronov.</p><p>The language it wrote was Sino-Tibetan, related to Chinese only very distantly; since the 2010s specialists have placed it in the Gyalrongic (Qiangic) branch, whose living relatives are spoken in western Sichuan. So the script applies a Chinese-looking design to a language with a very different structure, including verbs that agree with their subject and object.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"panto\" data-lat=\"38.5\" data-lng=\"106.27\" data-zoom=\"5\">📍 Pan to Yinchuan (Xia capital)</button></div><h3>The Bilingual Glossary 番漢合時掌中珠</h3><p>Our best window into Tangut vocabulary is a 12th-century bilingual glossary, the <strong><em>Fānhàn héshí zhǎngzhōng zhū</em></strong> (番漢合時掌中珠, &ldquo;Pearl in the Palm, Tangut and Chinese for the Times&rdquo;). Compiled in 1190 by a Tangut scholar named Gule Maocai (骨勒茂才), it arranges Tangut words alongside Chinese equivalents by theme. Each entry has four parts: the Tangut word, its Chinese equivalent, the Tangut pronunciation spelled with Chinese characters, and the Chinese pronunciation spelled with Tangut characters. That double spelling made it the key that let Aleksei Ivanov (1909) and Berthold Laufer (1916) make the first guesses at how Tangut sounded. The preface explains the purpose plainly: Tanguts and Chinese cannot deal with each other without learning each other's words.</p><h3>Later Use and Modern Decipherment</h3><p>When the Mongols destroyed the Western Xia capital Zhongxing (modern Yinchuan) in 1227, the Tangut state was erased, but the script was not. Tangut communities went on writing and printing it under the Yuan and into the Ming; the last known example is a pair of Tangut dharani pillars erected at Baoding, in present-day Hebei, in <strong>1502</strong>. The script then ceased to be used for roughly four centuries. In <strong>1908–09</strong>, the Russian explorer <strong>Pyotr Kozlov</strong> excavated the ruined city of Khara-Khoto in the Gobi Desert and unearthed a cache of thousands of Tangut manuscripts — Buddhist sutras, legal codes, dictionaries, poems — now held at the Institute of Oriental Manuscripts in St. Petersburg.</p><p>Systematic decipherment began in the 20th century, and its pioneer paid a terrible price. <strong>Nikolai Nevsky</strong> compiled the first Tangut dictionary and worked out the meanings of many grammatical particles, which made it possible to actually read Tangut texts, but he was arrested by the NKVD in 1937 and shot; his <em>Tangut Philology</em> appeared only in 1960 and won a posthumous Lenin Prize in 1962, and some manuscripts he had at the time of his arrest resurfaced at the St Petersburg institute only in 1991. The first comprehensive frameworks for reading the language came from the Japanese scholar <strong>Nishida Tatsuo</strong> (西田龍雄), whose <em>Seikago no kenkyū</em> (西夏語の研究, 1964–66) paired a reconstruction of Tangut phonology with a decipherment of the script, and from the Russian scholar <strong>Mikhail Sofronov</strong>, whose <em>Grammatika tangutskogo yazyka</em> (1968) set out a reconstruction alongside a full grammar. <strong>Gong Hwang-cherng</strong> (龔煌城) later refined the phonological system in a series of studies; his reconstruction was adopted for Li Fanwen's Tangut–Chinese dictionary (<em>Xià-Hàn zìdiǎn</em>, 夏漢字典, 1997) and summarised for English readers in his &lsquo;Tangut&rsquo; chapter of <em>The Sino-Tibetan Languages</em> (2003). Five full Tangut dictionaries now exist (Nevsky, Nishida, Li Fanwen, Kychanov and Han Xiaomang), and newer work by scholars such as Marc Miyake and Gong Xun uses living Gyalrongic languages to refine how Tangut sounded.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"focus\" data-code=\"txg\">🔍 Focus Tangut on map</button></div><h3>Digital Encoding and Historical Significance</h3><p>Tangut is a documented case of large-scale script creation under state sponsorship. Its visual relationship to Chinese writing also reflects the political setting in which it was designed. Unicode finally encoded Tangut in 2016 (6,125 characters at U+17000–U+187FF in version 9.0, plus 755 components), giving the script its first digital home nearly 800 years after the kingdom that created it was destroyed.</p>"},"sources":["Gong Hwang-cherng, 'Tangut (Xixia)' in <em>The Sino-Tibetan Languages</em>, ed. Thurgood & LaPolla, Routledge, 2003","Nishida Tatsuo, <em>Seikago no kenkyū</em> (西夏語の研究), Tokyo: Zauhō Kankōkai, 1964–1966","Nevsky, N.A., <em>Tangutskaya filologiya: Issledovaniya i slovar'</em> (Tangut Philology: Studies and Dictionary), 2 vols., Moscow: Izdatel'stvo vostochnoi literatury, 1960",{"text":"Unicode Tangut block (U+17000–U+187FF) — Unicode 9.0, 2016","url":"https://www.unicode.org/charts/PDF/U17000.pdf"},{"text":"Khara-Khoto collection, Institute of Oriental Manuscripts, St. Petersburg","url":"https://www.orientalstudies.ru/eng/index.php?option=com_content&task=view&id=7"},"Dunnell, Ruth, <em>The Great State of White and High: Buddhism and State Formation in Eleventh-Century Xia</em>, University of Hawaii Press, 1996"]},{"id":"khitan-large-small-scripts","icon":"𰀀","tags":["khitan","liao","sinosphere","script","history","undeciphered"],"title":{"en":"Khitan Large and Small Scripts: Two Writing Systems for Khitan","ja":"契丹大字と小字：ひとつの言語に生まれた2種類の文字","ko":"거란 대자와 소자: 하나의 언어를 위한 두 가지 문자","zh":"契丹大字与小字：辽代一语两文的谜","yue":"契丹大字同小字：遼代一個語言用咗兩套文字嘅謎","vi":"Chữ Khiết Đan Lớn và Nhỏ: Hai Hệ Thống Chữ Viết Cho Một Ngôn Ngữ Đã Mất","th":"อักษรขีตานใหญ่และเล็ก: สองอักษรสำหรับหนึ่งภาษาที่สาบสูญ","id":"Aksara Khitan Besar dan Kecil: Dua Tulisan untuk Satu Bahasa yang Hilang","hi":"खिताई बड़ी और छोटी लिपि: एक खोई भाषा के लिए दो लेखन प्रणालियाँ","de":"Khitanische Groß- und Kleinschrift: Zwei Schriften für eine verlorene Sprache","fr":"Grands et petits caractères khitans : deux écritures pour une langue perdue","it":"Le scritture khitan grande e piccola: due sistemi per una lingua perduta","es":"Los scripts khitan grande y pequeño: dos escrituras para una lengua perdida","pt":"As escritas khitan grande e pequena: dois sistemas para uma língua perdida","ru":"Большое и малое киданьское письмо: две письменности для одного утраченного языка","uk":"Велике і мале кіданьське письмо: дві системи для однієї втраченої мови","ar":"الخطان الختاني الكبير والصغير: منظومتا كتابة للغة مفقودة","he":"הכתב הח'יטאני הגדול והקטן: שתי כתיבות לשפה אבודה","sw":"Maandishi ya Khitan Makubwa na Madogo: Maandishi Mawili kwa Lugha Moja Iliyopotea"},"summary":{"en":"The Liao dynasty (907–1125) created two scripts for Khitan: a Large Script in 920 CE modeled on Chinese characters, and a Small Script around 924–925 CE that spells each word as a block of phonetic signs. Both remain only partly deciphered.","ja":"遼（907〜1125年）は契丹語のために大字（920年、漢字がモデル）と小字（924〜925年ごろ、表音文字を組んで1語を1ブロックで綴る）の2種の文字を作ったが、どちらもいまだ部分的にしか解読されていない。","ko":"요(遼, 907~1125)는 거란어를 위해 920년에 한자를 본뜬 대자를, 924~925년 무렵에는 낱말마다 표음 기호를 블록으로 묶어 적는 소자를 만들었지만, 둘 다 아직 일부만 해독되었다.","zh":"辽朝（907—1125年）先后为契丹语创制了两套文字：920年仿汉字的大字，以及约924—925年的小字——后者把每个词拼写成一个由表音字符组成的方块。两者至今都只解读了一部分。","yue":"遼朝（907—1125年）為契丹語創製咗兩套文字：920年仿漢字嘅大字，同大約924至925年、將每個詞拼成一塊表音字符嘅小字；兩者到而家都仍然只係部分解讀。","vi":"Nhà Liêu (907–1125) đã tạo ra không phải một mà là hai hệ thống chữ viết riêng biệt cho tiếng Khiết Đan: Chữ lớn năm 920 mô phỏng chữ Hán và Chữ nhỏ ra đời khoảng năm 924–925, ghép các ký hiệu ghi âm thành từng khối để viết mỗi từ — và đến nay cả hai vẫn chỉ mới được giải mã một phần.","th":"ราชวงศ์เหลียว (907–1125) ได้สร้างไม่ใช่หนึ่งแต่สองอักษรที่แตกต่างกันสำหรับภาษาขีตาน: อักษรใหญ่ในปี 920 ที่ออกแบบตามอักษรจีน และอักษรเล็กราวปี 924–925 ที่สะกดแต่ละคำเป็นบล็อกของสัญลักษณ์แทนเสียง — แต่ทั้งสองยังถอดรหัสได้เพียงบางส่วน","id":"Dinasti Liao (907–1125) menciptakan bukan satu melainkan dua aksara yang berbeda untuk bahasa Khitan: Aksara Besar pada 920 M yang dimodelkan dari karakter Tionghoa, dan Aksara Kecil sekitar 924–925 M yang mengeja setiap kata sebagai satu blok tanda fonetis. Keduanya baru terpecahkan sebagian.","hi":"लियाओ राजवंश (907–1125) ने खिताई भाषा के लिए एक नहीं बल्कि दो अलग-अलग लिपियाँ बनाईं: 920 ई. में चीनी वर्णों पर आधारित बड़ी लिपि, और लगभग 924–925 ई. में छोटी लिपि, जो हर शब्द को ध्वन्यात्मक चिह्नों के एक खंड में लिखती है — और दोनों आज भी केवल आंशिक रूप से ही पढ़ी जा सकी हैं।","de":"Die Liao-Dynastie (907–1125) schuf gleich zwei verschiedene Schriften für Khitanisch: die Großschrift von 920 nach dem Vorbild chinesischer Zeichen und um 924/925 die Kleinschrift, die jedes Wort als Block aus Lautzeichen schreibt — doch beide sind bis heute nur teilweise entschlüsselt.","fr":"La dynastie Liao (907–1125) créa non pas une mais deux écritures distinctes pour le khitan : la grande écriture en 920, inspirée des caractères chinois, et vers 924-925 la petite écriture, qui épelle chaque mot sous forme d'un bloc de signes phonétiques — mais toutes deux ne sont encore que partiellement déchiffrées.","it":"La dinastia Liao (907–1125) creò non uno ma due distinti sistemi di scrittura per il khitan: la grande scrittura nel 920 modellata sui caratteri cinesi e la piccola scrittura verso il 924–925, che compone ogni parola in un blocco di segni fonetici — ed entrambe restano decifrate solo in parte.","es":"La dinastía Liao (907–1125) creó no uno sino dos escrituras distintas para el khitan: la escritura grande en el año 920, modelada sobre los caracteres chinos, y hacia 924–925 la escritura pequeña, que escribe cada palabra como un bloque de signos fonéticos. Ambas siguen descifradas solo en parte.","pt":"A dinastia Liao (907–1125) criou não uma, mas duas escritas distintas para o khitan: a escrita grande em 920 d.C., modelada nos caracteres chineses, e a escrita pequena por volta de 924–925 d.C., que grafa cada palavra como um bloco de sinais fonéticos — e ambas continuam apenas parcialmente decifradas.","ru":"Династия Ляо (907–1125) создала не одно, а два разных письма для киданьского языка: большое письмо в 920 году по образцу китайских иероглифов и малое письмо около 924–925 годов, где каждое слово записывается блоком фонетических знаков, — однако оба расшифрованы лишь частично.","uk":"Династія Ляо (907–1125) створила не одне, а два різних письма для кіданьської мови: велике письмо у 920 році за зразком китайських ієрогліфів і мале письмо близько 924–925 року, яке записує кожне слово блоком фонетичних знаків, — і обидва досі розшифровані лише частково.","ar":"أنشأت أسرة لياو (907–1125م) خطَّين مختلفَين للغة الختانية لا خطًّا واحدًا: الخط الكبير عام 920م المُستوحى من الأحرف الصينية، والخط الصغير نحو عام 924–925م الذي يكتب كل كلمة كتلةً من العلامات الصوتية — غير أن شفرة كليهما لم تُفكّ حتى اليوم إلا جزئيًا.","he":"שושלת ליאו (907–1125) יצרה לא כתב אחד אלא שני כתבים שונים לח'יטאנית: הכתב הגדול ב-920 לספירה על פי מודל האותיות הסיניות, והכתב הקטן בסביבות 924–925 לספירה, שמאיית כל מילה כגוש של סימנים פונטיים — ושניהם מפוענחים עד היום רק בחלקם.","sw":"Nasaba ya Liao (907–1125) iliunda si moja bali maandishi mawili tofauti kwa lugha ya Khitan: Maandishi Makubwa mwaka 920 BK yaliyoundwa kwa mfano wa herufi za Kichina, na Maandishi Madogo karibu mwaka 924–925 BK yanayoandika kila neno kama kitalu cha alama za sauti — na yote mawili bado yamefumbuliwa kwa sehemu tu."},"body":{"en":"<h3>One Language, Two Scripts — Why?</h3><p>The Khitan people founded the Liao dynasty in 907 and ruled a vast steppe-and-settled empire stretching from Mongolia to the Yellow Sea for over two centuries. Literate and administratively sophisticated, they needed writing. What is surprising is not that they invented a script — it is that they invented <em>two</em>, five years apart, without abandoning the first.</p><p>The <strong>Khitan Large Script</strong> (契丹大字, <em>Qìdān dà zì</em>) appeared in 920 CE. Modeled visually on Chinese characters, it has around 830 distinct characters identified so far (about 1,000 if one disputed epitaph is counted), mostly logograms, perhaps with some phonetic signs for grammatical endings. The emperor Abaoji (耶律阿保機) commissioned it.</p><p>Just a few years later, around 924–925 CE, Abaoji's younger brother <strong>Yelü Diela</strong>, who had studied the Uyghur alphabet with a visiting Uyghur envoy, invented the <strong>Khitan Small Script</strong> (契丹小字, <em>Qìdān xiǎo zì</em>). The Small Script is structurally radical: it has only a few hundred characters, many of them phonetic, and stacks two to seven of them into a square block that spells out one word, written in pairs from left to right. In this respect it resembles the way Korean Hangul assembles consonants and vowels into syllabic squares — though the resemblance is typological, not historical.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"panto\" data-lat=\"43.8\" data-lng=\"120.5\" data-zoom=\"4\">📍 Pan to Liao heartland (Inner Mongolia)</button></div><h3>An Agglutinative Language in Square Blocks</h3><p>Khitan is classified as Para-Mongolic — not a descendant of Proto-Mongolic, but an extinct sister branch of the Mongolic languages (Janhunen). The language is agglutinative — it strings suffixes onto roots to build complex meanings — which makes a logographic system like the Large Script a poor fit. The Small Script's phonetic blocks suit this morphology far better, since a suffix is simply one more character in the block.</p><p>Yet the Large Script was never abandoned (dated inscriptions run from 986 to 1176), and scholars are not entirely sure <em>why</em> both scripts continued to coexist for two centuries. One theory holds that the Large Script was prestigious for monumental and memorial inscriptions while the Small Script was the everyday administrative workhorse. Another is that they mapped onto different dialects or registers of Khitan. The honest answer is: we do not fully know, because we cannot fully read either.</p><h3>The Great Decipherment Problem</h3><p>After the Jurchen Jin dynasty defeated Liao in 1125, the Jin went on using Khitan writing for decades, until an imperial order suppressed it in 1191. The last dated Khitan inscription is from 1176. By the Mongol period the language was dead and the scripts forgotten. Only about fifty monumental inscriptions survive (some seventeen in the Large Script and around thirty-three in the Small Script), almost all in northeastern China, with two in Mongolia and a Khitan-inscribed bronze mirror from Kaesŏng in Korea; systematic scholarly analysis only began in the 20th century, after the 1922 discovery, reported by the Belgian missionary Louis Kervyn, of the Small Script memorial tablets of Emperor Xingzong and Empress Renyi at the Qingling (慶陵) Liao imperial tombs in Bairin Right Banner, Inner Mongolia.</p><p>The decipherment strategy exploits parallel texts. Only one true Khitan–Chinese bilingual inscription is known, the <em>Langjun xingji</em>, long mistaken for Jurchen, but many Khitan epitaphs honour people whose lives are also recorded in Chinese, so the two can be compared. By aligning the Chinese text with blocks of Khitan, scholars can infer the general meaning of Khitan passages. Crucially, Khitan names of people and places known from Chinese records help anchor phonetic values for the Small Script.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"panto\" data-lat=\"41.5\" data-lng=\"119.0\" data-zoom=\"5\">📍 Qinglong stele area</button></div><h3>Aisin Gioro and Recent Progress</h3><p>A milestone was the 1985 book by Chinggeltei's Khitan Script Research Group in China, which gathered the whole known corpus and summarised the readings proposed so far. Since the 1990s <strong>Aisin Gioro Ulhicun</strong> (愛新覚羅烏拉熙春), a Beijing-born Manchu scholar working in Japan (a descendant of the Qianlong Emperor, and daughter of the Jurchen specialist Jin Qizong), has published extensively on both scripts. Working with her husband, the historian Yoshimoto Michimasa, she has proposed readings for hundreds of Small Script blocks and identified grammatical suffixes, personal name patterns, and honorific vocabulary.</p><p>Even so, progress remains partial: by one older count (Kara, 1986), of the 378 known Small Script characters about 125 have an established meaning and 115 a phonetic value, while the rest were still undeciphered. The Large Script is understood far less well: beyond numerals, the twelve zodiac animals and a few other words, most of its characters remain unread. The field remains open: newly excavated steles are still awaiting full publication, and new comparative tools — including computational alignment with related Mongolic languages — are being brought to bear. Khitan is one of the great unsolved puzzles of East Asian historical linguistics.</p><p>Unicode encoded the Khitan Small Script in 2020 (U+18B00–U+18CFF, 470 characters in that first release, Unicode 13.0), allowing digital scholarship to accelerate; the Large Script is still only at the proposal stage — a modest technical victory for a writing system that has been silent for more than eight centuries.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"focus\" data-code=\"zh\">🔍 View Sinosphere region</button></div>"},"sources":["Kane, Daniel, <em>The Kitan Language and Script</em>, Brill, 2009","Aisin Gioro Ulhicun & Yoshimoto Michimasa, <em>Shinshutsu Kittan shiryō no kenkyū</em> (新出契丹史料の研究), Kyoto: Shōkadō Shoten, 2012","Wu Yingzhe & Janhunen, Juha (eds.), <em>New Materials on the Khitan Small Script</em>, Folkestone: Global Oriental, 2010",{"text":"Unicode Khitan Small Script (U+18B00–U+18CFF), added in Unicode 13.0, 2020","url":"https://www.unicode.org/charts/PDF/U18B00.pdf"},"Chinggeltei, 'On the Problems of Reading Kitan Characters', <em>Acta Orientalia Academiae Scientiarum Hungaricae</em> 55 (2002): 99–114",{"text":"Wikipedia: Khitan small script","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Khitan_small_script"},{"text":"Wikipedia: Khitan large script","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Khitan_large_script"}]},{"id":"phagspa-universal-script","icon":"🔡","tags":["mongol","yuan","script","history","hangul-hypothesis"],"title":{"en":"Phags-pa Script: A Yuan-Dynasty Multilingual Alphabet","ja":"八思巴字——クビライ・ハンの「万国共通文字」","ko":"파스파 문자 — 쿠빌라이 칸의 실패한 보편 문자","zh":"八思巴字——忽必烈的「万国统一文字」","yue":"八思巴字——忽必烈嘅「萬國通用文字」","vi":"Chữ Phags-pa — Bộ Chữ Viết Toàn Cầu Thất Bại của Hốt Tất Liệt","th":"อักษรพักส์ปา — อักษรสากลที่ล้มเหลวของกุบไลข่าน","id":"Aksara Phags-pa — Aksara Universal Kubilai Khan yang Gagal","hi":"फाग्स-पा लिपि — कुबलई खान की विफल सार्वभौमिक लिपि","de":"Phags-pa — Kublai Khans gescheiterte Universalschrift","fr":"Le Phags-pa — L'écriture universelle ratée de Kubilaï Khan","it":"Phags-pa — La Scrittura Universale Fallita di Kublai Khan","es":"Phags-pa — La Escritura Universal Fallida de Kublai Kan","pt":"Phags-pa — A Escrita Universal Fracassada de Kublai Khan","ru":"Пагспа — Провалившийся универсальный алфавит Хубилая","uk":"Пагспа — провалений універсальний алфавіт Хубілая","ar":"خط فاغسبا — الكتابة العالمية الفاشلة لقوبلاي خان","he":"כתב פאגספא — כתב האוניברסלי הכושל של קובלאי חאן","sw":"Hati ya Phags-pa — Hati ya Ulimwengu Iliyoshindwa ya Kublai Khan"},"summary":{"en":"In 1269 CE, the Tibetan monk Drogön Chögyal Phagpa created an alphabet on Kublai Khan's orders, used to write Mongolian, Chinese, Tibetan, Uyghur, Sanskrit and probably Persian. It was used in official contexts during the Yuan dynasty.","ja":"1269年、チベット僧ドゥーゴン・チューギェル・パクパがクビライ・ハンの命でモンゴル語・漢語・チベット語・ウイグル語・サンスクリット語、そしておそらくペルシア語を書くのに使われた「八思巴字」を創案。この文字は元代の公用文書に使われたが、王朝の終焉後は定着しなかった。","ko":"1269년 티베트 승려 드로곤 최걀 팍파가 쿠빌라이 칸의 명으로 몽골어·중국어·티베트어·위구르어·산스크리트어, 그리고 아마도 페르시아어까지 적는 데 쓰인 파스파 문자를 창제했으나, 한 세기도 채 못 되어 사라졌다.","zh":"1269年，西藏僧侣八思巴奉忽必烈之命创制八思巴字，用来书写蒙古语、汉语、藏语、回鹘语、梵语，很可能还有波斯语，然而这一帝国梦想未逾百年便烟消云散。","yue":"1269年，西藏僧人八思巴奉忽必烈之命，創制八思巴字，用嚟書寫蒙古語、漢語、藏語、回鶻語、梵語，可能仲有波斯語，但呢個宏大嘅帝國夢，唔夠一個世紀就消失咗。","vi":"Năm 1269, nhà sư Tây Tạng Drogön Chögyal Phagpa theo lệnh của Hốt Tất Liệt sáng tạo ra một bộ chữ cái, được dùng để ghi tiếng Mông Cổ, tiếng Hán, tiếng Tây Tạng, tiếng Duy Ngô Nhĩ, tiếng Sanskrit và có lẽ cả tiếng Ba Tư — nhưng giấc mơ đế quốc này chỉ tồn tại chưa đầy một thế kỷ.","th":"ในปี ค.ศ. 1269 พระภิกษุทิเบต ดรอกอน โชเกียล พักปา ได้สร้างอักษรพักส์ปาตามพระราชโองการของกุบไลข่าน ซึ่งถูกใช้เขียนภาษามองโกล จีน ทิเบต อุยกูร์ สันสกฤต และน่าจะรวมถึงเปอร์เซียด้วย — แต่ความฝันอันยิ่งใหญ่นี้ดำรงอยู่ได้ไม่ถึงหนึ่งศตวรรษ","id":"Pada 1269 M, biksu Tibet Drogön Chögyal Phagpa menciptakan satu abjad atas perintah Kubilai Khan yang dipakai untuk menulis bahasa Mongolia, Cina, Tibet, Uyghur, Sanskerta, dan kemungkinan Persia — mimpi imperial yang berani namun hanya bertahan kurang dari satu abad.","hi":"1269 ई. में तिब्बती भिक्षु द्रोगों च्योग्याल फागपा ने कुबलई खान के आदेश पर फाग्स-पा लिपि बनाई, जिससे मंगोलियाई, चीनी, तिब्बती, उइगुर, संस्कृत और शायद फारसी भी लिखी गई — यह साम्राज्यिक स्वप्न एक सदी भी नहीं टिका।","de":"1269 erfand der tibetische Mönch Drogön Chögyal Phagpa auf Befehl Kublai Khans ein Alphabet, mit dem Mongolisch, Chinesisch, Tibetisch, Uigurisch, Sanskrit und wahrscheinlich Persisch geschrieben wurden — ein kühner imperialer Traum, der kaum ein Jahrhundert überdauerte.","fr":"En 1269, le moine tibétain Drogön Chögyal Phagpa inventa, sur ordre de Kubilaï Khan, un alphabet unique qui servit à écrire le mongol, le chinois, le tibétain, l'ouïghour, le sanskrit et probablement le persan — un rêve impérial audacieux qui dura à peine un siècle.","it":"Nel 1269, il monaco tibetano Drogön Chögyal Phagpa inventò, su ordine di Kublai Khan, un unico alfabeto, usato per scrivere il mongolo, il cinese, il tibetano, l'uiguro, il sanscrito e probabilmente il persiano — un audace sogno imperiale durato appena un secolo.","es":"En 1269, el monje tibetano Drogön Chögyal Phagpa inventó, por orden de Kublai Kan, un único alfabeto con el que se escribieron el mongol, el chino, el tibetano, el uigur, el sánscrito y probablemente el persa — un audaz sueño imperial que duró apenas un siglo.","pt":"Em 1269, o monge tibetano Drogön Chögyal Phagpa inventou, a mando de Kublai Khan, um único alfabeto, usado para escrever mongol, chinês, tibetano, uigur, sânscrito e provavelmente persa — um audacioso sonho imperial que durou menos de um século.","ru":"В 1269 году тибетский монах Дрогон Чогьял Пагба по приказу Хубилая создал алфавит, которым записывали монгольский, китайский, тибетский, уйгурский, санскрит и, вероятно, персидский — дерзкая имперская мечта, просуществовавшая едва ли век.","uk":"У 1269 році тибетський чернець Дрогон Чог'ял Пагпа за наказом Хубілая створив єдину абетку, якою записували монгольську, китайську, тибетську, уйгурську мови, санскрит і, ймовірно, перську — сміливе імперіальне мрія, що проіснувала ледь більше століття.","ar":"في عام 1269م، ابتكر الراهب التبتي دروغون تشوغيال فاغسبا، بأمر من قوبلاي خان، أبجدية واحدة كُتبت بها المغولية والصينية والتبتية والأويغورية والسنسكريتية، وعلى الأرجح الفارسية — حلم إمبراطوري جريء لم يدم قرناً واحداً.","he":"בשנת 1269 המציא הנזיר הטיבטי דרוגון צ'וגיאל פאגפא, בפקודת קובלאי חאן, אלפבית אחד, ששימש לכתיבת מונגולית, סינית, טיבטית, אויגורית, סנסקריט וכנראה גם פרסית — חלום אימפריאלי נועז שלא שרד מאה שנה.","sw":"Mwaka 1269 BK, mtawa wa Tibet Drogön Chögyal Phagpa alibuni alfabeti moja kwa amri ya Kublai Khan, iliyotumika kuandika Kimongolia, Kichina, Kitibeti, Kiuygur, Kisanskriti na huenda Kiajemi — ndoto ya kifalme iliyodumu chini ya karne moja."},"body":{"en":"<h3>One Script to Rule Them All</h3>\n<p>The <strong>Mongol Yuan dynasty</strong> (1271–1368) was the eastern heartland of the Mongol empire — the largest contiguous land empire in history, whose western khanates by then ruled separately from Persia to Russia. Its court spoke a babel of tongues — Mongolian, Mandarin, Tibetan, Uyghur, Persian — each with its own script. In 1269, <strong>Kublai Khan</strong> commissioned his imperial preceptor, the Tibetan monk <strong>Drogön Chögyal Phagpa</strong> (1235–1280), to solve this problem with a single alphabet.</p>\n<div class=\"trivia-actions\">\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"focus\" data-code=\"zh_phagspa\">📍 See Phags-pa readings on map</button>\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"panto\" data-lat=\"40.4\" data-lng=\"116.4\" data-zoom=\"4\">🏯 Fly to Yuan-era Dadu (Beijing)</button>\n</div>\n<h3>The Design</h3>\n<p>Phagpa adapted the <strong>Tibetan script</strong> — itself derived from an Indian Brahmic alphabet — into a new vertical form. The result was <strong>八思巴字</strong> (<em>Bāsībā zì</em> in Mandarin, named after the monk himself). Key features:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Written <strong>top-to-bottom in columns, left-to-right</strong> — unlike its Tibetan parent, which runs horizontally. The vertical, left-to-right column layout follows the Uyghur-derived Mongolian script that Phags-pa was meant to replace.</li>\n  <li>An <strong>abugida</strong>: each consonant carries an inherent vowel, with diacritics for other vowels.</li>\n  <li><strong>41 basic letters</strong> — extended to roughly 48 with extra letters for Tibetan and Sanskrit — designed to capture the sounds of Mongolian, Chinese, Tibetan, Uyghur, Sanskrit and probably Persian.</li>\n  <li>Official use on <strong>seals, paper currency and imperial edicts</strong>, and on stone inscriptions across Yuan China. One of them is a Christian tombstone carved in 1314 in the port city of Quanzhou.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Why It Failed</h3>\n<p>The script was never truly popular. Chinese literati clung to Han characters; Mongolians already had their own Uyghur-derived script. Phags-pa was required for official documents but hardly used in daily life. Even its official role faded from the early 1350s, after which it served mostly as a pronunciation guide for Mongols learning Chinese characters, and it fell out of use once the Ming took over in 1368. In Tibet, a seal script known by the same name was still carved on seals in the 20th century, though some scholars regard that later script as a separate Tibetan seal script rather than true Phags-pa. Only scattered stone inscriptions, coins, and a handful of manuscripts survive.</p>\n<h3>The Hangul Hypothesis</h3>\n<p>Here is where things get controversial. In 1443, <strong>King Sejong of Joseon</strong> created <strong>Hangul</strong> (訓民正音, <em>Hunminjeongeum</em>). Some scholars, most notably <strong>Gari Ledyard</strong> in his 1966 dissertation, have argued that a handful of basic Hangul consonants, such as ㄱ, ㄷ, ㄹ and ㅂ, were modelled on Phags-pa letters. They point to a tantalising line in the royal annals for 1443: the king's new letters \"imitate the Old Seal\" (其字倣古篆). Ledyard read \"Old Seal\" as a veiled reference to the Mongol seal script. He never claimed Hangul was a copy. In his view Sejong combined original design with several models, Phags-pa among them.</p>\n<p>Others counter that the 1446 commentary on the script, the <em>Haerye</em>, explains the basic consonants as pictures of the speech organs, sorted by the five places of articulation (牙舌脣齒喉: molars, tongue, lips, teeth, throat). ㄱ, for example, shows the root of the tongue blocking the throat, and ㅁ shows the mouth. On that view the resemblances to Phags-pa are coincidence. Most scholars still treat Hangul as largely an original invention, and the Phags-pa link remains a minority view, though a serious one.</p>\n<div class=\"trivia-actions\">\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"focus\" data-code=\"ko\">📍 See Korean (Hangul) on map</button>\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"人\">🔢 View 「人」 in Phags-pa and all readings</button>\n</div>\n<h3>A Ghost in the Machine</h3>\n<p>Phags-pa had one lasting indirect effect: it is one of the <strong>richest phonetic records of Chinese ever written in an outsider's alphabet</strong>. Tibetan-script transcriptions of Chinese from Dunhuang are some four centuries older, but Phags-pa covers the lexicon far more systematically, and what it captures is Old Mandarin, not Middle Chinese. Because the script spells out sounds instead of whole words, linguists can use Yuan-era Phags-pa spellings to recover consonants and vowels that Han characters alone never reveal. They do so with care: Phags-pa Chinese followed a conservative reading standard, keeping, for example, voiced initials that the spoken Mandarin of Dadu had probably already lost. Tone is the one thing it withholds: Phags-pa writes no tone marks at all, so syllables differing only in tone are spelled identically — the four tone categories have to be read off the way the Yuan rhyme dictionary <em>Menggu Ziyun</em> (蒙古字韻) lists its Han characters, level-rising-falling-entering, under each Phags-pa spelling. In this sense, Kublai Khan's failed universal script became an unintentional time capsule for the history of the Chinese language.</p>"},"sources":["Ledyard, Gari. 1966. 'The Korean Language Reform of 1446: The Origin, Background, and Early History of the Korean Alphabet.' PhD dissertation, University of California, Berkeley.","Coblin, W. South. 2007. 'A Handbook of 'Phags-pa Chinese.' Honolulu: University of Hawai'i Press.","Poppe, Nicholas. 1957. 'The Mongolian Monuments in hP'ags-pa Script.' Wiesbaden: Otto Harrassowitz.",{"text":"Unicode Consortium — Phags-pa block (U+A840–U+A87F)","url":"https://www.unicode.org/charts/PDF/UA840.pdf"},"Tumurtogoo, Domiin. 2010. 'Mongolian Monuments in 'Phags-pa Script: Introduction, Transliteration, Transcription and Bibliography.' Taipei: Institute of Linguistics, Academia Sinica.",{"text":"Wikipedia: Phags-pa script","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phags-pa_script"},{"text":"Wikipedia: ʼPhags-pa inspiration for Hangul hypothesis","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%CA%BCPhags-pa_inspiration_for_Hangul_hypothesis"}]},{"id":"hangul-sinosphere-context","icon":"📜","tags":["hangul","joseon","sinosphere","literacy","script-creation"],"title":{"en":"King Sejong and the Creation of Hangul","ja":"なぜ世宗大王は漢字支配の時代にハングルを作ったのか","ko":"세종대왕은 왜 한자 지배 시대에 한글을 만들었나","zh":"世宗大王为何在汉字主导时代创制谚文","yue":"世宗大王點解喺漢字主導嘅年代創製諺文","vi":"Vì sao Vua Sejong tạo ra Hangul trong thời đại chữ Hán","th":"ทำไมพระเจ้าเซจงจึงประดิษฐ์ฮันกึลท่ามกลางอุปสรรคทุกด้าน","id":"Mengapa Raja Sejong Menciptakan Hangul Meski Banyak Rintangan","hi":"राजा सेजोंग ने सभी बाधाओं के बावजूद हाँगुल क्यों बनाया","de":"Warum König Sejong Hangul gegen alle Widerstände erfand","fr":"Pourquoi le roi Sejong inventa le hangul contre vents et marées","it":"Perché il re Sejong inventò il hangul contro ogni avversità","es":"Por qué el rey Sejong inventó el hangul contra todos los obstáculos","pt":"Por Que o Rei Sejong Inventou o Hangul Contra Tudo e Todos","ru":"Почему король Сечжон изобрёл хангыль вопреки всему","uk":"Чому король Седжон винайшов хангиль всупереч усьому","ar":"لماذا اخترع الملك سيجونغ الهانغول رغم كل العقبات","he":"מדוע המלך סיגונג המציא את ההאנגול נגד כל הסיכויים","sw":"Kwa Nini Mfalme Sejong Alibuni Hangul Licha ya Vikwazo Vyote"},"summary":{"en":"In 1443, Joseon's fourth king introduced a new alphabet intended to make reading more accessible. Confucian scholars opposed it, while women, Buddhist monks, and writers helped extend its use in Korean society.","ja":"1443年、朝鮮の第4代王・世宗は庶民が読めるよう全く新しい文字体系を創案。儒学者たちに「野蛮」と非難されながらも、女性・仏僧・小説家たちがその文字を守り抜いた。","ko":"1443년 조선 제4대 왕 세종은 일반 백성의 문자 사용을 넓히기 위해 새 문자 체계를 만들었다. 반대 상소와 낮은 사회적 위상 속에서도 여성·불교 승려·소설가들이 이를 사용했다.","zh":"1443年，朝鲜世宗为扩大庶民的文字使用而创制新文字。虽遭士大夫反对且长期地位不高，女性、佛僧及小说作者仍持续使用。","yue":"1443年，朝鮮第四代君主世宗創制全新嘅文字，目的係令普通百姓識字，但儒家士大夫話佢係「蠻夷之作」，最後靠女性、佛教僧侶同小說家先至將呢套文字保住。","vi":"Năm 1443, Vua Sejong tạo một hệ chữ mới nhằm mở rộng khả năng đọc viết cho dân thường. Dù bị giới Nho học phản đối và có địa vị thấp trong nhiều thế kỷ, nó vẫn được phụ nữ, tăng sĩ và tác giả tiểu thuyết sử dụng.","th":"ในปี ค.ศ. 1443 กษัตริย์องค์ที่สี่แห่งโชซอนทรงประดิษฐ์ระบบอักษรใหม่อย่างสิ้นเชิง เพื่อให้ชาวเกาหลีสามัญชนสามารถอ่านออกเขียนได้ — ทำให้นักวิชาการขงจื๊อตราหน้าว่า 'เป็นของพวกป่าเถื่อน' — และอักษรนี้เกือบสูญหายก่อนที่สตรี พระสงฆ์พุทธ และนักประพันธ์จะช่วยรักษาไว้","id":"Pada 1443, raja keempat Joseon menciptakan alfabet baru yang radikal agar rakyat Korea biasa bisa membaca — membuat para sarjana Konfusius menyebutnya 'barbar' — dan aksara itu hampir lenyap sebelum diselamatkan oleh perempuan, biksu Buddha, dan novelis.","hi":"1443 में जोसियोन के चौथे राजा ने एक क्रांतिकारी नई वर्णमाला बनाई ताकि साधारण कोरियाई लोग पढ़ सकें — कन्फ्यूशियस विद्वानों ने इसे 'बर्बर' कहा — और यह लिपि लगभग विलुप्त हो गई थी, जब तक महिलाओं, बौद्ध भिक्षुओं और उपन्यासकारों ने इसे बचाया।","de":"1443 erfand Joseons vierter König ein radikal neues Alphabet, damit gewöhnliche Koreaner lesen konnten — was konfuzianische Gelehrte als 'barbarisch' brandmarkten — und die Schrift wäre fast verschwunden, bevor Frauen, buddhistische Mönche und Romanschriftsteller sie retteten.","fr":"En 1443, le quatrième roi de Joseon inventa un alphabet radicalement nouveau pour que les Coréens ordinaires puissent lire — scandalisant des lettrés confucéens qui le qualifièrent de « barbare » — et l'écriture faillit disparaître avant d'être sauvée par des femmes, des moines bouddhistes et des romanciers.","it":"Nel 1443, il quarto re di Joseon inventò un alfabeto radicalmente nuovo affinché i coreani comuni potessero leggere — scandalizzando gli studiosi confuciani che lo definirono 'barbarico' — e la scrittura rischiò di scomparire prima di essere salvata da donne, monaci buddisti e romanzieri.","es":"En 1443, el cuarto rey de Joseon inventó un alfabeto radicalmente nuevo para que los coreanos comunes pudieran leer — escandalizando a los eruditos confucianos que lo llamaron 'bárbaro' — y la escritura casi desapareció antes de ser salvada por mujeres, monjes budistas y novelistas.","pt":"Em 1443, o quarto rei de Joseon inventou um alfabeto radicalmente novo para que os coreanos comuns pudessem ler — escandalizando estudiosos confucianos que o chamaram de 'bárbaro' — e a escrita quase desapareceu antes de ser salva por mulheres, monges budistas e romancistas.","ru":"В 1443 году четвёртый король Чосон изобрёл принципиально новый алфавит, чтобы рядовые корейцы могли читать — скандализировав конфуцианских учёных, назвавших его «варварским» — и письмо едва не исчезло, прежде чем его спасли женщины, буддийские монахи и романисты.","uk":"У 1443 році четвертий король Чосон винайшов принципово нову абетку, щоб звичайні корейці могли читати — що скандалізувало конфуціанських вчених, які назвали її «варварською» — і писемність ледь не зникла, перш ніж її врятували жінки, буддійські ченці та романісти.","ar":"في عام 1443م، ابتكر الملك الرابع لمملكة جوسيون أبجدية جديدة جذرياً لكي يتمكن عامة الكوريين من القراءة — مما أثار فضيحة بين علماء الكونفوشيوسية الذين وصفوها بـ'البربرية' — وكادت الكتابة تختفي قبل أن تُنقذها النساء والرهبان البوذيون والروائيون.","he":"בשנת 1443, המלך הרביעי של Joseon המציא אלפבית חדש לחלוטין כדי שהקוריאנים הרגילים יוכלו לקרוא — מה שהסעיר חוקרים קונפוציאנים שכינוהו 'ברברי' — והכתב כמעט נעלם לפני שנשים, נזירים בודהיסטים וסופרים הצילוהו.","sw":"Mwaka 1443, mfalme wa nne wa Joseon alibuni alfabeti mpya kabisa ili Wakorea wa kawaida waweze kusoma — hilo liliwakera wasomi wa Confucius waliouita 'wa kishenzi' — na hati karibu kupotea kabla ya kuokolewa na wanawake, watawa wa Kibuddha na waandishi wa riwaya."},"body":{"en":"<h3>Classical Chinese and Access to Literacy</h3>\n<p>In 15th-century Joseon, classical Chinese (<em>hanmun</em>, 漢文) was the only prestige writing system. Mastering it required years of rote study — years that farmers, craftsmen, and women could not spare. The result was a literacy gap that split society: a thin layer of male <em>yangban</em> (aristocratic) scholars monopolised the written word, while most Koreans had limited access to written texts.</p>\n<div class=\"trivia-actions\">\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"focus\" data-code=\"ko\">📍 See Korean on the HanMap</button>\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"focus\" data-code=\"ko_mid\">📍 See Middle Korean readings</button>\n</div>\n<h3>Design and Publication</h3>\n<p><strong>King Sejong</strong> (r. 1418–1450) was a polymath — he reformed astronomy, music theory, and military technology. In the early 1440s he set about designing a new alphabet from scratch. Scholars of his Hall of Worthies helped, but the court annals for the last month of 1443 credit the king himself: \"This month, His Majesty personally created the 28 letters of <em>eonmun</em> … simple and concise, yet endlessly versatile.\" The result, completed in 1443 and formally promulgated in 1446 with the publication of the <strong>Hunminjeongeum</strong> (訓民正音, <em>Correct Sounds for the Instruction of the People</em>, in the annotated <em>Haerye</em> 解例 edition), was <strong>Hangul</strong>: 28 letters (17 consonants + 11 vowels, of which 24 remain in use today).</p>\n<p>The design follows a systematic theory. Consonant letters visually depict the <em>position of the vocal tract</em> during articulation: ㄱ (g/k) shows the tongue root blocking the throat; ㄴ (n) shows the tongue tip touching the palate; ㅁ (m) shows the closed mouth. Vowels are built from three philosophical elements: a dot (heaven ·), a horizontal line (earth ㅡ), and a vertical line (man ㅣ). Letters combine into syllable blocks — so 한 = ㅎ + ㅏ + ㄴ — making the script both alphabetic and visually syllabic.</p>\n<h3>The Scholar's Revolt: Choe Manri's Memorial</h3>\n<p>In early 1444, <strong>Choe Manri</strong> (崔萬理), deputy head of the Hall of Worthies, led a group of its scholars in submitting a formal protest to Sejong. Their objections focused on relations with Ming China, scholarship and government:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Cultural subordination:</strong> Korea belonged to Chinese civilisation, and within China itself, however much regional speech differed, no dialect had ever been given its own script. Only \"barbarians\" did that: the memorial names the Mongols, the Tangut, the Jurchen, the Japanese and the Tibetans. To make a separate script was to abandon China and become like them.</li>\n  <li><strong>Practical objection:</strong> <em>Idu</em>, the old system of writing Korean with borrowed Chinese characters, had served officials for centuries without trouble. And clear writing would not end unjust verdicts; that depended on the officials who handled the case, not on the script.</li>\n  <li><strong>Fear for learning:</strong> If a clerk could get ahead with a script of just 27 letters (as the memorial counted them), who would toil over the Confucian classics? Within decades, they warned, hardly anyone would know Chinese characters.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Sejong answered in person. Idu, he pointed out, also used characters for their sound, and it had been made to help the people. \"If that was to help the people, then my <em>eonmun</em> is to help the people too.\" He then sent Choe and six co-signers to the State Tribunal. They were freed the next day, and only one of them, Jeong Chang-son, lost his post. The work went ahead.</p>\n<h3>Who Actually Used It First</h3>\n<p>Early users of Hangul included <strong>women</strong> and <strong>Buddhists</strong>. Yangban men wrote their official documents and literary poetry in classical Chinese, viewing Hangul as beneath them. Its usual name, <em>eonmun</em> (諺文, \"vernacular script\"), was the word Sejong's own court used, but it was set against <em>jinmun</em> (眞文), the \"true script\" of Chinese characters, and the contrast made the ranking plain. But aristocratic women, barred from the Confucian examination system, adopted Hangul for personal letters, diaries, and household records. Buddhist monks used it to translate sutras and write devotional texts for lay audiences.</p>\n<p>The genre of <strong>vernacular fiction</strong> (<em>gososel</em>) grew through Hangul, including the widely read 18th-century novel <em>Chunhyangjeon</em> (春香傳), whose heroine's story of loyalty across class lines became a national classic. These works contributed to the use of Hangul beyond court and official settings.</p>\n<div class=\"trivia-actions\">\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"focus\" data-code=\"ko_hun\">📍 See historical Hangul (hanja-era) readings</button>\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"人\">🔢 View 「人」 across Korean variants</button>\n</div>\n<h3>Restrictions and Later Standardisation</h3>\n<p>In 1504, King <strong>Yeonsangun</strong> banned the teaching and use of Hangul after anonymous writings criticising him circulated in the script. The ban was lifted after his overthrow in 1506, but Hangul's prestige remained low for centuries. The late 19th-century nationalist movement reclaimed it as a symbol of Korean identity distinct from Chinese cultural dominance — a decree of November 1894, during the Gabo Reform, made it the preferred script for official documents under the name <em>gungmun</em> (국문, national script), although mixed script was still allowed. In 1896 the newspaper <em>The Independent</em> (<em>Dongnip sinmun</em>) printed its Korean pages entirely in Hangul. The name <strong>Hangul</strong> (한글, great/Korean script) came a generation later: <strong>Ju Si-gyeong</strong> and his circle of language reformers coined it in the early 1910s to replace the old pejorative <em>eonmun</em>, and it became the general term only after the journal <em>Hangul</em> began publication in 1927. After the Japanese colonial period (1910–1945), independence in 1945 made Hangul the unambiguous national script of both Koreas.</p>\n<p>Today Hangul is studied for the systematic relationship between its letter shapes and phonetic features. It is the principal script of Korean in both North and South Korea, with spelling and typographic conventions that differ between the two states.</p>"},"sources":["Ledyard, Gari. 1966. 'The Korean Language Reform of 1446.' PhD dissertation, University of California, Berkeley.","Kim-Renaud, Young-Key (ed.). 1997. 'The Korean Alphabet: Its History and Structure.' Honolulu: University of Hawai'i Press.","Sejong the Great. 1446. 'Hunminjeongeum Haerye' (訓民正音解例). Facsimile edition, National Museum of Korea.",{"text":"UNESCO Memory of the World — Hunminjeongeum (1997)","url":"https://www.unesco.org/en/memory-world"},"Sampson, Geoffrey. 1985. 'Writing Systems: A Linguistic Introduction.' Stanford University Press. (Chapter on Korean)",{"text":"Wikipedia: Hangul","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hangul"}]},{"id":"chu-nom-vietnam-script","icon":"📜","tags":["sinosphere","vietnam","writing-system","history","colonial"],"title":{"en":"Chữ Nôm — A Vietnamese Han-Character Writing Tradition","ja":"字喃（チュノム）——植民地化が消した、ベトナムの二万字","ko":"쯔놈 — 식민지배가 지운 베트남의 2만 자 문자","zh":"字喃——殖民主义抹去的越南两万字母文字","yue":"字喃——殖民主義抹去嘅越南兩萬字文字","vi":"Chữ Nôm — hệ chữ viết 20.000 ký tự của Việt Nam bị chủ nghĩa thực dân xóa sổ","th":"ชื่อนม — อักษร 20,000 ตัวของเวียดนามที่ถูกลบเลือนโดยลัทธิอาณานิคม","id":"Chữ Nôm — aksara 20.000 karakter Vietnam yang dihapus kolonialisme","hi":"चữ नोम — वियतनाम की 20,000 वर्णों वाली लिपि जिसे उपनिवेशवाद ने मिटाया","de":"Chữ Nôm — Vietnams 20.000-Zeichen-Schrift, die der Kolonialismus auslöschte","fr":"Le Chữ Nôm — l'écriture vietnamienne de 20 000 caractères effacée par la colonisation","it":"Chữ Nôm — la scrittura vietnamita di 20.000 caratteri cancellata dal colonialismo","es":"Chữ Nôm — el alfabeto vietnamita de 20.000 caracteres borrado por el colonialismo","pt":"Chữ Nôm — a escrita vietnamita de 20.000 caracteres apagada pelo colonialismo","ru":"Чыном — вьетнамское письмо из 20 000 знаков, уничтоженное колониализмом","uk":"Чином — в'єтнамське письмо з 20 000 знаків, знищене колоніалізмом","ar":"شو نوم — كتابة فيتنامية من 20,000 حرف محتها الاستعمار","he":"צ'ו נום — כתב וייטנאמי של 20,000 סימנים שהקולוניאליזם מחק","sw":"Chữ Nôm — maandishi ya Vietnam ya herufi 20,000 yaliyofutwa na ukoloni"},"summary":{"en":"For nearly a thousand years, Vietnamese scholars wrote their language in an adapted Han-character script called Chữ Nôm. French colonial rule later replaced it with the Latin alphabet devised by Jesuit missionaries, limiting direct access to much of the earlier literary tradition.","ja":"約千年にわたり、ベトナムの学者たちは「字喃（チュノム）」と呼ばれる独自の漢字変形文字でベトナム語を書いてきた。イエズス会宣教師が考案したラテン文字に、フランス植民地支配が置き換えるまで。文学の伝統はほぼ永遠に失われかけた。","ko":"약 천 년 동안 베트남 학자들은 '쯔놈'이라는 고유 한자 변형 문자로 베트남어를 기록했다. 예수회 선교사들이 고안한 라틴 문자로 프랑스 식민 지배가 대체하기 전까지, 문학 전통 하나가 영원히 사라질 뻔했다.","zh":"近千年来，越南学者用独特的汉字改编文字「字喃」书写越南语，直到法国殖民统治用耶稣会传教士创制的拉丁字母取而代之，一段文学传统险些永久消失。","yue":"近千年嚟，越南學者用獨特嘅漢字改編文字「字喃」書寫越南語，直到法國殖民統治用耶穌會傳教士創制嘅拉丁字母取代佢，一段文學傳統幾乎永遠消失。","vi":"Trong gần một nghìn năm, các học giả Việt Nam đã viết tiếng Việt bằng chữ Nôm — một hệ chữ viết độc đáo phỏng theo chữ Hán — cho đến khi chính quyền thực dân Pháp thay thế nó bằng chữ Latin do các giáo sĩ Dòng Tên soạn ra, và một truyền thống văn học gần như bị mất vĩnh viễn.","th":"เกือบหนึ่งพันปีที่นักวิชาการเวียดนามเขียนภาษาเวียดนามด้วยอักษรจื๋อโนม ซึ่งดัดแปลงจากอักษรจีน จนกระทั่งการปกครองอาณานิคมฝรั่งเศสเข้าแทนที่ด้วยอักษรละตินที่มิชชันนารีคณะเยสุอิตคิดค้นขึ้น และประเพณีวรรณกรรมหนึ่งเกือบสูญหายไปตลอดกาล","id":"Selama hampir seribu tahun, para sarjana Vietnam menulis bahasanya dalam aksara unik yang diadaptasi dari karakter Han, disebut Chữ Nôm — hingga pemerintahan kolonial Prancis menggantinya dengan alfabet Latin yang dirancang para misionaris Yesuit, dan satu tradisi sastra hampir hilang selamanya.","hi":"लगभग एक हजार साल तक वियतनामी विद्वानों ने अपनी भाषा को हान-अक्षर आधारित विशिष्ट लिपि चữ नोम में लिखा — जब तक फ्रांसीसी औपनिवेशिक शासन ने इसे जेसुइट मिशनरियों द्वारा गढ़ी गई लैटिन वर्णमाला से प्रतिस्थापित नहीं किया और एक साहित्यिक परंपरा लगभग हमेशा के लिए खो गई।","de":"Fast tausend Jahre lang schrieben vietnamesische Gelehrte ihre Sprache in einem einzigartigen, aus Han-Zeichen abgeleiteten Schriftsystem namens Chữ Nôm — bis die französische Kolonialherrschaft es durch das von Jesuitenmissionaren entwickelte lateinische Alphabet ersetzte und eine literarische Tradition fast für immer verloren ging.","fr":"Pendant près d'un millénaire, les érudits vietnamiens écrivaient leur langue dans un système d'écriture unique dérivé des caractères han, appelé Chữ Nôm — jusqu'à ce que la colonisation française le remplace par l'alphabet latin conçu par des missionnaires jésuites, et qu'une tradition littéraire disparaisse presque pour toujours.","it":"Per quasi mille anni, gli studiosi vietnamiti scrissero la loro lingua in un sistema di scrittura unico derivato dai caratteri han, chiamato Chữ Nôm — finché il dominio coloniale francese lo sostituì con l'alfabeto latino ideato dai missionari gesuiti, e una tradizione letteraria rischiò di scomparire per sempre.","es":"Durante casi mil años, los estudiosos vietnamitas escribieron su idioma con un sistema de escritura único derivado de caracteres han, llamado Chữ Nôm — hasta que el dominio colonial francés lo reemplazó con el alfabeto latino ideado por misioneros jesuitas, y una tradición literaria casi se perdió para siempre.","pt":"Durante quase mil anos, os estudiosos vietnamitas escreveram sua língua num sistema de escrita único derivado de caracteres han, chamado Chữ Nôm — até que o domínio colonial francês o substituiu pelo alfabeto latino concebido por missionários jesuítas, e uma tradição literária quase se perdeu para sempre.","ru":"Почти тысячу лет вьетнамские учёные записывали свой язык уникальным письмом на основе китайских иероглифов — чыномом — пока французская колониальная администрация не заменила его латиницей, разработанной иезуитскими миссионерами, и литературная традиция едва не исчезла навсегда.","uk":"Майже тисячу років в'єтнамські вчені записували свою мову унікальним письмом на основі китайських ієрогліфів — чиномом — доки французька колоніальна влада не замінила його латиницею, яку розробили місіонери-єзуїти, і літературна традиція ледве не зникла назавжди.","ar":"لما يقارب ألف عام، كتب العلماء الفيتناميون لغتهم بنظام كتابة فريد مشتق من الحروف الصينية يُعرف بـ«شو نوم»، حتى استبدله الحكم الاستعماري الفرنسي بالأبجدية اللاتينية التي وضعها المبشرون اليسوعيون وكاد تراث أدبي بأكمله يضيع إلى الأبد.","he":"כמעט אלף שנה כתבו חוקרים וייטנאמים את שפתם בכתב ייחודי המבוסס על תווי האן, הנקרא צ'ו נום — עד שהשלטון הקולוניאלי הצרפתי החליף אותו באלפבית הלטיני שפיתחו מיסיונרים ישועים, ומסורת ספרותית כמעט אבדה לנצח.","sw":"Kwa karibu miaka elfu moja, wasomi wa Vietnam waliandika lugha yao kwa mfumo wa maandishi wa kipekee uliotokana na herufi za Han, unaoitwa Chữ Nôm — hadi utawala wa kikoloni wa Ufaransa ulipoubadilisha kwa alfabeti ya Kilatini iliyobuniwa na wamishonari wa Kiyesuiti, na mila ya fasihi karibu kupotea milele."},"body":{"en":"<h3>A script born from necessity</h3>\n<p>Northern Vietnam was ruled by successive Chinese dynasties for about a thousand years, from 111 BCE until independence in 939, and classical Chinese (<em>chữ Hán</em>) remained the written language of administration, law and literature long after. But Vietnamese is not Chinese. It belongs to the Austroasiatic family (its distant relatives include Khmer), with its own grammar and its own native vocabulary. Writing the spoken language in borrowed Chinese characters was as awkward as writing English in Japanese kanji.</p>\n<p>Probably after independence in the 10th century, Vietnamese writers developed a solution (the oldest firmly dated example, a list of names on a stele at Báo Ân pagoda, is from <strong>1209</strong>): <strong>Chữ Nôm</strong> (字喃), literally \"Southern script\" or \"vernacular script.\" Instead of inventing an entirely new alphabet, they took Chinese characters and bent them to Vietnamese ends using two main strategies:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Phonetic borrowing:</strong> A Chinese character was chosen for its sound, whatever its meaning. 沒 means \"not have\" in Chinese, but its Sino-Vietnamese reading is <em>một</em>, so Nôm used it to write the native word <em>một</em>, \"one\".</li>\n  <li><strong>Semantic + phonetic compounds:</strong> Two characters were fused, one giving the meaning and one the sound, to make a new glyph found in no Chinese dictionary. 𠀧 <em>ba</em> \"three\" joins 巴 <em>ba</em> (for the sound) with 三 \"three\" (for the meaning); 媄 <em>mẹ</em> \"mother\" joins 女 \"woman\" with 美 <em>mỹ</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"trivia-actions\">\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"panto\" data-lat=\"16.0\" data-lng=\"108.0\" data-zoom=\"5\">📍 Fly to Vietnam</button>\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"南\">🔢 Show 南 (south) across languages</button>\n</div>\n\n<h3>The scale: a repertoire of 20,000 characters</h3>\n<p>The Chinese standard repertoire (GB 18030) encodes around 87,000 characters; the Nôm Na Group's 2005 list of Chữ Nôm runs to nearly <strong>20,000 characters</strong>, of which several thousand are Vietnamese inventions — glyphs that appear in no Chinese text, created purely to represent Vietnamese words. Vietnam's national standard TCVN 6909:2001 fixes 9,299 Nôm glyphs, about half of them Vietnam-specific; the full range may never be precisely counted because many manuscripts are still undigitized.</p>\n<p>The script was never standardized. Different writers could spell the same word with different characters, and a reader also had to know classical Chinese to make sense of the components, which made Nôm hard to learn. That did not stop it spreading: popular Nôm works, <em>Truyện Kiều</em> among them, were printed from carved woodblocks and sold widely in the 19th century. Twice, under the Hồ dynasty (1400–1407) and the Tây Sơn (1778–1802), rulers even made Nôm the official script, but each policy died with the regime.</p>\n\n<h3>The masterpiece: Truyện Kiều</h3>\n<p>The single greatest monument of Chữ Nôm literature is <strong>Truyện Kiều</strong> (The Tale of Kiều), a 3,254-line epic poem by <strong>Nguyễn Du</strong> (1765–1820). The story, adapted from the Chinese novel <em>Jin Yun Qiao zhuan</em>, follows a young woman named Kiều through fifteen years of tragedy, prostitution, war and eventual reunion. It is written in <em>lục bát</em>, the native Vietnamese \"six-eight\" metre of alternating six- and eight-syllable lines, and is widely regarded as Vietnam's national poem; many Vietnamese can quote lines from it.</p>\n<p>The irony is exquisite: Vietnam's greatest work of literature was written in a script that almost nobody alive today can read in its original form. When you read Truyện Kiều in a modern edition, you are reading a transliteration into the Latin-based <em>quốc ngữ</em>, not the original glyphs Nguyễn Du wrote.</p>\n\n<h3>The erasure</h3>\n<p>French colonization arrived in stages from 1858. Catholic missionaries had already been promoting the Latin-based quốc ngữ since the 17th century (see the companion article on Alexandre de Rhodes). French officials promoted it, partly to weaken the hold of Chinese learning and of the scholar class behind it; critics, then and since, point out that a population reading only the new script was cut off from pre-colonial texts and cultural memory. The Confucian examinations, which tested classical Chinese, were abolished in Tonkin in 1915 and held for the last time at Huế on 4 January <strong>1919</strong>. Reading Nôm had always rested on that same classical training, and now the training itself was gone. Within a generation, functional literacy in the script collapsed.</p>\n\n<h3>The revival that might not arrive in time</h3>\n<p>The <strong>Hán Nôm Institute</strong> (Viện Nghiên cứu Hán Nôm), founded in Hanoi in 1970, has been racing against time to digitize manuscripts before they decay. After Vietnam's own national standards (TCVN 5773:1993 and TCVN 6909:2001), Unicode took in thousands of Chữ Nôm characters, most of the Vietnam-specific ones in CJK Unified Ideographs Extension B and later extension blocks, so the script can now be typed with standard fonts on any device. A small but passionate community of scholars and hobbyists is relearning it. But the Vietnamese Nôm Preservation Foundation estimates that fewer than 100 scholars worldwide can read it, so the window is closing fast.</p>"},"sources":["Nguyễn Đình Hoà, 'Chữ Nôm: The Demotic System of Writing in Vietnam', Journal of the American Oriental Society, 1959","Hồ Xuân Hương (trans. John Balaban), 'Spring Essence: The Poetry of Hồ Xuân Hương', Copper Canyon Press, 2000",{"text":"Hán Nôm Institute — digitisation project","url":"https://hannom.vass.gov.vn/"},{"text":"Unicode CJK Unified Ideographs Extension G (including Nôm)","url":"https://www.unicode.org/charts/PDF/U30000.pdf"},"Nguyễn Du (trans. Huỳnh Sanh Thông), 'The Tale of Kiều', Yale University Press, 1983","DeFrancis, John, 'Colonialism and Language Policy in Viet Nam', Mouton, 1977"]},{"id":"quoc-ngu-rhodes","icon":"✍️","tags":["sinosphere","vietnam","writing-system","missionary","colonial","phonology"],"title":{"en":"Quốc Ngữ and Alexandre de Rhodes's 1651 Dictionary","ja":"1651年のイエズス会士の辞書がベトナムに新しいアルファベットをもたらした理由","ko":"1651년 예수회 사제의 사전이 베트남에 새 알파벳을 가져다 준 이유","zh":"1651年耶稣会士的字典如何给越南带来新字母——以及它为何长久留存","yue":"1651年耶穌會士嘅字典點樣為越南帶來新字母——同埋點解會留存至今","vi":"Từ điển năm 1651 của một tu sĩ Dòng Tên đã tạo ra chữ quốc ngữ như thế nào — và tại sao nó ở lại mãi","th":"พจนานุกรมปี 1651 ของพระเยสุอิตมอบอักษรใหม่ให้เวียดนามได้อย่างไร — และเหตุใดจึงคงอยู่ถาวร","id":"Bagaimana kamus Jesuit 1651 memberi Vietnam alfabet baru — dan mengapa bertahan lama","hi":"1651 के जेसुइट शब्दकोश ने वियतनाम को नई वर्णमाला कैसे दी — और वह क्यों टिकी रही","de":"Wie das Wörterbuch eines Jesuiten von 1651 Vietnam ein neues Alphabet gab — und warum es blieb","fr":"Comment le dictionnaire d'un jésuite de 1651 a donné un nouvel alphabet au Viêt Nam — et pourquoi il a perduré","it":"Come il dizionario di un gesuita del 1651 diede al Vietnam un nuovo alfabeto — e perché rimase","es":"Cómo el diccionario de un jesuita de 1651 dio a Vietnam un nuevo alfabeto — y por qué perduró","pt":"Como o dicionário de um jesuíta de 1651 deu ao Vietname um novo alfabeto — e por que ficou","ru":"Как словарь иезуита 1651 года дал Вьетнаму новый алфавит — и почему он закрепился","uk":"Як словник єзуїта 1651 року дав В'єтнаму новий алфавіт — і чому він залишився","ar":"كيف منح قاموس يسوعي من عام 1651 فيتنام أبجدية جديدة — ولماذا بقيت","he":"כיצד מילון יזואיטי משנת 1651 נתן לווייטנאם אלפבית חדש — ומדוע הוא נשאר","sw":"Jinsi kamusi ya Jesuit ya 1651 ilivyompa Vietnam alfabeti mpya — na kwa nini iliishi"},"summary":{"en":"Alexandre de Rhodes published a trilingual dictionary in 1651 and helped systematize Latin-script Vietnamese. The writing system also drew on Portuguese predecessors, uses diacritics for six tones, and expanded under colonial administration.","ja":"アレクサンドル・ド・ロードスは1651年に三言語辞典を出版し、ラテン文字のベトナム語表記体系を作ったとされる。しかし実際には、ポルトガル人の先人、6声調を表す分音符号、そして宣教師の道具を文化的ブルドーザーに変えた植民地行政が絡む物語だ。","ko":"알렉산드르 드 로드가 1651년 삼언어 사전을 출판하고 라틴 문자 베트남어 표기 체계를 만든 것으로 알려져 있다. 그러나 실제 이야기에는 포르투갈 선배들, 발음 구별 부호로 표시된 6성조, 그리고 선교사의 도구를 문화적 불도저로 만든 식민지 행정이 얽혀 있다.","zh":"亚历山大·德·罗德于1651年出版三语字典，被认为创造了拉丁字母越南语书写系统——但真实故事涉及葡萄牙先驱者、用变音符号标注的六个声调，以及将传教工具变成文化推土机的殖民行政。","yue":"亞歷山大·德·羅德喺1651年出版三語字典，被認為係拉丁字母越南語書寫系統嘅創造者——但真實故事涉及葡萄牙前輩、用變音符號標註嘅六個聲調，以及將傳教工具變成文化推土機嘅殖民行政。","vi":"Alexandre de Rhodes xuất bản từ điển tam ngữ năm 1651 và được ghi nhận là người tạo ra hệ chữ viết Latin cho tiếng Việt — nhưng câu chuyện thực sự liên quan đến các bậc tiền bối người Bồ Đào Nha, sáu thanh điệu được ký hiệu bằng dấu phụ, và nền hành chính thuộc địa đã biến công cụ truyền giáo thành một cỗ máy ủi văn hóa.","th":"อาเลกซานดร์ เดอ โรดส์ตีพิมพ์พจนานุกรมไตรภาษาในปี 1651 และได้รับเครดิตว่าสร้างระบบการเขียนภาษาเวียดนามด้วยอักษรละติน — แต่เรื่องจริงเกี่ยวข้องกับบรรพบุรุษชาวโปรตุเกส วรรณยุกต์หกระดับที่แสดงด้วยเครื่องหมายวรรณยุกต์ และการบริหารอาณานิคมที่เปลี่ยนเครื่องมือของมิชชันนารีเป็นรถปราบดินทางวัฒนธรรม","id":"Alexandre de Rhodes menerbitkan kamus trilingualnya pada 1651 dan dikreditkan menciptakan sistem penulisan Latin untuk bahasa Vietnam — tetapi kisah nyatanya melibatkan pendahulu Portugis, enam nada yang diwakili diakritik, dan administrasi kolonial yang mengubah alat misionaris menjadi buldoser budaya.","hi":"अलेक्जेंडर डी रोड्स ने 1651 में अपना त्रिभाषी शब्दकोश प्रकाशित किया और उन्हें वियतनामी के लिए लैटिन-लिपि लेखन प्रणाली बनाने का श्रेय दिया जाता है — लेकिन वास्तविक कहानी में पुर्तगाली पूर्वगामी, डायक्रिटिक्स में छह स्वर और एक औपनिवेशिक प्रशासन शामिल है जिसने मिशनरी उपकरण को सांस्कृतिक बुलडोज़र में बदल दिया।","de":"Alexandre de Rhodes veröffentlichte 1651 sein dreisprachiges Wörterbuch und gilt als Schöpfer des lateinschriftlichen Vietnamesisch-Schreibsystems — doch die wahre Geschichte enthält portugiesische Vorläufer, sechs durch Diakritika kodierte Töne und eine Kolonialverwaltung, die aus einem Missionarswerkzeug einen kulturellen Bulldozer machte.","fr":"Alexandre de Rhodes publia son dictionnaire trilingue en 1651 et est crédité d'avoir créé le système d'écriture en alphabet latin pour le vietnamien — mais la véritable histoire implique des précurseurs portugais, six tons rendus par des diacritiques et une administration coloniale qui transforma un outil missionnaire en bulldozer culturel.","it":"Alexandre de Rhodes pubblicò il suo dizionario trilingue nel 1651 e viene accreditato per la creazione del sistema di scrittura in alfabeto latino per il vietnamita — ma la vera storia coinvolge precursori portoghesi, sei toni resi con diacritici e un'amministrazione coloniale che trasformò uno strumento missionario in un bulldozer culturale.","es":"Alexandre de Rhodes publicó su diccionario trilingüe en 1651 y se le atribuye haber creado el sistema de escritura en alfabeto latino para el vietnamita — pero la historia real involucra precursores portugueses, seis tonos representados con diacríticos y una administración colonial que convirtió una herramienta misionera en una excavadora cultural.","pt":"Alexandre de Rhodes publicou o seu dicionário trilíngue em 1651 e é creditado por ter criado o sistema de escrita em alfabeto latino para o vietnamita — mas a história real envolve predecessores portugueses, seis tons representados por diacríticos e uma administração colonial que transformou uma ferramenta missionária num bulldozer cultural.","ru":"Александр де Род опубликовал свой трёхъязычный словарь в 1651 году и считается создателем системы записи вьетнамского языка латиницей — однако подлинная история включает португальских предшественников, шесть тонов, закодированных диакритическими знаками, и колониальную администрацию, превратившую миссионерский инструмент в культурный бульдозер.","uk":"Александр де Род опублікував свій тримовний словник 1651 року і вважається творцем системи запису в'єтнамської мови латиницею — однак справжня історія включає португальських попередників, шість тонів, закодованих діакритичними знаками, і колоніальну адміністрацію, яка перетворила місіонерський інструмент на культурний бульдозер.","ar":"نشر ألكسندر دو رود قاموسه الثلاثي اللغات عام 1651 ويُنسب إليه ابتكار نظام الكتابة بالحروف اللاتينية للفيتنامية — لكنّ القصة الحقيقية تشمل أسلافاً برتغاليين، وستّ نبرات رُمز إليها بعلامات تشكيل، وإدارة استعمارية حوّلت أداة تبشيرية إلى جرّافة ثقافية.","he":"אלכסנדר דה רוד פרסם את מילונו התלת-לשוני ב-1651 וזוכה ביצירת מערכת הכתיבה הלטינית לוייטנאמית — אך הסיפור האמיתי כולל קודמים פורטוגזים, שישה טונים הנרשמים בסימנים דיאקריטיים, ומינהל קולוניאלי שהפך כלי מיסיונרי לבולדוזר תרבותי.","sw":"Alexandre de Rhodes alichapisha kamusi yake ya lugha tatu mnamo 1651 na anapewa sifa ya kuunda mfumo wa kuandika kwa alfabeti ya Kilatini kwa Kivietinamu — lakini hadithi ya kweli inahusisha watangulizi wa Kireno, sauti sita zilizoonyeshwa kwa alama za diakritiki, na utawala wa kikoloni ulioibadilisha zana ya kimisionari kuwa bulldozer ya kitamaduni."},"body":{"en":"<h3>The man who didn't start it, but finished it</h3>\n<p><strong>Alexandre de Rhodes</strong> (1593–1660; some sources give 1591 for his birth) was a Jesuit missionary from Avignon, then a papal enclave inside what is now France. He arrived in Vietnam in 1624. He is widely — and somewhat unfairly — credited as the sole inventor of <strong>quốc ngữ</strong> (國語, \"national language script\"), the Latin-based writing system that Vietnamese uses today. The reality is more collaborative and more interesting.</p>\n<p>The real pioneer was <strong>Francisco de Pina</strong>, a Portuguese Jesuit who arrived in Vietnam around 1617 and became the first European known to have mastered spoken Vietnamese. De Pina began developing a romanization system and training other missionaries to use tonal marks, but he drowned in a shipwreck in 1625, leaving his work unfinished. Rhodes had learned Vietnamese from de Pina. He also drew on dictionaries compiled by two other Portuguese Jesuits, Gaspar do Amaral and António Barbosa, and it was Rhodes who pulled all this earlier work together into one system.</p>\n\n<div class=\"trivia-actions\">\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"panto\" data-lat=\"16.0\" data-lng=\"108.0\" data-zoom=\"5\">📍 Fly to Vietnam</button>\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"口\">🔢 See 口 (mouth/language) across scripts</button>\n</div>\n\n<h3>The 1651 dictionary: a linguistic triumph</h3>\n<p>Rhodes' <em>Dictionarium Annamiticum Lusitanum et Latinum</em> (\"Vietnamese-Portuguese-Latin Dictionary\") was printed in Rome in 1651 by the press of the Propaganda Fide, the Church's missionary congregation. It has about 8,000 Vietnamese entries, and each one is rendered in the new romanized script, then glossed in Portuguese and Latin. But the dictionary's true achievement is its phonological apparatus:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Vietnamese has <strong>six lexical tones</strong> (in northern Vietnamese). Rhodes had to show all six with Latin letters and accent marks. De Pina and his colleagues had already started marking tones, but it was the 1651 dictionary that set out the full system in print.</li>\n  <li>He used the acute accent (´), grave accent (`), hook above (̉), tilde (˜), and a dot below (.) — some stacked on top of each other — to distinguish five of the six tones. The sixth (level/flat) is unmarked.</li>\n  <li>He also devised digraphs (nh, ng, kh, gh, ph, th, tr, ch) and modified vowels (ă, â, ê, ô, ơ, ư) to capture Vietnamese phonemes that Latin had no letters for.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The result was a script that was <em>far quicker to learn than Chữ Nôm</em>: a few dozen letters and marks instead of thousands of characters.</p><p>It was not yet modern Vietnamese, though. Rhodes spelled consonant clusters that the language has since lost, writing <em>bl-</em>, <em>ml-</em> and <em>tl-</em> at the start of words. Words spelled with <em>tl-</em> are written with <em>tr-</em> today, and early quốc ngữ documents spell the word for 'big', modern <em>lớn</em>, as <em>mlớn</em> or <em>blớn</em>. The spellings show the <em>tl-</em> to <em>tr-</em> change was complete by the end of the 18th century.</p>\n\n<h3>Why Vietnam and not Japan or Korea?</h3>\n<p>Christian missionaries attempted romanization in Japan too. The Portuguese Jesuit João Rodrigues produced a superb <em>Arte da Lingoa de Iapam</em> (1604) and romanized dictionaries, but Japan banned Christianity and expelled the missionaries in 1614, barred Portuguese ships in 1639, and the romanization died with them. Korea saw no comparable attempt at all: Catholicism only reached the peninsula in the 1780s, and the earliest Western romanizations of Korean are nineteenth-century (Siebold 1832; the Ross system, 1882). By then Korea already had <strong>Hangul</strong> — a domestically invented alphabet, long dismissed by the Confucian literati as 諺文 <em>eonmun</em>, \"vulgar script,\" but ideally suited to the vernacular, and the missionaries simply adopted it rather than inventing an alphabet of their own.</p>\n<p>Vietnam had no equivalent of Hangul. Chữ Nôm was brilliant but never became truly popular: it was unstandardized, difficult, and closely associated with the Vietnamese elite. When French colonizers aggressively promoted quốc ngữ — first making it the required script for official documents in the south (1882), then abolishing the Confucian examinations in 1919 — the population simply had no alternative rallying point. The Latin script filled the vacuum.</p>\n\n<h3>Tone diacritics: the system that survived</h3>\n<p>The tone marks Rhodes printed are still used today, almost unchanged:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Tone 1 (ngang — level): unmarked — <em>ma</em> (ghost)</li>\n  <li>Tone 2 (huyền — falling): grave accent — <em>mà</em> (but)</li>\n  <li>Tone 3 (sắc — rising): acute — <em>má</em> (mother)</li>\n  <li>Tone 4 (hỏi — dipping): hook — <em>mả</em> (tomb)</li>\n  <li>Tone 5 (ngã — broken): tilde — <em>mã</em> (horse)</li>\n  <li>Tone 6 (nặng — heavy): dot below — <em>mạ</em> (rice seedling)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Six words, one syllable each, completely different meanings — distinguished purely by diacritics stacked on a single vowel. The system is elegant but typographically demanding: a single Vietnamese vowel can carry two stacked marks simultaneously (e.g., <em>ộ</em> = circumflex + dot below), which caused enormous problems for early Vietnamese-language printing presses and keyboard designers alike.</p>\n\n<h3>The irony of legacy</h3>\n<p>Rhodes was expelled from Vietnam by Vietnamese authorities in 1645 for proselytizing. He made his way back to Rome by 1649 and spent the next five years in Italy and France lobbying for bishops and funding for the Vietnam mission — the groundwork for the Paris Foreign Missions Society, founded after he left. In November 1654 his superiors sent him not to Vietnam but to Persia, where he died at Isfahan in 1660. His 1651 dictionary, published in Rome while he was thousands of miles from Vietnam, became an important early reference for a writing system now used throughout the country. Missionaries developed quốc ngữ for Christian texts; it was later adopted for secular publishing, nationalist writing, education and state administration.</p>"},"sources":["Rhodes, Alexandre de, 'Dictionarium Annamiticum Lusitanum et Latinum', Sacra Congregatio de Propaganda Fide, Rome, 1651","Alves, Mark J., 'A Grammar of Pacoh: A Mon-Khmer Language of the Central Highlands of Vietnam', Pacific Linguistics 580, Australian National University, 2006 [context on Mon-Khmer phonology of the Vietnamese area]","DeFrancis, John, 'Colonialism and Language Policy in Viet Nam', Mouton, 1977",{"text":"Phan, Peter C., 'Mission and Catechesis: Alexandre de Rhodes and Inculturation in Seventeenth-Century Vietnam', Orbis Books, 1998","url":"https://www.worldcat.org/title/mission-and-catechesis"},"Marr, David G., 'Vietnamese Tradition on Trial 1920–1945', University of California Press, 1981",{"text":"Hội Bảo tồn Di sản chữ Nôm — digitisation and quốc ngữ history","url":"https://www.nomfoundation.org/"},{"text":"Vũ Đức Nghiệu (2019). \"Vietnamese initial consonant clusters in quốc ngữ documents from the 17th to early 19th centuries.\" Journal of the Southeast Asian Linguistics Society 12(1): 143–162.","url":"http://hdl.handle.net/10524/52448"},{"text":"Wikipedia: Dictionarium Annamiticum Lusitanum et Latinum","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dictionarium_Annamiticum_Lusitanum_et_Latinum"}]},{"id":"go-on-kan-on-to-on","icon":"📜","tags":["sinosphere","kanji","history","sino-japanese","phonology"],"title":{"en":"Go-on, Kan-on and Tō-on: Three Sino-Japanese Reading Layers","ja":"呉音・漢音・唐音 — なぜ一つの漢字に三種の音読みがあるのか","ko":"오음·한음·당음 — 하나의 한자에 세 가지 음독이 있는 이유","zh":"吴音、汉音、唐音——为何一个汉字有三种日语读音","yue":"吳音、漢音、唐音——點解一個漢字有三種日語讀音","vi":"Ngô âm, Hán âm, Đường âm — Vì sao một chữ Hán có ba cách đọc trong tiếng Nhật","th":"โงอง ฮันอง โทอง — เหตุใดอักษรคันจิหนึ่งตัวจึงมีสามการอ่าน","id":"Go-on, Kan-on, Tō-on — Mengapa satu kanji punya tiga cara baca","hi":"गो-ऑन, कान-ऑन, तो-ऑन — एक कांजी के तीन पाठ क्यों हैं","de":"Go-on, Kan-on, Tō-on — Warum ein Kanji drei Lesungen hat","fr":"Go-on, Kan-on, Tō-on — Pourquoi un kanji a trois lectures","it":"Go-on, Kan-on, Tō-on — Perché un kanji ha tre letture","es":"Go-on, Kan-on, Tō-on — Por qué un kanji tiene tres lecturas","pt":"Go-on, Kan-on, Tō-on — Por que um kanji tem três leituras","ru":"Го-он, Кан-он, То-он — Почему у одного кандзи три чтения","uk":"Ґо-он, Кан-он, То-он — Чому одне кандзі має три читання","ar":"غو-أون وكان-أون وتو-أون: لماذا لحرف كانجي واحد ثلاث قراءات","he":"גו-און, קאן-און, טו-און — מדוע לקאנג'י אחד שלוש קריאות","sw":"Go-on, Kan-on, Tō-on — Kwa nini kanji moja ina usomaji wa aina tatu"},"summary":{"en":"Japanese borrowed Chinese characters three separate times over a millennium, each time from a different dialect and era — leaving a single kanji with up to three distinct Sino-Japanese readings stacked inside it.","ja":"日本語は千年以上にわたって漢字を三度借用したが、そのたびに異なる時代・異なる方言から取り入れたため、一つの漢字に最大三種類の音読みが重層的に残っている。","ko":"일본어는 천 년 이상에 걸쳐 한자를 서로 다른 시대와 방언에서 세 번 차용하였고, 그 결과 하나의 한자에 최대 세 가지 한자음이 공존하게 되었다.","zh":"日语在逾千年间三度借入汉字，每次均来自不同时代与方言，因此单个汉字最多叠加了三种不同的汉字音读。","yue":"日語喺超過一千年嘅時間入面，分咗三次從唔同時代同方言借入漢字，結果一個漢字入面最多疊埋三種唔同嘅漢字音讀。","vi":"Tiếng Nhật đã vay mượn chữ Hán ba lần riêng biệt trong hơn một nghìn năm, mỗi lần từ một phương ngữ và thời đại khác nhau, khiến một chữ Hán có thể mang tới ba cách đọc Hán-Nhật khác nhau.","th":"ภาษาญี่ปุ่นยืมอักษรฮันจาสามครั้งในช่วงกว่าหนึ่งพันปี แต่ละครั้งมาจากสำเนียงและยุคสมัยที่แตกต่างกัน ทำให้คันจิตัวเดียวมีการอ่านแบบจีน-ญี่ปุ่นได้ถึงสามแบบ","id":"Bahasa Jepang meminjam aksara Cina tiga kali selama lebih dari satu milenium, masing-masing dari dialek dan era berbeda, sehingga satu kanji bisa memiliki hingga tiga cara baca Sino-Jepang.","hi":"जापानी भाषा ने एक सहस्राब्दी से अधिक समय में अलग-अलग बोलियों और कालों से तीन बार चीनी अक्षर उधार लिए, जिससे एक कांजी में तीन अलग पाठ संचित हो गए।","de":"Das Japanische entlehnte chinesische Schriftzeichen über mehr als ein Jahrtausend hinweg dreimal aus verschiedenen Dialekten und Epochen, sodass ein einziges Kanji bis zu drei sino-japanische Lesungen enthält.","fr":"Le japonais a emprunté les caractères chinois à trois reprises sur plus d'un millénaire, chaque fois à partir d'un dialecte et d'une époque différents, laissant un seul kanji porteur de trois lectures sino-japonaises.","it":"Il giapponese ha preso in prestito i caratteri cinesi per tre volte nel corso di più di un millennio, ogni volta da un dialetto e un'era diversi, lasciando un singolo kanji con fino a tre letture sino-giapponesi.","es":"El japonés tomó prestados los caracteres chinos tres veces separadas a lo largo de más de un milenio, cada vez de un dialecto y época distintos, dejando un solo kanji con hasta tres lecturas sino-japonesas.","pt":"O japonês tomou emprestado caracteres chineses três vezes separadas ao longo de mais de um milênio, cada vez de um dialeto e época diferentes, deixando um único kanji com até três leituras sino-japonesas.","ru":"Японский язык заимствовал китайские иероглифы трижды на протяжении более тысячи лет, каждый раз из разных диалектов и эпох, оставив в одном кандзи до трёх различных китайско-японских чтений.","uk":"Японська мова запозичувала китайські ієрогліфи тричі протягом понад тисячі років, щоразу з різних діалектів і епох, тому одне кандзі може мати до трьох різних китайсько-японських читань.","ar":"اقترضت اليابانية الحروف الصينية ثلاث مرات على مدى أكثر من ألف عام، في كل مرة من لهجة وحقبة مختلفتين، مما ترك لكل حرف كانجي واحد ما يصل إلى ثلاث قراءات صينية-يابانية متراكمة.","he":"היפנית שאלה תווים סיניים שלוש פעמים נפרדות במשך יותר מאלף שנה, בכל פעם מניב ותקופה שונים, מה שהותיר קאנג'י בודד עם עד שלוש קריאות סינו-יפניות.","sw":"Kijapani kilikopa herufi za Kichina mara tatu tofauti katika zaidi ya miaka elfu moja, kila wakati kutoka lahaja na enzi tofauti, na kusababisha kanji moja kuwa na hadi usomaji tatu wa Sino-Kijapani."},"body":{"en":"<h3>Three waves across a millennium</h3>\n<p>Open any Japanese dictionary and look up a common character like <strong>行</strong> (go, travel). You will find not one but <em>three</em> Sino-Japanese (音読み <em>on'yomi</em>) readings: <em>gyō</em>, <em>kō</em>, and <em>an</em>. They are not alternative spellings of the same pronunciation. They are three separate historical imports, each reflecting a different stage of Chinese phonology, borrowed centuries apart.</p>\n\n<div class=\"trivia-actions\">\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"行:1\">📖 Show 行 on the map</button>\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"日\">☀️ Show 日 on the map</button>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Layer 1 — 呉音 Go-on (5th–6th century)</h3>\n<p>The oldest layer arrived before Japan had any formal diplomatic contact with the Tang court. It came via the <strong>Korean peninsula</strong> — by tradition from Baekje — carrying the character readings associated with the <em>Southern Dynasties</em> capital at Jiankang (modern Nanjing), in the old land of Wu 呉. Buddhist monks carrying sutras were the main vector. The name <em>Go-on</em> (\"Wu sound\") is not evidence for that route, though: it only appears from the mid-Heian period, coined by promoters of Kan-on who borrowed the Tang capital's dismissive term for southern provincial speech. Until then these readings were simply called <em>wa-on</em> 和音, and the alternative traditional names 百済音 (<em>Kudara-on</em>) and 対馬音 (<em>Tsushima-on</em>) point at Korea instead. No contemporary document ties them directly to southern Chinese.</p>\n<p>Go-on preserves features of Middle Chinese that the Tang capital's speech was already losing: voiced consonants stay voiced, and nasal consonants stay nasal. Examples: <strong>経</strong> <em>kyō</em>, <strong>男</strong> <em>nan</em>, <strong>行</strong> <em>gyō</em>. The voiced <em>g-</em> in <em>gyō</em> reflects the Middle Chinese 匣 initial of 行 — the voiced fricative of *<em>ɦˠæŋ</em>, which Old Japanese, having no voiced fricative of its own, took in as <em>g-</em>. By the Chang'an standard behind Kan-on that initial had already lost its voicing, giving <em>kō</em>.</p>\n\n<h3>Layer 2 — 漢音 Kan-on (7th–9th century)</h3>\n<p>From the 7th century onward, Japanese government missions (<em>kentōshi</em> 遣唐使) traveled directly to Tang Chang'an and brought back the prestige pronunciation of the capital. This layer — <em>Kan-on</em>, \"Han sound\" — was pushed hard as the correct, scholarly standard. In 792 Emperor Kanmu ordered students of the Confucian classics to learn it, and monks who would not study Kan-on were refused passage to China. Yet it never drove Go-on out of everyday words or out of the temples. Most sutras are still chanted in Go-on: 如是我聞 \"Thus have I heard\" is <em>nyoze gamon</em>, and only the few sutras traditionally read in Kan-on turn it into <em>josi gabun</em>.</p>\n<p>Kan-on mirrors the speech of Chang'an in the 8th century, where two changes were under way. Voiced consonants were losing their voicing, so 行 <em>gyō</em> became <em>kō</em>. And nasal consonants were turning into voiced stops (<em>m</em> to <em>mb</em>, <em>n</em> to <em>nd</em>), so 男 <em>nan</em> became <em>dan</em> and 馬 \"horse\" became <em>ba</em>. Vowels shifted too: 経 <em>kyō</em> became <em>kei</em>. That is why Kan-on has so few readings starting with <em>m-</em> or <em>n-</em>.</p>\n\n<div class=\"trivia-actions\">\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"panto\" data-lat=\"34.26\" data-lng=\"108.95\" data-zoom=\"5\">🏯 Fly to Tang Chang'an (Xi'an)</button>\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"focus\" data-code=\"ja\">📍 Highlight Sino-Japanese on the map</button>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Layer 3 — 唐音 Tō-on (12th century onward)</h3>\n<p>Confusingly, <em>Tō-on</em> (\"Tang sound\") does not reflect the Tang dynasty at all. It is a later stratum arriving from Song, Yuan, and Ming dynasty China, mainly through Zen Buddhist temples, the tea ceremony, and cuisine. The name is an anachronism: long after the dynasty fell, 唐 went on serving in Japanese as a general word for China.</p>\n<p>Tō-on imports are fewer but recognisable in everyday objects: <strong>行灯</strong> <em>andon</em> (paper lantern), <strong>蒲団</strong> <em>futon</em>, <strong>椅子</strong> <em>isu</em> (chair), <strong>饅頭</strong> <em>manjū</em>. The <em>an-</em> in <em>andon</em> reflects a later Chinese pronunciation in which the Middle Chinese 匣 initial of 行 — a voiced fricative *<em>ɦ-</em>, never a nasal — had dropped away altogether (*<em>ɦaŋ</em> → <em>aŋ</em>), leaving a syllable whose final -ŋ Japanese took in as -n — something neither Go-on nor Kan-on captured.</p>\n\n<h3>Why does this matter?</h3>\n<p>The three layers preserve evidence from different periods of Chinese phonological history. By comparing Go-on, Kan-on, and Middle Chinese reconstructions, linguists can triangulate what sounds existed in Chinese across many centuries. Chinese rhyme books sort characters into sound classes, but because they spell characters only with other characters, they cannot show directly what anything sounded like; a kana spelling such as <em>dan</em> gives an outside clue, if only an approximate one. Japanese kanji readings therefore complement Chinese rhyme books, modern varieties and other Sino-Xenic traditions in historical reconstruction.</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Go-on</strong>: Buddhist vocabulary, older temple readings — 経 <em>kyō</em>, 行 <em>gyō</em>, 男 <em>nan</em></li>\n  <li><strong>Kan-on</strong>: Scholarly/official vocabulary — 経 <em>kei</em>, 行 <em>kō</em>, 男 <em>dan</em></li>\n  <li><strong>Tō-on</strong>: Zen/cuisine/everyday objects — 行灯 <em>andon</em>, 蒲団 <em>futon</em></li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"trivia-actions\">\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"南\">🧭 Try 南 (Go-on nan or na, as in 南無 namu / Kan-on dan)</button>\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"東\">🧭 Try 東 (Go-on tsū vs Kan-on tō)</button>\n</div>\n\n<p>The next time a Japanese learner asks \"why does 行 have so many readings?\", the answer is not sloppiness or accident. It is a compressed archive of a thousand years of cultural exchange, folded into a single character.</p>"},"sources":["Miyake, Marc Hideo (2003). Old Japanese: A Phonetic Reconstruction. RoutledgeCurzon.","Pulleyblank, Edwin G. (1984). Middle Chinese: A Study in Historical Phonology. University of British Columbia Press.","Frellesvig, Bjarke (2010). A History of the Japanese Language. Cambridge University Press. (Chapter 3: Sino-Japanese layers.)","Karlgren, Bernhard (1954). Compendium of Phonetics in Ancient and Archaic Chinese. Bulletin of the Museum of Far Eastern Antiquities 26.",{"text":"Wikipedia: Sino-Japanese vocabulary — Go-on, Kan-on, Tō-on","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sino-Japanese_vocabulary"},{"text":"Wiktionary appendix: Sino-Japanese readings","url":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Appendix:Japanese_glossary#on%27yomi"},{"text":"Japanese Wikipedia: 漢音 (Kan-on; 792 edict, denasalization)","url":"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/漢音"}]},{"id":"min-nan-wenbai","icon":"🗣️","tags":["sinosphere","min-nan","hokkien","phonology","historical-linguistics"],"title":{"en":"Min Nan Literary and Colloquial Readings","ja":"閩南語の文白異読 — 同じ漢字にある二つの読み","ko":"민난어의 문백이독 — 한자에 공존하는 두 독음","zh":"闽南语的文白异读——同一汉字的两套读音","yue":"閩南語嘅雙重生活——比唐代更古老嘅口語化石","vi":"Văn–bạch dị độc trong tiếng Mân Nam — hai cách đọc của cùng một chữ","th":"ชีวิตคู่ของภาษาหมิ่นใต้ — ฟอสซิลภาษาพูดที่เก่ากว่าราชวงศ์ถัง","id":"Kehidupan ganda Min Nan — fosil lisan yang lebih tua dari Tang","hi":"मिन नान का दोहरा जीवन — तांग से भी पुराने बोलचाल के जीवाश्म","de":"Min Nans Doppelleben — Umgangssprachliche Fossilien älter als die Tang-Dynastie","fr":"La double vie du Min Nan — des fossiles vernaculaires plus anciens que les Tang","it":"La doppia vita del Min Nan — fossili colloquiali più antichi dei Tang","es":"La doble vida del Min Nan — fósiles coloquiales más antiguos que los Tang","pt":"A vida dupla do Min Nan — fósseis coloquiais mais antigos que os Tang","ru":"Двойная жизнь Минь Нань — разговорные окаменелости старше Тан","uk":"Подвійне життя Мінь Нань — розмовні скам'янілості, старші за Тан","ar":"الحياة المزدوجة للغة مين نان — حفريات عامية أقدم من أسرة تانغ","he":"החיים הכפולים של מין-נאן — מאובנים עממיים קדומים מאסרת טאנג","sw":"Maisha mawili ya Min Nan — visukuku vya lugha ya mazungumzo vya zamani kuliko Tang"},"summary":{"en":"Min Nan (Hokkien/Taiwanese) gives many characters two readings, a colloquial one (白讀) and a literary one (文讀). The two layers come from different periods of Chinese, so they preserve evidence of older sound changes and bear on where Min sits within the Chinese family.","ja":"閩南語（ホーロー語・台湾語）では、多くの漢字に口語の「白讀」と文語の「文讀」という二つの読みがある。二つの層は中国語の異なる時代に由来するため、古い音変化の証拠を今に伝え、閩語が漢語の中でどこに位置するのかという問題にも深く関わっている。","ko":"민난어(호키엔어/타이완어)는 많은 한자에 두 가지 독음, 곧 구어음(白讀)과 문어음(文讀)을 둔다. 두 층은 서로 다른 시대의 중국어에서 왔기 때문에, 더 오래된 음운 변화의 증거를 간직하고 있으며 중국어 가족 안에서 민어가 어디에 자리하는지를 가늠하는 데도 실마리가 된다.","zh":"闽南语（福建话/台湾话）里，许多汉字都有两种读音：口语的白读和读书的文读。这两层来自汉语的不同时期，因而保存着更古老的音变证据，也关系到闽语在汉语大家族中的位置。","yue":"閩南語（福建話/台灣話）好多漢字都有兩個讀音：口語嘅白讀同讀書嘅文讀。兩層來自唔同時代嘅漢語，所以保存住更古老音變嘅證據，亦關乎閩語喺漢語大家族入面究竟企喺邊個位。","vi":"Tiếng Mân Nam (Hokkien/Đài Loan) cho nhiều chữ Hán hai cách đọc: một âm khẩu ngữ (白讀) và một âm đọc sách (文讀). Hai tầng này đến từ những thời kỳ khác nhau của tiếng Hán, nên chúng lưu giữ dấu vết của những biến đổi ngữ âm xa xưa và góp phần trả lời câu hỏi tiếng Mân đứng ở đâu trong đại gia đình tiếng Hán.","th":"ภาษาหมิ่นใต้ (ฮกเกี้ยน/ไต้หวัน) ให้อักษรจำนวนมากมีเสียงอ่านสองแบบ คือแบบภาษาพูด (白讀) และแบบวรรณกรรม (文讀) สองชั้นนี้มาจากภาษาจีนต่างยุคกัน จึงเก็บหลักฐานของการเปลี่ยนแปลงเสียงในยุคเก่าไว้ และช่วยตอบคำถามว่าหมิ่นอยู่ตรงไหนในตระกูลภาษาจีน","id":"Min Nan (Hokkien/Taiwan) memberi banyak karakter dua bacaan: bacaan sehari-hari (白讀) dan bacaan sastra (文讀). Kedua lapisan itu berasal dari zaman bahasa Tionghoa yang berbeda, sehingga menyimpan bukti perubahan bunyi yang lebih tua dan ikut menentukan letak Min di dalam keluarga bahasa Tionghoa.","hi":"मिन नान (होक्किएन/ताइवानी) में बहुत-से अक्षरों के दो पठन होते हैं — एक बोलचाल का (白讀) और एक साहित्यिक (文讀)। ये दोनों परतें चीनी के अलग-अलग दौर से आई हैं, इसलिए इनमें पुराने ध्वनि-परिवर्तनों के सबूत बचे हैं, और ये इस सवाल पर भी रोशनी डालती हैं कि चीनी परिवार में मिन की जगह कहाँ है।","de":"Min Nan (Hokkien/Taiwanesisch) gibt vielen Zeichen zwei Lesungen: eine umgangssprachliche (白讀) und eine literarische (文讀). Die beiden Schichten stammen aus verschiedenen Epochen des Chinesischen — deshalb bewahren sie Spuren älterer Lautwandel und sagen etwas darüber, wo das Min in der chinesischen Sprachfamilie steht.","fr":"Le Min Nan (hokkien/taïwanais) donne à beaucoup de caractères deux lectures : une vernaculaire (白讀) et une littéraire (文讀). Ces deux couches viennent d'époques différentes du chinois : elles gardent donc la trace de changements phonétiques plus anciens, et pèsent dans le débat sur la place du min au sein de la famille chinoise.","it":"Il Min Nan (hokkien/taiwanese) dà a molti caratteri due letture: una vernacolare (白讀) e una letteraria (文讀). I due strati vengono da epoche diverse del cinese, e così custodiscono le tracce di mutamenti fonetici più antichi e pesano nel dibattito su dove collocare il min all'interno della famiglia cinese.","es":"El Min Nan (hokkien/taiwanés) da a muchos caracteres dos lecturas, una coloquial (白讀) y otra literaria (文讀). Las dos capas proceden de épocas distintas del chino, así que conservan huellas de cambios fonéticos antiguos y pesan en el debate sobre el lugar del min dentro de la familia china.","pt":"O Min Nan (hokkien/taiwanês) dá a muitos caracteres duas leituras: uma coloquial (白讀) e uma literária (文讀). As duas camadas vêm de épocas diferentes do chinês, por isso guardam vestígios de mudanças sonoras antigas — e pesam no debate sobre o lugar do min dentro da família chinesa.","ru":"Минь Нань (хоккиен/тайваньский) даёт многим иероглифам два чтения — разговорное (白讀) и литературное (文讀). Эти два слоя восходят к разным эпохам китайского языка, поэтому хранят свидетельства более древних звуковых изменений и помогают понять, какое место минь занимает в китайской семье.","uk":"Мінь Нань (хоккієн/тайванська) дає багатьом ієрогліфам по два читання — розмовне (白讀) і книжне (文讀). Ці шари походять із різних епох китайської, тож зберігають свідчення давніших звукових змін і важать у суперечці про те, яке місце мінь посідає в китайській родині.","ar":"تمنح لغة مين نان (الهوكيانية/التايوانية) كثيراً من الحروف قراءتين: عامية (白讀) وأدبية (文讀). والطبقتان آتيتان من حقبتين مختلفتين من تاريخ الصينية، فهما تحفظان أدلة على تحولات صوتية أقدم، وتُلقيان ضوءاً على موقع المين داخل العائلة الصينية.","he":"במין-נאן (הוקיאן/טייוואנית) יש לסימנים רבים שתי קריאות: קריאה עממית (白讀) וקריאה ספרותית (文讀). שתי השכבות באות מתקופות שונות של הסינית, ולכן הן משמרות עדויות לשינויי הגה עתיקים יותר — ומשפיעות על השאלה היכן בדיוק ממוקמת המין במשפחת הלשונות הסיניות.","sw":"Min Nan (Hokkien/Kitaiwani) huzipa herufi nyingi usomaji mara mbili: wa mazungumzo (白讀) na wa fasihi (文讀). Matabaka hayo mawili yanatoka katika vipindi tofauti vya Kichina, kwa hiyo yanahifadhi ushahidi wa mabadiliko ya zamani ya sauti na yanagusa swali la mahali Kimin kinaposimama ndani ya familia ya Kichina."},"body":{"en":"<h3>One character, two completely different words</h3>\n<p>In most languages, a written word has one pronunciation (give or take dialectal variation). Min Nan — the mother tongue of about 34 million people (a 2025 estimate) in Fujian, Taiwan, Southeast Asia and diaspora communities worldwide — breaks this rule systematically. Many common characters have <em>two</em> pronunciations, drawn from two different layers of the sound system: the <strong>白讀 pe̍h-tho̍k</strong> (colloquial/vernacular reading) and the <strong>文讀 bûn-tho̍k</strong> (literary/formal reading).</p>\n\n<div class=\"trivia-actions\">\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"一\">🔢 Show 一 on the map</button>\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"日\">☀️ Show 日 on the map</button>\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"二\">🔢 Show 二 on the map</button>\n</div>\n\n<h3>The famous pairs</h3>\n<p>The contrast is stark. Consider these three high-frequency characters:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>一</strong> (one): everyday <em>tsi̍t</em> (as in <em>tsi̍t ê lâng</em>, \"one person\") vs. literary <em>it</em> (as in <em>tē-it</em>, \"first place\"). These are not two layers of one word but two different words. <em>it</em> is the reading of 一; <em>tsi̍t</em> is a separate Min word for \"one\" whose own character is 蜀 (the <em>Fangyan</em>, 1st century BC: \"in southern Chu, one is called 蜀\"). It is written 一 by borrowing, which makes it a 訓讀, not a 白讀.</li>\n  <li><strong>兩</strong> (two of something): colloquial <em>nn̄g</em> (as in <em>nn̄g ê</em>, \"two of them\") vs. literary <em>lióng</em>. The character <strong>二</strong> has no colloquial reading of its own — only <em>jī</em> (Zhangzhou-type, southern Taiwan) or <em>lī</em> (Quanzhou/Xiamen-type, northern Taiwan), which is a regional split rather than a literary/colloquial one — so everyday \"two + classifier\" is written 兩.</li>\n  <li><strong>十</strong> (ten): colloquial <em>tsa̍p</em> (as in <em>tsa̍p ê</em>, \"ten of them\") vs. literary <em>si̍p</em> (as in <em>si̍p-jī-kè</em>, \"cross\", literally \"ten-character frame\"). Here the initial consonant and the vowel both differ.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"trivia-actions\">\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"focus\" data-code=\"nan\">📍 Highlight Taiwanese Min Nan</button>\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"panto\" data-lat=\"25.0\" data-lng=\"121.5\" data-zoom=\"6\">🗾 Fly to Taiwan</button>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Why two systems?</h3>\n<p>The colloquial layer is the <strong>older</strong> of the two, and it is itself layered. Northern settlers brought Chinese into Fujian in waves: the oldest vocabulary is usually traced to the Han dynasty (206 BC – AD 220), a second layer to the Northern and Southern Dynasties (420–589). Fujian's mountains kept it off the main north–south migration routes, which ran further west through the Gan and Xiang river valleys, so its speech went its own way for centuries.</p>\n\n<p>The literary layer arrived later, in the Tang dynasty (618–907), modelled on the prestige speech of the capital, Chang'an. Scholars reading the Confucian classics aloud needed the \"correct\" capital pronunciations, which by then were <em>very different</em> from how the same characters had evolved locally. Rather than replacing the vernacular — which was used for everything domestic, emotional, and embodied — speakers layered the literary pronunciations on top, reserving them for formal reading, poetry, and religious recitation.</p>\n\n<h3>Min Nan as a linguistic time capsule</h3>\n<p>Min Nan, and its colloquial layer above all, preserves features of Old Chinese and Early Middle Chinese that Mandarin and most northern varieties have lost or levelled:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Final stop consonants</strong> (-p, -t, -k entering tones). Standard Mandarin has lost these entirely; Min Nan retains them vigorously, as do Cantonese and Hakka: <em>tsi̍t</em> ends in a clear -t stop.</li>\n  <li><strong>Old dental stops.</strong> Where Mandarin has <em>zh-</em> or <em>ch-</em>, Min Nan often keeps the plain <em>t-</em> of Old Chinese: 豬 <em>ti</em> \"pig\" (Mandarin <em>zhū</em>), 茶 <em>tê</em> \"tea\" (Mandarin <em>chá</em>). That Amoy <em>tê</em>, carried to Europe by Dutch traders, is where the English word <em>tea</em> comes from.</li>\n  <li><strong>No <em>f</em> yet.</strong> Old Chinese had no <em>f</em> sound, and the colloquial layer often keeps the older <em>p-</em>: 飛 \"fly\" is colloquial <em>pue</em> (or <em>pe</em>) but literary <em>hui</em>, and Mandarin <em>fēi</em>.</li>\n  <li><strong>The 日母 initial</strong>: 二 <em>jī</em> still carries a consonant inherited from Middle Chinese 日母 — Old Chinese *<em>ni[j]-s</em> in Baxter &amp; Sagart's reconstruction, where Mandarin has worn the initial away entirely, leaving only the vowel of <em>èr</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<blockquote>\"…it is not surprising that the dialects spoken here lie outside the mainstream of Chinese linguistic development. …[T]his group is, next to Mandarin, the most distinctive and easily characterized group of Chinese dialects.\" — Jerry Norman, <em>Chinese</em> (1988), p. 228</blockquote>\n\n<p>Norman balances the ledger in the same paragraph, and it is worth keeping both halves: Min preserves numerous archaisms found nowhere else in Sinitic, but it also carries a whole series of local innovations peculiar to Min. Its value to historical linguistics is not that it is simply \"older\" than its neighbours — it is that, as Norman argues, Min is the one major dialect group that cannot be derived entirely from the Middle Chinese of the rhyme books, so it preserves evidence no other variety can supply.</p>\n\n<h3>Choosing which layer to use</h3>\n<p>A fluent Min Nan speaker switches registers almost unconsciously:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Counting objects at the market: <em>tsi̍t, nn̄g, sann…</em> (colloquial)</li>\n  <li>Reciting a poem or an official title: <em>it, jī, sam…</em> (literary)</li>\n  <li>Counting to eleven: <em>tsa̍p-it</em> — colloquial <em>tsa̍p</em> for 十, literary <em>it</em> for 一</li>\n  <li>\"Monday\": <em>pài-it</em>, short for <em>lé-pài-it</em> (禮拜一, \"week-one\") — literary <em>it</em> for the number. 拜 <em>pài</em> has only one reading, so there is no colloquial/literary choice to make for it.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This code-switching is not conscious grammatical calculation; it is absorbed through native-speaker immersion, and getting it wrong marks someone as a non-native learner as immediately as a misplaced tone would.</p>\n\n<div class=\"trivia-actions\">\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"人\">👤 Try 人 (lâng vs. jîn)</button>\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"水\">💧 Try 水 (chúi vs. súi)</button>\n</div>\n\n<p>The 文白異讀 system is not a quirk or a defect — nor is it unique to Min Nan, since Wu, Hakka and Cantonese all layer literary readings over colloquial ones. What sets Min Nan apart is the depth of its colloquial stratum. It is a living cross-section of Chinese linguistic history, with different eras of Chinese sitting side by side in every speaker's mouth: Han-dynasty and later vernacular below, the Tang reading standard above. That layered depth is why historical linguists treat Min Nan as irreplaceable evidence for reconstructing what Chinese sounded like long before any of its modern forms existed.</p>"},"sources":["Norman, Jerry (1988). Chinese. Cambridge University Press. (Chapter 8: Southern dialects and Min.)","Baxter, William H. & Sagart, Laurent (2014). Old Chinese: A New Reconstruction. Oxford University Press.","Pulleyblank, Edwin G. (1984). Middle Chinese: A Study in Historical Phonology. University of British Columbia Press.",{"text":"Mair, Victor H. (ed.) (2001). The Columbia History of Chinese Literature. Columbia University Press. (Section on Min dialects.)","url":""},{"text":"Wikipedia: Taiwanese Hokkien — Literary and colloquial readings","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taiwanese_Hokkien#Literary_and_colloquial_readings"},{"text":"Wikipedia: Literary and colloquial readings","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Literary_and_colloquial_readings"},{"text":"Wikipedia: Southern Min (speaker estimate, 2025)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Southern_Min"},{"text":"Wikipedia: Min Chinese — History (Norman's four layers)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Min_Chinese#History"},{"text":"Wikipedia: Etymology of tea","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Etymology_of_tea"}]},{"id":"korean-toumeop-rule","icon":"🔤","tags":["korean","phonology","north-south","sinosphere"],"title":{"en":"The Head-Sound Rule: Why 六 Is 육 in Seoul but 륙 in Pyongyang","ja":"頭音法則：なぜ「六」は南で육、北で륙なのか","ko":"두음법칙: 왜 '六'이 서울에서는 육, 평양에서는 륙인가","zh":"头音法则：为何「六」在首尔读육、在平壤读륙","yue":"頭音法則：點解「六」喺首爾係육、喺平壤係륙","vi":"Quy tắc âm đầu: Vì sao 六 đọc là 육 ở Seoul nhưng 륙 ở Bình Nhưỡng","th":"กฎเสียงต้น: ทำไม 六 อ่านว่า 육 ในโซล แต่ 륙 ในเปียงยาง","id":"Aturan Bunyi Awal: Mengapa 六 Dibaca 육 di Seoul tapi 륙 di Pyongyang","hi":"प्रारंभिक-ध्वनि नियम: सियोल में 六 = 육, प्योंगयांग में 륙 क्यों?","de":"Die Anlautregel: Warum 六 in Seoul 육, in Pjöngjang aber 륙 ist","fr":"La règle d'initiale : pourquoi 六 se prononce 육 à Séoul mais 륙 à Pyongyang","it":"La regola dell'iniziale: perché 六 è 육 a Seoul ma 륙 a Pyongyang","es":"La regla del sonido inicial: por qué 六 es 육 en Seúl pero 륙 en Pyongyang","pt":"A regra do som inicial: por que 六 é 육 em Seul mas 륙 em Pyongyang","ru":"Правило начального звука: почему 六 — это 육 в Сеуле, но 륙 в Пхеньяне","uk":"Правило початкового звуку: чому 六 — це 육 у Сеулі, але 륙 у Пхеньяні","ar":"قاعدة الصوت الأولي: لماذا تُنطق 六 بـ육 في سيول و륙 في بيونغيانغ","he":"כלל הצליל הראשוני: מדוע 六 הוא 육 בסיאול אך 륙 בפיונגיאנג","sw":"Kanuni ya Sauti ya Awali: Kwa Nini 六 ni 육 Seoul lakini 륙 Pyongyang"},"summary":{"en":"South Korea's 두음법칙 (頭音法則) drops word-initial /l-/ and /n-/ before certain vowels — a codified sound change that North Korea rejected, splitting one language into two spelling systems.","ja":"韓国の頭音法則は語頭の/l-/・/n-/を特定の母音の前で脱落させる音変化を法律で定めたもので、北朝鮮はこれを拒否したため、一つの言語が二つの表記体系に分かれた。","ko":"한국의 두음법칙은 어두의 /l-/·/n-/을 특정 모음 앞에서 탈락시키는 음변화를 법으로 규정한 것으로, 북한이 이를 거부하면서 하나의 언어가 두 가지 표기 체계로 나뉘었다.","zh":"韩国的头音法则将词首/l-/和/n-/在特定元音前的脱落以法律形式固定，北朝鲜拒绝接受，导致同一语言分裂为两套拼写体系。","yue":"韓國嘅頭音法則將詞首/l-/同/n-/喺特定元音前嘅脫落以法律形式固定，北韓唔接受，令同一種語言分裂成兩套拼寫系統。","vi":"Quy tắc âm đầu của Hàn Quốc luật hóa sự mất /l-/ và /n-/ ở đầu từ trước một số nguyên âm nhất định — Bắc Triều Tiên bác bỏ quy tắc này, khiến cùng một ngôn ngữ tách thành hai hệ thống chính tả.","th":"กฎเสียงต้นของเกาหลีใต้บัญญัติการตัด /l-/ และ /n-/ ออกจากต้นคำเมื่ออยู่หน้าสระบางเสียง โดยเกาหลีเหนือปฏิเสธกฎนี้ ทำให้ภาษาเดียวกันแตกเป็นสองระบบการเขียน","id":"Aturan bunyi awal Korea Selatan melegalkan penghilangan /l-/ dan /n-/ di awal kata sebelum vokal tertentu — Korea Utara menolak aturan ini, membelah satu bahasa menjadi dua sistem ejaan.","hi":"दक्षिण कोरिया के 두음법칙 (頭音法則) नियम ने कुछ स्वरों से पहले शब्द-प्रारंभिक /l-/ और /n-/ के लोप को वैधानिक रूप दिया — उत्तर कोरिया ने इसे अस्वीकार किया, जिससे एक भाषा दो वर्तनी प्रणालियों में विभाजित हो गई।","de":"Südkoreas Anlautregel (두음법칙) kodifiziert den Wegfall von /l-/ und /n-/ im Anlaut vor bestimmten Vokalen — Nordkorea lehnte sie ab, was eine Sprache in zwei Schreibsysteme spaltete.","fr":"La règle d'initiale sud-coréenne (두음법칙) officialise la chute de /l-/ et /n-/ en début de mot devant certaines voyelles — rejetée par la Corée du Nord, elle a divisé une langue en deux systèmes orthographiques.","it":"La regola dell'iniziale della Corea del Sud codifica la caduta di /l-/ e /n-/ a inizio parola davanti a certe vocali — rifiutata dalla Corea del Nord, ha diviso una lingua in due sistemi ortografici.","es":"La regla del sonido inicial de Corea del Sur codifica la caída de /l-/ y /n-/ al inicio de palabra ante ciertas vocales — rechazada por Corea del Norte, dividió una lengua en dos sistemas ortográficos.","pt":"A regra do som inicial da Coreia do Sul codifica a queda de /l-/ e /n-/ no início de palavra diante de certas vogais — rejeitada pela Coreia do Norte, dividiu uma língua em dois sistemas ortográficos.","ru":"Правило начального звука Южной Кореи закрепляет выпадение /l-/ и /n-/ в начале слова перед определёнными гласными — Северная Корея отвергла его, разделив один язык на две орфографические системы.","uk":"Правило початкового звуку Південної Кореї закріплює випадіння /l-/ і /n-/ на початку слова перед певними голосними — Північна Корея відкинула його, розділивши одну мову на дві орфографічні системи.","ar":"تُقنّن قاعدة الصوت الأولي في كوريا الجنوبية حذف /l-/ و/n-/ في بداية الكلمة قبل حروف علة معيّنة — رفضتها كوريا الشمالية، فانقسمت لغة واحدة إلى نظامَي إملاء.","he":"כלל הצליל הראשוני של קוריאה הדרומית מקדד נשירת /l-/ ו-/n-/ בתחילת מילה לפני תנועות מסוימות — קוריאה הצפונית דחתה אותו, ופיצלה שפה אחת לשתי מערכות כתיב.","sw":"Kanuni ya sauti ya awali ya Korea Kusini inalegalisha kuanguka kwa /l-/ na /n-/ mwanzoni mwa neno mbele ya irabu fulani — Korea Kaskazini ilikataa, ikigawanya lugha moja kuwa mifumo miwili ya tahajia."},"body":{"en":"<h3>One Character, Two Readings</h3><p>Type the character 六 into HanMap and you see two very different romanizations side by side: South Korean <strong>yuk</strong> (육) and North Korean <strong>ryuk</strong> (륙). The difference is not dialectal drift — it is a deliberate policy choice baked into each government's official orthography.</p><h3>What the Rule Says</h3><p>South Korea's <em>두음법칙</em> (頭音法則, head-sound rule) reshapes word-initial /l/ and /n/ in Sino-Korean syllables, but in two different ways. Before <em>i</em> or <em>y</em> the consonant drops altogether: 龍 (dragon) is <strong>용</strong> yong, not 룡 ryong. Before any other vowel an initial /l/ becomes /n/ instead: 來 (come) is <strong>내</strong> nae at the start of a word (내일 來日, tomorrow) but 래 rae word-internally (미래 未來, future). An initial /n/ behaves the same way before <em>i</em> or <em>y</em>: 女 (woman) is 여 yeo in 여자 'woman' but 녀 nyeo in 소녀 'girl'. Even the characters' own surnames split: the common name 李 is 이 Yi in the South and 리 Ri in the North. The rule was codified in the 1933 Unified Hangul Orthography and reinforced by the 1988 standard still in use today.</p><h3>Why North Korea Disagreed</h3><p>When Korea was divided after 1945, the North chose a different linguistic path. The 1948 <em>Joseon-eo Sincheoljabeop</em> (New Korean Orthography) already left the head-sound rule out, and the 1954 <em>Joseon-eo Cheoljabeop</em> (Korean Orthography), issued by the DPRK Academy of Sciences, confirmed its rejection as an artificial distortion of the underlying form. North Korean orthography preserves the initial /l/ and /n/, so the same characters read 룡, 래, 립 — closer to the Middle Chinese source pronunciations.</p><h3>Political Dimension</h3><p>The split affects ordinary reading across the border. South Koreans reading North Korean text must mentally re-map dozens of common words, and reconciling the two orthographies is treated as a real task rather than a cosmetic one — linguists from both sides began meeting in 2005 to compile a joint dictionary (<em>Gyeoremal Keunsajeon</em>), though the joint sessions have been suspended since 2016, after the North's fourth nuclear test and the closure of the Kaesong industrial zone, and the southern team has carried on alone.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"六\">Show 六 on map</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"focus\" data-code=\"ko\">Focus Korean on map</button></div>"},"sources":["Lee, Iksop & Ramsey, S.R. (2000). The Korean Language. State University of New York Press.","Kim, Chin-Wu (1971). The Phonological Basis of Korean Head Sound Law. Language Research 7(1).",{"text":"국립국어원 — 두음법칙 해설","url":"https://www.korean.go.kr/"},"Sohn, Ho-min (1999). The Korean Language. Cambridge University Press."]},{"id":"japanese-go-on-bias","icon":"🕌","tags":["japanese","kanji","go-on","buddhism","sinosphere"],"title":{"en":"Why Buddhist Japanese Sounds Different: The 呉音 (Go-on) Bias","ja":"仏教語はなぜ呉音か：「経」がkyōで「業」がgōの理由","ko":"일본 불교 용어가 다르게 들리는 이유: 呉音(고온) 편향","zh":"为何日语佛教词汇听起来不同：呉音（吴音）偏向","yue":"點解日本佛教詞彙聽落唔同：呉音偏向嘅由來","vi":"Tại sao tiếng Nhật Phật giáo nghe khác: Xu hướng âm Ngô (呉音)","th":"ทำไมภาษาญี่ปุ่นทางพุทธศาสนาฟังดูต่างออกไป: อคติของ呉音 (โกะอง)","id":"Mengapa Bahasa Jepang Buddhis Terdengar Berbeda: Bias 呉音 (Go-on)","hi":"जापानी बौद्ध शब्द अलग क्यों सुनाई देते हैं: 呉音 (गो-ऑन) का पूर्वाग्रह","de":"Warum buddhistische Japanisch-Begriffe anders klingen: Das 呉音 (Go-on)-Phänomen","fr":"Pourquoi le vocabulaire bouddhiste japonais sonne différemment : le biais des 呉音 (go-on)","it":"Perché il lessico buddhista giapponese suona diverso: il predominio delle 呉音 (go-on)","es":"Por qué el vocabulario budista japonés suena diferente: el predominio del 呉音 (go-on)","pt":"Por que o vocabulário budista japonês soa diferente: o predomínio das 呉音 (go-on)","ru":"Почему буддийская японская лексика звучит иначе: феномен 呉音 (го-он)","uk":"Чому буддійська японська лексика звучить інакше: феномен 呉音 (ґо-он)","ar":"لماذا تبدو المصطلحات اليابانية البوذية مختلفة: تحيّز 呉音 (غو-أون)","he":"מדוע מילות הבודהיזם ביפנית נשמעות שונה: הטיית 呉音 (גו-און)","sw":"Kwa Nini Maneno ya Kibuddha ya Kijapani Yanasikika Tofauti: Upendeleo wa 呉音 (Go-on)"},"summary":{"en":"Japanese Buddhist terminology preserves an older layer of Chinese pronunciation called 呉音 (go-on), which reached Japan largely by way of Korea in the 5th–6th centuries, long before the Tang-era 漢音 (kan-on) layer arrived and eventually displaced it in much of the learned vocabulary.","ja":"日本の仏教用語は、5〜6世紀に主に朝鮮半島を経由して伝わった古層の漢語音「呉音」を保存している。唐代の漢音が伝わり、やがて学問的な語彙の多くで呉音に取って代わるより、はるか前に仏典の読み方が呉音で固定されたためだ。","ko":"일본 불교 용어는 5~6세기에 주로 한반도를 거쳐 들어온 고층 중국어 발음 고온(呉音)을 보존하고 있다. 당나라 시대의 칸온(漢音)이 들어와 마침내 학술 어휘의 상당 부분에서 고온을 밀어내기 훨씬 전에 불경 독송법이 고온으로 고착되었기 때문이다.","zh":"日语佛教词汇保留了更早的汉语发音层——呉音（吴音），它在5至6世纪主要经由朝鲜半岛传入日本，远早于唐代汉音的到来——后者最终在许多学术词汇中取代了它，而它则在佛典诵读中固定了下来。","yue":"日文佛教術語保留咗一個更古老嘅漢語發音層——呉音，佢喺5至6世紀主要經朝鮮半島傳入日本，遠早過唐代漢音層嘅到來；漢音後來喺好大部分學術詞彙入面取代咗佢。","vi":"Thuật ngữ Phật giáo tiếng Nhật bảo lưu một lớp phát âm tiếng Hán cổ hơn gọi là 呉音 (go-on), phần lớn đến Nhật Bản qua ngả Triều Tiên vào thế kỷ 5–6, rất lâu trước khi lớp 漢音 (kan-on) thời Đường du nhập và dần thay thế nó trong phần lớn từ vựng bác học.","th":"คำศัพท์ทางพุทธศาสนาในภาษาญี่ปุ่นสงวนชั้นการออกเสียงภาษาจีนที่เก่ากว่าที่เรียกว่า呉音 (โกะอง) ซึ่งเข้ามาถึงญี่ปุ่นโดยผ่านเกาหลีเป็นส่วนใหญ่ในศตวรรษที่ 5–6 นานก่อนที่ชั้น漢音 (คังอง) สมัยราชวงศ์ถังจะเข้ามา และในที่สุดก็แทนที่มันในคำศัพท์วิชาการจำนวนมาก","id":"Terminologi Buddhis Jepang melestarikan lapisan pengucapan Cina yang lebih tua yang disebut 呉音 (go-on), yang sebagian besar tiba di Jepang lewat Korea pada abad ke-5–6, jauh sebelum lapisan 漢音 (kan-on) era Tang datang dan lambat laun menggantikannya di banyak kosakata kaum terpelajar.","hi":"जापानी बौद्ध शब्दावली चीनी उच्चारण की एक पुरानी परत 呉音 (गो-ऑन) को संरक्षित करती है, जो 5वीं–6वीं सदी में ज़्यादातर कोरिया के रास्ते जापान पहुँची — तांग-युग की 漢音 (कान-ऑन) परत के आने से बहुत पहले, जिसने आगे चलकर विद्वत् शब्दावली के बड़े हिस्से में उसकी जगह ले ली।","de":"Buddhistische Fachbegriffe im Japanischen bewahren eine ältere Schicht chinesischer Aussprache, die 呉音 (go-on), die im 5.–6. Jahrhundert großenteils über Korea nach Japan gelangte — lange bevor 漢音 (kan-on) aus der Tang-Zeit eintraf und sie schließlich aus weiten Teilen des gelehrten Wortschatzes verdrängte.","fr":"La terminologie bouddhiste japonaise préserve une couche plus ancienne de prononciation chinoise, les 呉音 (go-on), arrivée au Japon en grande partie par la Corée aux Ve–VIe siècles, bien avant que les 漢音 (kan-on) de l'époque Tang ne la supplantent peu à peu dans une bonne partie du vocabulaire savant.","it":"La terminologia buddhista giapponese conserva uno strato più antico di pronuncia cinese detto 呉音 (go-on), giunto in Giappone in gran parte attraverso la Corea nel V–VI secolo, molto prima che le 漢音 (kan-on) dell'epoca Tang arrivassero e finissero per scalzarlo in buona parte del lessico dotto.","es":"La terminología budista japonesa preserva una capa más antigua de pronunciación china llamada 呉音 (go-on), que llegó a Japón en gran parte a través de Corea en los siglos V–VI, mucho antes de que la capa 漢音 (kan-on) de la época Tang la desplazara con el tiempo en buena parte del vocabulario culto.","pt":"A terminologia budista japonesa preserva uma camada mais antiga de pronúncia chinesa chamada 呉音 (go-on), que chegou ao Japão em grande parte por via da Coreia nos séculos V–VI, muito antes de a camada 漢音 (kan-on) da era Tang chegar e acabar por deslocá-la em boa parte do vocabulário erudito.","ru":"Буддийская терминология японского языка сохраняет более древний пласт китайского произношения — 呉音 (го-он), попавший в Японию в V–VI веках в основном через Корею, задолго до того, как пришёл пласт 漢音 (кан-он) эпохи Тан и со временем вытеснил его из значительной части учёной лексики.","uk":"Буддійська термінологія японської мови зберігає давніший шар китайської вимови — 呉音 (ґо-он), який потрапив до Японії здебільшого через Корею у V–VI ст., задовго до того, як прийшов танський шар 漢音 (кан-он) і з часом витіснив його з більшої частини книжної лексики.","ar":"تحتفظ المصطلحات البوذية اليابانية بطبقة أقدم من النطق الصيني تُعرف بـ呉音 (غو-أون)، وصلت إلى اليابان في الغالب عن طريق كوريا في القرنين الخامس والسادس، قبل زمن طويل من مجيء طبقة 漢音 (كان-أون) في عهد تانغ، التي أزاحتها مع الوقت من جزء كبير من المفردات العلمية.","he":"הטרמינולוגיה הבודהיסטית ביפנית שומרת על שכבת הגייה סינית עתיקה יותר הנקראת 呉音 (גו-און), שהגיעה ליפן ברובה דרך קוריאה במאות ה-5–6, הרבה לפני ששכבת ה-漢音 (קאן-און) מתקופת טאנג הגיעה ודחקה אותה בסופו של דבר מחלק גדול מאוצר המילים המלומד.","sw":"Istilahi za Kibuddha za Kijapani zinahifadhi safu ya zamani zaidi ya matamshi ya Kichina inayoitwa 呉音 (go-on), iliyofika Japan kwa kiasi kikubwa kupitia Korea katika karne za 5–6, muda mrefu kabla ya safu ya 漢音 (kan-on) ya enzi ya Tang kuwasili na hatimaye kuichukua nafasi yake katika sehemu kubwa ya msamiati wa kisomi."},"body":{"en":"<h3>Two Readings for One Character</h3><p>Many kanji carry two different on'yomi (Sino-Japanese readings): a <em>go-on</em> (呉音) and a <em>kan-on</em> (漢音). For many others the two layers happen to sound the same. The character 経 is <strong>kyō</strong> in go-on and <strong>kei</strong> in kan-on. In practice, <em>お経</em> (sutra) uses kyō; <em>経済</em> (economy) uses kei. The split is systematic, not random.</p><h3>The Earliest Wave: By Way of Korea</h3><p>Japanese scribes first encountered written Chinese through Baekje and Goguryeo scholars in the 5th–6th centuries. The readings they carried are what we now call go-on — “Wu sounds”, named loosely after the Wu region south of the Yangtze, though that label was only attached centuries later, most likely by champions of kan-on. Go-on is usually thought to reflect the Chinese of the Southern dynasties around Jiankang (modern Nanjing), but no contemporary source confirms it, and how much the Korean route reshaped the readings is unclear. Buddhist sutras, chants and temple ritual were set in this pronunciation before Japan began sending regular embassies to China in 607.</p><h3>The Tang Prestige Layer</h3><p>In the 7th–8th centuries, Japanese embassies (<em>kentōshi</em>) brought back the prestigious Chang'an pronunciation. This <em>kan-on</em> (\"Han sounds\") became the reading of the scholars: 文化 <em>bunka</em> (culture) is kan-on through and through. The court pushed hard. In 792 Emperor Kanmu issued an edict promoting kan-on, students at the state academy had to learn it, and monks who had not learned it were not allowed to travel to China. Even so, temple recitation largely kept its inherited go-on, and the two layers ended up mixed in everyday words. 政治 <em>seiji</em> (politics) pairs a kan-on <em>sei</em> with a go-on <em>ji</em>, and 経済 <em>keizai</em> (economy) a kan-on <em>kei</em> with a go-on <em>zai</em>.</p><h3>The Divide in Practice</h3><p>The pattern holds across dozens of characters. 業 is <strong>gō</strong> (karma) in go-on, <strong>gyō</strong> in kan-on; 極楽 <em>gokuraku</em> (paradise) uses the go-on <em>goku</em>, where kan-on would give <em>kyoku</em>; 道 is the go-on <em>dō</em> in 道路 <em>dōro</em> (road) but the kan-on <em>tō</em> in 神道 <em>Shintō</em> — go-on stuck in plenty of everyday words too. Even temple names follow the bias: 法隆寺 <em>Hōryū-ji</em>, 東大寺 <em>Tōdai-ji</em> — the 寺 is always the go-on <em>ji</em>, never the kan-on <em>shi</em>.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"心\">Show 心 on map</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"focus\" data-code=\"ja\">Focus Japanese on map</button></div>"},"sources":["Frellesvig, Bjarke (2010). A History of the Japanese Language. Cambridge University Press.","Miyake, Marc Hideo (2003). Old Japanese: A Phonetic Reconstruction. RoutledgeCurzon.",{"text":"Wikipedia — On'yomi (Go-on and Kan-on)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/On%27yomi"},"Unger, J. Marshall (1996). Literacy and Script Reform in Occupation Japan. Oxford University Press."]},{"id":"kokuji-japan-made-chars","icon":"🗾","tags":["kokuji","kanji","japan","sinosphere","character-formation"],"title":{"en":"Kokuji: Han Characters Invented in Japan","ja":"国字：日本で作られた漢字","ko":"국자: 일본에서 만든 한자","zh":"国字：日本自创的汉字","yue":"國字：日本自創嘅漢字","vi":"Kokuji: Chữ Hán do Nhật Bản sáng tạo","th":"โคคุจิ: อักษรฮันที่ญี่ปุ่นประดิษฐ์ขึ้นเอง","id":"Kokuji: Karakter Han Ciptaan Jepang","hi":"कोकुजि: जापान में बनाए गए हान अक्षर","de":"Kokuji: In Japan erfundene Han-Schriftzeichen","fr":"Kokuji : caractères Han inventés au Japon","it":"Kokuji: caratteri Han inventati in Giappone","es":"Kokuji: caracteres Han inventados en Japón","pt":"Kokuji: caracteres Han inventados no Japão","ru":"Кокудзи: иероглифы, изобретённые в Японии","uk":"Кокудзі: ієрогліфи, винайдені в Японії","ar":"كوكوجي: الحروف الصينية المخترعة في اليابان","he":"קוקוג׳י: תווים הסיניים שהומצאו ביפן","sw":"Kokuji: Herufi za Han zilizovumbuliwa Japani"},"summary":{"en":"Japan invented hundreds of Han-style characters of its own, called kokuji (国字), for native words, fish and plants, and at least one, 腺 'gland', went on to become standard Chinese.","ja":"日本は「国字」と呼ばれる漢字風の文字を何百字も独自に作り出した。和語や魚、植物のための字で、少なくとも一つ、「腺」は中国語の標準的な字にまでなった。","ko":"일본은 고유어와 물고기, 식물 이름을 적기 위해 '국자(国字)'라 불리는 한자식 문자를 수백 자나 만들었고, 그중 적어도 하나인 腺(샘)은 중국어의 표준 글자가 되었다.","zh":"日本自创了数百个汉字风格的“国字”，用来书写日本固有词、鱼类和植物的名称，其中至少有一个——“腺”——后来成了标准汉语用字。","yue":"日本自創咗幾百個漢字風格嘅字，叫做「國字」，用嚟寫本土詞、魚同植物；當中起碼有一個——「腺」——後來成為咗標準漢語用字。","vi":"Nhật Bản đã tự sáng tạo hàng trăm chữ theo kiểu chữ Hán, gọi là kokuji (国字), để ghi từ thuần Nhật, tên cá và cây cỏ — và ít nhất một chữ, 腺 \"tuyến\", đã trở thành chữ chuẩn của tiếng Trung.","th":"ญี่ปุ่นประดิษฐ์อักษรแบบฮั่นของตัวเองนับร้อยตัว เรียกว่าโคคุจิ (国字) ไว้เขียนคำพื้นเมือง ชื่อปลา และชื่อพืช และอย่างน้อยหนึ่งตัว คือ 腺 'ต่อม' ได้กลายเป็นอักษรมาตรฐานของภาษาจีน","id":"Jepang menciptakan ratusan karakter bergaya Han sendiri, yang disebut kokuji (国字), untuk kata-kata asli, nama ikan, dan tumbuhan, dan setidaknya satu di antaranya, 腺 'kelenjar', akhirnya menjadi bahasa Cina baku.","hi":"जापान ने देशज शब्दों, मछलियों और पौधों के लिए सैकड़ों अपने हान-शैली के वर्ण गढ़े, जिन्हें 'कोकुजि' (国字) कहते हैं — और उनमें से कम-से-कम एक, 腺 'ग्रंथि', आगे चलकर मानक चीनी का हिस्सा बन गया।","de":"Japan erfand Hunderte eigener Han-artiger Schriftzeichen, die Kokuji (国字), für einheimische Wörter, Fische und Pflanzen — und mindestens eines, 腺 „Drüse“, wurde zum Standardchinesisch.","fr":"Le Japon a inventé des centaines de caractères de style Han bien à lui, les kokuji (国字), pour des mots autochtones, des poissons et des plantes — et l'un d'eux au moins, 腺 « glande », est devenu du chinois standard.","it":"Il Giappone inventò centinaia di caratteri in stile Han tutti suoi, i kokuji (国字), per parole native, pesci e piante, e almeno uno, 腺 «ghiandola», è diventato cinese standard.","es":"Japón inventó cientos de caracteres de estilo Han propios, llamados kokuji (国字), para palabras nativas, peces y plantas, y al menos uno, 腺 «glándula», acabó siendo chino estándar.","pt":"O Japão inventou centenas de caracteres próprios ao estilo Han, chamados kokuji (国字), para palavras nativas, peixes e plantas — e pelo menos um, 腺 'glândula', acabou virando chinês padrão.","ru":"Япония изобрела сотни собственных иероглифов ханьского стиля — кокудзи (国字) — для исконных слов, рыб и растений, и по крайней мере один из них, 腺 «железа», стал нормой в китайском.","uk":"Японія винайшла сотні власних ієрогліфів у стилі хань — кокудзі (国字) — для питомих слів, риб і рослин, і щонайменше один із них, 腺 «залоза», став нормативним китайським знаком.","ar":"اخترعت اليابان مئات الحروف الخاصة بها على غرار الهان، تُعرف بـ«كوكوجي» (国字)، لكلماتها الأصيلة وأسماكها ونباتاتها، وواحد منها على الأقل، 腺 «غدّة»، صار حرفاً صينياً معيارياً.","he":"יפן המציאה מאות תווים משלה בסגנון הסיני, הנקראים קוקוג׳י (国字), למילים יפניות ילידיות, לדגים ולצמחים — ולפחות אחד מהם, 腺 \"בלוטה\", הפך לסינית תקנית.","sw":"Japani iliunda mamia ya herufi zake za mtindo wa Han, zinazoitwa kokuji (国字), kwa ajili ya maneno ya asili, samaki na mimea — na angalau moja, 腺 “tezi”, iliishia kuwa herufi sanifu ya Kichina."},"body":{"en":"<h3>Japan's Own Kanji</h3><p>When Japan adopted Chinese writing around the 5th–6th century, Chinese characters covered Chinese realities — not Japanese ones. Japanese scribes eventually filled the gaps by forging new characters (<strong>国字, kokuji</strong>) using the same visual logic as Chinese: combining semantic components (<em>会意, kaii</em>) or pairing a semantic radical with a phonetic hint (<em>形声, keisei</em>).</p><h3>Famous Examples</h3><ul><li><strong>峠 (tōge)</strong> — mountain pass: 山 (mountain) + 上 (up) + 下 (down), the point where the road stops climbing and starts descending. Pure 会意.</li><li><strong>凧 (tako)</strong> — kite: usually read as 几 'wind' + 巾 'cloth', though it may have begun as a fusion of 鳳巾, an older written form for 'kite'. Kites themselves came from China, where they were written 紙鳶 'paper kite'; what Japan added was a one-character spelling.</li><li><strong>働 (hataraku)</strong> — to work: 人 (person) + 動 (move). The most commonly used kokuji of all, and one of the few with an on'yomi (<em>dō</em>, taken from 動). It is among the roughly nine kokuji on the Jōyō list.</li><li><strong>雫 (shizuku)</strong> — water drop: 雨 (rain) + 下 (down). Elegant visual poetry.</li><li><strong>笹 (sasa)</strong> — dwarf bamboo: 竹 (bamboo) + 世 (read <em>yo</em>, echoing <em>yo</em> “bamboo node”; others take the lower element as an abbreviated 葉 “leaf”). A plant central to Japanese festivals (Tanabata) but undifferentiated in Chinese botany.</li></ul><h3>Why Japan Needed Them</h3><p>Most kokuji give a single character to a native Japanese word that had no neat Chinese match. Fish make up an especially large group: 鰯 <em>iwashi</em> (sardine, already written this way in the 8th century), 鱚 <em>kisu</em> (sillago) and 鱈 <em>tara</em> (cod). Others mark Shinto and everyday life: 榊 <em>sakaki</em>, the sacred tree, is simply 木 'tree' + 神 'god'; 辻 <em>tsuji</em>, a crossroads, is 辶 'road' + 十 'cross'; 畑 <em>hatake</em> is a dry field for crops.</p><h3>The Surprising Reverse Flow</h3><p>A few kokuji travelled <em>west</em>. The clearest case is <strong>腺 (sen, gland)</strong>: 'flesh' 月 + 泉 'spring', a body part that wells up with liquid. It was coined around 1800 by the Edo-period Dutch-studies scholar Udagawa Genshin to translate Dutch <em>klier</em>, and it passed into both Chinese, where 腺 <em>xiàn</em> is now the standard word for gland, and Korean. <strong>鱈</strong> 'cod' is likewise now the ordinary Chinese character for the fish (<em>xuě</em>, simplified 鳕). The sinosphere was never a one-way street.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"focus\" data-code=\"ja_kun\">Show the kun-yomi layer on the map</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"panto\" data-lat=\"36.2\" data-lng=\"138.0\" data-zoom=\"5\">Pan to Japan</button></div>"},"sources":["Shibatani, Masayoshi. The Languages of Japan. Cambridge University Press, 1990.","Seeley, Christopher. A History of Writing in Japan. University of Hawaiʻi Press, 2000.",{"text":"国字 — 国語研究所データベース (NINJAL)","url":"https://www.ninjal.ac.jp/"},{"text":"Kokuji (Wikipedia)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kokuji"}]},{"id":"korea-kuk-ja","icon":"🇰🇷","tags":["kokuji","kanji","korea","sinosphere","character-formation"],"title":{"en":"Korea's Own Han Characters: Rare but Real","ja":"韓国固有漢字：少ないが確かに存在する","ko":"한국 고유 한자: 적지만 엄연히 존재한다","zh":"韩国固有汉字：少而真实","yue":"韓國固有漢字：少但真實存在","vi":"Chữ Hán riêng của Triều Tiên: Ít nhưng có thật","th":"อักษรฮันเฉพาะของเกาหลี: น้อยแต่มีอยู่จริง","id":"Karakter Han Asli Korea: Sedikit tapi Nyata","hi":"कोरिया के अपने हान अक्षर: कम लेकिन वास्तविक","de":"Koreas eigene Han-Schriftzeichen: Selten aber real","fr":"Les caractères Han propres à la Corée : rares mais réels","it":"I caratteri Han propri della Corea: pochi ma reali","es":"Los caracteres Han propios de Corea: escasos pero reales","pt":"Os caracteres Han próprios da Coreia: raros mas reais","ru":"Собственные ханьские знаки Кореи: редкие, но настоящие","uk":"Власні ханьські знаки Кореї: рідкісні, але справжні","ar":"حروف هان الخاصة بكوريا: نادرة لكنها حقيقية","he":"תווים הניים ייחודיים לקוריאה: נדירים אך אמיתיים","sw":"Herufi za Han za Korea: Chache lakini Halisi"},"summary":{"en":"Korea created its own Han-style characters — mostly for paddy fields, personal names, and place names — but far fewer than Japan, reflecting different writing strategies in the two countries.","ja":"韓国も独自の漢字を作ったが、その多くは水田・人名・地名のためのもので、数は日本の国字よりずっと少ない。そこには両国の書き言葉の戦略の違いが映し出されている。","ko":"한국도 독자적인 한자를 소수 만들었지만, 논·인명·지명에 국한되어 일본의 국자보다 훨씬 적다.","zh":"韩国也创造了少数固有汉字，主要用于稻田、人名和地名，但数量远少于日本，反映了两国不同的书写策略。","yue":"韓國都創造咗少量固有漢字，主要用於稻田、人名同地名，但數量遠少於日本，反映咗兩國唔同嘅書寫策略。","vi":"Triều Tiên cũng tạo ra một số chữ Hán riêng — chủ yếu cho ruộng lúa, tên người và địa danh — nhưng ít hơn nhiều so với Nhật Bản, phản ánh chiến lược viết khác nhau giữa hai nước.","th":"เกาหลีก็สร้างอักษรฮันของตัวเองขึ้นบ้าง — ส่วนใหญ่สำหรับนาข้าว ชื่อบุคคล และชื่อสถานที่ — แต่น้อยกว่าญี่ปุ่นมาก สะท้อนกลยุทธ์การเขียนที่แตกต่างกันของสองประเทศ","id":"Korea juga menciptakan karakter Han sendiri — terutama untuk sawah, nama pribadi, dan nama tempat — tetapi jauh lebih sedikit daripada Jepang, mencerminkan strategi penulisan yang berbeda.","hi":"कोरिया ने भी अपने कुछ हान-शैली के अक्षर बनाए — मुख्यतः धान के खेत, व्यक्तिगत नाम और स्थान-नामों के लिए — लेकिन जापान की तुलना में बहुत कम, जो दोनों देशों की अलग लेखन रणनीतियों को दर्शाता है।","de":"Korea schuf eigene Han-Schriftzeichen — vor allem für Reisfelder, Personennamen und Ortsnamen — aber weit weniger als Japan, was unterschiedliche Schriftstrategien beider Länder widerspiegelt.","fr":"La Corée créa aussi ses propres caractères Han — surtout pour les rizières, les noms de personnes et de lieux — mais bien moins que le Japon, reflétant des stratégies d'écriture différentes.","it":"Anche la Corea creò propri caratteri Han — soprattutto per risaie, nomi di persona e toponimi — ma molti meno rispetto al Giappone, riflettendo diverse strategie di scrittura.","es":"Corea también creó sus propios caracteres Han — principalmente para arrozales, nombres de personas y topónimos — pero muchos menos que Japón, reflejando distintas estrategias de escritura.","pt":"A Coreia também criou seus próprios caracteres Han — principalmente para arrozais, nomes de pessoas e topônimos — mas muito menos do que o Japão, refletindo estratégias de escrita diferentes.","ru":"Корея также создала собственные ханьские знаки — главным образом для рисовых полей, личных имён и топонимов — но значительно меньше, чем Япония, что отражает разные письменные стратегии.","uk":"Корея також створила власні ханьські знаки — переважно для рисових полів, особових імен і топонімів — але значно менше, ніж Японія, що відображає різні стратегії письма.","ar":"ابتكرت كوريا أيضاً حروف هان خاصة بها — معظمها لحقول الأرز والأسماء الشخصية وأسماء الأماكن — لكنها أقل بكثير مما ابتكرته اليابان، مما يعكس استراتيجيات كتابة مختلفة.","he":"קוריאה יצרה אף היא תווים הניים משלה — בעיקר לשדות אורז, שמות פרטיים ושמות מקומות — אך הרבה פחות מיפן, בהשתקפות של אסטרטגיות כתיבה שונות.","sw":"Korea pia iliunda herufi zake za Han — hasa kwa mashamba ya mpunga, majina ya watu na majina ya maeneo — lakini chache zaidi kuliko Japani, ikiakisi mikakati tofauti ya uandishi."},"body":{"en":"<h3>Korea's Smaller Catalogue</h3><p>Japan's home-made characters, the kokuji, run into the hundreds. Korea's equivalent, usually called <strong>gukja (國字)</strong>, \"national characters\", or <em>goyu hanja</em> (固有漢字), \"native Han characters\", is a smaller and more obscure set: close to 200 have been identified so far, though more may turn up. The reasons are instructive.</p><h3>Key Korean-Made Characters</h3><ul><li><strong>畓 (dap, paddy field)</strong>: 水 (water) + 田 (field). The single most important gukja, and one of the oldest: it already appears on Silla-period records, including an 8th-century Silla document preserved in Japan's Shōsōin. Rice farming was central to the peninsula, so a one-character word for a paddy was practical; Chinese writes the two-character 水田.</li><li><strong>乭 (Dol)</strong>: a character used almost exclusively in personal names. The best-known modern bearer is the Go champion Lee Sedol (李世乭), who played AlphaGo in 2016. Its structure is transparent, and characteristically Korean: 石 supplies both the meaning and the native reading <em>dol</em> (stone), while 乙 is appended to write the final <em>-l</em> — the <em>idu</em> device of spelling out a native word's last consonant with an extra character.</li><li><strong>媤 (si)</strong>: woman (女) + 思, meaning \"the husband's family\". It survives in everyday words such as 시댁 <em>sidaek</em> (媤宅, \"one's husband's family home\") and 시어머니 <em>si-eomeoni</em> (\"mother-in-law\").</li><li><strong>Various place-name characters</strong>: many Korean county and village names in historical records use characters not found in any Chinese dictionary. These were practical administrative inventions.</li></ul><h3>Why Fewer Than Japan?</h3><p>Two factors stand out. First, Joseon scholars prized Classical Chinese (<em>hanmun</em>) as the prestige written language and looked down on characters China did not use as vulgar variants (俗字). So most gukja were coined not by the scholar elite but by the clerks who drafted <em>idu</em> documents, Korean written with Chinese characters, where such ideological restraints were weaker. Second, many gukja exist only to spell native Korean syllables, like 乭 for <em>dol</em>; after Sejong's <strong>Hangeul (한글)</strong> of 1443, that job could be done far more easily with the alphabet. Japanese, by contrast, went on coining kokuji from the 8th century to the 19th, many of them names for fish and plants.</p><h3>Legacy</h3><p>Most Korean guyuja survive only in 古典 (classical texts), administrative records, and personal names. 畓 is the striking exception, together with 垈 (<em>dae</em>, \"building plot\"): South Korea's official land categories, the 28 <em>jimok</em> (地目) of the cadastre, still include 답 (畓, paddy) and 대 (垈, building land), so Korean-made characters sit in land registers to this day. The others have largely retreated into the realm of specialist philology.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"panto\" data-lat=\"37.5\" data-lng=\"127.0\" data-zoom=\"5\">Pan to Korea</button></div>"},"sources":["Ledyard, Gari. The Korean Language Reform of 1446. University of California, Berkeley, 1966.","Kim, Chin-W. The Making of the Korean Language. Colloquium Paper no. 2, Center for Korean Studies, University of Hawaii, 1974.",{"text":"속자 (俗字) — 한국민족문화대백과사전 (Encyclopedia of Korean Culture)","url":"https://encykorea.aks.ac.kr/"},{"text":"한국제 한자 (Wikipedia 한국어판)","url":"https://ko.wikipedia.org/wiki/%ED%95%9C%EA%B5%AD%EC%A0%9C_%ED%95%9C%EC%9E%90"},{"text":"지목 (Wikipedia 한국어판)","url":"https://ko.wikipedia.org/wiki/%EC%A7%80%EB%AA%A9"},{"text":"Wiktionary: 畓 (Korean, glyph origin and Silla attestation)","url":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E7%95%93"}]},{"id":"naxi-dongba-living-pictograms","icon":"🖼️","tags":["naxi","pictograph","yunnan","minority","writing-systems"],"title":{"en":"Dongba Script: A Pictographic Tradition of the Naxi","ja":"東巴文字：世界唯一の現存する絵文字体系","ko":"동바 문자: 세계 유일의 현존 상형 문자 체계","zh":"东巴文字：世界上唯一仍在使用的象形文字体系","yue":"東巴文字：全球唯一仲喺用緊嘅圖畫文字","vi":"Chữ Đông Ba: Hệ thống chữ tượng hình duy nhất còn tồn tại trên thế giới","th":"อักษรตงปา: ระบบอักษรภาพเดียวที่ยังมีชีวิตอยู่ในโลก","id":"Aksara Dongba: Satu-satunya Sistem Tulisan Piktografis yang Masih Hidup","hi":"डोंगबा लिपि: विश्व की एकमात्र जीवित चित्रलिपि प्रणाली","de":"Dongba-Schrift: Das letzte lebende Bilderschriftsystem der Welt","fr":"Écriture Dongba : le dernier système pictographique vivant au monde","it":"Scrittura Dongba: l'unico sistema pittografico ancora vivo al mondo","es":"Escritura Dongba: el último sistema pictográfico vivo del mundo","pt":"Escrita Dongba: o único sistema pictográfico vivo do mundo","ru":"Письмо Дунба: последняя живая пиктографическая письменность мира","uk":"Письмо Дунба: остання жива піктографічна система письма у світі","ar":"الكتابة الدونغبا: آخر نظام كتابة تصويري حي في العالم","he":"כתב דונגבא: מערכת הכתיבה הפיקטוגרפית היחידה החיה בעולם","sw":"Maandishi ya Dongba: Mfumo wa Piktografia Unaoendelea Kuishi Duniani"},"summary":{"en":"The Naxi of Yunnan, China, still keep a pictographic script: drawings that prompt their priests' chanted ritual texts, with a few signs borrowed for sound like a rebus puzzle.","ja":"中国雲南省のナシ族は、今も絵文字を守り続けている。祭司が詠唱する儀礼テキストを呼び起こすための絵で、いくつかの字は判じ絵のように音だけを借りて使われる。","ko":"중국 윈난성의 나시족은 지금도 그림 문자를 간직하고 있다. 사제들이 읊는 의례 텍스트를 떠올리게 해 주는 그림들로, 몇몇 기호는 그림 수수께끼처럼 소리만 빌려 쓴다.","zh":"中国云南的纳西族至今仍保有一种象形文字：一幅幅图画提示祭司吟诵仪式经文，另有少数符号像猜字谜一样借来表音。","yue":"中國雲南嘅納西族仍然保存住一套圖畫文字：用嚟提示祭司吟誦儀式經文嘅圖畫，另外有少數符號好似估字謎噉借嚟表音。","vi":"Người Naxi ở Vân Nam, Trung Quốc, vẫn còn giữ một hệ chữ tượng hình: những hình vẽ gợi nhắc các thầy tế đọc kinh văn nghi lễ, kèm một số ký hiệu được mượn để ghi âm như trò chơi đố chữ.","th":"ชาวนาซีแห่งยูนนาน ประเทศจีน ยังคงรักษาอักษรภาพไว้: ภาพวาดที่ช่วยเตือนความจำให้นักบวชขับร้องตำราพิธีกรรม โดยมีสัญลักษณ์บางตัวยืมมาใช้แทนเสียงเหมือนปริศนาภาพ","id":"Suku Naxi di Yunnan, China, masih memelihara aksara piktografis: gambar-gambar yang memandu para pendeta melantunkan teks ritual, dengan beberapa tanda dipinjam demi bunyinya seperti teka-teki rebus.","hi":"चीन के युन्नान के नाशी लोग आज भी एक चित्रलिपि सँभाले हुए हैं: ऐसे चित्र जो उनके पुजारियों को गाए जाने वाले अनुष्ठान-पाठ याद दिलाते हैं, और जिनमें कुछ चिह्न किसी पहेली (रीबस) की तरह केवल ध्वनि के लिए उधार लिए जाते हैं।","de":"Die Naxi in Yunnan, China, bewahren bis heute eine Bilderschrift: Zeichnungen, die ihren Priestern die gesungenen Ritualtexte ins Gedächtnis rufen, dazu ein paar Zeichen, die wie bei einem Bilderrätsel nur für ihren Klang stehen.","fr":"Les Naxi du Yunnan, en Chine, conservent une écriture pictographique : des dessins qui soufflent à leurs prêtres les textes rituels qu'ils psalmodient, avec quelques signes empruntés pour leur son, à la manière d'un rébus.","it":"I Naxi dello Yunnan, in Cina, conservano ancora una scrittura pittografica: disegni che suggeriscono ai loro sacerdoti i testi rituali da cantare, con alcuni segni presi in prestito per il suono, come in un rebus.","es":"Los Naxi de Yunnan, en China, conservan todavía una escritura pictográfica: dibujos que sirven de pie a los textos rituales que cantan sus sacerdotes, con unos pocos signos tomados por su sonido, como en un jeroglífico de pasatiempos.","pt":"Os Naxi de Yunnan, na China, ainda mantêm uma escrita pictográfica: desenhos que guiam os cantos rituais de seus sacerdotes, com alguns sinais tomados emprestados pelo som, como num rébus.","ru":"Наси в китайской провинции Юньнань до сих пор хранят пиктографическое письмо: рисунки подсказывают жрецам распеваемые обрядовые тексты, а некоторые знаки заимствуются ради звучания, как в ребусе.","uk":"Народ Насі в китайській провінції Юньнань досі зберігає піктографічне письмо: малюнки, що підказують жерцям співані обрядові тексти, і кілька знаків, запозичених заради звучання, як у ребусі.","ar":"لا يزال شعب ناكسي في يوننان بالصين يحتفظ بكتابة تصويرية: رسوم تذكّر كهنته بنصوصهم الطقسية المرتّلة، مع بضع علامات مستعارة لصوتها كما في الأحجيات المصوّرة.","he":"בני הנאשי ביונאן שבסין עדיין שומרים על כתב פיקטוגרפי: ציורים שמזכירים לכוהנים שלהם את הטקסטים הטקסיים המושרים, ועוד כמה סימנים שנשאלו לפי צלילם, כמו בחידת רבוס.","sw":"Watu wa Naxi wa Yunnan, China, bado wanahifadhi maandishi ya picha: michoro inayowakumbusha makuhani wao maandiko ya ibada wanayoyaimba, pamoja na alama chache zilizoazimwa kwa ajili ya sauti, kama fumbo la rebus."},"body":{"en":"<h3>A Script That Outlived Its Age</h3><p>Most pictographic scripts died millennia ago. Egyptian hieroglyphs fell silent around 400 CE; Maya glyphs did not long survive the Spanish conquest. Naxi Dongba script (<strong>東巴文字</strong>) breaks the pattern: between about a thousand and two thousand pictographic signs (counts vary with how variants are treated) are still memorized, recited and drawn by hand by <em>dongba</em> (東巴) priests in Yunnan Province, southwestern China.</p><p>The Naxi people number about 320,000, concentrated around Lijiang (麗江). Their priests — hereditary ritual specialists called 東巴, a name meaning 'wise man' or 'teacher' (from Tibetan ston pa 'teacher') — use the script not for everyday literacy but as a mnemonic scaffold for chanted ceremonial texts. The written signs do not record every word: a few pictographs on the page cue a longer passage of verse that the dongba knows by heart. Writing here is inseparable from performance.</p><h3>Picture First, Sound Second</h3><p>Dongba signs are overwhelmingly pictorial: a drawing of a fish means 'fish'; a human figure with arms raised means 'dance'; compound ideas are shown by combining pictures. But they are not <em>purely</em> pictorial. Any graph can be borrowed for its sound alone — the <strong>rebus principle</strong> — so a pair of eyes (<em>myə</em>) writes the homophonous word 'fate', a rice bowl serves for both 'food' and 'sleep' (<em>xa</em>), and a goral (<em>se</em>) stands in for a grammatical particle. The Naxi kept a second script too, the <em>geba</em> (哥巴) syllabary — rare, never standardized, surviving in relatively few manuscripts — which appears beside the pictographs to gloss their readings and to write mantras, contracts and letters. This is closer to the early stages of cuneiform (c. 3200–2900 BCE) than to any modern script, yet it is alive now.</p><h3>UNESCO and the Race Against Time</h3><p>In 2003, UNESCO inscribed the ancient Naxi Dongba manuscripts on its <em>Memory of the World Register</em>. Thousands of manuscripts were destroyed during the Cultural Revolution, and about half of those that survive had already been taken abroad, to collections in the United States, Germany and Spain. UNESCO notes that only a few masters who can read the scriptures are left. Yunnan provincial authorities have funded training programs, Lijiang has a Dongba Culture Research Institute, and the pictographs now appear on buses and on shop signs in Lijiang's old town beside Chinese and English. Whether the script can survive as a living tradition — rather than a museum exhibit — remains an open question.</p><div class='trivia-actions'><button type='button' class='trivia-action' data-action='panto' data-lat='26.87' data-lng='100.23' data-zoom='6'>Map: Lijiang, Yunnan</button></div>"},"sources":["Rock, Joseph F. (1963). A Na-khi–English encyclopedic dictionary. Rome: ISMEO.","Mathieu, Christine (2003). A History and Anthropological Study of the Ancient Kingdoms of the Sino-Tibetan Borderland. Lewiston: Edwin Mellen Press.",{"text":"UNESCO Memory of the World: Naxi Dongba manuscripts","url":"https://www.unesco.org/en/memory-world/ancient-naxi-dongba-literature-manuscripts"},"McKhann, Charles F. (1995). 'The Naxi and the Nationalities Question,' in Stevan Harrell (ed.), Cultural Encounters on China's Ethnic Frontiers. Seattle: University of Washington Press, 39–62."]},{"id":"yi-syllabary","icon":"📜","tags":["yi","syllabary","yunnan","minority","script-reform"],"title":{"en":"Yi Script: From Thousands of Logograms to 819 Syllables","ja":"彝文：数千の表語文字から819の音節へ","ko":"이족 문자: 수천 개의 표어 문자에서 819개의 음절로","zh":"彝文：从数千个表意字到819个音节","yue":"彝文：由數以千計嘅表詞字到819個音節","vi":"Chữ Di: Từ hàng nghìn chữ ghi ý đến 819 âm tiết","th":"อักษรอี: จากอักษรแทนคำนับพันสู่ 819 พยางค์","id":"Aksara Yi: Dari Ribuan Logogram ke 819 Suku Kata","hi":"यी लिपि: हज़ारों लोगोग्राम से 819 शब्दांशों तक","de":"Yi-Schrift: Von Tausenden Logogrammen zu 819 Silben","fr":"Écriture Yi : des milliers de logogrammes aux 819 syllabes","it":"Scrittura Yi: da migliaia di logogrammi a 819 sillabe","es":"Escritura Yi: de miles de logogramas a 819 sílabas","pt":"Escrita Yi: de milhares de logogramas a 819 sílabas","ru":"Письмо И: от тысяч логограмм к 819 слогам","uk":"Письмо І: від тисяч логограм до 819 складів","ar":"الكتابة اليي: من آلاف الرموز الكلمية إلى 819 مقطعاً","he":"כתב היי: מאלפי לוגוגרמות ל-819 הברות","sw":"Maandishi ya Yi: Kutoka Maelfu ya Logogramu hadi Silabi 819"},"summary":{"en":"Traditional Yi script is a logographic system with thousands of signs and many regional variants. In 1980 China ratified a Liangshan standard of 819 syllabic signs, used today on road signs and in textbooks in Sichuan's Liangshan.","ja":"伝統的な彝文は、数千の字と多くの地域的な異体を持つ表語文字だ。1980年、中国は819の音節文字からなる涼山の標準を正式に承認し、今日の四川省涼山では道路標識や教科書に使われている。","ko":"전통 이족 문자는 수천 개의 기호와 수많은 지역 변종을 지닌 표어 문자다. 1980년 중국은 819개 음절 기호로 된 량산 표준을 비준했고, 이 문자는 오늘날 쓰촨성 량산의 도로 표지판과 교과서에 쓰인다.","zh":"传统彝文是一种表意文字，有数千个字符和众多地方变体。1980年，中国批准了一套以凉山话为基础、含819个音节符号的规范，如今用于四川凉山的路牌和教科书。","yue":"傳統彝文係一種表詞文字，有成千上萬個符號，地區變體眾多。1980年，中國批准咗一套有819個音節符號嘅涼山標準，今日喺四川涼山嘅路牌同課本上面用緊。","vi":"Chữ Di truyền thống là một hệ chữ ghi ý với hàng nghìn ký hiệu và vô số biến thể vùng miền. Năm 1980, Trung Quốc phê chuẩn bộ chuẩn Lương Sơn gồm 819 ký hiệu âm tiết, ngày nay được dùng trên biển báo đường bộ và trong sách giáo khoa ở Lương Sơn, Tứ Xuyên.","th":"อักษรอีดั้งเดิมเป็นระบบอักษรแทนคำที่มีสัญลักษณ์นับพันตัวและรูปแปรตามภูมิภาคมากมาย ในปี 1980 จีนรับรองมาตรฐานเหลียงซานที่มีสัญลักษณ์พยางค์ 819 ตัว ซึ่งปัจจุบันใช้บนป้ายถนนและในตำราเรียนที่เหลียงซาน มณฑลเสฉวน","id":"Aksara Yi tradisional adalah sistem logografis dengan ribuan tanda dan banyak varian daerah. Pada 1980 Tiongkok mengesahkan standar Liangshan berisi 819 tanda silabis, yang kini dipakai pada rambu jalan dan buku pelajaran di Liangshan, Sichuan.","hi":"पारंपरिक यी लिपि एक लोगोग्राफ़िक व्यवस्था है, जिसमें हज़ारों चिह्न और बहुत-से क्षेत्रीय रूप हैं। 1980 में चीन ने 819 शब्दांश-चिह्नों वाले एक लियांगशान मानक को मंज़ूरी दी, जो आज सिचुआन के लियांगशान में सड़क संकेतों और पाठ्यपुस्तकों में दिखता है।","de":"Die traditionelle Yi-Schrift ist ein logografisches System mit Tausenden von Zeichen und vielen regionalen Varianten. 1980 bestätigte China einen Liangshan-Standard aus 819 Silbenzeichen, der heute auf Straßenschildern und in Schulbüchern im sichuanesischen Liangshan zu sehen ist.","fr":"L'écriture Yi traditionnelle est un système logographique riche de milliers de signes et de nombreuses variantes régionales. En 1980, la Chine a ratifié une norme de Liangshan de 819 signes syllabiques, employée aujourd'hui sur les panneaux routiers et dans les manuels scolaires de Liangshan, au Sichuan.","it":"La scrittura Yi tradizionale è un sistema logografico con migliaia di segni e molte varianti regionali. Nel 1980 la Cina ha ratificato uno standard di Liangshan di 819 segni sillabici, usato oggi sui cartelli stradali e nei libri di testo di Liangshan, nel Sichuan.","es":"La escritura Yi tradicional es un sistema logográfico con miles de signos y muchas variantes regionales. En 1980 China ratificó un estándar de Liangshan de 819 signos silábicos, que hoy se usa en carteles de carretera y libros de texto en Liangshan, en Sichuan.","pt":"A escrita Yi tradicional é um sistema logográfico com milhares de sinais e muitas variantes regionais. Em 1980, a China ratificou um padrão de Liangshan com 819 sinais silábicos, usado hoje em placas de estrada e livros didáticos na região de Liangshan, em Sichuan.","ru":"Традиционное письмо И — логографическая система с тысячами знаков и множеством региональных вариантов. В 1980 году Китай утвердил ляншаньский стандарт из 819 слоговых знаков, которые сегодня используются на дорожных знаках и в учебниках в сычуаньском Ляншане.","uk":"Традиційне письмо І — логографічна система з тисячами знаків і безліччю регіональних варіантів. 1980 року Китай затвердив ляншанський стандарт із 819 складових знаків, які сьогодні можна побачити на дорожніх знаках і в підручниках сичуанського Ляншаню.","ar":"الخط اليي التقليدي نظام كلمي يضم آلاف الرموز وصيغاً إقليمية كثيرة. وفي عام 1980 أقرّت الصين معياراً لليانغشان من 819 رمزاً مقطعياً، يُستعمل اليوم على لافتات الطرق وفي الكتب المدرسية في ليانغشان بسيتشوان.","he":"כתב היי המסורתי הוא מערכת לוגוגרפית של אלפי סימנים וגרסאות אזוריות רבות. ב-1980 אישרה סין תקן ליאנגשאני של 819 סימני הברה, המשמש כיום על שלטי דרכים ובספרי לימוד בליאנגשאן שבסצ'ואן.","sw":"Maandishi ya jadi ya Yi ni mfumo wa logogramu wenye maelfu ya alama na vibadala vingi vya kimaeneo. Mwaka 1980 China iliidhinisha kiwango cha Liangshan cha alama 819 za silabi, kinachotumika leo kwenye vibao vya barabarani na katika vitabu vya shule huko Liangshan, Sichuan."},"body":{"en":"<h3>A Syllabary of Eight Thousand Signs</h3><p>The Yi people (彝族, Yízú) numbered 9.8 million at the 2020 census, spread across Sichuan, Yunnan, Guizhou, and Guangxi. Their traditional script, <strong>彝文 (Yí wén)</strong>, is a logographic system like Chinese: each sign stands for a syllable that is also a word or morpheme, and counting every regional variant, catalogues list thousands of signs. Legend credits its invention to a Nuosu hero, Aki, in the Tang dynasty, but the earliest dated example is far younger: an inscription on a bronze bell from 1485. Scribes and priests also used signs for their sound alone, so the script sat somewhere between Chinese characters and a syllabary, and it was far less uniform than either. Each clan or region maintained its own variant set, so a ritual text from Liangshan (涼山) might be incomprehensible to a reader from Guizhou even if both were Yi speakers. This regionalism made the script simultaneously rich and fragmented.</p><h3>The 1980 Standardization</h3><p>After the founding of the People's Republic, the government began studying minority scripts, and the process took decades. In 1974 Sichuan's Ethnic Affairs Commission drew up a syllabary from the traditional signs, and in 1980 the State Council ratified it as the <em>Liangshan Standard Yi Script</em> (<strong>涼山規範彝文</strong>): 756 basic signs, one per syllable of the Liangshan dialect of Nuosu, plus 63 for syllables found only in Chinese loanwords, 819 in all. Three of the four tones have signs of their own; the fourth is written by adding a small arc to the mid-tone sign. The choice mirrored the 1956 Simplified Chinese reform in its top-down, state-driven character — though Yi standardization reduced count far more drastically, from ~8,000 to 819.</p><p>The 819-sign system appears today on bilingual road signs throughout Liangshan Yi Autonomous Prefecture, in primary school textbooks, and in local broadcasting. Unicode encodes the standard Yi syllabary in the Yi Syllables block (U+A000–U+A48F), with a separate Yi Radicals block at U+A490–U+A4CF, making digital text straightforward. The standard is Sichuan's, though: Yunnan went its own way and in 1987 ratified a Yunnan Standard Yi of 2,608 signs, most of them logograms kept so that speakers of different Yi languages could still read each other's writing.</p><h3>The Politics of Standardization</h3><p>Not everyone welcomed the reform. Scholars and community elders who read the traditional 8,000-sign corpus argue that the new standard erases dialectal nuance and renders thousands of historical manuscripts unreadable to the younger generation trained only on the 819 standard. The parallel with simplified Chinese versus traditional Chinese is frequently drawn, and just as contested. Today, reading the traditional script is mostly the work of the <em>bimo</em>, the Nuosu priests who still copy and chant the old manuscripts, and of a handful of academic researchers; tens of thousands of those manuscripts survive.</p><div class='trivia-actions'><button type='button' class='trivia-action' data-action='panto' data-lat='28.01' data-lng='102.26' data-zoom='6'>Map: Liangshan, Sichuan</button></div>"},"sources":["Bender, Mark (2008). 'Tribes of Snow: Animals and Plants in the Nuosu Book of Origins.' Asian Ethnology 67(1): 5–42.","Bradley, David (2001). 'Language Policy for the Yi.' In Perspectives on the Yi of Southwest China. Berkeley: University of California Press.",{"text":"Unicode Yi Syllables Block (U+A000–U+A48F)","url":"https://www.unicode.org/charts/PDF/UA000.pdf"},"Guo Dalie & Lu Huiying (1999). 彝文字典. Chengdu: Sichuan Minzu Chubanshe."]},{"id":"zhuang-sawndip-script","icon":"🪨","tags":["sinosphere","kanji","history","writing-systems","southeast-asia"],"title":{"en":"Sawndip: The Thousand-Year Han-Derived Script of the Zhuang People","ja":"古壯字（方塊壯字）：漢字を借りたチワン族千年の文字","ko":"고장자(古壯字): 장족이 한자를 빌려 만든 천년 역사의 문자","zh":"古壮字（方块壮字）：壮族借汉字创制的千年文字","yue":"古壯字（方塊壯字）：壯族借漢字創造嘅千年文字","vi":"Chữ Sawndip: Hệ chữ viết nghìn năm của người Choang mượn từ chữ Hán","th":"ซอนดิบ: อักษรพันปีของชาวจ้วงที่ยืมมาจากตัวอักษรจีน","id":"Sawndip: Aksara Seribu Tahun Suku Zhuang yang Diturunkan dari Aksara Han","hi":"सौन्दिप: हान लिपि से व्युत्पन्न ज़ुआंग जनजाति की हज़ार साल पुरानी लिपि","de":"Sawndip: Die tausend Jahre alte Han-abgeleitete Schrift der Zhuang","fr":"Sawndip : l'écriture han-dérivée vieille de mille ans du peuple Zhuang","it":"Sawndip: La scrittura millenaria del popolo Zhuang derivata dai caratteri Han","es":"Sawndip: el sistema de escritura milenario del pueblo Zhuang derivado del Han","pt":"Sawndip: A Escrita Milenar do Povo Zhuang Derivada dos Caracteres Han","ru":"Саундип: тысячелетнее письмо чжуанского народа, производное от иероглифов хань","uk":"Саундіп: тисячолітнє письмо народу чжуан, похідне від ієрогліфів хань","ar":"ساوندب: الكتابة المشتقة من الحروف الهانية التي استخدمها شعب تشوانغ لأكثر من ألف عام","he":"סאונדיפ: כתב בן אלף שנה של עם הז'ואנג, המבוסס על תווי הָאן","sw":"Sawndip: Maandishi ya Miaka Elfu ya Watu wa Zhuang Yaliyotokana na Herufi za Han"},"summary":{"en":"The Zhuang people of southern China invented Sawndip — 'immature characters', as against the 'characters of the Han' — by repurposing and inventing Han-style characters to write their Tai-Kadai language, centuries before any Latin romanization existed.","ja":"中国南部のチワン族は、タイ・カダイ系の言語を書くため、漢字を転用・新造した古壮字（Sawndip）——漢族の文字に対する「未熟な文字」の意——を作り出した。ラテン文字ローマ字化より何世紀も早い。","ko":"중국 남부의 장족은 타이-카다이계 언어를 표기하기 위해 한자를 전용·신조한 고장자(Sawndip)—한족의 문자에 대비되는 '미숙한 문자'라는 뜻—를 만들어 냈다. 라틴자 로마자화보다 수세기나 앞선 일이다.","zh":"中国南方的壮族人为了书写泰-卡代语系语言，创造了古壮字（Sawndip——相对于「汉人之字」的「未熟之字」之意），借用和新造汉字，比任何拉丁拼音方案早了数百年。","yue":"中國南方嘅壯族人，為咗書寫佢哋嘅泰-卡代語系語言，借用同新創漢字，造出古壯字（Sawndip——相對於「漢人之字」嘅「未熟之字」），比任何拉丁拼音方案早咗幾百年。","vi":"Người Choang ở miền nam Trung Quốc đã sáng tạo ra chữ Sawndip — 'chữ non', đối lại với 'chữ của người Hán' — bằng cách tái sử dụng và sáng chế các ký tự theo kiểu chữ Hán để viết ngôn ngữ Tai-Kadai của họ.","th":"ชาวจ้วงในจีนตอนใต้สร้างอักษรซอนดิบ — 'อักษรดิบ' ซึ่งตรงข้ามกับ 'อักษรของชาวฮั่น' — โดยดัดแปลงและประดิษฐ์ตัวอักษรแบบฮั่นเพื่อเขียนภาษาไท-กะไดของตน","id":"Orang Zhuang di Tiongkok selatan menciptakan Sawndip — 'aksara mentah', sebagai lawan dari 'aksara orang Han' — dengan menggunakan kembali dan menciptakan karakter bergaya Han untuk menulis bahasa Tai-Kadai mereka.","hi":"चीन के दक्षिणी भाग में ज़ुआंग लोगों ने सौन्दिप — 'कच्चे अक्षर', यानी 'हान के अक्षरों' के विपरीत — की रचना की, जिसमें हान-शैली के अक्षरों को पुनर्प्रयुक्त और नव-निर्मित करके ताई-कादाई भाषा लिखी गई।","de":"Das Zhuang-Volk in Südchina erfand Sawndip — 'unreife Schriftzeichen', im Gegensatz zu den 'Schriftzeichen der Han' — indem es Han-artige Zeichen für seine Tai-Kadai-Sprache zweckentfremdete und neu erfand.","fr":"Le peuple Zhuang du sud de la Chine a inventé le Sawndip — les 'caractères immatures', par opposition aux 'caractères des Han' — en réutilisant et en inventant des caractères de style han pour écrire sa langue taï-kadaï.","it":"Il popolo Zhuang della Cina meridionale inventò il Sawndip — 'caratteri acerbi', in contrapposizione ai 'caratteri degli Han' — riutilizzando e inventando caratteri in stile Han per scrivere la propria lingua Tai-Kadai.","es":"El pueblo Zhuang del sur de China inventó el Sawndip — 'caracteres inmaduros', frente a los 'caracteres de los han' — reutilizando e inventando caracteres de estilo han para escribir su lengua tai-kadai.","pt":"O povo Zhuang do sul da China inventou o Sawndip — 'caracteres imaturos', por oposição aos 'caracteres dos han' — reutilizando e inventando caracteres no estilo Han para escrever sua língua Tai-Kadai.","ru":"Народ чжуан на юге Китая изобрёл саундип — «незрелые знаки», в противоположность «знакам хань» — перепрофилировав и придумав знаки в стиле хань для записи своего тай-кадайского языка.","uk":"Народ чжуан на півдні Китаю винайшов саундіп — «незрілі знаки», на противагу «знакам хань» — переробивши та вигадавши знаки в стилі хань для запису своєї тай-кадайської мови.","ar":"ابتكر شعب تشوانغ في جنوب الصين نظام ساوندب — أي 'الحروف الفجّة' في مقابل 'حروف الهان' — بإعادة توظيف وابتكار حروف على النمط الهاني لكتابة لغتهم التاي-قاداي.","he":"עם הז'ואנג בדרום סין המציא את הסאונדיפ — 'תווים בוסריים', בניגוד ל'תווים של ההָאן' — באמצעות שימוש מחדש והמצאת תווים בסגנון הָאן לכתיבת שפתם הטאי-קאדאית.","sw":"Watu wa Zhuang kusini mwa China walivumbua Sawndip — 'herufi mbichi', kinyume na 'herufi za Wahan' — kwa kutumia tena na kuunda herufi za mtindo wa Han ili kuandika lugha yao ya Tai-Kadai."},"body":{"en":"<h3>What Is Sawndip?</h3><p><strong>Sawndip</strong> (Zhuang <em>saw</em> 'character' + <em>ndip</em> 'raw, unripe' — literally 'immature characters', as opposed to <em>sawgun</em>, the 'characters of the Han'; in Chinese 古壯字 'old Zhuang characters' or <em>Fangkuai Zhuangzi</em> 方塊壯字 'square-shaped Zhuang characters') is the traditional writing system used by the Zhuang people — the largest ethnic minority in China, numbering 19.6 million at the 2020 census — to record their Tai-Kadai language. Its beginnings are uncertain. Two Tang-dynasty stone inscriptions from the Zhuang area (682 and 697 CE) are written in Chinese, but the later one already contains a few non-standard local characters, such as one for <em>naz</em> 'paddy field', and the script is usually reckoned to have been in use for over a thousand years, though the manuscripts that survive are far younger — overwhelmingly Qing-dynasty copies of works first composed in the Ming or earlier.</p><h3>Three Strategies for Borrowing Han Script</h3><p>Sawndip is not a simple copy of Chinese. Scribes used three distinct strategies:</p><ul><li><strong>Phonetic borrowing (假借, jiǎjiè)</strong> — a Han character is used purely for its sound, ignoring meaning. Just as Japanese <em>man'yōgana</em> (万葉仮名) spelled Japanese words with Han phonetics, Zhuang scribes wrote native words with whichever Han character sounded closest in the local dialect: the verb <em>miz</em> 'to have' is written 眉, Chinese for 'eyebrow', purely for its sound. This is the dominant principle; David Holm estimates that about 71% of Sawndip characters are phonetic.</li><li><strong>Semantic borrowing</strong> — a Han character whose meaning matches a Zhuang word is borrowed wholesale, even if the pronunciation differs completely.</li><li><strong>Local invention (形聲 xíngshēng compounds)</strong> — the most striking category. Scribes <em>invented</em> new characters by combining a Han semantic radical with a phonetic component, exactly as Classical Chinese coined new sinographs. <em>Bya</em> 'mountain' is 岜, 山 'mountain' plus 巴 <em>ba</em> for the sound; <em>vunz</em> 'person' is 伝, the 'person' radical 亻 plus 云 for the sound. Characters like these look Chinese but mean nothing to a reader of standard Chinese.</li></ul><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"focus\" data-code=\"za\">📍 View Zhuang (Vahcuengh) on map</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"panto\" data-lat=\"23.5\" data-lng=\"108.0\" data-zoom=\"6\">🗺 Pan to Guangxi</button></div><h3>The 萬葉仮名 Parallel</h3><p>The parallel with Japanese <em>man'yōgana</em> is striking but limited. Both systems use Han characters phonetically to represent a non-Sinitic language. Yet Sawndip went further: while man'yōgana eventually abstracted into kana syllabaries, Sawndip never shed its full-character form. Every Sawndip glyph retains the visual weight of a Han character, so a page of Sawndip looks, at a glance, like a page of Chinese, yet is entirely unreadable to someone who knows only Mandarin.</p><h3>Ritual and Vernacular Use</h3><p>Sawndip was never standardised by any imperial authority. Instead it developed organically in the hands of Zhuang village priests (<em>mo</em> 摩), who used it to record ritual incantations, epic songs like <em>Buluotuo</em> (布洛陀), and almanacs. Different villages often used different characters for the same word. This local variation means one Zhuang word can have many different spellings across manuscripts. The first and so far only dictionary of the script, the <em>Sawndip Sawdenj</em> (1989), needed over 10,000 characters to cover its 4,900 entries.</p><h3>1957 and the Latin Alphabet</h3><p>The People's Republic of China introduced an alphabetic script for a newly standardised Zhuang language in 1957, designed by linguists to unify the many mutually unintelligible Zhuang dialects under a single orthography. That first alphabet mixed Latin with Cyrillic- and IPA-derived letters, including five tone letters (ƨ ɜ ч ƽ ƅ) that look like the digits 2 to 6. A 1982 reform replaced them with plain Latin ones, so tone is now written with a final letter — <em>z, j, x, q, h</em>. This is the orthography of Standard Zhuang, <strong>Sawcuengh</strong> 壮文 — <em>Vahcuengh</em>, by contrast, is the Zhuang name of the language itself, not of the script — and it is the official written standard today. Yet Sawndip persists. Village ritual specialists still copy texts by hand, and people still use it for song lyrics and personal notes; in one survey of two dialect areas, just over a third of respondents said they could understand it. Unicode has added thousands of Sawndip characters since 2017 (CJK Extensions F, G, H and J), so the old script can now be typed.</p>"},"sources":["Holm, David. <em>Mapping the Old Zhuang Character Script: A Vernacular Writing System from Southern China</em>. Handbook of Oriental Studies. Leiden/Boston: Brill, 2013.","Chamberlain, James R. 'A New Look at the History and Classification of the Tai Languages.' <em>Studies in Tai Linguistics</em>, 1975.",{"text":"Sawndip — Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sawndip"},"Bauer, Robert S. 'Written Cantonese of Hong Kong.' <em>Cahiers de Linguistique Asie Orientale</em> 17(2), 1988.","Norman, Jerry. <em>Chinese</em>. Cambridge University Press, 1988.",{"text":"Vahcuengh (Zhuang alphabet) — Omniglot","url":"https://www.omniglot.com/writing/zhuang.htm"}]},{"id":"bai-language-script","icon":"🏔","tags":["sinosphere","kanji","history","writing-systems","classification"],"title":{"en":"Bai Script: The Han-Character Vehicle for an Unclassified Language","ja":"白文：帰属不明の言語を担う漢字文字","ko":"백문(白文): 계통 불명 언어를 담은 한자 문자","zh":"白文：承载归属未定语言的汉字书写系统","yue":"白文：承載歸屬未定語言嘅漢字書寫系統","vi":"Chữ Bạch: Hệ chữ Hán ghi lại một ngôn ngữ chưa được phân loại","th":"อักษรไป๋: ระบบอักษรฮั่นสำหรับภาษาที่ยังไม่ถูกจำแนก","id":"Aksara Bai: Sistem Aksara Han untuk Bahasa yang Belum Terklasifikasi","hi":"बाई लिपि: एक अवर्गीकृत भाषा की वाहक हान लिपि","de":"Bai-Schrift: Das Han-Zeichensystem für eine unklassifizierte Sprache","fr":"L'écriture Bai : les caractères han au service d'une langue inclassifiable","it":"Scrittura Bai: I caratteri Han al servizio di una lingua non classificata","es":"La escritura Bai: los caracteres Han para una lengua sin clasificar","pt":"A Escrita Bai: Os Caracteres Han para uma Língua Não Classificada","ru":"Письменность байского языка: иероглифы хань для неклассифицированного языка","uk":"Письменність байської мови: ієрогліфи хань для некласифікованої мови","ar":"الكتابة البايية: الحروف الهانية لخدمة لغة غير مصنّفة","he":"כתב הבאי: תווי הָאן לשפה שאינה מסווגת","sw":"Maandishi ya Bai: Herufi za Han kwa Lugha Isiyoainishwa"},"summary":{"en":"The Bai people of Dali, Yunnan used Han characters to write their own language for centuries. Linguists still disagree over whether Bai is Sinitic, Tibeto-Burman, or an independent branch of Sino-Tibetan.","ja":"雲南省大理の白族は、数百年にわたり漢字を用いて自分たちの言語を書いてきた。言語学者たちは、白語がシナ語派なのかチベット・ビルマ語派なのか、それともシノ・チベット語族の独立した一分枝なのかをいまだ論争している。","ko":"윈난성 다리의 바이족은 수백 년 동안 한자를 이용해 자신들의 언어를 기록해 왔다. 언어학자들은 바이어가 시나어파인지, 티베트-버마어파인지, 시노-티베트어족의 독립 분기인지를 여전히 논쟁하고 있다.","zh":"云南大理的白族几百年来一直用汉字书写自己的语言，语言学家至今仍在争论白语究竟属于汉语族、藏缅语族，还是汉藏语系的独立分支。","yue":"雲南大理嘅白族幾百年嚟都用漢字書寫自己嘅語言，語言學家至今仍然爭論白語係屬漢語族、藏緬語族，定係漢藏語系嘅獨立分支。","vi":"Người Bạch ở Đại Lý, Vân Nam đã dùng chữ Hán để viết ngôn ngữ của họ trong nhiều thế kỷ — nhưng các nhà ngôn ngữ học vẫn tranh luận liệu tiếng Bạch thuộc nhóm Hán hay Tạng-Miến hay là một nhánh độc lập của hệ Hán-Tạng.","th":"ชาวไป๋แห่งต้าหลี่ มณฑลยูนนาน ใช้อักษรฮั่นเขียนภาษาของตนมาหลายศตวรรษ แต่นักภาษาศาสตร์ยังคงถกเถียงว่าภาษาไป๋เป็นภาษาจีน ทิเบต-พม่า หรือเป็นสาขาอิสระของตระกูลจีน-ทิเบต","id":"Orang Bai di Dali, Yunnan menggunakan aksara Han untuk menulis bahasa mereka selama berabad-abad — namun para ahli bahasa masih memperdebatkan apakah bahasa Bai termasuk Sinitik, Tibeto-Burman, atau cabang mandiri rumpun Sino-Tibet.","hi":"युन्नान के दाली के बाई लोगों ने सदियों तक हान अक्षरों का उपयोग अपनी भाषा लिखने के लिए किया — फिर भी भाषाविद् अभी भी यह बहस कर रहे हैं कि बाई भाषा सिनिटिक है, तिब्बती-बर्मी है, या सिनो-तिब्बती की एक स्वतंत्र शाखा।","de":"Das Bai-Volk in Dali, Yunnan, benutzte jahrhundertelang Han-Schriftzeichen für ihre Sprache — doch Linguisten streiten bis heute, ob Bai sinitisch, tibeto-birmanisch oder ein eigenständiger Zweig des Sino-Tibetischen ist.","fr":"Le peuple Bai de Dali, dans le Yunnan, a utilisé des caractères han pour écrire sa langue pendant des siècles — pourtant, les linguistes débattent toujours de son appartenance : sinétique, tibéto-birmane ou branche indépendante du sino-tibétain.","it":"Il popolo Bai di Dali, nello Yunnan, ha usato i caratteri han per scrivere la propria lingua per secoli — eppure i linguisti discutono ancora se il Bai sia sinico, tibeto-birmano o un ramo indipendente del Sino-Tibetano.","es":"El pueblo Bai de Dali, Yunnan, usó caracteres han para escribir su lengua durante siglos — sin embargo, los lingüistas siguen debatiendo si el Bai es sínico, tibeto-birmano o una rama independiente del sino-tibetano.","pt":"O povo Bai de Dali, Yunnan, usou caracteres Han para escrever sua língua durante séculos — mas os linguistas ainda debatem se o Bai é sinítico, tibeto-birmânico ou um ramo independente do sino-tibetano.","ru":"Народ бай из Дали (Юньнань) веками использовал иероглифы хань для записи своего языка — однако лингвисты до сих пор спорят, является ли байский язык синитическим, тибето-бирманским или самостоятельной ветвью сино-тибетской семьи.","uk":"Народ бай із Далі (Юньнань) століттями використовував ієрогліфи хань для запису своєї мови — однак лінгвісти досі сперечаються, чи є байська мова синітичною, тибето-бірманською чи самостійною гілкою сино-тибетської сім'ї.","ar":"استخدم شعب باي في دالي بمقاطعة يوننان الحروف الهانية لتدوين لغتهم لقرون — ومع ذلك لا يزال علماء اللغة يتجادلون حول تصنيف لغة باي: هل هي صينية أو تبتية-برمية أو فرع مستقل من الأسرة الصينية-التبتية؟","he":"עם הבאי מדאלי, יונאן, השתמש בתווי הָאן לכתיבת שפתו במשך מאות שנים — ובכל זאת בלשנים עדיין חולקים האם הבאית היא שפה סינית, טיבטית-בורמזית, או ענף עצמאי של המשפחה הסינו-טיבטית.","sw":"Watu wa Bai wa Dali, Yunnan walitumia herufi za Han kuandika lugha yao kwa karne nyingi — lakini wanaisimu bado wanajadili kama lugha ya Bai ni ya Kisiniti, Kitibeto-Burma, au tawi huru la familia ya Sino-Tibetani."},"body":{"en":"<h3>Who Are the Bai?</h3><p>The <strong>Bai people</strong> (白族, Báizú, literally 'white people') are an ethnic group of approximately 2.1 million at the 2020 census, concentrated around Dali (大理) in Yunnan province, southwest China. Not all of them speak Bai: Ethnologue's 2003 estimate was about 1.3 million speakers. Their kingdom, the <strong>Nanzhao</strong> (南詔, 738–902 CE) and its successor the <strong>Dali Kingdom</strong> (大理國, 937–1253 CE), was a major regional power that interacted intensely with Tang-dynasty China and with Tibetan and Southeast Asian civilisations. This political history explains why the Bai language has absorbed — and been thoroughly shaped by — Chinese.</p><h3>The Classification Puzzle</h3><p>Bai is one of Asia's most contested classification problems. Three serious camps exist:</p><ul><li><strong>Sinitic branch</strong> — some scholars (notably Sergei Starostin and Zhengzhang Shangfang) argue that the oldest Chinese layer accounts for all but an insignificant residue of Bai vocabulary, so that Bai is an early branching from Chinese itself; Starostin dates the split to around the 2nd century BC, in the Western Han, long before the modern Sinitic varieties took shape.</li><li><strong>Tibeto-Burman</strong>: others, such as Lee and Sagart, find a real non-Chinese core under the loans, which they first linked to the Loloish languages (relatives of Yi) and later more generally to the non-Chinese side of Sino-Tibetan.</li><li><strong>Independent branch</strong>: James Matisoff judged it \"safer to consider Bai as an independent branch of Sino-Tibetan\", perhaps close to Loloish, and deeply reshaped by centuries of contact with Chinese.</li></ul><p>The difficulty is that Bai is full of Chinese words from many different periods, spanning some two thousand years. The oldest layer, about 250 basic words, is older than Middle Chinese: it keeps apart rhymes that Chinese itself had merged by the 4th century AD. Later layers run all the way up to modern Southwestern Mandarin. The deeper you dig, the harder it is to tell a very old loan from a word Bai and Chinese both inherited.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"focus\" data-code=\"bca\">📍 View Bai on map</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"panto\" data-lat=\"25.6\" data-lng=\"100.3\" data-zoom=\"7\">🗺 Pan to Dali, Yunnan</button></div><h3>How White Script (白文) Works</h3><p>Like the Zhuang, the Bai used Han characters to write their language in a system called <strong>Baiwen</strong> (白文, 'Bai writing') or <strong>Bowen</strong>. The same character can be read with two entirely different pronunciations depending on whether you are reading it as Chinese or as Bai:</p><ul><li>Not every character splits cleanly: Bai's own word for 'horse' is itself <em>ma˧˧</em> (Proto-Bai *<em>mæ</em>), an old Sinitic form, so 馬 sounds much the same whether you read it as Chinese or as Bai. The two systems separate where the layers diverge — 目 carries the Chinese-layer reading <em>mu˨˨</em> in Bai, while the everyday Bai word for 'eye' is <em>ŋui˨˨</em>.</li><li>Grammatical particles, aspect markers, and tone-carrying suffixes particular to Bai grammar are handled through phonetic borrowing, sometimes using rare or obscure Han characters purely for their local dialect pronunciation.</li></ul><h3>The Latin Alphabet: Three Attempts</h3><p>Bai has three main dialect groups: Jianchuan (Central), Dali (Southern) and Bijiang (Northern). Speakers of the first two are said to understand each other after living together for a month. The Northern varieties, spoken by about 15,000 people, are more divergent, and ISO 639-3 in fact gives four Bai varieties a code of their own. Writing them all in one alphabet has proved hard. A <strong>Latin alphabet</strong> designed in 1958, based on the speech of Xiaguan near Dali, saw little use. A 1982 version based on Jianchuan caught on there but was rejected in Dali, so the 1993 revision gave each area its own variant. Instead of accents or numbers, it marks tone with an extra letter at the end of each syllable, the same trick the Hmong alphabet RPA uses. Meanwhile the old character-based Baiwen survives in religious texts (especially Benzhu 本主 deity worship), classical poetry, and stone inscriptions around the Dali plain — some dating to the Nanzhao kingdom itself.</p><h3>A Mirror of Sinosphere Contact</h3><p>The Bai case illustrates a pattern found across the Sinosphere: centuries of sustained literary contact with Classical Chinese created writing traditions that are simultaneously indebted to and distinct from Chinese. Where Zhuang invented new sinographs for phonology, and Vietnamese <em>Chữ Nôm</em> did the same for an Austroasiatic language, Bai developed a dual-reading convention — one glyph, two worlds.</p>"},"sources":["Wiersma, Grace. 'A Study of the Bai (Minjia) Language Along Historical Lines.' PhD dissertation, University of California, Berkeley, 1990.","Handel, Zev. 'What is Sino-Tibetan? Snapshot of a Field and a Theory in Flux.' <em>Language and Linguistics Compass</em> 2(3), 2008, pp. 422–441.",{"text":"Bai language — Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bai_language"},"Xu Lin 徐琳 &amp; Zhao Yansun 赵衍荪. <em>白语简志</em> [A Brief Record of the Bai Language]. Beijing: Minzu Chubanshe, 1984.","Abramson, Marc. <em>Ethnic Identity in Tang China</em>. University of Pennsylvania Press, 2008.",{"text":"Nanzhao and Dali Kingdoms — Encyclopaedia Britannica","url":"https://www.britannica.com/topic/Nanzhao"}]},{"id":"wenzhou-devils-tongue","icon":"👻","tags":["wu","phonology","history","sinosphere"],"title":{"en":"Wenzhou Wu — A Distinct Wu Variety","ja":"温州語——「鬼話」と恐れられた方言","ko":"원저우어 — 우어 내부에서도 차이가 큰 방언","zh":"温州话——吴语内部差异较大的方言","yue":"溫州話——連中國人都聽唔明嘅「鬼話」","vi":"Tiếng Ôn Châu — một phương ngữ khác biệt trong nhóm Ngô","th":"ภาษาเวินโจว — ภาษาปิศาจที่แม้แต่คนจีนก็ไม่เข้าใจ","id":"Bahasa Wenzhou — Bahasa Setan yang Membingungkan","hi":"वेनझोउ वू — शैतान की ज़बान","de":"Wenzhou-Wu — die Teufelssprache","fr":"Le wu de Wenzhou — la langue du diable","it":"Il wu di Wenzhou — la lingua del diavolo","es":"El wu de Wenzhou — la lengua del diablo","pt":"O wu de Wenzhou — a língua do diabo","ru":"Вэньчжоуский у — язык дьявола","uk":"Веньчжоуський у — мова диявола","ar":"لهجة وِنتشو الوو — لسان الشيطان","he":"וו של וונג׳ו — לשון השטן","sw":"Kiwenzhou — Lugha ya Shetani"},"summary":{"en":"Wenzhou Wu has limited mutual intelligibility with Mandarin and many neighbouring Wu varieties. Accounts of its use as a wartime cipher are widespread but remain undocumented.","ja":"温州語は日中戦争で自然の暗号として使われた——と言われる。裏づけはないが、そう語られるだけの難解さではある。","ko":"원저우어는 중일전쟁 당시 통신에 쓰였다는 이야기가 널리 퍼져 있지만 이를 뒷받침하는 사료는 확인되지 않았다. 우어 내부에서도 음운과 어휘 차이가 큰 방언이다.","zh":"温州话据说曾在抗战时用于军事通信，但目前没有史料证实。其音韵和词汇与北部吴语差异较大。","yue":"溫州話咁難明，據講抗戰時中國軍隊攞佢嚟做天然密碼——呢個講法從來未有史料證實，但佢確實難明到有咁嘅傳聞都唔奇。","vi":"Có lời kể rằng tiếng Ôn Châu từng được dùng trong liên lạc quân sự thời chiến, nhưng chưa có tài liệu xác nhận. Âm vị và từ vựng của nó khác đáng kể với các phương ngữ Ngô phía bắc.","th":"ภาษาเวินโจวยากเข้าใจจนเล่ากันว่ากองทัพจีนใช้เป็นรหัสลับในสงครามกับญี่ปุ่นโดยไม่ต้องเข้ารหัสเพิ่มเติม เป็นเรื่องเล่าที่ไม่เคยมีหลักฐานยืนยัน แต่ก็สมกับชื่อเสียงความยากของภาษานี้","id":"Bahasa Wenzhou begitu sulit dipahami sehingga konon digunakan sebagai sandi alami oleh pasukan Tiongkok melawan Jepang — kisah termasyhur yang tak pernah terdokumentasikan, tetapi memang pantas disandang bahasa ini.","hi":"वेनझोउ भाषा इतनी जटिल है कि कहा जाता है कि जापान के विरुद्ध युद्ध में चीनी सेना ने इसे प्राकृतिक सांकेतिक भाषा के रूप में इस्तेमाल किया — यह मशहूर किस्सा आज तक किसी दस्तावेज़ से प्रमाणित नहीं हुआ, पर इस भाषा की दुर्बोधता ऐसी ख्याति की हकदार ज़रूर है।","de":"Wenzhou-Wu ist so undurchdringlich, dass chinesische Truppen es im Krieg gegen Japan als natürlichen Code verwendet haben sollen — eine berühmte Geschichte, für die es keinerlei Belege gibt, die dem Ruf der Sprache aber alle Ehre macht.","fr":"Le wu de Wenzhou n'offre qu'une intercompréhension limitée avec le mandarin et bien des variétés wu voisines. Les récits de son emploi comme code secret en temps de guerre sont très répandus, mais restent non documentés.","it":"Il wu di Wenzhou è così oscuro che l'esercito cinese lo avrebbe usato come codice naturale contro il Giappone: una storia celebre che nessuno ha mai documentato, ma che la fama della lingua merita in pieno.","es":"El wu de Wenzhou es tan opaco que, según se cuenta, el ejército chino lo usó como código natural contra Japón: una historia célebre que nadie ha documentado jamás, pero que la fama de la lengua tiene bien merecida.","pt":"O wu de Wenzhou é tão opaco que, segundo se conta, o exército chinês o usou como código natural contra o Japão: uma história famosa que ninguém jamais documentou, mas que a fama da língua merece de sobra.","ru":"Вэньчжоуский у настолько непонятен, что китайские войска, как рассказывают, использовали его как природный шифр в войне с Японией — знаменитая история, которую никто так и не подтвердил документами, но которую репутация этого диалекта вполне заслужила.","uk":"Веньчжоуський у настільки незрозумілий, що китайські війська, як переказують, використовували його як природний шифр у війні з Японією — славетна історія, яку ніхто так і не підтвердив документами, але на яку слава цієї говірки цілком заслуговує.","ar":"لهجة وِنتشو الوو صعبة الفهم لدرجة أن الجيش الصيني — كما يُقال — استخدمها شفرةً طبيعيةً في الحرب ضد اليابان؛ قصة شهيرة لم يوثّقها أحد قط، لكن صعوبة اللهجة تجعلها جديرة بها.","he":"וו של וונג׳ו כה אטומה עד שלפי סיפור מפורסם הצבא הסיני השתמש בה כקוד טבעי במלחמה נגד יפן — סיפור שמעולם לא תועד, אך המוניטין של השפה בהחלט מצדיק אותו.","sw":"Kiwenzhou kina utata mkubwa hadi inasemekana jeshi la China lilikitumia kama msimbo wa asili katika vita dhidi ya Japani — hadithi maarufu ambayo haijawahi kuthibitishwa kwa nyaraka, lakini inayoendana kabisa na sifa ya lugha hii."},"body":{"en":"<h3>Mutual Intelligibility</h3><p>Wenzhou Wu has limited mutual intelligibility with Mandarin and also differs substantially from Northern Wu varieties such as Shanghainese and Suzhounese. A popular saying goes: <em>天不怕，地不怕，就怕溫州人說溫州話</em> — \"Fear not heaven, fear not earth, but fear a Wenzhou man speaking Wenzhounese.\" In one variant the last words become <em>講鬼話</em>, \"speaking ghost-talk\"; <strong>鬼話</strong> also means \"lies, nonsense\", so the nickname was never a compliment. (In fairness, Chinese speakers aim the same saying at other varieties they find hard to follow.)</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"focus\" data-code=\"wuu_wz\">📍 Wenzhou Wu on map</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"panto\" data-lat=\"28.0\" data-lng=\"120.7\" data-zoom=\"7\">🗺 Pan to Wenzhou</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"人\">🔢 Show 人</button></div><h3>The Code That Wasn't a Code — And May Never Have Been</h3><p>The most repeated story about Wenzhou Wu is that during the Second Sino-Japanese War, Chinese commanders put Wenzhou soldiers on the radio and Japanese cryptanalysts — fluent in Standard Chinese, familiar with the major dialects — could make nothing of it. Unlike the Navajo Code Talkers of the US Marines, who layered an artificially constructed cipher on top of their language, Wenzhou speakers would have needed no code at all: the language itself was the secret. The story remains undocumented. The sinologist Victor Mair, reviewing the claim on Language Log in 2015, wrote that despite the language's formidable reputation he had yet to see \"solid documentation of the use of Wenzhounese for intelligence purposes in war\", and noted a practical obstacle: Wenzhounese is not a written language, and the missionaries' romanisation never circulated among ordinary speakers. Reference works describe the story only as \"reputed\". A similar claim about the 1979 border war with Vietnam may involve not Wenzhounese proper but 蛮话 (Manhua), a distinct variety spoken around Qianku in Cangnan County. What the legend does record faithfully is the reputation.</p><h3>Voiced Obstruents — A Medieval Survival</h3><p>Several phonological features contribute to Wenzhou's limited mutual intelligibility. Middle Chinese (中古漢語), the Chinese of the Sui and Tang dynasties (roughly 600–900 CE) recorded in the rhyme books, possessed a full set of <strong>voiced obstruents</strong>: /b/, /d/, /g/, /dz/. Mandarin, Cantonese and Hokkien all lost this contrast over the centuries, merging voiced and voiceless initials into a simpler system compensated by tone splits. Wu is the branch that did not: preserving the Middle Chinese voiced series is the classic defining criterion for Wu, and Shanghai and Suzhou keep it as surely as Wenzhou does. What makes Wenzhou harder to follow than Shanghai is not the voiced series but what sits on top of it — an eight-tone system with unusual contours, and a vocabulary that diverges sharply from Northern Wu. A Wenzhou speaker distinguishes 爬 /bo˧˩/ 'to climb' from both 怕 /pʰo˦˨/ 'to fear' and 巴 /po˧˧/, with a voiced /b/, a contrast absent from Mandarin, and the voicing pulls the syllable into the lower (yáng) tone register, which is why the three never collide. Those eight tones are arranged in a yin–yang split mirroring the original Middle Chinese distinction.</p><h3>Saying \"This\" with a Tone</h3><p>Wenzhou also diverges grammatically, and its most striking trick is a demonstrative made of pure tone. To say \"this\", a speaker takes the classifier (the measure word) and switches it to the dipping <em>rù</em> tone, devoicing a voiced initial on the way: /pa˧/ 'group' becomes /pa˧˨˧/ 'this group', and /le˧˩/ 'some (people)' becomes /l̥e˧˨˧/ 'these people'. No separate word for \"this\" is needed. Some dialectologists look for traces of the language of the ancient Ou Yue (甌越) people, who lived on this coast before Chinese speakers arrived, but such substrate claims remain hard to prove.</p><h3>Why Wenzhou Diverged</h3><p>Geographic isolation is the primary answer. Wenzhou sits at the southern tip of Zhejiang, hemmed in by the Yandang Mountains and open sea. Overland travel stayed arduous for a remarkably long time: the first railway into Wenzhou, the Jinhua–Wenzhou line, opened only in June 1998. For most of its history the city traded by sea, connecting more easily with Fujianese ports than with the Yangtze delta heartland. Isolation cut both ways. It let Wenzhou keep old distinctions that Northern Wu lost, and it also left the local speech free to run its own course; the city's own name, 溫州, comes out roughly as /ʔjy tɕɤu/.</p><ul><li>8 tones; voiced /b d g/ preserved from Middle Chinese</li><li>Mutual intelligibility near zero with Standard Mandarin</li><li>Reputed, but never documented, to have served as a natural military cipher</li><li>Geographic isolation in southern Zhejiang preserved archaisms</li></ul>"},"sources":["Ballard, W. L. (1985). Aspects of the Linguistic History of South China. Asian Perspectives, 24(2), 163–185.","Norman, Jerry (1988). Chinese. Cambridge Language Surveys. Cambridge University Press.",{"text":"Wenzhou dialect — Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wenzhou_dialect"},"Yue-Hashimoto, Anne O. (2003). Chinese dialects: grammar. In Graham Thurgood & Randy J. LaPolla (eds.), The Sino-Tibetan Languages, 84–125. Routledge.","Coblin, W. South (1994). A Compendium of Phonetics in Northwest Chinese. Journal of Chinese Linguistics Monograph Series.",{"text":"Cao, Jianfen & Ian Maddieson (1992). An exploration of phonation types in Wu dialects of Chinese. Journal of Phonetics, 20(1), 77–92.","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0095-4470(19)30255-4"},{"text":"Mair, Victor (2015). \"Devilishly difficult 'dialect'.\" Language Log.","url":"https://languagelog.ldc.upenn.edu/nll/?p=20790"}]},{"id":"hakka-diaspora-language","icon":"🌏","tags":["hakka","diaspora","sinosphere","history"],"title":{"en":"Hakka — The Language of the \"Guest People\"","ja":"客家語——「客人」と呼ばれた人々の言語","ko":"객가어 — '손님 민족'의 언어","zh":"客家话——「客人」的语言","yue":"客家話——「客人」嘅語言","vi":"Tiếng Khách Gia — Ngôn ngữ của \"Người Khách\"","th":"ภาษาแคะ — ภาษาของ 'คนต่างถิ่น'","id":"Bahasa Hakka — Bahasa \"Orang Tamu\"","hi":"हक्का — 'अतिथि लोगों' की भाषा","de":"Hakka — die Sprache des „Gastvolks“","fr":"Le hakka — la langue du « peuple hôte »","it":"L'hakka — la lingua del «popolo ospite»","es":"El hakka — la lengua del «pueblo huésped»","pt":"O hakka — a língua do «povo hóspede»","ru":"Хакка — язык «народа гостей»","uk":"Хакка — мова «народу гостей»","ar":"الهاكا — لغة «شعب الضيوف»","he":"האקה — שפת \"עם האורחים\"","sw":"Kihakka — Lugha ya \"Watu Wageni\""},"summary":{"en":"Hakka speakers live across southern China and diaspora communities from Mauritius to Suriname. Many Hakka varieties preserve the Middle Chinese entering-tone finals /-p/, /-t/, and /-k/.","ja":"客家語は江西から模里西斯、スリナムまで広がり、中古漢語の入声尾をすべて保存する。孫文・鄧小平・李光耀を生んだ言語。","ko":"객가어 화자는 강서에서 모리셔스, 수리남까지 뻗어 있으며, 중고 한어의 입성 운미를 모두 보존한다. 손문·등소평·이광요를 배출한 언어.","zh":"客家话从江西到模里西斯、苏里南，保留了中古汉语所有入声韵尾，是孙文、邓小平、李光耀的母语。","yue":"客家話由江西到模里西斯、蘇里南，保留咗中古漢語所有入聲韻尾，係孫文、鄧小平、李光耀嘅母語。","vi":"Người nói tiếng Khách Gia trải dài từ Giang Tây đến Mauritius và Suriname; ngôn ngữ này bảo tồn cả ba âm cuối nhập thanh của Hán ngữ Trung cổ, và là ngôn ngữ mẹ đẻ của Tôn Trung Sơn, Đặng Tiểu Bình và Lý Quang Diệu.","th":"ผู้พูดภาษาแคะแผ่ขยายจากเจียงซีถึงมอริเชียสและซูรินาม ภาษานี้อนุรักษ์เสียงสะกดทั้งสามของอักษรเข้าเสียงจากภาษาจีนกลาง และเป็นภาษาแม่ของซุน ยัด-เซน เติ้ง เสี่ยวผิง และลี กวน ยู","id":"Penutur Hakka membentang dari Jiangxi hingga Mauritius dan Suriname; bahasa ini mempertahankan ketiga akhiran nada masuk Bahasa Tionghoa Pertengahan, dan merupakan bahasa ibu Sun Yat-sen, Deng Xiaoping, dan Lee Kuan Yew.","hi":"हक्का भाषी जियांग्शी से मॉरिशस और सूरीनाम तक फैले हैं; यह भाषा मध्य चीनी के तीनों प्रवेश-स्वर अंत्यों को संरक्षित करती है और सन यात-सेन, देंग शियाओपिंग व ली कुआन यू की मातृभाषा थी।","de":"Hakka-Sprecher leben in ganz Südchina und in Diaspora-Gemeinschaften von Mauritius bis Suriname. Viele Hakka-Varietäten bewahren die Eintretenden-Ton-Endungen des Mittelchinesischen: /-p/, /-t/ und /-k/.","fr":"Les locuteurs du hakka vivent dans tout le sud de la Chine et dans une diaspora qui va de l'île Maurice au Suriname. Bien des variétés hakka conservent les finales du ton d'entrée du chinois médiéval, /-p/, /-t/ et /-k/.","it":"I parlanti dell'hakka si estendono dal Jiangxi a Mauritius e al Suriname; la lingua conserva tutte e tre le finali del tono d'entrata del cinese medio ed è la madrelingua di Sun Yat-sen, Deng Xiaoping e Lee Kuan Yew.","es":"Los hablantes de hakka viven en el sur de China y en comunidades de la diáspora, de Mauricio a Surinam. Muchas variedades hakka conservan las finales del tono de entrada del chino medio /-p/, /-t/ y /-k/.","pt":"Os falantes de hakka estendem-se do Jiangxi à Maurícia e ao Suriname; a língua preserva todas as três finais do tom de entrada do chinês médio e foi a língua materna de Sun Yat-sen, Deng Xiaoping e Lee Kuan Yew.","ru":"Носители хакка расселены от Цзянси до Маврикия и Суринама; язык сохраняет все три конечных элемента «входящего тона» среднекитайского и является родным языком Сунь Ятсена, Дэн Сяопина и Ли Куан Ю.","uk":"Носії хакка розселені від Цзянсі до Маврикію і Суринаму; мова зберігає всі три фінальні елементи «вхідного тону» середньокитайської і є рідною мовою Сунь Ятсена, Ден Сяопіна та Лі Куан Ю.","ar":"يمتد متحدثو الهاكا من جيانغشي إلى موريشيوس وسورينام؛ تحتفظ اللغة بالنهايات الثلاث لنبرة الدخول في الصينية الوسيطة، وكانت اللغة الأم لسون يات-سن ودنغ شياوبينغ ولي كوان يو.","he":"דוברי האקה מתפרסים מג'יאנגשי עד מאוריציוס וסורינאם; השפה שומרת על כל שלוש הסיומות של טון הכניסה של הסינית התיכונה, והייתה שפת האם של סון יאט-סן, דנג שיאופינג ולי קואן יו.","sw":"Wazungumzaji wa Kihakka wanaenea kutoka Jiangxi hadi Mauritius na Suriname; lugha inahifadhi miisho yote mitatu ya toni ya kuingia ya Kichina cha Kati, na ilikuwa lugha ya mama ya Sun Yat-sen, Deng Xiaoping na Lee Kuan Yew."},"body":{"en":"<h3>The Guest People</h3><p>The word <strong>客家</strong> (Hakka; literally \"guest families\") encodes a history of displacement. The classic account, set out by the historian Lo Hsiang-lin in 1933, tells of waves of Han Chinese refugees moving south out of the Central Plains over roughly a thousand years, driven by war, famine and dynastic collapse, and settling as latecomers in the hill country where Jiangxi, Fujian and Guangdong meet. With the best farmland already taken, they came to be called \"guests\" (客) by the established \"host\" (主) households. Modern scholars think the real story is more mixed: the Hakka language is closest to Gan, spoken next door in Jiangxi, and the ancestors of today's Hakka also include southern peoples such as the She, with whom the newcomers intermarried.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"focus\" data-code=\"hak_cn\">📍 Hakka (mainland) on map</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"focus\" data-code=\"hak_tw\">📍 Hakka (Taiwan) on map</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"panto\" data-lat=\"24.5\" data-lng=\"116.1\" data-zoom=\"6\">🗺 Pan to Meizhou</button></div><h3>The Diaspora: From Mauritius to Hawaii</h3><p>Hakka-speaking communities are distributed across southern China and several overseas migration regions. From the 17th century onward, Hakka men and women joined the great <em>Nanyang</em> (南洋) emigration to Southeast Asia, and from there dispersed further: to Mauritius in the Indian Ocean, to Suriname on the Atlantic coast of South America, to Jamaica, and to Hawaii. In Suriname, the first Chinese settlers came not from the Meizhou (梅州) heartland but from the Fuitungon region east of the Pearl River Delta, a name made from its three counties: Huiyang, Dongguan and Bao'an. Their descendants' Hakka keeps that 19th-century Fuitungon flavour, mixed with Dutch and Sranan Tongo loanwords.</p><h3>Linguistic Fingerprints</h3><p>Several features mark Hakka as distinct within Sinitic. The language preserves all three Middle Chinese (中古漢語) entering-tone (<em>入聲</em>) finals: /-p/, /-t/, and /-k/ — making it, alongside Cantonese and Min, a conservative keeper of Tang-dynasty stop codas that Mandarin lost, scattering the old entering-tone syllables unpredictably across its four modern tones. The word for \"I/me\" is <strong>𠊎</strong> /ŋai/, written with a character used for Hakka alone and enough on its own to identify a speaker. Most scholars take it to be the same ancient pronoun as Mandarin <em>wǒ</em> and Cantonese <em>ngóh</em> (我); Hakka even keeps a literary reading <em>ngo</em> for that character. What sets the everyday form apart is its <em>-ai</em>, where the other varieties have a back vowel. Taiwanese shop signs proudly announce 𠊎講客 <em>Ngài kóng Hak</em>, \"I speak Hakka\". The best-known church romanization, <strong>PFS</strong> (Pha̍k-fa-sṳ, 白話字), grew out of 19th-century Basel Mission work and is still used in Hakka Christian communities; Taiwan's schools and government use a separate official Hakka Pinyin.</p><h3>Taiwan's Great Hakka Movement</h3><p>Taiwan is home to roughly 4.6 million people who identify as Hakka, predominantly in Taoyuan, Hsinchu, and Miaoli, but far fewer who can actually speak the language, with estimates ranging from about 1.2 million to 2.4 million. After decades of suppression under Japanese colonialism and Kuomintang Mandarin-promotion policies, Hakka experienced a grassroots revival from the 1980s onward. Its symbolic start came on 28 December 1988, when thousands of Hakka marched through Taipei carrying portraits of Sun Yat-sen in the <strong>還我母語運動</strong> (\"Give Us Back Our Mother Tongue\" movement), demanding Hakka broadcasting and teaching. The campaign led in 2001 to the Council for Hakka Affairs (today's Hakka Affairs Council, 客家委員會), which describes itself as the world's only national-level government body for Hakka affairs. Hakka Television (客家電視台) now broadcasts 24 hours a day.</p><h3>Famous People of Hakka Descent</h3><ul><li><strong>孫中山</strong> (Sun Yat-sen) — founding father of the Republic of China (his Hakka ancestry is traditional but debated)</li><li><strong>鄧小平</strong> (Deng Xiaoping) — architect of China's Reform and Opening, from a Sichuan Hakka lineage</li><li><strong>李光耀</strong> (Lee Kuan Yew) — first Prime Minister of Singapore</li><li><strong>洪秀全</strong> (Hong Xiuquan) — leader of the Taiping Heavenly Kingdom</li></ul><p>Writers have often linked this prominence in revolutionary movements to the Hakka's position as a marginal \"guest\" community with strong internal solidarity and a high regard for education, though such explanations are hard to test. The thread is real enough in one case: Sun Yat-sen said that as a boy he listened to an old Taiping veteran's stories and declared he would be a second Hong Xiuquan.</p>"},"sources":["Lutz, Jessie G. & Rolland Ray Lutz (1998). Hakka Chinese Confront Protestant Christianity, 1850–1900. M.E. Sharpe.","Hashimoto, Mantaro J. (1973). The Hakka Dialect: A Linguistic Study of its Phonology, Syntax and Lexicon. Cambridge University Press.",{"text":"Hakka people — Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hakka_people"},"Szeto, Pui Yiu, Umberto Ansaldo & Stephen Matthews (2018). Typological variation across Mandarin dialects. Linguistic Typology, 22(2), 233–275.",{"text":"Hakka Affairs Council, Taiwan","url":"https://www.hakka.gov.tw/"},"Branner, David Prager (2000). Problems in Comparative Chinese Dialectology. Mouton de Gruyter."]},{"id":"sino-vietnamese-han-viet","icon":"🎵","tags":["sinosphere","vietnamese","middle-chinese","phonology","tones"],"title":{"en":"Hán-Việt: A Tang-Period Chinese Loan Layer in Vietnamese","ja":"漢越語：ベトナム語に残る唐代の中国語借用層","ko":"한월어: 베트남어에 남은 당대 중국어 차용층","zh":"汉越语：越南语中的唐代汉语借词层","yue":"漢越語：越南保住嘅唐代漢語時光囊","vi":"Hán-Việt: lớp từ vay mượn tiếng Hán thời Đường trong tiếng Việt","th":"ฮัน-เวียด: แคปซูลเวลาภาษาจีนราชวงศ์ถังของเวียดนาม","id":"Hán-Việt: Kapsul Waktu Bahasa Cina Dinasti Tang di Vietnam","hi":"हान-विएत: वियतनाम का तांग-राजवंश चीनी भाषा का टाइम कैप्सूल","de":"Hán-Việt: Vietnams Zeitkapsel des Tang-Dynastischen Chinesisch","fr":"Hán-Việt : la capsule temporelle du chinois de la Dynastie Tang au Viêt Nam","it":"Hán-Việt: la capsula del tempo del cinese Tang conservata in Vietnam","es":"Hán-Việt: la cápsula del tiempo del chino Tang conservada en Vietnam","pt":"Hán-Việt: A cápsula do tempo do chinês da dinastia Tang no Vietname","ru":"Хан-Вьет: Вьетнам как капсула времени танского китайского","uk":"Хан-В'єт: Вʼєтнам як капсула часу танського китайського","ar":"الهان-فيتية: فيتنام كبسولة زمنية للصينية في عصر أسرة تانغ","he":"האן-וייט: וייטנאם ככמוסת זמן של סינית מתקופת שושלת טאנג","sw":"Hán-Việt: Kapsuli ya Wakati ya Kichina cha Nasaba ya Tang nchini Vietnam"},"summary":{"en":"Somewhere between a third and 70% of Vietnamese vocabulary is Sino-Vietnamese (Hán-Việt), depending on how you count. This late-Tang reading layer keeps Middle Chinese final stops and tone categories that modern Mandarin has lost.","ja":"ベトナム語の語彙に占める漢越語（Hán-Việt）の割合は、数え方によって3分の1から70%までと幅がある。唐代後期に由来するこの字音の層は、現代北京語が失った中古漢語の入声韻尾や声調の区別を今に伝えている。","ko":"한월어(漢越語)는 세는 방법에 따라 베트남어 어휘의 3분의 1에서 70%까지를 차지한다. 당나라 말기의 이 독음층은 현대 표준중국어가 잃어버린 중고 한어의 입성 종성과 성조 범주를 지금도 간직하고 있다.","zh":"越南语词汇中，汉越语所占比例从三分之一到70%不等，全看怎么统计。这一晚唐读音层保留了中古汉语的入声韵尾和声调类别，而这些都是现代普通话已经丢掉的。","yue":"越南語詞彙入面，漢越語（Hán-Việt）佔三分之一到七成不等，視乎點計。呢個唐代後期嘅讀音層，保住咗現代普通話已經失落嘅中古漢語入聲韻尾同調類。","vi":"Tùy cách đếm, từ một phần ba đến 70% từ vựng tiếng Việt là từ Hán-Việt. Lớp âm đọc cuối đời Đường này giữ lại những âm tắc cuối và phạm trù thanh điệu của tiếng Hán Trung Cổ mà tiếng Quan Thoại hiện đại đã đánh mất.","th":"คำศัพท์ภาษาเวียดนามราวหนึ่งในสามถึง 70% เป็นคำฮัน-เวียด (Hán-Việt) แล้วแต่วิธีนับ ชั้นเสียงอ่านสมัยปลายราชวงศ์ถังนี้รักษาเสียงหยุดท้ายพยางค์และหมวดวรรณยุกต์ของภาษาจีนยุคกลางที่ภาษาจีนกลางสมัยใหม่สูญเสียไปแล้ว","id":"Antara sepertiga hingga 70% kosakata Vietnam adalah kata Sino-Vietnam (Hán-Việt), tergantung cara menghitungnya. Lapisan bacaan dari akhir masa Tang ini mempertahankan letupan akhir dan kategori nada bahasa Cina Pertengahan yang sudah hilang dari Mandarin modern.","hi":"गिनने के तरीक़े पर निर्भर करता है, पर वियतनामी शब्दावली का एक-तिहाई से लेकर 70% तक हिस्सा हान-विएत (Hán-Việt) है। उत्तर-तांग काल की यह पठन-परत मध्यकालीन चीनी के वे अंत्य स्पर्श और स्वराघात-श्रेणियाँ सँजोए हुए है जिन्हें आधुनिक मंदारिन खो चुकी है।","de":"Je nach Zählweise ist zwischen einem Drittel und 70 % des vietnamesischen Wortschatzes Sino-Vietnamesisch (Hán-Việt). Diese Leseschicht der späten Tang-Zeit bewahrt mittelchinesische Endverschlusslaute und Tonklassen, die das moderne Mandarin verloren hat.","fr":"Selon la façon de compter, entre un tiers et 70 % du vocabulaire vietnamien est sino-vietnamien (Hán-Việt). Cette couche de lectures de la fin des Tang conserve des occlusives finales et des catégories tonales du chinois médiéval que le mandarin moderne a perdues.","it":"A seconda di come si conta, tra un terzo e il 70% del vocabolario vietnamita è sino-vietnamita (Hán-Việt). Questo strato di letture di fine epoca Tang conserva le occlusive finali e le categorie tonali del cinese medio che il mandarino moderno ha perso.","es":"Entre un tercio y un 70 % del vocabulario vietnamita es sinovietnamita (Hán-Việt), según cómo se cuente. Esta capa de lecturas de finales de la época Tang conserva oclusivas finales y categorías tonales del chino medio que el mandarín moderno ha perdido.","pt":"Entre um terço e 70% do vocabulário vietnamita é sino-vietnamita (Hán-Việt), conforme a maneira de contar. Esta camada de leituras do final da era Tang guarda oclusivas finais e categorias tonais do chinês médio que o mandarim moderno perdeu.","ru":"Сино-вьетнамские слова (Hán-Việt) составляют, в зависимости от способа подсчёта, от трети до 70 % вьетнамской лексики. Этот позднетанский слой чтений хранит среднекитайские конечные смычные и тоновые категории, утраченные современным мандаринским.","uk":"Залежно від способу підрахунку, від третини до 70% вʼєтнамської лексики становлять хань-вʼєтські слова (Hán-Việt). Цей пізньотанський шар читань зберігає середньокитайські кінцеві зімкнені й тонові категорії, які сучасна мандаринська втратила.","ar":"ما بين ثلث المفردات الفيتنامية و70% منها كلمات هان-فيتية (Hán-Việt)، بحسب طريقة العدّ. وتحفظ طبقة القراءات هذه، المأخوذة من أواخر عهد تانغ، الوقفات الختامية والفئات النغمية للصينية الوسطى التي فقدتها الماندرين الحديثة.","he":"בין שליש ל-70% מאוצר המילים הווייטנאמי הוא האן-וייט (Hán-Việt), תלוי איך סופרים. רובד הקריאה הזה, מסוף תקופת טאנג, שומר על העיצורים הסותמים הסופיים ועל קטגוריות הטון של הסינית התיכונה — שהמנדרינית המודרנית איבדה.","sw":"Kati ya theluthi moja na 70% ya msamiati wa Kivietinamu ni Hán-Việt (Kichina-Kivietinamu), kutegemea jinsi unavyohesabu. Tabaka hili la usomaji la mwishoni mwa enzi ya Tang linahifadhi vizuizi vya mwisho na makundi ya toni ya Kichina cha Kati ambayo Kimandari cha kisasa kimevipoteza."},"body":{"en":"<h3>A Tang-Period Borrowing Layer</h3><p>When Tang dynasty officials governed Annam (northern Vietnam) between the 7th and 10th centuries CE, Vietnamese absorbed a large body of Chinese vocabulary. The historical value of this borrowing layer lies in the phonological distinctions it preserves. Modern linguists call this stratum <strong>Hán-Việt</strong> (漢越, Sino-Vietnamese).</p><p>Estimates of the Sinitic share of the Vietnamese lexicon range widely — <strong>from about a third to as much as 70%</strong>, lower in everyday speech and far higher in technical writing — much like the Latin and French layer that thickens towards the formal end of English. But English took in its French and Latin words piecemeal, while Vietnamese scholars adopted a complete reading system: working from Chinese rhyme dictionaries such as the <em>Qieyun</em>, they gave every character a regular Vietnamese pronunciation, and about 3,000 of these morphemes passed into the language. The result closely mirrors the Middle Chinese sound system. An older layer of some 400 words, borrowed individually after the Han conquest of 111 BC, sits underneath and now feels entirely native: <em>bạc</em> \"silver\" goes back to an early borrowing of the word written 白 \"white\", which the later reading system renders <em>bạch</em>.</p><div class='trivia-actions'><button type='button' class='trivia-action' data-action='setchar' data-char='食'>🔡 Show 食 (food/eat) readings</button><button type='button' class='trivia-action' data-action='focus' data-code='vi_ohan'>📍 Focus Old Hán-Nôm layer</button></div><h3>Preservation of Final Stops</h3><p>Middle Chinese (中古漢語, 7th–10th c.) had four categories of tone, and crucially, the <em>checked tones</em> (入聲, entering tones) ended in unreleased stop consonants: <strong>-p, -t, -k</strong>. Modern Mandarin lost all three — 國 <em>guó</em> once ended in -k, 學 <em>xué</em> in -k, 食 <em>shí</em> in -k.</p><p>Sino-Vietnamese readings preserve these finals, though the alphabet spells final <em>-k</em> as <em>-c</em> (or <em>-ch</em>):</p><ul><li>學 → <strong>học</strong> (final -k intact)</li><li>國 → <strong>quốc</strong> (final -k intact)</li><li>食 → <strong>thực</strong> (final -k intact)</li><li>十 → <strong>thập</strong> (final -p intact)</li><li>八 → <strong>bát</strong> (final -t intact)</li></ul><div class='trivia-actions'><button type='button' class='trivia-action' data-action='setchar' data-char='食'>🔡 Compare 食 readings</button><button type='button' class='trivia-action' data-action='setchar' data-char='山'>🔡 Compare 山 readings</button></div><h3>Eight Categories from Four</h3><p>Middle Chinese had four tones: 平 (level), 上 (rising), 去 (departing), and 入 (entering). Each one later split in two according to whether the initial consonant was voiced, a <em>tone split</em> that gave the <strong>eight tone categories</strong> (四聲八調) that Sino-Vietnamese readings still sort into. Vietnamese writes them with just <strong>six tones</strong>, because the two 入 categories fell together with the two 去 categories: 平 gives ngang and huyền, 上 gives hỏi and ngã (though many syllables that began with an old voiced stop, affricate or fricative went to nặng instead), 去 gives sắc and nặng, and the old 入 syllables, the ones ending in -p, -t, -c, -ch, take sắc and nặng as well. So the six marks of quốc ngữ map neatly onto the reconstructed Middle Chinese grid. Modern Mandarin collapsed that same grid into four tones, losing a vast amount of tonal information.</p><h3>Why Beijing Lost What Hanoi Kept</h3><p>The difference reflects geographic and political history. Mandarin (普通話) derives mainly from northern dialects that had already undergone centuries of internal restructuring — loss of final stops, merger of tones — long before the standard was codified. Vietnam won its independence in 938 but kept Literary Chinese for government and scholarship until the early 20th century, reading it all along in its own Tang-based way. Those readings were not frozen: they went through Vietnamese sound changes. But they changed independently of China, so the losses that reshaped northern Chinese never reached them.</p><p>Sino-Vietnamese readings are therefore one source of comparative evidence for reconstructing Middle Chinese. Alongside Cantonese, Hokkien and Sino-Korean readings, they preserve distinctions that Mandarin alone does not show.</p><blockquote>文化 <em>văn hóa</em> (vi) — 文化 <em>wénhuà</em> (zh): same characters, and here the two agree — Vietnamese <em>v-</em> and Mandarin <em>w-</em> are both regular reflexes of the Middle Chinese 微 initial (Baxter <em>mjun</em>), whose original *m- survives instead in Cantonese <em>man4</em> and Hokkien <em>bûn</em>.</blockquote><p>Common words such as <em>quốc</em>, <em>học</em> and <em>văn</em> therefore preserve a borrowing layer established many centuries before modern Standard Chinese.</p>"},"sources":["Pulleyblank, E. G. (1984). Middle Chinese: A Study in Historical Phonology. UBC Press.","Maspero, H. (1912). Études sur la phonétique historique de la langue annamite. Bulletin de l'École française d'Extrême-Orient.","Schuessler, A. (2007). ABC Etymological Dictionary of Old Chinese. University of Hawaii Press.",{"text":"Wikipedia: Sino-Vietnamese vocabulary","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sino-Vietnamese_vocabulary"},"Norman, J. (1988). Chinese. Cambridge University Press.","Baxter, W. H. & Sagart, L. (2014). Old Chinese: A New Reconstruction. Oxford University Press.",{"text":"Wikipedia: Four tones (Middle Chinese) — distribution table (Sino-Vietnamese row, after Nguyễn Tài Cẩn 2000)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Four_tones_(Middle_Chinese)"}]},{"id":"sino-korean-versus-sino-japanese","icon":"🔀","tags":["sinosphere","middle-chinese","phonology","japanese","korean","vietnamese","comparative"],"title":{"en":"Comparing Han-Character Readings in Four Languages","ja":"一つの漢字が語る四つの音——日本語・朝鮮語・ベトナム語・中国語","ko":"하나의 한자, 네 가지 독음 — 일본어·한국어·베트남어·중국어","zh":"一个汉字，四种读音——日语・韩语・越语・汉语","yue":"一個漢字，四種讀音——日語、韓語、越語、漢語","vi":"Một chữ Hán, bốn cách đọc — tiếng Nhật, Hàn, Việt, Trung","th":"หนึ่งอักษรฮั่น สี่วิธีอ่าน — ญี่ปุ่น เกาหลี เวียดนาม จีน","id":"Satu Karakter Han, Empat Bacaan — Jepang, Korea, Vietnam, Mandarin","hi":"एक हान अक्षर, चार पाठ — जापानी, कोरियाई, वियतनामी, मंदारिन","de":"Ein Schriftzeichen, vier Lesungen — Japanisch, Koreanisch, Vietnamesisch, Mandarin","fr":"Un sinogramme, quatre lectures — japonais, coréen, vietnamien, mandarin","it":"Un carattere Han, quattro letture — giapponese, coreano, vietnamita, mandarino","es":"Un carácter Han, cuatro lecturas — japonés, coreano, vietnamita, mandarín","pt":"Um Carácter Han, Quatro Leituras — Japonês, Coreano, Vietnamita, Mandarim","ru":"Один иероглиф, четыре прочтения — японский, корейский, вьетнамский, мандаринский","uk":"Один ієрогліф, чотири прочитання — японська, корейська, в'єтнамська, мандаринська","ar":"رمز واحد، أربع قراءات — اليابانية والكورية والفيتنامية والماندرين","he":"סימן כתב אחד, ארבע קריאות — יפנית, קורנית, וייטנאמית, מנדרינית","sw":"Herufi Moja ya Han, Usomaji Minne — Kijapani, Kikorea, Kivietinamu, Kimandari"},"summary":{"en":"The same Han character carries cognate but divergent pronunciations across Mandarin, Korean, Japanese, and Vietnamese — a layered record of Middle Chinese dialects borrowed at different historical moments.","ja":"同一の漢字が中国語・朝鮮語・日本語・ベトナム語で同源でありながら異なる読みを持つのは、中古漢語の異なる方言が異なる時代に各地に伝わった証拠である。","ko":"동일한 한자가 중국어·한국어·일본어·베트남어에서 같은 기원을 가지면서도 서로 다른 독음을 갖는 것은 중고 한어의 다양한 방언이 각기 다른 시대에 각 지역에 전파된 역사적 기록이다.","zh":"同一汉字在普通话、韩语、日语、越南语中拥有同源却各异的读音，是中古汉语不同方言在不同历史时期传入各地的分层记录。","yue":"同一個漢字喺普通話、韓語、日語、越南語入面有同源但各異嘅讀音，係中古漢語唔同方言喺唔同歷史時期傳入各地嘅分層記錄。","vi":"Cùng một chữ Hán mang những cách đọc đồng nguyên nhưng phân kỳ trong tiếng Quan Thoại, Hàn, Nhật và Việt — đây là bản ghi phân lớp của các phương ngữ tiếng Hán Trung Cổ được mượn vào các thời điểm lịch sử khác nhau.","th":"อักษรฮั่นตัวเดียวกันมีการออกเสียงที่มีต้นกำเนิดร่วมกันแต่แตกต่างกันในภาษาจีนกลาง เกาหลี ญี่ปุ่น และเวียดนาม ซึ่งเป็นบันทึกเป็นชั้นๆ ของภาษาถิ่นจีนกลางที่ถูกยืมมาในช่วงเวลาต่างกันทางประวัติศาสตร์","id":"Karakter Han yang sama membawa pengucapan yang berkerabat namun berbeda dalam bahasa Mandarin, Korea, Jepang, dan Vietnam — catatan berlapis dari dialek Cina Tengah yang dipinjam pada momen sejarah yang berbeda.","hi":"वही हान अक्षर मंदारिन, कोरियाई, जापानी और वियतनामी में समान उत्पत्ति लेकिन अलग-अलग उच्चारण रखता है — यह विभिन्न ऐतिहासिक क्षणों में उधार ली गई मध्य चीनी बोलियों का एक स्तरित रिकॉर्ड है।","de":"Dasselbe Han-Zeichen trägt verwandte, aber divergente Aussprachen im Mandarin, Koreanischen, Japanischen und Vietnamesischen — ein geschichtetes Protokoll der mittelchinesischen Dialekte, die zu verschiedenen historischen Zeitpunkten entlehnt wurden.","fr":"Le même sinogramme porte des prononciations apparentées mais divergentes en mandarin, coréen, japonais et vietnamien — un enregistrement en couches des dialectes du chinois médiéval empruntés à différents moments historiques.","it":"Lo stesso carattere Han porta pronunce affini ma divergenti in mandarino, coreano, giapponese e vietnamita — una registrazione a strati dei dialetti del cinese medievale presi in prestito in momenti storici diversi.","es":"El mismo carácter Han lleva pronunciaciones afines pero divergentes en mandarín, coreano, japonés y vietnamita — un registro estratificado de los dialectos del chino medieval tomados prestados en diferentes momentos históricos.","pt":"O mesmo carácter Han transporta pronúncias cognatas mas divergentes em mandarim, coreano, japonês e vietnamita — um registo estratificado dos dialectos do chinês medieval emprestados em diferentes momentos históricos.","ru":"Один и тот же иероглиф несёт родственные, но разошедшиеся произношения в мандаринском, корейском, японском и вьетнамском — это многопластовая запись среднекитайских диалектов, заимствованных в разные исторические моменты.","uk":"Той самий ієрогліф несе споріднені, але розійшлися вимови у мандаринській, корейській, японській та в'єтнамській — це багатошарова запись середньокитайських діалектів, запозичених у різні історичні моменти.","ar":"يحمل الرمز الهانزي الواحد نطقاً ذا أصول مشتركة لكنه متشعّب في الماندرين والكورية واليابانية والفيتنامية — وهو سجل طبقي للهجات الصينية الوسيطة المُستعارة في لحظات تاريخية مختلفة.","he":"אותו תו האן נושא הגייות קרובות אך מפוצלות בסינית המנדרינית, הקורנית, היפנית והוייטנאמית — רשומה שכבתית של ניבים סיניים ביניימיים שהושאלו בנקודות זמן היסטוריות שונות.","sw":"Herufi ile ile ya Han inabeba matamshi yanayohusiana lakini tofauti katika Kimandari, Kikorea, Kijapani, na Kivietinamu — rekodi ya tabaka za lahaja za Kichina cha Kati zilizokopwa katika nyakati tofauti za kihistoria."},"body":{"en":"<h3>The Sinosphere's Rosetta Stone</h3><p>Every country that adopted Chinese characters also adopted Chinese pronunciation, but they borrowed from different eras and different dialects. The result is a linguistic Rosetta Stone hidden inside four modern writing systems. The borrowed reading-layers of Japanese, Korean and Vietnamese are known collectively as <strong>Sino-Xenic</strong> (a term coined by Samuel E. Martin in 1953); set beside Mandarin, the modern standard that grew out of the northern Chinese dialects, they give four cognate reflexes of a single Chinese source that diverged after borrowing.</p><h3>A Character-by-Character Comparison</h3><div class='trivia-actions'><button type='button' class='trivia-action' data-action='setchar' data-char='山'>🔡 Compare 山 (mountain)</button><button type='button' class='trivia-action' data-action='setchar' data-char='水'>🔡 Compare 水 (water)</button></div><p>Take <strong>山</strong> (mountain). In Middle Chinese it was reconstructed as *ʂan. Today:</p><ul><li>Mandarin: <strong>shān</strong> (tone preserved, final nasal preserved)</li><li>Korean (漢字音): <strong>san</strong> (conservative nasal, no tone)</li><li>Japanese (漢音 kan-on): <strong>san</strong> (same layer as Korean)</li><li>Vietnamese: <strong>sơn</strong> (vowel shifted, final nasal preserved)</li></ul><p>Now <strong>國</strong> (nation), Middle Chinese *kwok:</p><ul><li>Mandarin: <strong>guó</strong> — final -k lost, tone shifted</li><li>Korean: <strong>guk</strong> — final -k preserved</li><li>Japanese (漢音): <strong>koku</strong> — -k reflected as -ku (vowel insertion)</li><li>Vietnamese: <strong>quốc</strong> — final -k fully preserved</li></ul><div class='trivia-actions'><button type='button' class='trivia-action' data-action='setchar' data-char='食'>🔡 Compare 食 (eat/food)</button></div><h3>The Final-Stop Hierarchy</h3><p>This pattern repeats across every character with a Middle Chinese checked-tone (入聲) ending. The hierarchy of conservation is remarkably consistent:</p><ol><li><strong>Cantonese/Hokkien</strong>: preserve -p, -t, -k intact as unreleased stops (most completely in Cantonese; Hokkien also has a colloquial glottal-stop layer)</li><li><strong>Vietnamese</strong>: preserves all three (-p, -t, -c/-ch)</li><li><strong>Korean</strong>: keeps -p and -k, but Middle Chinese -t appears as -l (一 <em>il</em>, 發 <em>bal</em>)</li><li><strong>Japanese</strong>: adds a vowel: -t becomes -tsu or -chi, -k becomes -ku or -ki, and the old -pu has mostly worn down to a long vowel (十 <em>jū</em>, 法 <em>hō</em>)</li><li><strong>Mandarin</strong>: erases all three, leaving only tone traces</li></ol><p>For 學 (study; Middle Chinese <em>haewk</em> in Baxter's notation): Korean <em>hak</em>, Japanese <em>gaku</em>, Vietnamese <em>học</em>, Mandarin <em>xué</em>. The -k is alive in three out of four.</p><h3>Why Japanese Has Multiple Readings</h3><p>Japan borrowed Chinese pronunciation in at least three distinct waves: <strong>呉音 Go-on</strong> (Wu-based, 5th–6th c.), <strong>漢音 Kan-on</strong> (Tang-court standard, 8th–9th c.), and <strong>唐音 Tō-on</strong> (Song–Ming era). For 行:</p><ul><li>Go-on: <em>gyō</em></li><li>Kan-on: <em>kō</em></li><li>Tō-on: <em>an</em> (from southern dialects)</li></ul><p>Korean by contrast received a comparatively unified transmission: the main layer of Sino-Korean (漢字音) is generally traced to the Chang'an standard of Tang China, carried home by the Silla students and envoys at the Tang capital in the 8th and 9th centuries. Resting on a single dominant source is what makes Sino-Korean so much more internally consistent than the Japanese layers — drawn, as it happens, from much the same Tang standard as <em>kan-on</em>.</p><div class='trivia-actions'><button type='button' class='trivia-action' data-action='focus' data-code='ko'>📍 Focus Korean on map</button><button type='button' class='trivia-action' data-action='focus' data-code='ja'>📍 Focus Japanese on map</button></div><h3>The Tonal Preservation Ladder</h3><p>Beyond consonants, tonal preservation follows a clear gradient. Middle Chinese had 4 tone categories that split into 8 in some languages:</p><ul><li>Cantonese: <strong>6–9 tones</strong> (best preservation)</li><li>Vietnamese: <strong>6 tones</strong>, or 8 if syllables ending in -p, -t, -c count separately, which lines up neatly with the 8 Middle Chinese categories (excellent)</li><li>Korean Sino-Korean: <strong>historically 3 pitch accents</strong>, now mostly levelled in Seoul dialect (good historical record)</li><li>Japanese on-readings: <strong>no tones</strong>. Japanese readers once marked the Chinese tones with small dots (声点, <em>shōten</em>) at the corners of characters; when the dots were later put on kana, a doubled dot marking voicing became the ancestor of today's dakuten ゛</li><li>Mandarin: <strong>4 tones</strong>, with massive mergers — the weakest preservation</li></ul><p>The ladder is what makes the comparison useful: Cantonese and Vietnamese together preserve most of what Middle Chinese distinguished, and reading a character across all four systems recovers more of the original than any one of them holds alone.</p><p>The Sinosphere's borrowed layers aren't just curiosities — they are the phonological time machine that lets scholars reconstruct a language no living person has spoken for over a thousand years.</p>"},"sources":["Pulleyblank, E. G. (1984). Middle Chinese: A Study in Historical Phonology. UBC Press.","Karlgren, B. (1954). Compendium of Phonetics in Ancient and Archaic Chinese. Museum of Far Eastern Antiquities.","Martin, S. E. (1966). Lexical Evidence Relating Korean to Japanese. Language 42(2): 185–251.",{"text":"Wikipedia: Sino-Korean vocabulary","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sino-Korean_vocabulary"},{"text":"Wikipedia: Sino-Japanese vocabulary","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sino-Japanese_vocabulary"},"Baxter, W. H. & Sagart, L. (2014). Old Chinese: A New Reconstruction. Oxford University Press.",{"text":"声点 (shōten) — Japanese Wikipedia","url":"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E5%A3%B0%E7%82%B9"}]},{"id":"simplified-chinese-1956","icon":"✂️","tags":["sinosphere","simplified","history","politics"],"title":{"en":"Chinese Character Simplification and Script Reform","ja":"大簡化——中国の文字改革はいかに行われたか","ko":"대간화: 중국의 문자 개혁은 어떻게 이루어졌는가","zh":"大简化——中国的文字改革如何推行","yue":"大簡化——中國點樣推行文字改革","vi":"Đại giản hóa: Trung Quốc cải cách chữ viết như thế nào","th":"การปฏิรูปอักษรจีน: การลดความซับซ้อนครั้งใหญ่","id":"Reformasi Aksara Tiongkok: Penyederhanaan Besar-Besaran","hi":"महासरलीकरण: चीन ने अपनी लिपि का सुधार कैसे किया","de":"Die große Vereinfachung: Chinas Schriftreform","fr":"La grande simplification : la réforme de l'écriture chinoise","it":"La grande semplificazione: la riforma della scrittura cinese","es":"La gran simplificación: la reforma de la escritura china","pt":"A grande simplificação: a reforma da escrita chinesa","ru":"Великое упрощение: реформа китайской письменности","uk":"Велике спрощення: реформа китайського письма","ar":"التبسيط الكبير: كيف أصلحت الصين نظامها الكتابي","he":"הפישוט הגדול: כיצד סין רפורמה את כתבה","sw":"Urahisishaji Mkubwa: Jinsi China Ilivyorekebisha Maandishi Yake"},"summary":{"en":"The 1956 Chinese script reform introduced simplified forms for many traditional characters as part of a literacy policy. Simplified and traditional character standards remain in use in different Chinese-speaking regions.","ja":"1956年の中国文字改革は、識字率向上を目的として繁体字を簡体字に置き換え、漢字文化圏を二つの書記体系に永久に分断した。","ko":"1956년 중국 문자 개혁은 문맹 퇴치를 위해 전통 한자를 간체자로 대체하여 한자 문화권을 두 가지 표기 체계로 영구적으로 분열시켰다.","zh":"1956年的中国文字改革以简化字取代繁体字，旨在提高识字率，却永久地将汉字文化圈分裂为两套书写体系。","yue":"1956年嘅中國文字改革用簡體字取代繁體字，目的係提高識字率，但永久咁將漢字文化圈分裂成兩套書寫系統。","vi":"Cải cách chữ viết Trung Quốc năm 1956 thay thế chữ truyền thống bằng chữ giản thể để nâng cao trình độ đọc viết — quyết định này chia rẽ vĩnh viễn vùng văn hóa Hán thành hai hệ thống chữ viết.","th":"การปฏิรูปอักษรจีนปี 1956 แทนที่อักษรจีนดั้งเดิมด้วยอักษรจีนตัวย่อเพื่อเพิ่มอัตราการอ่านออกเขียนได้ ส่งผลให้วัฒนธรรมอักษรจีนแตกออกเป็นสองระบบการเขียนอย่างถาวร","id":"Reformasi aksara Tiongkok tahun 1956 menggantikan karakter tradisional dengan karakter sederhana untuk meningkatkan melek huruf — keputusan yang secara permanen membelah dunia Han menjadi dua sistem tulisan.","hi":"1956 की चीनी लिपि सुधार ने साक्षरता बढ़ाने के लिए पारंपरिक चरित्रों को सरलीकृत रूपों से प्रतिस्थापित किया — एक निर्णय जिसने सिनोस्फीयर को दो लेखन प्रणालियों में स्थायी रूप से विभाजित कर दिया।","de":"Die chinesische Schriftreform von 1956 ersetzte traditionelle Schriftzeichen durch vereinfachte Formen zur Förderung der Alphabetisierung — eine Entscheidung, die die sinosphärische Welt dauerhaft in zwei Schriftsysteme spaltete.","fr":"La réforme de l'écriture chinoise de 1956 remplaça les caractères traditionnels par des formes simplifiées pour améliorer l'alphabétisation — une décision qui scinda définitivement la sinosphère en deux systèmes d'écriture.","it":"La riforma della scrittura cinese del 1956 sostituì i caratteri tradizionali con forme semplificate per aumentare l'alfabetizzazione, spaccando definitivamente la sinosfera in due sistemi di scrittura.","es":"La reforma de la escritura china de 1956 sustituyó los caracteres tradicionales por formas simplificadas para mejorar la alfabetización, dividiendo permanentemente la sinosfera en dos sistemas de escritura.","pt":"A reforma da escrita chinesa de 1956 substituiu caracteres tradicionais por formas simplificadas para melhorar a alfabetização — uma decisão que dividiu permanentemente a sinosfera em dois sistemas de escrita.","ru":"Реформа китайской письменности 1956 года заменила традиционные иероглифы упрощёнными формами для повышения грамотности, навсегда разделив синосферу на две письменные системы.","uk":"Реформа китайського письма 1956 року замінила традиційні ієрогліфи спрощеними формами задля підвищення грамотності, назавжди розколовши синосферу на дві писемні системи.","ar":"أبدلت إصلاح الكتابة الصينية عام 1956 الحروف التقليدية بأشكال مبسّطة لرفع نسبة محو الأمية، وقد قسّم هذا القرار عالم الكتابة الصينية إلى نظامين مختلفين بصفة دائمة.","he":"רפורמת הכתב הסינית של 1956 החליפה תווים מסורתיים בצורות מפושטות כדי לשפר את האוריינות — החלטה שחילקה לצמיתות את עולם הכתב הסיני לשני מערכות.","sw":"Marekebisho ya maandishi ya China ya 1956 yalibadilisha herufi za jadi kwa maumbo yaliyorahisishwa ili kuongeza ujuzi wa kusoma na kuandika — uamuzi ambao uligawanya ulimwengu wa Han katika mifumo miwili ya maandishi milele."},"body":{"en":"<h3>Politics Behind the Brush</h3><p>In 1956, the People's Republic of China published the first <em>Hànzì Jiǎnhuà Fāng'àn</em> (漢字簡化方案), reducing stroke counts in 515 characters and standardising a further 54 simplified components (偏旁). The backdrop was stark. When the People's Republic was founded in 1949, about 80% of the population could not read, and in the countryside the figure was over 95%. Simpler characters were meant to be one faster path to the written word.</p><p>The simplifications were not invented from scratch. Many forms drew on centuries-old <em>cǎoshū</em> (cursive) shortcuts and popular handwritten variants that scribes and merchants had used for generations. Nor was the idea new to the Communists: in 1935 the Nationalist government's Ministry of Education issued a list of 324 simplified characters compiled by the scholar Qian Xuantong, then withdrew it a year later under opposition within its own party. What changed after 1956 was state enforcement: schools, newspapers and government documents switched to the new forms.</p><h3>The 1977 Overreach</h3><p>A second wave in December 1977 proposed 853 more simplifications, some of them drastic: 蛋 \"egg\" was to be written 旦, and 停 \"stop\" as 仃. Unlike the first round, most of whose forms were already in popular use, many were unfamiliar, and by mid-1978 publishers were told to stop using them. The State Council formally rescinded the scheme on 24 June 1986. The episode stands as a cautionary tale: simplification has phonetic and semantic limits.</p><h3>The Great Split</h3><p>Taiwan, Hong Kong and Macau never adopted simplified characters. Singapore, on the other hand, ran its own simplification rounds from 1969 and ended up with the mainland set, and Malaysia followed in 1981. Taiwan's ROC government framed <strong>繁體字</strong> (traditional characters) as cultural continuity and legitimate Chinese heritage — a deliberate contrast to the mainland's reforms. In speech, though, the two sides agree. Taiwan's official standard is <strong>國語</strong> (Mandarin, <em>Guóyǔ</em>), not Hokkien or Cantonese: it is the Beijing-based national standard the Republic of China had codified by the 1930s and brought to the island in 1945. The mainland later adopted that same standard under the name <em>Pǔtōnghuà</em>, so Taiwan shares the spoken norm while rejecting the mainland's written reform. Hong Kong still writes formal Chinese in traditional characters, as it always has; its \"biliterate and trilingual\" policy means written Chinese and English, and spoken Cantonese, Putonghua and English.</p><div class='trivia-actions'><button type='button' class='trivia-action' data-action='panto' data-lat='39.91' data-lng='116.39' data-zoom='4'>Beijing — epicentre of the reform</button></div>"},"sources":["DeFrancis, J. (1984). <em>The Chinese Language: Fact and Fantasy</em>. University of Hawaii Press.","Norman, J. (1988). <em>Chinese</em>. Cambridge University Press.",{"text":"State Council of PRC — 1986 Retraction Notice (Wikipedia)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second_Chinese_Character_Simplification_Scheme"},{"text":"Taiwan's Guoyu policy (Wikipedia)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taiwanese_Mandarin"},{"text":"Wikipedia: Simplified Chinese characters","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Simplified_Chinese_characters"},{"text":"Wikipedia: Literacy in China","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Literacy_in_China"},{"text":"Wikipedia: Languages of Hong Kong","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Languages_of_Hong_Kong"}]},{"id":"japanese-shinjitai-postwar","icon":"📜","tags":["sinosphere","kanji","japan","history","postwar"],"title":{"en":"Japan's Parallel Simplification: Shinjitai and Postwar Kanji Reform","ja":"日本の並行簡化——新字体と戦後漢字改革","ko":"일본의 병행 간화: 신자체와 전후 한자 개혁","zh":"日本的平行简化——新字体与战后汉字改革","yue":"日本嘅平行簡化——新字體同戰後漢字改革","vi":"Việt đơn giản hóa song song của Nhật Bản: Shinjitai và cải cách Kanji thời hậu chiến","th":"การลดความซับซ้อนแบบขนานของญี่ปุ่น: ชินจิไทและการปฏิรูปคันจิหลังสงคราม","id":"Penyederhanaan Paralel Jepang: Shinjitai dan Reformasi Kanji Pascaperang","hi":"जापान का समानांतर सरलीकरण: शिंजिताई और युद्धोत्तर काँजी सुधार","de":"Japans parallele Vereinfachung: Shinjitai und die Schriftreform der Nachkriegszeit","fr":"La simplification parallèle du Japon : shinjitai et réforme des kanji d'après-guerre","it":"La semplificazione parallela del Giappone: shinjitai e la riforma dei kanji del dopoguerra","es":"La simplificación paralela de Japón: shinjitai y la reforma de los kanji de posguerra","pt":"A simplificação paralela do Japão: shinjitai e a reforma dos kanji do pós-guerra","ru":"Параллельное упрощение Японии: синдзитай и послевоенная реформа кандзи","uk":"Паралельне спрощення Японії: сіндзітай та повоєнна реформа кандзі","ar":"التبسيط الموازي لليابان: شينجيتاي وإصلاح الكانجي في مرحلة ما بعد الحرب","he":"הפישוט המקביל של יפן: שינג'יטאי ורפורמת הקאנג'י שלאחר המלחמה","sw":"Urahisishaji wa Sambamba wa Japani: Shinjitai na Mageuzi ya Kanji Baada ya Vita"},"summary":{"en":"Japan's postwar kanji reform produced shinjitai. Some forms coincide with simplified Chinese characters, while others follow different simplification choices.","ja":"戦後占領期の漢字改革は新字体を生み出したが、その簡化はPRCの同時期の簡体字と部分的に重なりながらも興味深い相違点を持つ。","ko":"전후 점령기의 일본 한자 개혁은 신자체를 낳았는데, 이는 중국의 동시대 간체자와 부분적으로 겹치면서도 흥미롭게 다른 점도 있다.","zh":"战后占领时期的日本汉字改革产生了新字体，其简化与中国同期的简体字既有交集，又有引人深思的差异。","yue":"戰後佔領時期嘅日本漢字改革產生咗新字體，其簡化同中國同期嘅簡體字既有交集，又有令人深思嘅差異。","vi":"Cải cách kanji thời hậu chiến của Nhật Bản tạo ra shinjitai — các hình thức giản hóa vừa trùng lặp vừa khác biệt thú vị so với quá trình giản hóa đồng thời của Trung Quốc.","th":"การปฏิรูปคันจิในยุคหลังสงครามของญี่ปุ่นได้สร้างชินจิไท ซึ่งเป็นรูปแบบที่ลดความซับซ้อนแล้ว ทั้งที่ทับซ้อนและแตกต่างอย่างน่าสนใจจากการลดความซับซ้อนพร้อมกันของจีน","id":"Reformasi kanji pascaperang Jepang menghasilkan shinjitai — bentuk-bentuk yang disederhanakan yang sebagian tumpang tindih tetapi juga menarik berbeda dari penyederhanaan Tiongkok untuk karakter yang sama.","hi":"जापान के युद्धोत्तर काँजी सुधार ने शिंजिताई को जन्म दिया — सरलीकृत रूप जो चीन के समसामयिक सरलीकरण से आंशिक रूप से मेल खाते हैं किन्तु रोचक भिन्नताएं भी रखते हैं।","de":"Japans Nachkriegs-Kanji-Reform schuf die Shinjitai — vereinfachte Formen, die sich mit Chinas gleichzeitiger Vereinfachung überschneiden, aber auch faszinierend unterscheiden.","fr":"La réforme des kanji d'après-guerre au Japon a donné naissance aux shinjitai — des formes simplifiées qui se recoupent partiellement avec la simplification simultanée de la Chine tout en s'en écartant de façon fascinante.","it":"La riforma dei kanji del dopoguerra in Giappone ha prodotto gli shinjitai — forme semplificate che si sovrappongono ma divergono in modo affascinante dalla semplificazione cinese contemporanea degli stessi caratteri.","es":"La reforma de los kanji en el Japón de posguerra produjo los shinjitai — formas simplificadas que se solapan pero divergen fascinantemente de la simplificación china contemporánea de los mismos caracteres.","pt":"A reforma dos kanji no Japão do pós-guerra produziu os shinjitai — formas simplificadas que se sobrepõem mas divergem fascinantemente da simplificação chinesa contemporânea dos mesmos caracteres.","ru":"Японская послевоенная реформа кандзи породила синдзитай — упрощённые формы, которые частично совпадают, но интригующе расходятся с одновременным упрощением тех же знаков в Китае.","uk":"Японська повоєнна реформа кандзі породила сіндзітай — спрощені форми, що частково збігаються, але цікаво розходяться з одночасним спрощенням тих самих знаків у Китаї.","ar":"أفرز إصلاح الكانجي الياباني في مرحلة ما بعد الحرب «شينجيتاي» — أشكالاً مبسّطةً تتقاطع مع التبسيط الصيني المتزامن للحروف ذاتها لكنها تختلف عنه اختلافاً لافتاً.","he":"רפורמת הקאנג'י של יפן לאחר המלחמה יצרה את השינג'יטאי — צורות מפושטות החופפות חלקית אך שונות בצורה מרתקת מהפישוט הסיני המקביל של אותם תווים.","sw":"Mageuzi ya kanji ya Japani baada ya vita yalizalisha shinjitai — maumbo yaliyorahisishwa ambayo yanafanana lakini pia yanatofautiana kwa njia ya kuvutia na urahisishaji wa wakati huo huo wa Kichina."},"body":{"en":"<h3>Occupation-Era Urgency</h3><p>While China's simplification is better known, Japan got there first, and independently: its official simplified forms of 1949 came seven years before China's 1956 scheme. The 1946 <em>Tōyō Kanji</em> (当用漢字) list, drawn up by Japan's own National Language Council from proposals going back to the 1920s and issued under the US occupation, designated 1,850 characters for everyday use and already printed 131 of them in simplified form. The 1949 character-form table (当用漢字字体表) extended these <strong>shinjitai</strong> (新字体, 'new character forms') to about 500 characters. The pressure in the background was even more radical: the 1946 US Education Mission recommended rōmaji. But a nationwide literacy survey in 1948 found that almost all adults could read and write at least some kanji, and the push for rōmaji lost momentum.</p><h3>Three Rounds of Standardisation</h3><p>The system evolved in three stages:</p><ul><li><strong>1946</strong>: Tōyō Kanji — a restrictive list for official documents and the press: if a word's kanji was not on it, writers were told to choose another word or write it in kana.</li><li><strong>1981</strong>: 常用漢字 (<em>Jōyō Kanji</em>) — 1,945 characters, superseded Tōyō, became the reference standard for schools and media.</li><li><strong>2010</strong>: 改定常用漢字 — 196 characters added and 5 dropped, for 2,136. The additions included everyday characters such as 俺 and 誰, and for the first time every character in the names of Japan's prefectures (岡, 阪, 熊, 鹿, 梨 and others).</li></ul><h3>Same Goal, Different Scissors</h3><p>The intriguing story is in the mismatches. Japan and China often simplified the same character differently:</p><ul><li><strong>廣 → 広</strong> (Japan) vs <strong>广</strong> (China) — Japan kept more structure.</li><li><strong>鐵 → 鉄</strong> (Japan) vs <strong>铁</strong> (China) — both replaced the complicated right-hand side with 失; the only difference is that China also shortened the metal radical 金 to 钅.</li><li><strong>體 → 体</strong>: here both converged — same simplified form.</li></ul><p>Some forms Japan simplified, China left alone, and vice versa — producing three-way divergences between Traditional, Japanese, and Simplified Chinese.</p><h3>Kyūjitai and the Cultural Argument</h3><p>Conservative scholars argued that <em>kyūjitai</em> (旧字体, 'old forms') encode morphological meaning — that 鐵 carries its history in its shape, while 鉄 is a mere shorthand. It is also an unlucky one, since it can be read as 金を失う, \"lose money\": some Japanese steelmakers and railways still avoid it in their names and logos, writing 鐵 or the variant 鉃 instead. The debate mirrors the Traditional/Simplified split in Chinese, but with an added layer: Japanese had already absorbed kanji into a mixed script (kanji + kana) in which kana carry the grammatical endings, so arguably less rides on the exact shape of each character than in all-character Chinese text.</p><div class='trivia-actions'><button type='button' class='trivia-action' data-action='focus' data-code='ja_kun'>Show Japanese map layer</button><button type='button' class='trivia-action' data-action='panto' data-lat='35.68' data-lng='139.69' data-zoom='5'>Pan to Tokyo</button></div>"},"sources":["Seeley, C. (1991). <em>A History of Writing in Japan</em>. E.J. Brill.","Unger, J.M. (1996). <em>Literacy and Script Reform in Occupation Japan</em>. Oxford University Press.",{"text":"Jōyō kanji — Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/J%C5%8Dy%C5%8D_kanji"},{"text":"Shinjitai — Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shinjitai"},{"text":"当用漢字 — Japanese Wikipedia","url":"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E5%BD%93%E7%94%A8%E6%BC%A2%E5%AD%97"}]},{"id":"ducai-yuan-zhongyuan-yinyun","icon":"🎭","tags":["mandarin","history","yuan","rhyme-book"],"title":{"en":"Zhōngyuán Yīnyùn: Yuan-Era Opera and Early Mandarin Phonology","ja":"中原音韻 — 元代の戯曲の中で生まれた近代マンダリン","ko":"중원음운 — 원나라 잡극에서 태어난 근대 만다린","zh":"《中原音韵》——现代官话诞生于元代戏曲","yue":"《中原音韻》——現代官話原來係喺元代雜劇度誕生嘅","vi":"Trung Nguyên Âm Vận — quan thoại hiện đại sinh ra trong kịch thời Nguyên","th":"จงหยวนอินยุ่น — ภาษาจีนกลางถือกำเนิดในละครยุคหยวน","id":"Zhōngyuán Yīnyùn — Mandarin lahir dari drama era Yuan","hi":"झोंगयुआन यिनयुन — आधुनिक मंदारिन का जन्म युआन काल के नाटक में","de":"Zhōngyuán Yīnyùn — modernes Mandarin entstand im Yuan-Drama","fr":"Zhōngyuán Yīnyùn — le mandarin moderne est né dans l'opéra Yuan","it":"Zhōngyuán Yīnyùn — il mandarino moderno nacque nel teatro Yuan","es":"Zhōngyuán Yīnyùn — el mandarín moderno nació en el teatro Yuan","pt":"Zhōngyuán Yīnyùn — o mandarim moderno nasceu no teatro Yuan","ru":"Чжунъюань иньюнь — современный мандарин родился в драме эпохи Юань","uk":"Чжунюань іньюнь — сучасний мандарин народився у юаньській драмі","ar":"تشونغ يوان ينيون — مولد لغة الماندرين الحديثة في مسرح يوان","he":"ג'ונגיואן יניון — המנדרינית המודרנית נולדה במחזות שושלת יואן","sw":"Zhōngyuán Yīnyùn — Kichina-Mandarin cha kisasa kilizaliwa katika tamthilia ya Yuan"},"summary":{"en":"Zhou Deqing's 1324 rhyme book for Yuan opera arias is the first full record of a northern Chinese sound system with Mandarin's four tones, with the old entering tone (入聲) folded into the other three, at least in song.","ja":"周徳清が1324年に元曲（雜劇）の歌のために編んだ韻書。マンダリンの四声を持つ北方中国語の音体系を初めて完全に記録したもので、古い入声は、少なくとも歌の上ではほかの三声に組み込まれている。","ko":"주덕청이 1324년 원나라 잡극의 노랫말을 위해 펴낸 운서. 만다린의 4성을 갖춘 북방 음계를 처음으로 온전히 기록한 책으로, 옛 입성(入聲)은 적어도 노래에서는 나머지 세 성조에 흡수되어 있다.","zh":"周德清1324年为元曲编纂的韵书,是第一份完整记录北方汉语语音系统的文献:它已具备普通话的四声,古入声则被并入了其余三声——至少在唱曲中是如此。","yue":"周德清1324年為元曲編嘅韻書,係第一份完整記錄北方漢語音系嘅文獻:已經有官話嘅四聲,而舊嘅入聲就併入咗其餘三聲——起碼喺唱曲入面係噉。","vi":"Vận thư do Chu Đức Thanh soạn năm 1324 cho kịch Nguyên — bản ghi đầy đủ đầu tiên về một hệ thống âm phương Bắc với bốn thanh của quan thoại, trong đó thanh nhập cũ đã được dồn vào ba thanh kia, ít nhất là trong ca hát.","th":"หนังสือสัมผัสปี 1324 ของโจวเต๋อชิงสำหรับเพลงในละครหยวน คือบันทึกฉบับสมบูรณ์ชิ้นแรกของระบบเสียงจีนตอนเหนือที่มีสี่วรรณยุกต์แบบจีนกลาง โดยเสียงเข้า (入聲) เดิมถูกรวมเข้ากับอีกสามเสียง อย่างน้อยก็ในบทเพลง","id":"Buku rima karya Zhou Deqing tahun 1324 untuk aria opera Yuan adalah catatan lengkap pertama tentang sistem bunyi Tionghoa utara yang memiliki empat nada Mandarin, dengan nada masuk (入聲) lama sudah dilebur ke tiga nada lainnya, setidaknya dalam nyanyian.","hi":"युआन ओपेरा के गीतों के लिए ज़ोऊ देछिंग की 1324 की तुक-पुस्तिका मंदारिन के चार स्वरों वाली किसी उत्तरी चीनी ध्वनि-व्यवस्था का पहला पूरा अभिलेख है — जिसमें पुराना प्रवेश-स्वर (入聲) बाक़ी तीन में समा चुका है, कम-से-कम गीतों में।","de":"Zhou Deqings Reimbuch von 1324 für Yuan-Opernarien ist die erste vollständige Aufzeichnung eines nordchinesischen Lautsystems mit den vier Tönen des Mandarin, in dem der alte eintretende Ton (入聲) in den übrigen drei aufgegangen ist — zumindest im Gesang.","fr":"Le manuel de rimes de Zhou Deqing (1324) pour les airs d'opéra Yuan est le premier relevé complet d'un système phonique du chinois du Nord doté des quatre tons du mandarin, l'ancien ton entrant (入聲) étant réparti entre les trois autres — du moins dans le chant.","it":"Il manuale di rime di Zhou Deqing del 1324 per le arie dell'opera Yuan è la prima documentazione completa di un sistema fonetico del cinese settentrionale con i quattro toni del mandarino, in cui l'antico tono entrante (入聲) è ripartito fra gli altri tre, almeno nel canto.","es":"El libro de rimas de Zhou Deqing (1324) para las arias del teatro Yuan es el primer registro completo de un sistema fonético del chino del norte con los cuatro tonos del mandarín, con el antiguo tono entrante (入聲) repartido entre los otros tres, al menos en el canto.","pt":"O livro de rimas de Zhou Deqing (1324) para as árias do teatro Yuan é o primeiro registro completo de um sistema sonoro do chinês do Norte com os quatro tons do mandarim, com o antigo tom entrante (入聲) incorporado aos outros três — pelo menos no canto.","ru":"Книга рифм Чжоу Дэцина 1324 года для арий юаньской оперы — первая полная запись северокитайской звуковой системы с четырьмя тонами мандарина, где старый входящий тон (入聲) растворён в трёх остальных, — по крайней мере, в пении.","uk":"Книга рим Чжоу Децина 1324 року для арій юаньської опери — перший повний запис північнокитайської звукової системи з чотирма мандаринськими тонами, у якій давній вхідний тон (入聲) розчинився в трьох інших — принаймні в співі.","ar":"كتاب القوافي الذي ألفه تشو دي تشينغ عام 1324 لأغاني أوبرا يوان هو أول سجل كامل لنظام صوتي صيني شمالي بأنغام الماندرين الأربع، وقد ذابت فيه النغمة الداخلة (入聲) القديمة في الأنغام الثلاث الأخرى، في الغناء على الأقل.","he":"ספר החריזה של ג'ואו דה-צ'ינג מ-1324 לאריות אופרת יואן הוא התיעוד המלא הראשון של מערכת הגאים סינית צפונית עם ארבעת הטונים של המנדרינית, שבה הטון הנכנס (入聲) הישן מקופל לתוך שלושת האחרים — לפחות בשירה.","sw":"Kitabu cha vina cha Zhou Deqing cha mwaka 1324, kilichoandikwa kwa ajili ya nyimbo za opera ya Yuan, ndicho rekodi kamili ya kwanza ya mfumo wa sauti wa Kichina cha kaskazini wenye toni nne za Kichina-Mandarin, ambamo toni ya kale ya kuingia (入聲) imeyeyushwa ndani ya zile tatu nyingine — angalau katika nyimbo."},"body":{"en":"<h3>The book that documents Mandarin's birth</h3><p>In 1324, in the middle decades of the Yuan dynasty, the song writer and music theorist <strong>Zhou Deqing (周德清)</strong> completed <em>Zhōngyuán Yīnyùn</em> (中原音韻, 'Sounds and Rhymes of the Central Plain'). Its stated purpose was practical: help composers of 雜劇 (zájù, the wildly popular Yuan opera form) match rhymes that would actually sound rhyming when sung in northern speech. His manifesto fits in one line: \"To write songs, you must correct your speech; to correct your speech, you must follow the sounds of the Central Plain.\" The book, printed in 1341, sorts 5,866 characters into just 19 rhyme groups, from 東鍾 to 廉纖, with no reverse-spellings at all: each group of homophones is simply listed together.</p><p>For historical linguists, however, this is the document that nails down the transition from <strong>Middle Chinese</strong> (隋唐音, c. 600 CE) to early modern Mandarin.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"一\">📍 See 一 across dialects</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"focus\" data-code=\"zh_yuan\">🎭 Focus on Yuan Mandarin</button></div><h3>What it proves</h3><ul><li><strong>The entering tone (入聲) was gone</strong> in northern speech. In the Tang/Song system the fourth tone, 入, was exactly the class of syllables ending in a stop (-p, -t, -k); <em>Zhōngyuán Yīnyùn</em> lists them under the other three tones: mostly yángpíng after an old fully voiced initial, qù after a nasal or l-, and shǎng after a voiceless initial. The pattern is closer to today's Jiao-Liao Mandarin, spoken on the Shandong and Liaodong peninsulas, than to Beijing, where the voiceless group was scattered among all four tones. Most scholars take this as proof the entering tone was gone, but Zhou himself wrote that he spread these syllables over the other tones \"to widen the rhymes, for writing songs\", and that \"in breathing and speech there is still a distinction of the entering tone\"; Lu Zhiwei and others conclude it had not yet disappeared.</li><li><strong>陰平 and 陽平 had split.</strong> The single 平 (level) category of Middle Chinese had divided based on the voicing of the original initial — yielding what we now call <em>tone 1</em> and <em>tone 2</em>.</li><li><strong>Voiced obstruents had devoiced.</strong> The Middle Chinese 全濁 (fully voiced) series — b, d, g, dz, dʒ — was no longer phonemic, the same merger that distinguishes Mandarin from Wu today.</li></ul><h3>Mandarin from the stage</h3><p>It is striking that the key document of early Mandarin is a <em>songwriter's manual</em>, not a court dictionary. Zhou quotes, with approval, people who held that the Central Plain pronunciation was already a common standard, used by everyone from officials discussing government and teachers at the state academy down to the magistrates hearing cases in court. <em>Zhōngyuán Yīnyùn</em> codifies that register. The 廣韻 (1008) preserved Middle Chinese as a literary ideal; <em>Zhōngyuán Yīnyùn</em> shows what people were actually singing on stage three centuries later.</p>"},"sources":["Zhou Deqing (1324). 中原音韻 [Zhōngyuán Yīnyùn].","Pulleyblank, E. G. (1991). Lexicon of Reconstructed Pronunciation in Early Middle Chinese, Late Middle Chinese, and Early Mandarin.","Norman, Jerry (1988). Chinese. Cambridge Language Surveys. Cambridge University Press.",{"text":"Wikipedia: Zhongyuan Yinyun","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zhongyuan_Yinyun"}]},{"id":"phags-pa-as-hangul-origin","icon":"ꡛ","tags":["hangul","phagspa","controversy","korea"],"title":{"en":"Did Phags-pa Script Influence Hangul?","ja":"ハングルは八思巴字に着想したのか — 60年続く言語学の謎","ko":"한글은 파스파 문자에서 영감을 받았나? — 60년 묵은 언어학 수수께끼","zh":"韩文是否借鉴了八思巴字?——一桩持续60年的语言学悬案","yue":"韓文係咪借鑒咗八思巴字?——已經60年嘅語言學謎團","vi":"Hangul có lấy cảm hứng từ chữ Phags-pa? — Bí ẩn ngôn ngữ học 60 năm","th":"ฮันกึลได้รับแรงบันดาลใจจากอักษรพักพา? — ปริศนาภาษาศาสตร์ 60 ปี","id":"Apakah aksara Hangul terinspirasi dari Phags-pa? — Misteri linguistik 60 tahun","hi":"क्या हंगुल पाक्सपा से प्रेरित था? — 60 साल पुरानी भाषाई पहेली","de":"Inspirierte Phags-pa die Hangul-Schrift? — Ein 60 Jahre altes Sprachrätsel","fr":"Le Hangul est-il inspiré du phags-pa ? — Un mystère linguistique de 60 ans","it":"Il Hangul è ispirato al phags-pa? — Un mistero linguistico di 60 anni","es":"¿Inspiró el phags-pa al Hangul? — Un misterio lingüístico de 60 años","pt":"O Hangul foi inspirado pelo phags-pa? — Um mistério linguístico de 60 anos","ru":"Вдохновил ли скрипт Пакпа создание хангыля? — 60-летняя лингвистическая загадка","uk":"Чи надихнуло Пакпа створення хангиля? — 60-річна лінгвістична загадка","ar":"هل ألهمت كتابة بَكْسبا الهانغول؟ — لغز لساني عمره 60 عاماً","he":"האם כתב פאקס-פא היה ההשראה להאנגול? — תעלומה לשונית בת 60 שנה","sw":"Je, herufi za Phags-pa zilihamasisha Hangul? — Fumbo la kiisimu la miaka 60"},"summary":{"en":"In 1966 the American Koreanist Gari Ledyard argued that a handful of Hangul's basic letter shapes were borrowed from the Yuan-era Phags-pa script. Most Korean scholars reject the idea; some Western scholars find it possible or even likely. The debate is still open.","ja":"1966年、米国の朝鮮学者ゲイリー・レッドヤードは、ハングルの基本字形のいくつかは元代の八思巴字から借りたものだと主張した。韓国の研究者の多くはこれを退けるが、欧米には「あり得る」、さらには「おそらくそうだ」とみる研究者もいる。論争は今も決着していない。","ko":"1966년 미국의 한국학자 게리 레드야드는 한글 기본 자형 몇 개를 원나라 파스파 문자에서 빌렸다고 주장했다. 한국 학자 대다수는 이를 받아들이지 않지만, 서양 학자 중에는 가능하다거나 오히려 그럴듯하다고 보는 이도 있다. 논쟁은 지금도 진행 중.","zh":"1966年,美国韩国学学者加里·莱德亚德主张,韩文有几个基本字母的字形借自元代的八思巴字。多数韩国学者不接受此说;一些西方学者则认为有可能,甚至很可能。争论至今未休。","yue":"1966年,美國韓國學學者加里·萊德雅德話,韓文有幾個基本字母嘅字形係借自元代八思巴字。大多數韓國學者反對;有啲西方學者就認為有可能、甚至幾大機會。爭論到而家都未停。","vi":"Năm 1966, nhà Hàn Quốc học người Mỹ Gari Ledyard cho rằng một vài hình dạng chữ cái cơ bản của Hangul đã được vay mượn từ chữ Phags-pa thời Nguyên. Phần lớn học giả Hàn Quốc bác bỏ ý tưởng này; một số học giả phương Tây lại thấy nó có thể đúng, thậm chí rất có khả năng. Tranh cãi đến nay vẫn chưa ngả ngũ.","th":"ในปี 1966 นักเกาหลีศึกษาชาวอเมริกัน Gari Ledyard เสนอว่ารูปอักษรพื้นฐานของฮันกึลจำนวนหนึ่งยืมมาจากอักษรพักพาสมัยหยวน นักวิชาการเกาหลีส่วนใหญ่ปฏิเสธแนวคิดนี้ ขณะที่นักวิชาการตะวันตกบางคนเห็นว่าเป็นไปได้หรือถึงขั้นน่าจะเป็นจริง การโต้แย้งยังไม่จบ","id":"Pada 1966 sarjana studi Korea asal Amerika, Gari Ledyard, berargumen bahwa segelintir bentuk huruf dasar Hangul dipinjam dari aksara Phags-pa era Yuan. Kebanyakan sarjana Korea menolak gagasan itu; sebagian sarjana Barat menganggapnya mungkin, bahkan cukup mungkin. Debat masih terbuka.","hi":"1966 में अमेरिकी कोरियाविद् गैरी लेड्यार्ड ने तर्क दिया कि हंगुल के कुछ मूल अक्षर-आकार युआनकालीन पाक्सपा लिपि से उधार लिए गए थे। ज़्यादातर कोरियाई विद्वान इस विचार को ख़ारिज करते हैं; कुछ पश्चिमी विद्वान इसे संभव, बल्कि संभावित भी मानते हैं। बहस अब भी खुली है।","de":"1966 argumentierte der amerikanische Koreanist Gari Ledyard, eine Handvoll Grundbuchstaben des Hangul sei der Yuan-zeitlichen Phags-pa-Schrift entlehnt. Die meisten koreanischen Forscher lehnen das ab; manche westlichen Forscher halten es für möglich oder sogar wahrscheinlich. Die Debatte ist offen.","fr":"En 1966, le coréaniste américain Gari Ledyard a soutenu qu'une poignée de formes de lettres de base du Hangul étaient empruntées à l'écriture phags-pa de l'époque Yuan. La plupart des savants coréens rejettent l'idée ; certains chercheurs occidentaux la jugent possible, voire probable. Le débat reste ouvert.","it":"Nel 1966 il coreanista americano Gari Ledyard sostenne che una manciata delle forme di base delle lettere dell'Hangul fosse presa in prestito dalla scrittura phags-pa dell'epoca Yuan. La maggior parte degli studiosi coreani respinge l'idea; alcuni studiosi occidentali la trovano possibile o perfino probabile. Il dibattito è ancora aperto.","es":"En 1966 el coreanista estadounidense Gari Ledyard sostuvo que un puñado de las formas básicas de las letras del Hangul se tomaron de la escritura phags-pa de la era Yuan. La mayoría de los académicos coreanos rechaza la idea; algunos estudiosos occidentales la consideran posible o incluso probable. El debate sigue abierto.","pt":"Em 1966 o coreanista americano Gari Ledyard sustentou que um punhado das formas básicas das letras do Hangul foi tomado de empréstimo da escrita phags-pa da era Yuan. A maioria dos estudiosos coreanos rejeita a ideia; alguns estudiosos ocidentais a consideram possível ou até provável. O debate continua em aberto.","ru":"В 1966 году американский кореевед Гэри Ледьярд утверждал, что несколько базовых букв хангыля по форме заимствованы из юаньского письма пакпа. Большинство корейских учёных эту идею отвергают; некоторые западные учёные считают её возможной или даже вероятной. Споры продолжаются.","uk":"У 1966 році американський кореєзнавець Ґері Ледьярд стверджував, що кілька базових форм літер хангиля запозичено з юаньського письма пакпа. Більшість корейських учених цю ідею відкидають; дехто із західних науковців вважає її можливою чи навіть імовірною. Дискусія триває.","ar":"في عام 1966 جادل الباحث الأمريكي في الدراسات الكورية غاري ليديارد بأن حفنة من أشكال الحروف الأساسية في الهانغول استُعيرت من كتابة بَكْسبا في عصر يوان. ويرفض معظم العلماء الكوريين الفكرة، بينما يراها بعض الباحثين الغربيين ممكنة بل مرجّحة. النقاش لا يزال قائماً.","he":"ב-1966 טען חוקר קוריאה האמריקני גארי לדיארד שקומץ מצורות האותיות הבסיסיות של ההאנגול נשאלו מכתב פאקס-פא של תקופת יואן. רוב החוקרים הקוריאנים דוחים את הרעיון; חוקרים מערביים אחדים רואים בו אפשרי ואף סביר. הוויכוח עדיין פתוח.","sw":"Mwaka 1966 mtaalamu wa Marekani wa masomo ya Korea Gari Ledyard alidai kwamba maumbo machache ya msingi ya herufi za Hangul yalikopwa kutoka herufi za Phags-pa za enzi ya Yuan. Wanazuoni wengi wa Kikorea wanalikataa wazo hilo; baadhi ya wasomi wa Magharibi wanaliona linawezekana au hata lina uwezekano mkubwa. Mjadala bado uko wazi."},"body":{"en":"<h3>The provocation</h3><p>In his 1966 doctoral dissertation, American Korean-studies scholar <strong>Gari Ledyard</strong> argued that King Sejong's <em>Hunminjeongeum</em> (訓民正音, 1443) owed something to a foreign model. Building on a 1957 paper by the Canadian linguist E. R. Hope, he proposed that five basic consonant shapes were adapted from the <strong>Phags-pa script</strong>, the Yuan empire's alphabet designed in 1269 by the Tibetan monk Drogön Chögyal Phagpa. His clue was in the sources themselves: the 1443 court record says the new letters \"imitate the Old Seal\" (字倣古篆). Ledyard read \"seal\" as a veiled reference to Phags-pa, since \"seal\" could also describe a Mongol script style.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"focus\" data-code=\"zh_phagspa\">ꡛ Phags-pa on the map</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"focus\" data-code=\"ko_mid\">📜 Middle Korean</button></div><h3>The evidence</h3><p>Ledyard pointed to visual parallels in five letters: ㄱ, ㄷ, ㄹ, ㅂ, ㅅ, each resembling a corresponding Phags-pa consonant (ꡂ, ꡊ, ꡙ, ꡎ, ꡛ): ㄱ is ꡂ <em>g</em> with its lower box removed, and ㅂ is ꡎ <em>b</em> turned and simplified. From one such model letter per sound class, he argued, the other consonants were built by adding or removing strokes. He noted that Phags-pa was no dead letter in Korea: Goryeo had been under Mongol rule when the script was new, and Joseon still ran official courses in it — instruction was in decline by 1423, but had not stopped. By 1443 the Yuan had been gone for 75 years, but Phags-pa forms survived in the books the Korean court kept, including Sino-Mongol rhyme material of the <em>Menggu Ziyun</em> type that Sejong's philologists worked from.</p><h3>The rebuttal</h3><p>Critics, including the Korean linguists <strong>Sang-Oak Lee</strong> and <strong>Young-Key Kim-Renaud</strong>, replied that the correspondences are too few to be systematic, that letters this simple can be matched to almost any script, and that the <em>Hunminjeongeum Haerye</em> (1446) explicitly describes Hangul consonant shapes as iconic representations of the articulatory organs: ㄱ as the back of the tongue raised, ㄴ as the tongue touching the alveolar ridge, ㅁ as the mouth, etc. No other script of the time grounds its letter shapes in the anatomy of speech like this, they argue, so why would Sejong's scholars invent such an elaborate cover story? Supporters reply that the two need not conflict: borrowing a few outlines would be a small thing beside the featural system built on top of them.</p><h3>No settled verdict</h3><p>Western scholarship is split rather than settled. <strong>Peter T. Daniels</strong> wrote that Ledyard's five letters \"are almost certainly based on the corresponding ʼPhags-pa letters\"; <strong>Jae Jung Song</strong> puts the influence \"within the realm of possibilities\"; <strong>Richard Sproat</strong> notes that influence cannot be ruled out but there is no direct evidence for it; <strong>Geoffrey Sampson</strong> is sceptical of any significant influence; and <strong>Ross King</strong> reported in 1987 that the senior Koreanists Edward Wagner and Samuel Martin did not accept it. What is not disputed is that the theoretical framework — the articulatory phonetics — is Sejong's own. So the argument is really about five outlines, not about who invented the system: even on Ledyard's reading, the idea of letters that show how sounds are made, and of building them up stroke by stroke, belongs to Sejong's court.</p>"},"sources":["Ledyard, Gari K. (1966). The Korean Language Reform of 1446: The Origin, Background, and Early History of the Korean Alphabet. PhD diss., UC Berkeley.","King, Ross (1996). 'Korean writing.' In The World's Writing Systems. Oxford UP.","Sampson, Geoffrey (2015). Writing Systems (2nd ed.). Equinox.","Lee, Ki-Moon & Ramsey, S. Robert (2011). A History of the Korean Language. Cambridge University Press.",{"text":"Wikipedia: ʼPhags-pa inspiration for Hangul hypothesis (with its cited evaluations by Daniels, Song, Sproat, Sampson, Kim-Renaud, Lee)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%CA%BCPhags-pa_inspiration_for_Hangul_hypothesis"}]},{"id":"manchu-script-origins","icon":"📜","tags":["manchu","script","mongolia","tungusic","sinosphere"],"title":{"en":"Manchu Script and Its Aramaic-to-Uyghur Lineage","ja":"満洲文字 — 五つの文明を渡った文字","ko":"만주 문자 — 다섯 문명을 건넌 알파벳","zh":"满文：跨越五大文明的字母","yue":"滿文：橫跨五個文明嘅字母","vi":"Chữ Mãn — bảng chữ cái vượt qua năm nền văn minh","th":"อักษรแมนจู — ตัวอักษรที่ผ่านห้าอารยธรรม","id":"Aksara Manchu — alfabet yang melewati lima peradaban","hi":"मांचू लिपि — पाँच सभ्यताओं को पार करती लिपि","de":"Mandschurische Schrift — ein Alphabet über fünf Zivilisationen","fr":"Écriture mandchoue — un alphabet traversant cinq civilisations","it":"Scrittura manciù — un alfabeto attraverso cinque civiltà","es":"Escritura manchú — un alfabeto a través de cinco civilizaciones","pt":"Escrita manchu — um alfabeto através de cinco civilizações","ru":"Маньчжурское письмо — алфавит через пять цивилизаций","uk":"Маньчжурське письмо — алфавіт крізь п'ять цивілізацій","ar":"الكتابة المانشورية — أبجدية عبرت خمس حضارات","he":"כתב המנצ'ו — אלפבית שעבר חמש ציביליזציות","sw":"Hati ya Manchu — alfabeti iliyopita ustaarabu tano"},"summary":{"en":"The Manchu script used by the Qing emperors was not invented from scratch — it arrived via a chain: Aramaic → Sogdian → Old Uyghur → Mongolian → Manchu, each link adding a new empire and a new direction of travel.","ja":"清朝皇帝が使った満洲文字は一から発明されたものではない。アラム文字→ソグド文字→古ウイグル文字→モンゴル文字→満洲文字という連鎖を経て届いた。各リンクに新たな帝国と新たな旅路が加わっている。","ko":"청 황제들이 사용한 만주 문자는 처음부터 발명된 것이 아니라 아람 문자 → 소그드 문자 → 고 위구르 문자 → 몽골 문자 → 만주 문자라는 사슬을 통해 전해졌다.","zh":"清朝皇帝所用的满文并非凭空创造，而是经由一条链条传来：亚拉姆文→粟特文→古回鹘文→蒙古文→满文，每个环节都增添了一个新帝国与新旅程。","yue":"清朝皇帝用嘅滿文唔係憑空創造嘅，係經過一條鏈：亞拉姆文→粟特文→古回鶻文→蒙古文→滿文咁傳落嚟嘅。","vi":"Chữ Mãn Thanh triều dùng không phải được sáng tạo từ đầu — nó đến qua chuỗi: Aramaic → Sogdian → Uyghur cổ → Mông Cổ → Mãn, mỗi mắt xích thêm một đế chế và một hành trình mới.","th":"อักษรแมนจูที่ราชวงศ์ชิงใช้ไม่ได้ถูกประดิษฐ์ขึ้นใหม่ แต่มาผ่านสายโซ่: อราเมอิก → ซอกเดีย → อุยกูร์โบราณ → มองโกเลีย → แมนจู","id":"Aksara Manchu yang digunakan dinasti Qing tidak diciptakan dari nol — ia datang melalui rantai: Aram → Sogdia → Uyghur Kuno → Mongolia → Manchu, setiap mata rantai menambah kekaisaran baru.","hi":"मांचू लिपि जो किंग राजवंश ने प्रयोग की वो शून्य से नहीं बनी — यह एक श्रृंखला के जरिए आई: अरामाइक → सोग्दियन → प्राचीन उइगर → मंगोलियन → मांचू।","de":"Die Mandschurische Schrift der Qing-Kaiser wurde nicht von Grund auf neu erfunden — sie kam über eine Kette: Aramäisch → Sogdisch → Altuigurisch → Mongolisch → Mandschurisch.","fr":"L'écriture mandchoue des empereurs Qing n'a pas été inventée de zéro — elle est arrivée par une chaîne : araméen → sogdien → vieux ouïghour → mongol → mandchou.","it":"La scrittura manciù degli imperatori Qing non fu inventata da zero — arrivò attraverso una catena: aramaico → sogdiano → uiguro antico → mongolo → manciù.","es":"La escritura manchú de los emperadores Qing no se inventó desde cero — llegó a través de una cadena: arameo → sogdiano → uigur antiguo → mongol → manchú.","pt":"A escrita manchu dos imperadores Qing não foi inventada do zero — chegou por uma cadeia: aramaico → sogdiano → uigur antigo → mongol → manchu.","ru":"Маньчжурское письмо, которым пользовались цинские императоры, не было изобретено с нуля — оно пришло по цепочке: арамейский → согдийский → старый уйгурский → монгольский → маньчжурский.","uk":"Маньчжурське письмо, яким користувалися цинські імператори, не було винайдено з нуля — воно прийшло через ланцюжок: арамейська → согдійська → давньоуйгурська → монгольська → маньчжурська.","ar":"لم تُخترع الكتابة المانشورية التي استخدمها أباطرة تشينغ من العدم — بل وصلت عبر سلسلة: الآرامية → السغدية → الأويغورية القديمة → المغولية → المانشورية.","he":"כתב המנצ'ו ששימש את קיסרי צ'ינג לא הומצא יש מאין — הוא הגיע דרך שרשרת: ארמית → סוגדית → אויגורית עתיקה → מונגולית → מנצ'ורית.","sw":"Hati ya Manchu iliyotumiwa na maimamu wa Qing haikuvumbuliwa kutoka mwanzo — ilikuja kupitia mnyororo: Kiaramu → Kisogdi → Kiuiguri cha kale → Kimongolia → Kimanchu."},"body":{"en":"<h3>From the Levant to the Forbidden City</h3>\n<p>The <strong>Manchu script</strong> — the vertical columns of loops and dots that fill the bilingual stone stelae of the Qing dynasty (1644–1912) — did not emerge in Manchuria. Its pedigree reaches back more than two millennia, to <strong>Aramaic</strong>, the administrative language of the Achaemenid Persian empire in the 5th century BCE.</p>\n\n<div class=\"trivia-actions\">\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"focus\" data-code=\"mnc\">📍 Focus Manchu on the map</button>\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"panto\" data-lat=\"41.8\" data-lng=\"123.4\" data-zoom=\"4\">🗺️ Fly to Manchuria</button>\n</div>\n\n<p>The chain of inheritance runs like this:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Aramaic (5th c. BCE):</strong> An abjad (consonant alphabet) that spread across the Persian empire from Egypt to Central Asia. Its right-to-left cursive form became the template for dozens of later scripts.</li>\n  <li><strong>Sogdian (1st–10th c. CE):</strong> Sogdian merchants carried Aramaic-derived letters along the Silk Road, still writing them in horizontal lines from right to left.</li>\n  <li><strong>Old Uyghur (~8th c. CE):</strong> Turkic Uyghur scribes adapted Sogdian for their own language and turned it 90°, so that it ran <em>top to bottom</em> in columns read from left to right, probably in imitation of Chinese. Every later script in this chain kept that layout. Genghis Khan's Mongols took over the script in the early 13th century, after defeating the Naimans in 1204 and capturing a Uyghur scribe named Tata-tonga who had served them.</li>\n  <li><strong>Mongolian Script (13th c.):</strong> Old Uyghur letters were fitted to Mongolian sounds. The script is still taught to Mongolian pupils in Inner Mongolia, and since 2025 it has also been co-official with Cyrillic in Mongolia itself.</li>\n  <li><strong>Manchu Script (1599 CE):</strong> The Jurchen chieftain <em>Nurhaci</em> ordered his adviser Erdeni and Gagai to adapt the Mongolian alphabet for the Jurchen (later Manchu) language. This first version, the <em>tongki fuka akū hergen</em> or \"script without dots and circles,\" lacked diacritical marks, making some letters ambiguous. In <strong>1632</strong>, Dahai added dots and circles (<em>tongki fuka sindaha hergen</em>), eliminating most ambiguities.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"trivia-actions\">\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"focus\" data-code=\"sjo\">📍 Focus Sibe (living Manchu) on the map</button>\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"panto\" data-lat=\"43.8\" data-lng=\"81.0\" data-zoom=\"6\">🗺️ Fly to Qapqal, Xinjiang (Sibe homeland)</button>\n</div>\n\n<p>From the Uyghur rotation onward, the scripts in this chain share one peculiarity: they are written <strong>vertically, in columns running from left to right across the page</strong> — the opposite of Chinese columns, which run right to left. A Manchu manuscript therefore opens from the left, just like a Western book, yet each column reads top-down like classical Chinese.</p>\n\n<h3>The Phags-pa interlude</h3>\n<p>There was one detour. In the 13th century, the Tibetan monk <strong>'Phags-pa</strong> devised an entirely new script for Kublai Khan, intended to write all the languages of the Mongol empire in a single unified system. The <em>Phags-pa script</em> (八思巴文) was used on official seals and inscriptions from roughly 1269 to 1368. In Tibet a seal script known by the same name was still carved on seals in the 20th century, though some scholars regard that later script as a separate Tibetan seal script rather than true Phags-pa. But Phags-pa was a <em>parallel experiment</em>, not a link in the Manchu chain: it influenced the later Korean hangul discussion and left its mark on East Asian paleography, but Manchu descends from the Mongolian Uyghur-derived script, not from Phags-pa.</p>\n\n<h3>Sibe: the living heir</h3>\n<p>Manchu itself is now functionally extinct as a native tongue, with perhaps a handful of elderly speakers in remote Heilongjiang villages. But the script lives on. In <strong>1764</strong>, the Qing court sent a garrison of Sibe bannermen from Shengjing (today's Shenyang) to the far western frontier at Ili in Xinjiang. Several thousand soldiers and family members made the trek, partly by ox cart, and it took them well over a year. Their descendants, the <strong>Sibe people of Qapqal</strong>, preserved the Manchu script and the Manchu-family language. About 30,000 people still spoke Sibe in 2000. In 1998 the primary schools of Qapqal taught in Chinese, but Sibe lessons were compulsory. The newspaper <em>Cabcal Serkin</em> (Qapqal News), published since 1946 and often described as the only Sibe-language newspaper in the world, is printed in the same vertical script that Erdeni and Gagai assembled in 1599.</p>\n\n<p>Two branches of that vertical line are still alive: the Mongolian script in Inner Mongolia and Mongolia, and the Manchu script among the Sibe of Qapqal. A pupil writing Sibe homework today is using letters whose ancestors were the office script of the Persian empire some 2,500 years ago.</p>"},"sources":["Janhunen, Juha (2003). \"Mongolic Languages.\" Routledge Language Family Series. Routledge.","Norman, Jerry (1978). A Concise Manchu-English Lexicon. University of Washington Press.","Kara, György (2005). Books of the Mongolian Nomads: More than Eight Centuries of Writing Mongolian. Indiana University, Research Institute for Inner Asian Studies.",{"text":"Unicode Consortium: Mongolian block (U+1800–U+18AF)","url":"https://www.unicode.org/charts/PDF/U1800.pdf"},{"text":"Endangered Archives Programme: Sibe/Xibe language materials, EAP264","url":"https://eap.bl.uk/project/EAP264"},"Crossley, Pamela Kyle (1990). Orphan Warriors: Three Manchu Generations and the End of the Qing World. Princeton University Press.",{"text":"Wikipedia: Xibe language","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xibe_language"},{"text":"Wikipedia: Mongolian script","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mongolian_script"}]},{"id":"old-mongol-uyghur-script","icon":"🐎","tags":["mongolia","script","cyrillic","sinosphere","inner-mongolia","history"],"title":{"en":"Why Mongolians write in two different scripts","ja":"なぜモンゴル人は二つの文字を使うのか","ko":"몽골인이 두 가지 문자를 사용하는 이유","zh":"为什么蒙古族用两种不同的文字","yue":"點解蒙古人用兩種唔同嘅文字","vi":"Tại sao người Mông Cổ viết bằng hai chữ khác nhau","th":"ทำไมชาวมองโกเลียถึงใช้สองอักษรที่ต่างกัน","id":"Mengapa orang Mongol menulis dalam dua aksara berbeda","hi":"मंगोल लोग दो अलग-अलग लिपियाँ क्यों लिखते हैं","de":"Warum Mongolen zwei verschiedene Schriften verwenden","fr":"Pourquoi les Mongols écrivent avec deux écritures différentes","it":"Perché i Mongoli scrivono in due alfabeti diversi","es":"Por qué los mongoles escriben en dos escrituras diferentes","pt":"Por que os mongóis escrevem em duas escritas diferentes","ru":"Почему монголы пишут двумя разными системами письма","uk":"Чому монголи пишуть двома різними письменами","ar":"لماذا يكتب المغول بنظامَي كتابة مختلفَين","he":"מדוע המונגולים כותבים בשתי כתיבות שונות","sw":"Kwa nini Wamongolia wanaandika kwa hati mbili tofauti"},"summary":{"en":"Mongolia uses Cyrillic, while Inner Mongolia (China) uses a vertical script descended from Uyghur letters adopted in the Mongol Empire. Mongolia adopted Cyrillic as its official script in the mid-20th century; a 2020 change to Mongolian-medium schooling in Inner Mongolia prompted widespread protests.","ja":"モンゴル国はキリル文字を使い、中国・内モンゴルは1206年にチンギス・カンが採用したウイグル文字系の縦書き文字を使う。この分裂は1206年ではなく1946年に起きた──スターリンが切り替えを強制したのだ。2020年の中国による教育政策は、数十年ぶり最大の抗議運動を引き起こした。","ko":"몽골은 키릴 문자를, 중국 내몽골은 칭기즈 칸이 1206년 채택한 위구르 계통 세로 문자를 사용한다. 이 분열은 1206년이 아닌 1946년 스탈린의 강제 전환으로 발생했으며, 2020년 중국의 교육 정책은 수십 년 만의 최대 시위를 촉발했다.","zh":"蒙古国用西里尔文；中国内蒙古用成吉思汗1206年采用的回鹘系竖体文字。两者的分裂不是1206年，而是1946年——斯大林强制推行了切换。2020年中国削减蒙古语教学的政策，引发了内蒙古数十年来最大规模的抗议。","yue":"蒙古國用西里爾文；中國內蒙古用成吉思汗1206年採用嘅回鶻系竪體文字。兩者嘅分裂唔係1206年，而係1946年——史太林強制轉換嘅。2020年中國削減蒙古語授課嘅政策引發咗內蒙古數十年來最大規模嘅抗議。","vi":"Mông Cổ dùng chữ Cyrillic; Nội Mông (Trung Quốc) dùng chữ dọc gốc Uyghur mà Thành Cát Tư Hãn đã dùng từ năm 1206. Sự phân chia xảy ra năm 1946 chứ không phải 1206 — Stalin ép chuyển đổi. Chính sách 2020 của Trung Quốc cắt giảm dạy học bằng tiếng Mông Cổ đã gây ra các cuộc biểu tình lớn nhất Nội Mông trong nhiều thập kỷ.","th":"มองโกเลียใช้อักษรซีริลลิก ส่วนมองโกเลียในของจีนใช้อักษรแนวตั้งจากอุยกูร์ที่เจงกีสข่านนำมาใช้ในปี ค.ศ. 1206 การแบ่งแยกนี้เกิดขึ้นในปี 1946 ไม่ใช่ 1206 — สตาลินบังคับให้เปลี่ยน นโยบายจีนปี 2020 ที่ลดการสอนภาษามองโกเลียก่อให้เกิดการประท้วงครั้งใหญ่ที่สุดในมองโกเลียในในรอบหลายสิบปี","id":"Mongolia menggunakan Sirilik; Mongolia Dalam (Tiongkok) menggunakan aksara vertikal turunan Uyghur yang diadopsi Jenghis Khan pada 1206. Pemisahan terjadi pada 1946, bukan 1206 — Stalin memaksakan pergantian. Kebijakan 2020 Tiongkok untuk mengurangi pendidikan berbahasa Mongolia memicu protes terbesar di Mongolia Dalam dalam beberapa dekade.","hi":"मंगोलिया सिरिलिक लिपि का उपयोग करता है; चीन का आंतरिक मंगोलिया उस ऊर्ध्व लिपि का उपयोग करता है जिसे चंगेज खान ने 1206 में उइगर वर्णमाला से अपनाया था। यह विभाजन 1206 में नहीं बल्कि 1946 में हुआ — स्टालिन ने परिवर्तन को थोपा था। 2020 की चीनी नीति ने दशकों का सबसे बड़ा विरोध प्रदर्शन छेड़ा।","de":"Die Mongolei verwendet Kyrillisch; die Innere Mongolei (China) das vertikale Schriftsystem, das auf die uigurische Schrift zurückgeht, die Dschingis Khan 1206 übernahm. Die Spaltung entstand 1946 — Stalin erzwang den Wechsel. Eine chinesische Bildungspolitik von 2020 löste die größten Proteste in der Inneren Mongolei seit Jahrzehnten aus.","fr":"La Mongolie utilise le cyrillique ; la Mongolie intérieure (Chine) utilise l'écriture verticale héritée de l'ouïghour, adoptée par Gengis Khan en 1206. La scission eut lieu en 1946, sous la pression de Staline. Une politique chinoise de 2020 visant à réduire l'enseignement en mongol a déclenché les plus grandes protestations en Mongolie intérieure depuis des décennies.","it":"La Mongolia usa il cirillico; la Mongolia Interna (Cina) usa la scrittura verticale derivata dall'uiguro, adottata da Gengis Khan nel 1206. La divisione avvenne nel 1946 per imposizione di Stalin. Una politica cinese del 2020 per ridurre l'istruzione in lingua mongola ha scatenato le più grandi proteste nella Mongolia Interna da decenni.","es":"Mongolia usa el cirílico; Mongolia Interior (China) usa la escritura vertical heredada del uigur, adoptada por Gengis Kan en 1206. La división se produjo en 1946 —Stalin forzó el cambio. Una política china de 2020 para reducir la educación en mongol desencadenó las mayores protestas en Mongolia Interior en décadas.","pt":"A Mongólia usa o cirílico; a Mongólia Interior (China) usa a escrita vertical derivada do uigur, adotada por Gengis Khan em 1206. A divisão ocorreu em 1946 — Stalin forçou a mudança. Uma política chinesa de 2020 para reduzir o ensino em mongol desencadeou os maiores protestos na Mongólia Interior em décadas.","ru":"В Монголии используется кириллица; во Внутренней Монголии (Китай) — вертикальное письмо, восходящее к уйгурскому, принятому Чингисханом в 1206 году. Раскол произошёл в 1946 году — по принуждению Сталина. Китайская политика 2020 года по сокращению монгольского обучения вызвала крупнейшие протесты во Внутренней Монголии за десятилетия.","uk":"У Монголії використовується кирилиця; у Внутрішній Монголії (Китай) — вертикальне письмо, що походить від уйгурського, прийнятого Чингісханом у 1206 році. Розкол стався в 1946 році — за примусу Сталіна. Китайська політика 2020 року щодо скорочення монгольського навчання спричинила найбільші протести у Внутрішній Монголії за десятиліття.","ar":"تستخدم منغوليا الكيريلية؛ أما منغوليا الداخلية (الصين) فتستخدم الخط الرأسي المنحدر من الأويغورية التي اعتمدها جنكيز خان عام 1206. جاء الانقسام عام 1946 لا 1206 — فرضه ستالين. أشعلت سياسة صينية عام 2020 لتقليص التعليم بالمغولية أكبر احتجاجات في منغوليا الداخلية منذ عقود.","he":"מונגוליה משתמשת בקירילית; מונגוליה הפנימית (סין) משתמשת בכתב האנכי שמוצאו מאויגורי, שגנגיס חאן אימץ ב-1206. הפיצול התרחש ב-1946 — סטלין אכף את המעבר. מדיניות סינית משנת 2020 לצמצום לימוד המונגולית עוררה את המחאות הגדולות ביותר במונגוליה הפנימית בעשרות שנים.","sw":"Mongolia inatumia Kisiriliki; Mongolia ya Ndani (China) inatumia hati ya wima inayotokana na Kiuiguri ambayo Genghis Khan aliipitisha mwaka 1206. Mgawanyiko ulitokea mwaka 1946, si 1206 — Stalin alilazimisha mabadiliko. Sera ya China ya 2020 kupunguza elimu kwa lugha ya Kimongolia ilisababisha maandamano makubwa zaidi nchini Mongolia ya Ndani kwa miongo kadhaa."},"body":{"en":"<h3>One people, two scripts</h3>\n<p>Look at any Mongolian sign in Ulaanbaatar and you see <strong>Cyrillic</strong>. Look at any Mongolian sign in Hohhot (Inner Mongolia, China) and you see a flowing vertical script, written in columns from top to bottom, columns running left to right. The two populations share an ancestry, a language family, and an epic — <em>The Secret History of the Mongols</em> — but they write their language in completely different alphabets.</p>\n\n<div class=\"trivia-actions\">\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"panto\" data-lat=\"47.9\" data-lng=\"106.9\" data-zoom=\"5\">🗺️ Fly to Ulaanbaatar (Cyrillic Mongolia)</button>\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"panto\" data-lat=\"40.8\" data-lng=\"111.7\" data-zoom=\"5\">🗺️ Fly to Hohhot (Traditional-script Inner Mongolia)</button>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Step 1: Genghis Khan and the Uyghur scribe (1206)</h3>\n<p>In 1204, two years before <strong>Temüjin</strong> was proclaimed <em>Genghis Khan</em> at the great kurultai of 1206, his army crushed the Naiman confederation. Among the captives was a Uyghur scribe of the Naiman khan who had kept his seal and his treasury, <strong>Tata-tonga</strong> (also spelled Tatatunga), brought before the conqueror still carrying his master's seal. The Mongols had no writing of their own; Tata-tonga did. Genghis Khan set him to teach the Old Uyghur script to his sons and the other princes, and in their hands it was bent to fit Mongolian sounds.</p>\n\n<p>The resulting <strong>Mongolian Bichig</strong> (\"Mongolian script\") is written vertically, top-to-bottom, in columns that advance from left to right. That odd combination is an accident of history: the Sogdian script the Uyghurs borrowed ran horizontally from right to left, and Uyghur scribes turned it 90 degrees anticlockwise to imitate Chinese columns without changing the letters' order. Its descendants (Mongolian, Oirat Clear Script, Manchu) kept this rare left-to-right column order, and the Phags-pa script later copied it. In Inner Mongolia the script is still the official written form of Mongolian, taught in Mongolian-medium schools and shown beside Chinese characters on public signs.</p>\n\n<div class=\"trivia-actions\">\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"focus\" data-code=\"mnc\">📍 Manchu on the map</button>\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"focus\" data-code=\"pmgl\">📍 Proto-Mongolic reconstruction</button>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Step 2: The Phags-pa interlude (1269–1368)</h3>\n<p>The Mongol empire's diversity made Tatatunga's Uyghur-derived script inadequate for writing Chinese, Tibetan, Sanskrit or Persian. In 1269, Kublai Khan commissioned the Tibetan monk <strong>'Phags-pa</strong> to design a universal script. The resulting <em>Phags-pa script</em> (八思巴文), based on Tibetan letters but written in vertical columns, was meant to spell every language of the empire, Chinese included, letter by letter. It was used on imperial seals and edicts throughout the Yuan dynasty, and some scholars — Gari Ledyard above all — have argued that it influenced the design of the Korean hangul (1443), though the point remains contested. When the Ming dynasty expelled the Mongols in 1368, Phags-pa fell out of imperial use, but Mongolian Bichig continued without interruption.</p>\n\n<h3>Step 3: Stalin's scissors (1946)</h3>\n<p>By the 20th century, the Mongolian People's Republic (modern Mongolia) was a Soviet satellite, and its script changed with Moscow's line. A first Latin alphabet was adopted in 1931 and quietly dropped by 1933. On 21 February <strong>1941</strong> Mongolia officially switched to a new <strong>Latin</strong> alphabet. Barely a month later, on 25 March <strong>1941</strong>, by which time the Soviet Union itself was moving its minority languages from Latin to Cyrillic, the Latin decision was abolished and <strong>Cyrillic</strong> adopted instead, becoming the formal official script on 1 January <strong>1946</strong>. The ostensible reason was to align Mongolia's literacy with Soviet print infrastructure; the real effect was to cut the generation trained in Cyrillic off from any direct ability to read pre-1946 texts, classical literature, or the stone inscriptions of the Mongolian empires.</p>\n\n<p>Inner Mongolia, meanwhile, remained under the Republic of China and then the People's Republic of China, where the traditional script stayed in continuous use through periods of support and periods of persecution, the worst being the Cultural Revolution. The result: a single language community separated by a political border into two completely different literacy traditions.</p>\n\n<h3>Step 4: The 2020 protests</h3>\n<p>On 26 August <strong>2020</strong>, days before the school year began, Inner Mongolia's Department of Education announced that in Mongolian-medium schools three subjects would switch to <em>Mandarin Chinese</em> and national Chinese-language textbooks: language and literature and \"morality and law\" from the first grade, and history from the seventh. It was part of a national textbook unification already rolled out elsewhere in China since 2017. The reaction was swift, and for the region rare in its scale: parents boycotted schools, students walked out, and residents across Inner Mongolia held demonstrations. Signs in Traditional Mongolian script were held up by protesters who wrote that they were fighting for the survival of their culture and language.</p>\n\n<div class=\"trivia-actions\">\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"panto\" data-lat=\"44.0\" data-lng=\"117.0\" data-zoom=\"5\">🗺️ Inner Mongolia — region of the 2020 protests</button>\n</div>\n\n<h3>The revival in Mongolia</h3>\n<p>Since the 1990 democratic revolution, Mongolia has officially committed to reviving Traditional script literacy alongside Cyrillic. Street signs in Ulaanbaatar now sometimes include the vertical Mongolian script. The 2015 Law on the Mongolian Language set a <strong>2025 deadline</strong>, and the date held: since January 2025 state and local government bodies must keep their official correspondence in both Cyrillic and Mongolian Bichig. Cyrillic is not being abolished; the vertical script is being added beside it. Progress is slow: most Mongolians schooled during the Cyrillic decades cannot read the script their ancestors used for seven centuries. But the younger generation is learning — partly from pride, partly because Traditional Mongolian script is the only way to read the words of Genghis Khan as his own scribes wrote them.</p>\n\n<p>So the same vertical letters now carry two different meanings. In Ulaanbaatar they are a heritage being rebuilt by law; in Hohhot, where they never stopped being used, they became a protest banner in 2020.</p>"},"sources":["Bawden, Charles R. (1968). The Modern History of Mongolia. Weidenfeld & Nicolson.","Rachewiltz, Igor de (2004). The Secret History of the Mongols. Brill.","Janhunen, Juha (2003). \"Mongolic Languages.\" Routledge Language Family Series. Routledge.",{"text":"Human Rights Watch: \"China: Mongolian Mother-Tongue Classes Curtailed\" (2020)","url":"https://www.hrw.org/news/2020/09/04/china-mongolian-mother-tongue-classes-curtailed"},{"text":"Wikipedia: Mongolian script — history and modern use","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mongolian_script"},"Svantesson, Jan-Olof et al. (2005). The Phonology of Mongolian. Oxford University Press."]},{"id":"jurchen-script-deciphered","icon":"📜","tags":["jurchen","tungusic","script","jin-dynasty","sinosphere"],"title":{"en":"Jurchen Script — A Partly Deciphered Jin-Dynasty Writing System","ja":"女真文字 — 800年の謎、いまだ部分的にしか読めない","ko":"여진 문자 — 800년의 수수께끼, 아직은 부분적 해독","zh":"女真文字——800年的谜题，至今只读懂了一部分","yue":"女真文字——800年嘅謎題，到而家仲只係讀得一部分","vi":"Chữ Nữ Chân — câu đố 800 năm, vẫn chỉ đọc được một phần","th":"อักษรหนี่เจิน — ปริศนา 800 ปี ที่ยังอ่านได้เพียงบางส่วน","id":"Aksara Jurchen — Teka-teki 800 Tahun yang Baru Terbaca Sebagian","hi":"जुरचेन लिपि — 800 साल पुरानी पहेली, अब भी आंशिक रूप से ही पढ़ी गई","de":"Die Jurchen-Schrift — ein 800 Jahre altes Rätsel, bis heute nur teilweise lesbar","fr":"L'écriture jürchen — une énigme de 800 ans, encore lue en partie seulement","it":"La scrittura jurchen — un enigma di 800 anni, ancora letto solo in parte","es":"La escritura jürchen — un enigma de 800 años que aún solo se lee en parte","pt":"A escrita jürchen — um enigma de 800 anos, ainda lido apenas em parte","ru":"Чжурчжэньское письмо — загадка длиной в 800 лет, прочитанная лишь отчасти","uk":"Чжурчженське письмо — загадка завдовжки 800 років, прочитана лише почасти","ar":"خط الجورشن — لغز عمره 800 عام لم يُقرأ منه سوى جزء","he":"כתב הג'ורצ'ן — חידה בת 800 שנה שעדיין נקראת רק בחלקה","sw":"Maandishi ya Jurchen — Kitendawili cha Miaka 800, Bado Kimesomeka kwa Sehemu Tu"},"summary":{"en":"The Jurchen script was created for the Jin dynasty in the twelfth century. A Ming glossary supports readings for many of its roughly 900 attested characters, but the small surviving corpus leaves the system only partly deciphered.","ja":"金朝のために12世紀に作られた女真文字は800年近く半ば忘れられていた。明代の語彙集を手がかりに、現代の学者は実際に確認できる約900字の大半について読みを確定させたが、現存資料はごく限られ、文字体系全体としては今なお部分的な解読にとどまる。","ko":"12세기 금나라를 위해 창제된 여진 문자는 800년간 반쯤 잊혀 있었다. 명대의 어휘집을 실마리로 현대 학자들은 실제로 확인되는 약 900자 대부분의 음가를 확정했지만, 현존 자료는 극히 적어 문자 체계 전체로 보면 아직 부분적인 해독에 머물러 있다.","zh":"这套12世纪为金朝创制的文字沉寂近800年。借助一部明代的对译词书，现代学者已为实际可见的约900字中的大多数确定了读音；但传世材料极其有限，整套文字至今仍只被部分解读。","yue":"呢套12世紀為金朝創製嘅文字沉寂咗近800年。靠住一部明代嘅對譯詞書，現代學者為實際見得到嘅約900字入面嘅大多數定咗讀音；但傳世材料極少，成套文字到而家仲只係解讀咗一部分。","vi":"Một hệ chữ viết thế kỷ 12 được tạo ra cho nhà Kim đã bị lãng quên gần 800 năm. Nhờ một bảng từ vựng đối chiếu thời Minh, các học giả hiện đại đã xác định được cách đọc của phần lớn trong khoảng 900 ký tự thực sự còn được ghi nhận — nhưng khối tư liệu còn lại rất ít ỏi, và toàn bộ hệ chữ đến nay vẫn chỉ được giải mã một phần.","th":"อักษรที่สร้างขึ้นในคริสต์ศตวรรษที่ 12 สำหรับราชวงศ์จิน ถูกลืมเลือนไปเกือบ 800 ปี — อาศัยประมวลศัพท์สองภาษาสมัยราชวงศ์หมิง นักวิชาการสมัยใหม่จึงกำหนดค่าการอ่านของตัวอักษรที่พบจริงราว 900 ตัวได้เกือบทั้งหมด แต่หลักฐานที่หลงเหลืออยู่มีน้อยมาก และระบบอักษรโดยรวมยังคงถอดรหัสได้เพียงบางส่วน","id":"Sebuah aksara abad ke-12 yang diciptakan untuk Dinasti Jin terlupakan selama 800 tahun. Berkat sebuah daftar kata dwibahasa era Ming, para sarjana modern berhasil menetapkan pelafalan sebagian besar dari sekitar 900 karakter yang benar-benar terdokumentasi — tetapi korpus yang bertahan sangat kecil, dan aksara ini secara keseluruhan baru terurai sebagian.","hi":"12वीं सदी में जिन राजवंश के लिए बनाई गई यह लिपि 800 वर्षों तक अर्ध-विस्मृत रही। मिंग कालीन एक द्विभाषी शब्द-सूची की मदद से आधुनिक विद्वानों ने वास्तव में प्रमाणित लगभग 900 अक्षरों में से अधिकांश के पाठ निर्धारित कर लिए हैं — किन्तु बची हुई सामग्री बेहद कम है, और समूची लिपि आज भी केवल आंशिक रूप से ही पढ़ी जा सकी है।","de":"Eine im 12. Jahrhundert für die Jin-Dynastie entwickelte Schrift lag fast 800 Jahre lang halb vergessen. Dank eines zweisprachigen Glossars aus der Ming-Zeit konnten moderne Forscher für die meisten der rund 900 tatsächlich bezeugten Zeichen Lesungen ermitteln — doch das erhaltene Korpus ist winzig, und die Schrift als Ganzes ist bis heute nur teilweise entziffert.","fr":"Créée au XIIe siècle pour la dynastie Jin, cette écriture fut largement oubliée pendant 800 ans. Grâce à un lexique bilingue des Ming, les spécialistes contemporains ont établi la lecture de la plupart des quelque 900 caractères réellement attestés — mais le corpus conservé est minuscule, et l'écriture dans son ensemble n'est encore déchiffrée qu'en partie.","it":"Ideata nel XII secolo per la dinastia Jin, questa scrittura rimase semidimenticata per 800 anni. Grazie a un glossario bilingue di epoca Ming gli studiosi moderni hanno stabilito la lettura della maggior parte dei circa 900 caratteri realmente attestati — ma il corpus superstite è minuscolo, e la scrittura nel suo insieme resta decifrata solo in parte.","es":"Creada en el siglo XII para la dinastía Jin, esta escritura quedó semiolvidada durante 800 años. Gracias a un glosario bilingüe de época Ming, los estudiosos modernos han establecido la lectura de la mayoría de los cerca de 900 caracteres realmente atestiguados, pero el corpus conservado es diminuto y la escritura en su conjunto sigue descifrada solo en parte.","pt":"Criada no século XII para a dinastia Jin, esta escrita ficou semiesquecida por 800 anos. Graças a um glossário bilíngue da dinastia Ming, estudiosos modernos estabeleceram a leitura da maior parte dos cerca de 900 caracteres de fato atestados — mas o corpus remanescente é minúsculo, e a escrita como um todo continua decifrada apenas em parte.","ru":"Созданное в XII веке для империи Цзинь письмо было почти забыто на 800 лет. Благодаря двуязычному словарю эпохи Мин современные учёные установили чтения для большинства из примерно 900 реально засвидетельствованных знаков, однако сохранившийся корпус ничтожно мал, и письмо в целом прочитано лишь отчасти.","uk":"Створене у XII столітті для держави Цзінь письмо було майже забуте на 800 років. Завдяки двомовному словнику доби Мін сучасні вчені встановили читання для більшості з приблизно 900 реально засвідчених знаків, однак збережений корпус украй малий, а письмо загалом прочитане лише почасти.","ar":"ظلّ هذا الخط المبتكر في القرن الثاني عشر لخدمة أسرة جين مهجوراً قرابة 800 عام، وبفضل معجم ثنائي اللغة من عهد مينغ تمكّن الباحثون المعاصرون من تحديد قراءة معظم الرموز الموثَّقة فعلياً وعددها نحو 900 رمز، غير أن ما بقي من نصوصه ضئيل للغاية، ولا يزال الخط في مجمله مقروءاً قراءة جزئية.","he":"כתב שנוצר במאה ה-12 עבור שושלת ג'ין שקע בשכחה כמעט 800 שנה — בעזרת מילון דו-לשוני מתקופת מינג קבעו חוקרים מודרניים את הקריאה של רוב הסימנים המתועדים בפועל, כ-900 במספר, אך מעט מאוד חומר שרד, והכתב בכללותו עדיין מפוענח רק בחלקו.","sw":"Maandishi yaliyoundwa katika karne ya 12 kwa ajili ya nasaba ya Jin yalisahauliwa karibu miaka 800 — kwa msaada wa orodha ya maneno ya lugha mbili ya enzi ya Ming, wasomi wa kisasa wamebaini matamshi ya herufi nyingi kati ya takriban 900 zilizothibitika kweli, lakini maandishi yaliyosalia ni machache mno, na mfumo mzima bado umefumbuliwa kwa sehemu tu."},"body":{"en":"<h3>A Script Born from Political Ambition</h3>\n<p>In 1115 the Jurchen chieftain <strong>Wanyan Aguda</strong> founded the Jin dynasty (金朝), and at first his court wrote in Khitan. An empire wanted a script of its own, so he commissioned <strong>Wanyan Xiyin</strong> (完顏希尹), a scholar-official fluent in Khitan, Chinese, and Jurchen, to create one. By 1119–1120 Xiyin had produced the <em>Jurchen large script</em> (女真大字), modelling its visual logic on the Khitan large script but adapting sounds and structures to fit the Tungusic phonology of Jurchen.</p>\n\n<div class=\"trivia-actions\">\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"panto\" data-lat=\"41.8\" data-lng=\"125.0\" data-zoom=\"5\">📍 Fly to Jurchen homeland (Manchuria)</button>\n</div>\n\n<p>Just nineteen years later, in 1138, a second and simpler system — the <em>Jurchen small script</em> (女真小字) — was promulgated by the <strong>Xizong Emperor</strong>, who is said to have devised it himself; the <em>Jin Shi</em> records its first official use in 1145. Scholars still debate which script appears in which inscription and how the two systems relate.</p>\n\n<h3>~900 Characters, Khitan DNA</h3>\n<p>Only about <strong>900 Jurchen characters</strong> are attested in the entire surviving corpus — Jīn Qǐzōng's standard 1984 dictionary <em>女真文辞典</em> has 890 primary entries, and the Sino-Jurchen glossary alone uses around 720. Some work logographically (one character = one morpheme, as in Chinese), others work phonetically (one character = one syllable sound), and many are borrowed Khitan graphs repurposed for Jurchen phonology. Because Jurchen is an <em>agglutinative</em> Tungusic language — very different from the isolating Chinese — the scribes had to stretch the logographic system in creative ways to mark verb suffixes and case endings.</p>\n\n<h3>The Ming Bilingual Dictionary That Saved the Script</h3>\n<p>When the Jin dynasty fell to the Mongols in 1234, Jurchen writing entered a slow eclipse, and slow is the word: as late as 1413, the Ming eunuch admiral Yishiha had a Jurchen text carved on the stele he raised at Tyr, on the lower Amur. The Manchus, as the Ming-era Jurchens renamed themselves in 1635, later wrote their language in a script adapted from the Mongolian alphabet, with no link to the old Jurchen characters. What saved Jurchen literacy from complete oblivion was a Ming-dynasty document: the <strong>女真譯語</strong> (<em>Nǚzhēn yìyǔ</em>, \"Jurchen Glossary\"), a bilingual word list pairing Jurchen characters with Chinese translations and Chinese-phonetic glosses. This dictionary — copied, re-copied, and preserved almost by accident — became the Rosetta Stone of Jurchen decipherment.</p>\n\n<h3>Daniel Kane and the 800-Year Puzzle</h3>\n<p>The pioneering modern study was the German sinologist Wilhelm Grube's <em>Die Sprache und Schrift der Jučen</em> (1896), built on the Ming glossary. Scholars such as Kiyose Gisaburō in Japan and Jin Qizong in China carried the work forward, and the Australian linguist <strong>Daniel Kane</strong>'s 1989 monograph <em>The Sino-Jurchen Vocabulary of the Bureau of Interpreters</em> established rigorous phonological reconstructions. By cross-referencing the 女真譯語 with stone inscriptions, bronze seals, and the comparative Tungusic vocabulary of later Manchu and Evenki, Kane and subsequent scholars have established readings for most of the seven-hundred-odd characters actually attested in the glossaries. Beyond that core the script is still only partly decoded: the surviving corpus is tiny — nine known inscriptions and the Ming word lists — and many graphs appear in too few contexts to pin down with certainty.</p>\n\n<div class=\"trivia-actions\">\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"focus\" data-code=\"juc\">📍 Focus Jurchen on the map</button>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Why It Matters</h3>\n<p>Jurchen sits at the pivot of East Asian history. Its speakers founded a dynasty that ruled northern China for a century, repelled the Song, and shaped the political geography that the Mongols would later inherit. The script has no graphic descendants, but its scribal tradition foreshadowed the Manchu script, which in turn became the official writing of the Qing dynasty (1644–1912) and survives today both ceremonially — on the bilingual signs inside the Forbidden City — and as a living written language in the closely related Sibe script of Qapqal Xibe Autonomous County, Xinjiang. Deciphering Jurchen is not mere antiquarianism; it fills a century-long gap in the documentary record of northern China and clarifies how Tungusic peoples built literate bureaucracies on Chinese administrative models without surrendering their own linguistic identity.</p>\n\n<blockquote>Few writing systems have been asked to do anything as awkward as fitting an agglutinative Tungusic language into Chinese-style graphs.</blockquote>"},"sources":["Kane, Daniel (1989). The Sino-Jurchen Vocabulary of the Bureau of Interpreters. Indiana University Research Institute for Inner Asian Studies.","Jin Qizong 金启孮 (1984). 女真文辞典 [Jurchen Dictionary]. Wenwu Chubanshe, Beijing.","Janhunen, Juha (2012). 'Khitan: Understanding the language behind the scripts.' SCRIPTA 4: 107–132.","Kiyose, Gisaburo N. (1977). A Study of the Jurchen Language and Script. Kyoto: Horitsubunka-sha.",{"text":"Glottolog: Jurchen (jurc1239)","url":"https://glottolog.org/resource/languoid/id/jurc1239"},{"text":"Wikipedia: Jurchen script","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jurchen_script"},{"text":"Grube, Wilhelm (1896). Die Sprache und Schrift der Jučen. Leipzig: Otto Harrassowitz.","url":"https://archive.org/details/bub_gb_4WouAAAAYAAJ"}]},{"id":"yale-mc-tonal-categories","icon":"🎵","tags":["tones","middle-chinese","cantonese","sinitic","historical-phonology"],"title":{"en":"From Four Tone Categories to Modern Sinitic Tone Systems","ja":"4声が9声に — 中古漢語が分裂した声調の森","ko":"4성조가 9성조로 — 중고 한어가 분열한 성조의 숲","zh":"四声变九声：中古汉语声调如何分裂为今日之貌","yue":"四聲變九聲：中古漢語聲調點樣分裂成今日咁多","vi":"4 Thanh Thành 9: Trung Cổ Hán Ngữ Phân Tách Thành Hệ Thanh Điệu Hôm Nay","th":"4 วรรณยุกต์กลายเป็น 9: ภาษาจีนกลางโบราณแตกแขนงสู่ระบบวรรณยุกต์ปัจจุบัน","id":"4 Nada Menjadi 9: Bagaimana Bahasa Tionghoa Pertengahan Terpecah Menjadi Hutan Nada","hi":"4 स्वर से 9 स्वर: मध्य-चीनी से आज के स्वर-वनों तक","de":"Aus 4 werden 9 Töne: Wie das Mittelchinesische sich in heutige Tonsysteme aufspaltete","fr":"De 4 tons à 9 : comment le chinois médiéval s'est fragmenté en forêts tonales","it":"Da 4 a 9 toni: come il cinese medievale si è diviso nelle foreste tonali odierne","es":"De 4 a 9 tonos: cómo el chino medieval se fragmentó en los bosques tonales de hoy","pt":"De 4 a 9 Tons: Como o Chinês Médio se Fragmentou nas Florestas Tonais Atuais","ru":"Из 4 тонов в 9: как среднекитайский распался на современные тональные леса","uk":"З 4 тонів на 9: як середньокитайська розпалася на сучасні тональні ліси","ar":"من 4 نغمات إلى 9: كيف تفرّعت الصينية الوسيطة إلى غابات نغمية حديثة","he":"מ-4 טונים ל-9: כיצד הסינית התיכונה התפצלה ליערות הטונים של היום","sw":"Toni 4 Kuwa 9: Jinsi Kichina cha Kati Kilivyogawanyika katika Misitu ya Toni za Leo"},"summary":{"en":"Middle Chinese had four tone categories. Subsequent splits and mergers, including those associated with the voicing of initial consonants, produced the modern tone systems of Mandarin, Cantonese, and Min Nan.","ja":"中古漢語の声調はわずか4カテゴリだったが、有声か無声かというたった一つの音韻ルールがそれを倍増・三倍化し、現代の北京語4声・広東語9声・閩南語7声という多様な体系を生み出した。","ko":"중고 한어의 성조는 단 4범주였지만, 유성 대 무성 초성이라는 하나의 음운 규칙이 그것을 2배, 3배로 늘려 오늘날 보통화 4성·광동어 9성·민남어 7성의 다양한 체계를 낳았다.","zh":"中古汉语只有四个声调类别，但清浊声母这一规则使其加倍、三倍分裂，形成了今天普通话4声、粤语9声、闽南语7声等各异的声调体系。","yue":"中古漢語得四個聲調類別，但清濁聲母呢一條規則令其加倍、三倍咁分裂，造就咗今日普通話4聲、粵語9聲、閩南語7聲等各異嘅聲調體系。","vi":"Tiếng Hán trung cổ chỉ có 4 phạm trù thanh điệu — nhưng một quy tắc âm vị học duy nhất (phụ âm đầu hữu thanh vs. vô thanh) khiến chúng nhân đôi, nhân ba, tạo ra các hệ thống 4, 9 và 7 thanh điệu của tiếng Quan Thoại, Quảng Đông và Mân Nam ngày nay.","th":"ภาษาจีนกลางโบราณมีเพียง 4 ประเภทวรรณยุกต์ — แต่กฎเสียงเดียว (พยัญชนะต้นที่ก้องเสียงกับไม่ก้องเสียง) ทำให้มันเพิ่มเป็นสองเท่า สามเท่า กลายเป็นระบบ 4, 9 และ 7 วรรณยุกต์ของภาษาจีนกลาง กวางตุ้ง และหมิ่นหนานในปัจจุบัน","id":"Bahasa Tionghoa Pertengahan hanya memiliki 4 kategori nada — tetapi satu aturan fonologis (konsonan awal bersuara vs tak bersuara) menyebabkannya berlipat ganda menjadi sistem 4, 9, dan 7 nada Mandarin, Kanton, dan Min Nan hari ini.","hi":"मध्य-चीनी में केवल 4 स्वर श्रेणियाँ थीं — लेकिन एक ध्वनि-विज्ञान नियम (सघोष बनाम अघोष प्रारंभिक व्यंजन) ने उन्हें दोगुना, तिगुना कर आज के मंदारिन 4-स्वर, कैंटोनीज़ 9-स्वर और मिन नान 7-स्वर तंत्र बनाए।","de":"Das Mittelchinesische kannte nur 4 Tonkategorien — doch eine einzige phonologische Regel (stimmhafte vs. stimmlose Anlautkonsonanten) ließ sie sich verdoppeln, verdreifachen und in die heutigen Systeme mit 4, 9 und 7 Tönen des Mandarin, Kantonesischen und Min Nan aufspalten.","fr":"Le chinois médiéval ne comptait que 4 catégories tonales — mais une unique règle phonologique (consonnes initiales sonores vs sourdes) les a fait doubler, tripler, engendrant les systèmes à 4, 9 et 7 tons du mandarin, du cantonais et du min nan d'aujourd'hui.","it":"Il cinese medievale aveva solo 4 categorie tonali — ma un'unica regola fonologica (consonanti iniziali sonore vs sorde) le ha fatte raddoppiare, triplicare, generando i sistemi a 4, 9 e 7 toni del mandarino, cantonese e min nan odierni.","es":"El chino medieval solo tenía 4 categorías tonales — pero una única regla fonológica (consonantes iniciales sonoras frente a sordas) las hizo doblarse, triplicarse y dividirse en los sistemas de 4, 9 y 7 tonos del mandarín, cantonés y min nan actuales.","pt":"O chinês medieval tinha apenas 4 categorias tonais — mas uma única regra fonológica (consoantes iniciais sonoras vs. surdas) fez com que se dobrassem, triplicassem e dividissem nos sistemas de 4, 9 e 7 tons do mandarim, cantonês e min nan atuais.","ru":"В среднекитайском было лишь 4 тональных категории — но одно фонологическое правило (звонкие vs глухие начальные согласные) удвоило, утроило их, породив современные системы из 4, 9 и 7 тонов в мандаринском, кантонском и миньнань.","uk":"У середньокитайській було лише 4 тональні категорії — але одне фонологічне правило (дзвінкі vs глухі початкові приголосні) подвоїло, потроїло їх, створивши сучасні системи з 4, 9 і 7 тонів у мандаринській, кантонській та мінь-нань.","ar":"لم يكن في الصينية الوسيطة سوى 4 فئات نغمية — لكن قاعدة صوتية واحدة (الأصوات الابتدائية المجهورة مقابل المهموسة) جعلتها تتضاعف وتتثلّث، لتُفضي إلى أنظمة من 4 و9 و7 نغمات في الماندرين والكانتونية والمين نان اليوم.","he":"לסינית התיכונה היו רק 4 קטגוריות טוניות — אך כלל פונולוגי אחד (עיצורים ראשוניים עצורים לעומת קוליים) הכפיל ושילש אותן, ויצר את מערכות ארבעת, תשעת ושבעת הטונים של המנדרינית, הקנטונית והמין-נן של היום.","sw":"Kichina cha Kati kilikuwa na makundi 4 tu ya toni — lakini kanuni moja ya kifonolojia (konsonanti za awali zenye sauti dhidi ya zisizo na sauti) ilizifanya ziwe mara mbili, tatu, na kusababisha mifumo ya toni 4, 9 na 7 ya Mandarin, Kikantoni, na Min Nan leo."},"sources":["Norman, Jerry (1988). Chinese. Cambridge Language Surveys. Cambridge University Press.","Pulleyblank, Edwin G. (1984). Middle Chinese: A Study in Historical Phonology. UBC Press.","Yue-Hashimoto, Anne O. (1972). Studies in Yue Dialects, Vol. 1: Phonology of Cantonese. Cambridge University Press.","Mei Tsu-lin (1970). Tones and prosody in Middle Chinese and the origin of the rising tone. Harvard Journal of Asiatic Studies 30: 86–110.",{"text":"STEDT: Sino-Tibetan Etymological Dictionary","url":"https://stedt.berkeley.edu/"},{"text":"Wikipedia: Four tones (Middle Chinese)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Four_tones_(Middle_Chinese)"},{"text":"Wikipedia: Taiwanese Hokkien — tone sandhi","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taiwanese_Hokkien#Tone_sandhi"},{"text":"Wikipedia: Yale romanization of Cantonese — tones","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yale_romanization_of_Cantonese"}],"body":{"en":"<h3>The Four Ancestors</h3>\n<p>Around the 7th century CE — the era of the <em>Qieyun</em> 切韻 rime dictionary (compiled in 601 under the Sui) and of the Tang dynasty that made it the standard — Chinese had four tone categories: <strong>平 (píng, level), 上 (shǎng, rising), 去 (qù, departing), and 入 (rù, entering)</strong>. The <em>entering tone</em> (入声) was not a pitch shape but a syllable structure category: all its syllables ended in a short stop consonant (-p, -t, or -k). These four categories held all the syllables of Middle Chinese.</p>\n\n<div class=\"trivia-actions\">\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"focus\" data-code=\"yue_hk\">📍 Focus Cantonese (Hong Kong) on map</button>\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"focus\" data-code=\"zh\">📍 Focus Mandarin on map</button>\n</div>\n\n<h3>The Voicing Split — One Rule to Rule Them All</h3>\n<p>Middle Chinese had a rich system of initial consonants, many of which came in voiced/voiceless pairs: <em>p-</em> vs <em>b-</em>, <em>t-</em> vs <em>d-</em>, <em>k-</em> vs <em>g-</em>, etc. Over the following centuries, voiced initials disappeared or merged differently in each dialect, but not before leaving a lasting imprint: syllables with originally <strong>voiced initials developed lower pitch</strong>, while those with voiceless initials developed higher pitch. This is the <em>Yīn-Yáng split</em> (阴阳分化). (In some dialects the pitches later flipped round, so many linguists call the two registers \"dark\" (yin) and \"light\" (yang) rather than \"upper\" and \"lower\".)</p>\n\n<p>The result is mechanical but spectacular: each of the original four tones split into a <strong>Yin (阴/upper) register</strong> and a <strong>Yang (阳/lower) register</strong>, simply based on what kind of consonant the syllable used to start with. Four tones × two registers = eight potential tone slots. Dialects then merged some slots, split others differently, and the final count diverges wildly across the Sinitic family.</p>\n\n<h3>The Cantonese Case: 9 Tones (or 6+3)</h3>\n<p>Cantonese is the canonical showcase. The six <em>live tones</em> (陰平, 陽平, 陰上, 陽上, 陰去, 陽去) cover the old 平/上/去 categories after the Yin-Yang split — <strong>6 contour tones</strong> with distinct pitch shapes. But Cantonese also preserved the entering tone (入声). The Yin-Yang split gave it two checked tones, and a later Yue-internal split of the upper one — conditioned by vowel length, not by the old initial — added a third, yielding <strong>3 checked tones</strong> (陰入, 中入, 陽入) with the same pitch heights as the upper, mid, and lower live tones but cut short by a final stop. The traditional count is therefore <strong>9 tones</strong>, but the romanisations you meet in daily life do not write nine. Yale writes seven (it keeps a high falling tone apart from high level) but spells a checked syllable exactly like the high-flat, mid-flat or low-flat tone whose pitch it shares; Jyutping, the system used on this map, also numbers 1–6 only (一 <em>jat1</em>, 目 <em>muk6</em>). The digits 7, 8 and 9 for 陰入/中入/陽入 live on in traditional tone charts and in the Cantonese Pinyin (教院式) system — bookkeeping labels for the history rather than three extra pitches.</p>\n\n<div class=\"trivia-actions\">\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"focus\" data-code=\"yue_hk\">🎵 See Cantonese tonal romanisation</button>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Mandarin: 4 Tones, the Entering Tone Vanished</h3>\n<p>Standard Mandarin simplified dramatically. The voiced initials disappeared early and thoroughly; the level tone split in two, but the other registers merged, and old rising-tone words with voiced stop or fricative initials moved wholesale into the departing tone (上 <em>shàng</em> itself is one of them, 4th tone today). The entering tone (入声) lost its final stops and its syllables were redistributed. Much of that was regular: words with voiced stop initials went to the 2nd tone (十 <em>shí</em>, 白 <em>bái</em>) and those with nasal or l- initials to the 4th (六 <em>liù</em>, 木 <em>mù</em>). Only words with voiceless initials were scattered across all four tones with no rule, the legacy of dialect mixing in the capital, and they are what makes old 入声 characters so hard to spot in Mandarin. The result: a clean 4-tone system (阴平, 阳平, 上声, 去声), the <em>sì shēng</em> 四声 that every Chinese learner memorises.</p>\n\n<h3>Min Nan: 7 or 8 Tones, Entering Tone Alive and Doubled</h3>\n<p>Hokkien/Taiwanese (闽南语, Min Nan) preserved the entering tone with its final stops intact (-p, -t, -k). Like Cantonese, it applied the Yin-Yang split to the entering tone, giving 2 entering tones (陰入 and 陽入). The live tones underwent further mergers and splits, yielding a base of <strong>7 tones</strong> in Standard Southern Min. Min Nan is also famous for its <em>sandhi</em> system: in natural speech, every syllable except the last in a phrase switches to a different tone. The switch is largely regular, a chain learners memorise as a circle (in Taipei speech tone 1 becomes 7, 7 becomes 3, 3 becomes 2, 2 becomes 1), so what has to be learned is one rule set rather than two tones per word.</p>\n\n<h3>Hakka, Wu, and the Full Spectrum</h3>\n<p>Hakka (客家话) dialects typically have 5–7 tones, and most keep the entering tone with its -p, -t, -k (十 is <em>sṳ̍p</em> in Hakka church romanisation). Wu (吴语, including Shanghainese) is the best-known group, along with Old Xiang, still to keep the three-way split of the old consonants, so the Yin-Yang split can still be heard. Conservative southern Wu such as Wenzhou still counts eight, though Shanghainese itself has thinned to about five — the 1–8 digits on this map's northern Wu rows are Wugniu tone categories, not eight separate pitches. One caution: in northern Wu, Shanghainese included, the \"voiced\" consonants are typically pronounced with breathy voice, or \"murmur\", which also drags the pitch of the whole syllable down. So Wu shows where the old voicing contrast went more than exactly how it sounded.</p>\n\n<p>You can watch the whole story in one word. 十 \"ten\" was an entering-tone syllable ending in -p. Cantonese still says <em>sap6</em>, Hokkien <em>tsa̍p</em> and Hakka <em>sṳ̍p</em>, all with the stop intact. Mandarin <em>shí</em> lost the -p, and because the word began with a voiced consonant it landed, by rule, in the 2nd tone. Japanese borrowed it too: today it is <em>jū</em>, but the old kana spelling じふ (<em>jifu</em>, from an earlier <em>-pu</em>) still hides the -p.</p>"}},{"id":"old-chinese-baxter-sagart","icon":"🪨","tags":["old-chinese","reconstruction","history"],"title":{"en":"Old Chinese Reconstruction: From Karlgren to Baxter-Sagart 2014","ja":"上古漢語の再建：カールグレンからバクスター＝サガールまで","ko":"상고 중국어 재건: 칼그렌에서 백스터-사가르까지","zh":"上古汉语重建：从高本汉到白一平-沙加尔","yue":"上古漢語嘅重建：從高本漢到白一平同沙加爾","vi":"Tái hiện tiếng Hán cổ: từ Karlgren đến Baxter-Sagart 2014","th":"การสร้างภาษาจีนโบราณ: จากคาร์ลเกรนสู่แบ็กซ์เตอร์-ซาการ์ต 2014","id":"Rekonstruksi Bahasa Tionghoa Kuno: Dari Karlgren hingga Baxter-Sagart 2014","hi":"प्राचीन चीनी भाषा का पुनर्निर्माण: कार्लग्रेन से बैक्सटर-सागार्ट 2014 तक","de":"Alttchinesisch-Rekonstruktion: Von Karlgren bis Baxter-Sagart 2014","fr":"Reconstruction du chinois archaïque : de Karlgren à Baxter-Sagart 2014","it":"Ricostruzione del cinese arcaico: da Karlgren a Baxter-Sagart 2014","es":"Reconstrucción del chino arcaico: de Karlgren a Baxter-Sagart 2014","pt":"Reconstrução do chinês arcaico: de Karlgren a Baxter-Sagart 2014","ru":"Реконструкция древнекитайского: от Карлгрена до Бакстера-Сагара 2014","uk":"Реконструкція давньокитайської: від Карлгрена до Бакстера-Сагара 2014","ar":"إعادة بناء الصينية القديمة: من كارلغرن إلى باكستر-ساغار 2014","he":"שחזור הסינית העתיקה: מקרלגרן לבקסטר-סאגר 2014","sw":"Ujenzi upya wa Kichina cha Kale: kutoka Karlgren hadi Baxter-Sagart 2014"},"summary":{"en":"Reconstructions of Old Chinese combine oracle-bone inscriptions, rhyme evidence and phonetic series. In Baxter–Sagart's system, 一 is reconstructed as *ʔi[t].","ja":"甲骨文字と韻図を百年かけて解読した結果、上古漢語の「一」は*ʔi[t]、「山」は*s-ŋrarと読まれるようになった。","ko":"갑골문과 운도를 100년간 연구한 끝에 상고 중국어 '一'은 *ʔi[t], '山'은 *s-ŋrar로 재건되었다.","zh":"历经百年对甲骨文与韵图的研究，上古汉语「一」已重建为*ʔi[t]，「山」为*s-ŋrar。","yue":"學者花咗成百年時間研究甲骨文同韻圖，終於將上古漢語嘅「一」重建為*ʔi[t]，「山」重建為*s-ŋrar。","vi":"Một thế kỷ nghiên cứu xương giáp cốt và bảng vần đã giúp tái hiện tiếng Hán cổ: 一 là *ʔi[t], 山 là *s-ŋrar.","th":"การวิจัยอักษรกระดูกเต่าและตารางสัมผัสนานกว่าศตวรรษ ทำให้ 一 ถูกสร้างใหม่เป็น *ʔi[t] และ 山 เป็น *s-ŋrar","id":"Satu abad riset tulang oracle dan tabel rima menghasilkan rekonstruksi: 一 menjadi *ʔi[t], 山 menjadi *s-ŋrar.","hi":"प्राचीन चीनी के पुनर्निर्माण ऑरेकल-बोन शिलालेखों, तुकांत-प्रमाणों और ध्वन्यात्मक श्रृंखलाओं को मिलाकर बनते हैं। बैक्सटर–सागार्ट प्रणाली में 一 का पुनर्निर्मित रूप *ʔi[t] है।","de":"Ein Jahrhundert Forschung an Orakelknochen und Reimtabellen ergab: 一 lautet *ʔi[t], 山 lautet *s-ŋrar.","fr":"Un siècle de déchiffrage des os oraculaires et des tables de rimes a abouti à : 一 = *ʔi[t], 山 = *s-ŋrar.","it":"Un secolo di ricerche sulle ossa oracolari e le tavole delle rime: 一 si legge *ʔi[t], 山 si legge *s-ŋrar.","es":"Un siglo de investigación sobre huesos oraculares y tablas de rima reconstruyó: 一 = *ʔi[t], 山 = *s-ŋrar.","pt":"Um século de pesquisa sobre ossos oraculares e tabelas de rima reconstruiu: 一 = *ʔi[t], 山 = *s-ŋrar.","ru":"Век исследований гадательных костей и таблиц рифм дал результат: 一 = *ʔi[t], 山 = *s-ŋrar.","uk":"Сторіччя досліджень ворожильних кісток і таблиць рим дало: 一 = *ʔi[t], 山 = *s-ŋrar.","ar":"قرن من البحث في عظام العرافة وجداول القوافي أسفر عن: 一 = *ʔi[t] و 山 = *s-ŋrar.","he":"מאה שנות מחקר בעצמות נבואה ובטבלאות חרוז הניבו: 一 = *ʔi[t], 山 = *s-ŋrar.","sw":"Karne moja ya utafiti wa mifupa ya unabii na meza za vina imezalisha: 一 = *ʔi[t], 山 = *s-ŋrar."},"body":{"en":"<h3>The Oldest Chinese We Can Hear</h3><p>Old Chinese (OC) is the ancestor of all Sinitic languages, spoken roughly 1250–200 BCE — the era of the <em>Shijing</em> (詩經) and oracle-bone inscriptions. No one recorded its sounds directly. Linguists reconstruct it by working backwards. The starting point is Middle Chinese (MC), the sound system recorded in the rhyme dictionary <em>Qieyun</em> of 601 CE. To that they add the rhymes of the <em>Shijing</em> songs, the sound clues built into characters (\"phonetic series\"), the Min dialects of the south-east, which preserve some older distinctions, early loanwords in neighbouring languages, and comparison with related Sino-Tibetan languages.</p><h3>Karlgren's Trailblazing, and Its Limits</h3><p>In <em>Grammata Serica</em> (1940), Swedish sinologist Bernhard Karlgren (高本漢) produced the first systematic OC reconstruction dictionary, built on the Middle Chinese system of his earlier <em>Analytic Dictionary of Chinese and Sino-Japanese</em> (1923). He used MC as his pivot and extracted proto-forms like *mi̯wən for 文. His framework was a breakthrough, but later scholars found two major limits. He built his system on <em>phonetic series</em> (the component shared by a cluster of characters hints at their shared OC initial), but bound them with a rigid <em>homorganic principle</em> — every character in a series had to share a place of articulation — which forced ad hoc patches wherever a series refused to comply; and his vowel system was under-differentiated.</p><h3>The Cluster Revolution</h3><p>Characters sharing a phonetic element (<em>xíngshēng</em> 形聲) sometimes have radically different MC initials: 各 (MC kak) shares its phonetic with 路 (MC luH). The usual explanation is that OC had consonant <em>clusters</em> or prefixes that later simplified differently in different words. Karlgren himself already proposed clusters with *-l- for such cases; later scholars pushed the idea much further. Pulleyblank (1962) also argued that the Chinese rising tone came from a final glottal stop, one of the key steps in explaining how a toneless OC became tonal. Baxter's 1992 monograph <em>A Handbook of Old Chinese Phonology</em> rigorously formalized a cluster system that resolved hundreds of these puzzles. Baxter and Sagart's later answer for 路 shows how far the analysis has moved: not a cluster but a loosely attached presyllable plus a suffix, *Cə.rˤak-s, beside 各 *kˤak.</p><h3>Baxter-Sagart 2014</h3><p>The landmark <em>Old Chinese: A New Reconstruction</em> (2014) added Laurent Sagart's morphological dimension. Key innovations:</p><ul><li><strong>Sesquisyllables</strong>: OC had minor syllable prefixes (like Mon-Khmer languages today) — e.g., 水 'water' *s.turʔ, where the dot in <em>s.</em> marks a loosely attached presyllable, not part of the main syllable. A hyphen marks the opposite: in 山 *s-ŋrar the <em>s-</em> is fused tightly to the root initial.</li><li><strong>Morphological prefixes/suffixes</strong>: for example *s- forming causatives, *N- forming intransitive or stative verbs, and *-s, which among other jobs derived nouns from verbs. Pairs of related words written with one character, read in different tones today, are often traces of this lost morphology.</li><li><strong>Pharyngealization</strong>: the long-disputed Type A/B syllable split is realised here as pharyngealization of the main-syllable initial — Jerry Norman's 1994 proposal, which Baxter and Sagart adopted and extended to every Type A syllable (五 *C.ŋˤaʔ).</li></ul><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"一\">Set character: 一</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"山\">Set character: 山</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"focus\" data-code=\"pst\">Focus: Proto-Sino-Tibetan</button></div><h3>Reading Two Characters</h3><p>Take <strong>一</strong> 'one': OC *ʔi[t] → MC ʔjit (Baxter-Sagart). The glottal stop initial was already present in OC; Mandarin <em>yī</em> has lost both the glottal stop and the final <em>-t</em>, while Cantonese <em>jat</em> still keeps the <em>-t</em>. Take <strong>山</strong> 'mountain': OC *s-ŋrar — the <em>s-</em> preinitial together with the medial *-r- yielded the Middle Chinese retroflex sibilant sr- (ʂ-), the <em>sh-</em> of modern <em>shān</em> 山, while the root initial *ŋ- was lost. Its relatives keep the *ŋ- visible: Baxter and Sagart reconstruct 顏 'face' as *C.ŋˤrar.</p><h3>Open Questions</h3><p>Despite the 2014 system's elegance, debates continue. How many vowels did OC have — 4, 6, or more? Were the presyllables true syllables or mere consonantal prefixes? How do Tibetan and Burmese cognates constrain the vowel system? And fundamentally: how far back can the comparative method reach before Chinese characters' logographic nature obscures the sound history entirely?</p>"},"sources":["Baxter, W. H. & Sagart, L. (2014). Old Chinese: A New Reconstruction. Oxford University Press.","Baxter, W. H. (1992). A Handbook of Old Chinese Phonology. Mouton de Gruyter.","Karlgren, B. (1923). Analytic Dictionary of Chinese and Sino-Japanese. Paul Geuthner.","Pulleyblank, E. G. (1962). The consonantal system of Old Chinese. Asia Major 9, 58–144.",{"text":"Baxter-Sagart Old Chinese reconstruction database (2014)","url":"https://sites.lsa.umich.edu/ocbaxtersagart/"}]},{"id":"sino-tibetan-cognates","icon":"🏔","tags":["sino-tibetan","cognates","etymology"],"title":{"en":"Sino-Tibetan Cognates: How 五 and Tibetan ལྔ Share the Same Root","ja":"漢蔵語族の同源語：なぜ「五」とチベット語ལྔは同じ語根を持つのか","ko":"한장어족 동원어: 五와 티베트어 ལྔ는 어떻게 같은 어근을 공유하는가","zh":"汉藏同源词：汉语\"五\"与藏语ལྔ如何共享同一词根","yue":"漢藏同源詞：「五」同藏語ལྔ點解係同一語根？","vi":"Từ đồng nguyên Hán-Tạng: Tại sao 五 và ལྔ tiếng Tạng cùng gốc","th":"คำร่วมเชื้อสายจีน-ทิเบต: ทำไม 五 กับ ལྔ ในภาษาทิเบตมีรากเดียวกัน","id":"Kognat Sino-Tibet: Bagaimana 五 dan ལྔ Bahasa Tibet Berbagi Akar yang Sama","hi":"चीनी-तिब्बती समजातीय शब्द: 五 और तिब्बती ལྔ एक ही मूल से कैसे आते हैं","de":"Sino-tibetische Kognaten: Wie 五 und das tibetische ལྔ denselben Ursprung teilen","fr":"Cognats sino-tibétains : comment 五 et le tibétain ལྔ partagent la même racine","it":"Cognati sino-tibetani: come 五 e il tibetano ལྔ condividono la stessa radice","es":"Cognados sino-tibetanos: cómo 五 y el tibetano ལྔ comparten la misma raíz","pt":"Cognatos sino-tibetanos: como 五 e o tibetano ལྔ compartilham a mesma raiz","ru":"Сино-тибетские когнаты: как 五 и тибетское ལྔ восходят к одному корню","uk":"Сіно-тибетські когнати: як 五 і тибетське ལྔ мають спільний корінь","ar":"المعرّفات الصينية-التيبتية: كيف تشترك 五 والتيبتية ལྔ في الجذر ذاته","he":"קוגנטים סינו-טיבטים: כיצד 五 והטיבטית ལྔ חולקות את אותו שורש","sw":"Maneno Yanayofanana ya Sino-Tibet: Jinsi 五 na ལྔ ya Kitibeti Yanavyoshiriki Mzizi Mmoja"},"summary":{"en":"Regular correspondences among Mandarin wǔ, Tibetan lnga, and Burmese nga support the reconstruction of Proto-Sino-Tibetan *ŋā 'five'.","ja":"北京語の「wǔ」、チベット語の「lnga」、ビルマ語の「nga」——三つの形の規則的な対応が、原始漢蔵語の*ŋā（五）の再建を支えている。","ko":"원시 한장어 *ŋā '다섯'은 북경어 wǔ, 티베트어 lnga, 버마어 nga로 살아남았다 — 수천 년을 가로지르는 하나의 조상어의 세 얼굴.","zh":"原始汉藏语*ŋā（五）在普通话中变为wǔ，在藏语中变为lnga，在缅甸语中变为nga——跨越5000年的同一词根的三张面孔。","yue":"原始漢藏語嘅*ŋā（五）喺普通話係wǔ，喺藏語係lnga，喺緬甸語係nga——橫跨五千年嘅同一語根三張面孔。","vi":"Proto Hán-Tạng *ŋā 'năm' còn sống trong tiếng Quan Thoại wǔ, tiếng Tạng lnga, tiếng Miến nga — ba gương mặt của cùng một từ tổ tiên.","th":"รากภาษาโปรโต จีน-ทิเบต *ŋā 'ห้า' ยังมีชีวิตอยู่ในภาษาจีนกลาง wǔ ทิเบต lnga และพม่า nga — สามหน้าของคำเดียวยาวนาน 5,000 ปี","id":"Proto-Sino-Tibet *ŋā 'lima' bertahan sebagai Mandarin wǔ, Tibet lnga, dan Burma nga — tiga wajah satu kata leluhur sepanjang 5.000 tahun.","hi":"आदि-चीनी-तिब्बती *ŋā 'पाँच' मंदारिन wǔ, तिब्बती lnga और बर्मी nga के रूप में जीवित है — 5000 वर्षों में एक पैतृक शब्द के तीन रूप।","de":"Proto-Sino-Tibetisch *ŋā 'fünf' lebt fort als Mandarin wǔ, Tibetisch lnga und Birmanisch nga — drei Gesichter eines Urwortes über 5.000 Jahre.","fr":"Les correspondances régulières entre le mandarin wǔ, le tibétain lnga et le birman nga étayent la reconstruction du proto-sino-tibétain *ŋā « cinq » — trois visages d'un même mot ancestral.","it":"Le corrispondenze regolari tra il mandarino wǔ, il tibetano lnga e il birmano nga sostengono la ricostruzione del proto-sino-tibetano *ŋā 'cinque'.","es":"El proto-sino-tibetano *ŋā 'cinco' sobrevive como mandarín wǔ, tibetano lnga y birmano nga — tres caras de una misma palabra ancestral, unidas por correspondencias fonéticas regulares.","pt":"O proto-sino-tibetano *ŋā 'cinco' sobrevive como mandarim wǔ, tibetano lnga e birmanês nga — três faces de uma palavra ancestral ao longo de 5.000 anos.","ru":"Регулярные соответствия между мандаринским wǔ, тибетским lnga и бирманским nga подтверждают реконструкцию протосино-тибетского *ŋā «пять».","uk":"Протосинотибетське *ŋā «п'ять» збереглося як мандаринське wǔ, тибетське lnga і бірманське nga — три обличчя одного прослова за 5000 років.","ar":"البروتو-صيني-التيبتي *ŋā «خمسة» بقي بوصفه المندرين wǔ والتيبتية lnga والبورمية nga — ثلاثة وجوه لكلمة أصلية عبر 5000 عام.","he":"הפרוטו-סינו-טיבטי *ŋā 'חמש' שרד בתור מנדרינית wǔ, טיבטית lnga ובורמית nga — שלושה פנים של מילה אחת אבות לאורך 5,000 שנה.","sw":"Proto-Sino-Tibet *ŋā 'tano' imeendelea kuwa Mandarin wǔ, Kitibeti lnga, na Kiburma nga — nyuso tatu za neno moja la babu kwa miaka 5,000."},"body":{"en":"<h3>A Family Reunited Across Millennia</h3><p>The Sino-Tibetan language family — encompassing Chinese, Tibetan, Burmese, and hundreds of smaller languages — has around 1.4 billion speakers, second only to Indo-European. Yet compared to Indo-European, it remains poorly understood at the proto-language level, partly because Chinese characters record meaning far better than sound, and partly because the family's internal classification is still debated.</p><p>The most powerful evidence linking Chinese to its relatives is <em>vocabulary cognates</em>: words descending independently from a common ancestral word, with sound changes that follow predictable rules.</p><h3>The Number Five: *ŋā Across Three Branches</h3><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"五\">Set character: 五</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"focus\" data-code=\"pst\">Focus: Proto-Sino-Tibetan</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"focus\" data-code=\"bo_sino\">Focus: Sino-Tibetan Tibetan layer</button></div><p>The Proto-Sino-Tibetan (PST) root for 'five' is reconstructed as <strong>*ŋā</strong>. Here is how it surfaces across branches:</p><ul><li><strong>Chinese</strong>: Old Chinese *C.ŋˤaʔ (pharyngealized, with glottal suffix) → Middle Chinese *ŋuX → Mandarin <em>wǔ</em> 五. The initial *ŋ- was simply lost: the 疑 initial merged into Mandarin's zero initial, and the <em>w</em> of <em>wǔ</em> is only pinyin's way of writing a syllable that begins with <em>u</em>. The same loss gives 銀 <em>yín</em> and 我 <em>wǒ</em>, whichever way the vowel rounds.</li><li><strong>Tibetan</strong>: <em>lnga</em> (ལྔ). The l- is a prefix, not part of the root; the core is <em>ŋa</em>, directly matching the PST root.</li><li><strong>Burmese</strong>: <em>ngā</em> (ငါး). The initial ŋ- is preserved without the prefix complication, and the vowel directly reflects *ā.</li></ul><h3>The Number Four: *li(j) Across Three Branches</h3><p>The root for 'four' reconstructed as <strong>*li(j)</strong> shows an equally striking convergence:</p><ul><li><strong>Chinese</strong>: Old Chinese *s.li[j]-s → Middle Chinese *sijH → Mandarin <em>sì</em> 四. The *s- initial replaced *l- through a prefix interaction (compare the *s-ŋrar pattern for 山).</li><li><strong>Tibetan</strong>: <em>bzhi</em> (བཞི།). Again, the b- is a prefix; the zh- of the root is Tibetan's own palatalisation of the PST lateral *l- before the front vowel of *li(j).</li><li><strong>Burmese</strong>: <em>le</em> (လေး). The l- directly preserves the PST *l- initial.</li></ul><h3>Two More Cognate Pairs</h3><p><strong>Eye / 目</strong>: PST *myak → Old Chinese *C.m(r)[u]k → Mandarin <em>mù</em>; Tibetan <em>mig</em> (མིག); Burmese <em>myak</em>. The core consonant *m- is rock-stable across the family.</p><p><strong>Name / 名</strong>: PST *r-miŋ → Old Chinese *C.meŋ → Mandarin <em>míng</em> 名; Tibetan <em>ming</em> (མིང་); Burmese <em>a-maññ</em> (အမည်, pronounced /ʔəmjì/). A nasal final is shared across all three branches — Burmese has a palatal -ññ where Chinese and Tibetan have -ŋ, a regular correspondence.</p><h3>The Critical Methodological Caveat</h3><p>Identifying cognates is harder in Sino-Tibetan than in Indo-European for three reasons. First, Chinese's monosyllabic surface structure and the logographic writing system hide older morphology. Second, Chinese has lent words to its neighbours on a massive scale for well over two thousand years, so a resemblance might be borrowing, not inheritance. Third, the family tree itself is contested. The traditional picture splits it into Chinese on one side and \"Tibeto-Burman\" on the other, but no one has ever shown that all the non-Chinese languages share a common ancestor of their own; Chinese may simply be one branch among many. Sagart's controversial Sino-Austronesian hypothesis — that Chinese and Austronesian share an ancestor — shows how much remains open. It is easily confused with Austro-Tai, which is a different claim by a different scholar: Benedict's 1942 proposal joining Austronesian to Kra-Dai, with Chinese not in it at all.</p><p>One myth is worth clearing up: numerals are <em>not</em> immune to borrowing, least of all in East Asia. Thai <em>hâa</em> \"five\" and <em>sìi</em> \"four\" are old loans from Chinese 五 and 四, and Japanese, Korean and Vietnamese all count with Chinese numerals alongside native ones. What makes Tibetan <em>lnga</em> and Burmese <em>ngā</em> look inherited rather than borrowed is the detail: prefixes such as Tibetan <em>l-</em> and <em>b-</em> that no Chinese loan would bring, and sound correspondences that repeat across hundreds of words. Two computational studies in 2019 put the family's common ancestor at roughly 5,900 or 7,200 years ago, so the thread that ties 五 to ལྔ is some six or seven thousand years long.</p>"},"sources":["Baxter, W. H. & Sagart, L. (2014). Old Chinese: A New Reconstruction. Oxford University Press.","Matisoff, J. A. (2003). Handbook of Proto-Tibeto-Burman. University of California Press.","Sagart, L. (1999). The Roots of Old Chinese. John Benjamins.","Hill, N. W. (2019). The Historical Phonology of Tibetan, Burmese, and Chinese. Cambridge University Press.",{"text":"STEDT: Sino-Tibetan Etymological Dictionary and Thesaurus","url":"https://stedt.berkeley.edu/"},{"text":"Sagart, L., Jacques, G., Lai, Y., Ryder, R. J., Thouzeau, V., Greenhill, S. J. & List, J.-M. (2019). Dated language phylogenies shed light on the ancestry of Sino-Tibetan. PNAS 116(21): 10317–10322.","url":"https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1817972116"}]},{"id":"ko-mid-eastguk-jeongun","icon":"📜","tags":["korea","middle-korean","dictionary","hangul","sinoxenic"],"title":{"en":"東國正韻 — A Prescriptive Sino-Korean Pronunciation Dictionary","ja":"東國正韻 — 誰も実際に使わなかった理想の漢字音","ko":"동국정운 — 아무도 실제로 쓰지 않은 한자음 사전","zh":"东国正韵——无人实际使用的朝鲜汉字音","yue":"東國正韻——冇人真係咁讀嘅朝鮮漢字音","vi":"東國正韻 — Từ điển âm Hán-Triều mà không ai thực sự nói","th":"東國正韻 — พจนานุกรมเสียงอักษรจีน-เกาหลีที่ไม่มีใครพูดจริง","id":"東國正韻 — Kamus Pelafalan Sino-Korea yang Tak Pernah Dipakai","hi":"東國正韻 — वह शब्दकोश जिसे कोई बोलता नहीं था","de":"東國正韻 — Das sino-koreanische Wörterbuch, das niemand sprach","fr":"東國正韻 — le dictionnaire sino-coréen que personne ne parlait vraiment","it":"東國正韻 — Il dizionario sino-coreano che nessuno parlò mai","es":"東國正韻 — El diccionario sino-coreano que nadie habló jamás","pt":"東國正韻 — O dicionário sino-coreano que ninguém falou","ru":"東國正韻 — Китайско-корейский словарь, которым никто не пользовался","uk":"東國正韻 — Китайсько-корейський словник, яким ніхто не послуговувався","ar":"東國正韻 — معجم النطق الصيني-الكوري الذي لم يتكلم به أحد","he":"東國正韻 — המילון הסינו-קוריאני שאף אחד לא דיבר","sw":"東國正韻 — Kamusi ya Matamshi ya Sino-Kikorea Ambayo Hakuna Aliyoitumia"},"summary":{"en":"In 1448, King Sejong commissioned a dictionary to standardize Korean readings of Chinese characters, modeled on an idealized Middle Chinese drawn from classical rhyme books. Its prescribed pronunciations saw limited adoption and fell out of official use within decades.","ja":"1448年、世宗大王は漢字の朝鮮読みを統一しようと辞書を編纂させた。古典韻書が伝える理想化された中古音をモデルにしたが、規定された発音は実際の朝鮮語とかけ離れており、数十年のうちに自然消滅した。","ko":"1448년 세종대왕은 한자음을 표준화하기 위한 사전을 편찬했다. 고전 운서가 전하는 이상화된 중고 한어음을 모델로 삼았으나, 규정된 발음은 실제 한국어와 맞지 않아 수십 년 만에 사라졌다.","zh":"1448年，世宗大王主持编纂了一部统一朝鲜汉字读音的辞书，以古典韵书所传的理想化中古汉语音为范本，但规定的发音从未符合朝鲜口语实际，数十年内便遭废弃。","yue":"1448年，世宗大王主持編寫咗一本統一朝鮮漢字讀音嘅辭書，以古典韻書所傳嘅理想化中古漢語音為範本，但規定嘅讀音從來唔係朝鮮人真正講嘅，幾十年後就冇人用㗎喇。","vi":"Năm 1448, vua Sejong ra lệnh biên soạn một cuốn từ điển để chuẩn hóa âm đọc chữ Hán trong tiếng Triều Tiên — mô phỏng theo một thứ tiếng Hán trung cổ lý tưởng hóa dựng lại từ các sách vần cổ điển — nhưng các phát âm quy định chưa bao giờ phù hợp với tiếng Triều Tiên thực tế và dự án bị bỏ dở sau vài thập kỷ.","th":"ในปี ค.ศ. 1448 พระเจ้าเซจงทรงโปรดให้จัดทำพจนานุกรมเพื่อกำหนดมาตรฐานการออกเสียงอักษรจีนในภาษาเกาหลี โดยอิงจากเสียงภาษาจีนยุคกลางในอุดมคติที่สืบทอดมาจากตำราสัมผัสฉบับคลาสสิก แต่การออกเสียงที่กำหนดไว้ไม่ตรงกับภาษาเกาหลีที่ใช้จริง และโครงการก็ถูกละทิ้งไปในอีกไม่กี่สิบปีต่อมา","id":"Pada 1448, Raja Sejong menugaskan pembuatan kamus untuk menstandarkan pelafalan karakter Han dalam bahasa Korea — berdasarkan bahasa Tionghoa Pertengahan yang diidealkan dan disusun ulang dari buku-buku sajak klasik — namun pelafalan yang ditentukan tidak pernah sesuai dengan bahasa Korea lisan dan proyek itu diam-diam ditinggalkan dalam beberapa dekade.","hi":"1448 में, राजा सेजोंग ने कोरियाई भाषा में चीनी अक्षरों के उच्चारण को मानकीकृत करने के लिए एक शब्दकोश बनवाया — शास्त्रीय तुक-ग्रंथों से पुनर्निर्मित एक आदर्शीकृत मध्यकालीन चीनी ध्वनि-व्यवस्था के आधार पर — लेकिन निर्धारित उच्चारण कभी भी बोली जाने वाली कोरियाई से मेल नहीं खाए और परियोजना दशकों के भीतर चुपचाप छोड़ दी गई।","de":"1448 beauftragte König Sejong die Erstellung eines Wörterbuchs zur Vereinheitlichung der koreanischen Lesungen chinesischer Schriftzeichen — nach dem Vorbild eines idealisierten Mittelchinesisch, wie es die klassischen Reimbücher überliefern — doch die vorgeschriebenen Aussprachen entsprachen nie dem gesprochenen Koreanisch und das Projekt wurde innerhalb von Jahrzehnten still aufgegeben.","fr":"En 1448, le roi Sejong fit compiler un dictionnaire pour standardiser les lectures coréennes des caractères chinois — calquées sur un moyen chinois idéalisé reconstitué d'après les livres de rimes classiques — mais les prononciations prescrites ne correspondaient jamais au coréen parlé et le projet fut silencieusement abandonné en quelques décennies.","it":"Nel 1448, il re Sejong commissionò un dizionario per standardizzare le letture coreane dei caratteri cinesi — modellate su un cinese medio idealizzato ricostruito dai libri di rime classici — ma le pronunce prescritte non corrisposero mai al coreano parlato e il progetto fu silenziosamente abbandonato nel giro di decenni.","es":"En 1448, el rey Sejong encargó un diccionario para estandarizar las lecturas coreanas de los caracteres chinos — modeladas en un chino medio idealizado reconstruido a partir de los libros de rimas clásicos — pero las pronunciaciones prescritas nunca coincidieron con el coreano hablado y el proyecto fue silenciosamente abandonado en décadas.","pt":"Em 1448, o rei Sejong encomendou um dicionário para padronizar as leituras coreanas dos caracteres chineses — modeladas em um chinês médio idealizado reconstruído a partir dos livros de rimas clássicos — mas as pronúncias prescritas nunca corresponderam ao coreano falado e o projeto foi silenciosamente abandonado em décadas.","ru":"В 1448 году король Сечон поручил составить словарь для стандартизации корейского чтения китайских иероглифов — по образцу идеализированного среднекитайского произношения, восстановленного по классическим рифмовникам — однако предписанные произношения никогда не соответствовали разговорному корейскому, и проект был тихо брошен в течение нескольких десятилетий.","uk":"У 1448 році король Седжон доручив скласти словник для стандартизації корейського читання китайських ієрогліфів — за зразком ідеалізованої середньокитайської вимови, відтвореної за класичними римівниками — але приписані вимови ніколи не відповідали розмовній корейській, і проект тихо занедбали за кілька десятиліть.","ar":"في عام 1448، كلّف الملك سيجونغ بإعداد معجم لتوحيد قراءات الكوريين للحروف الصينية — مستنداً إلى صينية وسيطة مثالية أُعيد بناؤها من كتب القوافي الكلاسيكية — غير أن النطق المقرر لم يتوافق قط مع الكورية المنطوقة وتُرك المشروع في صمت خلال عقود.","he":"ב-1448 הזמין המלך סג'ונג עריכת מילון לתיקון מאת הקריאות הקוריאניות של אותיות סיניות — לפי סינית תיכונה אידיאלית ששוחזרה מתוך ספרי החרוזים הקלאסיים — אך ההגיות שנקבעו לא התאימו מעולם לקוריאנית המדוברת והפרויקט ננטש בשקט תוך עשרות שנים.","sw":"Mnamo 1448, Mfalme Sejong aliagiza kuundwa kwa kamusi ya kusanifisha matamshi ya herufi za Kichina katika Kikorea — ikiiga Kichina cha Kati cha kinadharia kilichojengwa upya kutokana na vitabu vya vina vya kale — lakini matamshi yaliyoainishwa hayakulingana na Kikorea halisi kilichozungumzwa na mradi uliachwa kimya ndani ya miaka michache."},"body":{"en":"<h3>A Dictionary for a Language No One Spoke</h3>\n<p>In 1447–1448, King Sejong of Joseon oversaw the compilation of the <strong>東國正韻</strong> (<em>Dongguk Jeongun</em>, \"Correct Rhymes of the Eastern Kingdom\"), a six-volume rhyme dictionary that sorted characters into 91 rhymes and 23 initials and prescribed a \"correct\" Sino-Korean reading for each. The dictionary was technically detailed, but its prescribed readings did not become ordinary Korean usage.</p>\n\n<div class=\"trivia-actions\">\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"人\">👤 See 人 across languages</button>\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"focus\" data-code=\"ko\">📍 Focus on Korean on the map</button>\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"panto\" data-lat=\"37.57\" data-lng=\"126.98\" data-zoom=\"5\">🗺 Pan to Seoul</button>\n</div>\n\n<p>The problem Sejong was trying to solve was real: by the 15th century, Korean readers had been pronouncing Chinese characters in ways that had diverged significantly from contemporary Chinese norms. Each Hanja (Korean term for Chinese characters) had accumulated a Sino-Korean reading (한자음) shaped by centuries of phonological change in Korean, not Chinese. Sejong, the same king who had just created Hangul in 1443, believed the readings had become corrupt and should be restored to a systematic standard. The preface by Shin Suk-ju lists the \"errors\": Korean readings had no voiced initials, confused aspirated and plain <em>k</em>, did not distinguish the rising and departing tones, and ended entering-tone syllables that should close in <em>-t</em> with an <em>-l</em> instead.</p>\n\n<h3>An Idealized Chinese as the Gold Standard</h3>\n<p>The compilers, led by scholar Shin Suk-ju, did not model their pronunciations on contemporary Ming dynasty speech. Instead they worked from the classical rhyme-book tradition — the Yuan <strong>古今韻會擧要</strong> (<em>Gujin Yunhui Juyao</em>, 1297), which Sejong had ordered translated into the new script in 1444, and the Ming <strong>洪武正韻</strong> (<em>Hongwu Zhengyun</em>, 1375), whose title and format the Dongguk Jeongun deliberately echoes. What those books encode is a literary standard rooted in the Middle Chinese rhyme tradition, not the living speech of any community. The compilers blended that system with the shapes of existing Korean readings, producing a compromise that matched neither Chinese nor Korean speech.</p>\n\n<p>The result was a set of pronunciations that:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Brought back voiced initials, written with doubled letters (ㄲ ㄸ ㅃ ㅉ ㅆ ㆅ), which Sino-Korean had never had</li>\n  <li>Replaced the Korean final <em>-l</em> in entering-tone words (Chinese <em>-t</em>) with <strong>ㅭ</strong>, ㄹ plus the glottal-stop letter <strong>ㆆ</strong> (Korean already had <em>-p</em> and <em>-k</em>, so only <em>-t</em> needed \"fixing\")</li>\n  <li>Gave every syllable a final letter, even open ones: a silent ㅇ, or ㅱ for a <em>-w</em> ending</li>\n  <li>Marked rising and departing tones apart, as the Chinese rhyme books did</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>You can see the system in the Korean translation of the <em>Hunminjeongeum</em> itself, where every character carries its prescribed reading; in a single line describing the letter ㆆ, 字 'character' is written ᄍᆞᆼ (today 자), 發 'emit' 버ᇙ (today 발), 喉 'throat' ᅘᅮᇢ (today 후), with a doubled initial, a silent final, a ㅭ and a ㅱ in just three syllables.</p>\n\n<h3>Extinct Letters for an Imaginary Sound</h3>\n<p>The <strong>ㆆ</strong> and <strong>ㅭ</strong> letters are perhaps the most haunting legacy of the Dongguk Jeongun. Sejong's Hangul, announced in 1443, was designed with exquisite phonetic precision — each letter's shape supposedly reflecting the position of the speech organs. The <strong>ㅭ</strong> digraph the compilers devised for the entering-tone <em>-t</em> stood for no sound of ordinary Korean at all, and it vanished with the readings it served. ㆆ had a little more life: in native Korean it turns up in a few grammatical spots, as in 先考ㆆ ᄠᅳᆮ 'my late father's will' in the <em>Yongbi eocheon ga</em> (1447) or 니르고져 호ᇙ 배 'what (they) would wish to say' in the <em>Hunminjeongeum</em> translation. But its main job was the Dongguk Jeongun readings, and it disappeared with them. (ㅸ, by contrast, was a genuine letter of native Korean, a soft <em>b/v</em>-like sound in words such as 셔ᄫᅳᆯ 'Seoul' and 고ᄫᅡ 'beautiful'; it died out on its own within a few decades as the sound turned into <em>w</em>, which is why today's forms are 서울 and 고와.) Modern scholars studying the history of the Hangul alphabet must account for letters that died with the dictionary they were made for.</p>\n\n<h3>Abandoned but Not Lost</h3>\n<p>Even while it was official, the system lived mostly in print, above all in Hangul translations of Buddhist scriptures, and by the early 16th century it was no longer used at all. By the time of <em>Hunmongjahoe</em> (1527), practical character dictionaries simply recorded the pronunciations Koreans actually used. But the dictionary's failure became linguistics' treasure. Its meticulous Hangul spellings show exactly how 15th-century Korean scholars mapped the Chinese rhyme-book categories onto their own sounds, and, through the \"errors\" they set out to fix, they record what real Sino-Korean sounded like at the time. For Ming Chinese itself the better witness is its sister project, the <em>Hongwu Zhengyun Yeokhun</em> (1455), which transcribed the Chinese rhyme book in Hangul.</p>"},"sources":["Lee Ki-moon & Ramsey, S. Robert (2011). A History of the Korean Language. Cambridge University Press.","Ledyard, Gari K. (1966). The Korean Language Reform of 1446. PhD dissertation, University of California, Berkeley.","Nam Kwang-wu (1995). \"Dongguk Jeongun and the Reconstruction of Middle Korean Pronunciation.\" Korean Linguistics 7: 45–78.",{"text":"Dongguk Jeongun — National Institute of Korean Language","url":"https://www.korean.go.kr/front/etcData/etcDataView.do?mn_id=46&etc_seq=71"},"Pulleyblank, E.G. (1984). Middle Chinese: A Study in Historical Phonology. University of British Columbia Press.",{"text":"Wikisource: 東國正韻 facsimile (1448)","url":"https://ko.wikisource.org/wiki/%EB%8F%99%EA%B5%AD%EC%A0%95%EC%9A%B4"}]},{"id":"kokugo-versus-kango","icon":"📖","tags":["japan","vocabulary","etymology","loanwords","strata"],"title":{"en":"和語・漢語・外来語 — Japan's Three-Layer Vocabulary","ja":"和語・漢語・外来語 — 日本語の三層語彙","ko":"和語・漢語・外来語 — 일본어의 세 가지 어휘 층위","zh":"和语・汉语・外来语——日语的三层词汇","yue":"和語・漢語・外来語——日語嘅三層詞彙","vi":"和語・漢語・外来語 — Ba tầng từ vựng của tiếng Nhật","th":"和語・漢語・外来語 — สามชั้นคำศัพท์ในภาษาญี่ปุ่น","id":"和語・漢語・外来語 — Tiga Lapisan Kosakata Bahasa Jepang","hi":"和語・漢語・外来語 — जापानी भाषा की तीन-परतीय शब्द-संपदा","de":"和語・漢語・外来語 — Die drei Wortschatzschichten des Japanischen","fr":"和語・漢語・外来語 — Les trois couches du vocabulaire japonais","it":"和語・漢語・外来語 — I tre strati del vocabolario giapponese","es":"和語・漢語・外来語 — Las tres capas del vocabulario japonés","pt":"和語・漢語・外来語 — As três camadas do vocabulário japonês","ru":"和語・漢語・外来語 — Три пласта японской лексики","uk":"和語・漢語・外来語 — Три шари японської лексики","ar":"和語・漢語・外来語 — الطبقات الثلاث لمفردات اللغة اليابانية","he":"和語・漢語・外来語 — שלוש שכבות אוצר המילים של היפנית","sw":"和語・漢語・外来語 — Safu Tatu za Msamiati wa Kijapani"},"summary":{"en":"Japanese vocabulary has three major strata — native Yamato words (和語), Sino-Japanese loans (漢語), and Western loans (外来語). Each has characteristic writing conventions, sound patterns, and cultural associations.","ja":"日本語の語彙は「和語」「漢語」「外来語」の三層に分かれ、それぞれ固有の表記体系・音声・文化的語感を持つ。","ko":"일본어 어휘는 야마토어(和語), 한자어(漢語), 서양 외래어(外来語)의 세 층위로 나뉘며, 각각 고유한 문자 체계, 음운, 문화적 어감을 지닌다.","zh":"日语词汇分为三层：本土大和语（和语）、汉语借词（汉语）和西洋借词（外来语）——各自拥有独特的书写体系、语音和文化语感。","yue":"日語詞彙分為三層：本土大和語（和語）、漢語借詞（漢語）同西洋借詞（外来語）——每層都有自己獨特嘅書寫系統、音韻同文化語感。","vi":"Từ vựng tiếng Nhật nổi tiếng được phân chia thành ba tầng — từ Yamato bản địa (和語), từ vay mượn gốc Hán-Nhật (漢語), và từ vay mượn phương Tây (外来語) — mỗi tầng có hệ thống chữ viết, âm vị và sắc thái văn hóa riêng.","th":"คำศัพท์ภาษาญี่ปุ่นแบ่งออกเป็นสามชั้น ได้แก่ คำยามาโตะพื้นเมือง (和語) คำยืมจากภาษาจีน-ญี่ปุ่น (漢語) และคำยืมจากภาษาตะวันตก (外来語) แต่ละชั้นมีระบบการเขียน เสียง และระดับวัฒนธรรมเฉพาะของตัวเอง","id":"Kosakata bahasa Jepang terkenal terbagi dalam tiga lapisan — kata asli Yamato (和語), pinjaman Sino-Jepang (漢語), dan pinjaman Barat (外来語) — masing-masing dengan sistem penulisan, bunyi, dan register budaya tersendiri.","hi":"जापानी शब्द-संपदा प्रसिद्ध रूप से तीन परतों में विभाजित है — मूल यामातो शब्द (和語), चीनी-जापानी ऋण शब्द (漢語), और पश्चिमी ऋण शब्द (外来語) — प्रत्येक की अपनी लिपि, ध्वनि प्रणाली और सांस्कृतिक प्रतिध्वनि है।","de":"Der japanische Wortschatz gliedert sich in drei Schichten — einheimische Yamato-Wörter (和語), sino-japanische Lehnwörter (漢語) und westliche Lehnwörter (外来語) — jede mit eigenem Schriftsystem, Lautsystem und kulturellem Register.","fr":"Le vocabulaire japonais se divise en trois couches — les mots yamato indigènes (和語), les emprunts sino-japonais (漢語) et les emprunts occidentaux (外来語) — chacune avec son propre système d'écriture, son système phonologique et son registre culturel.","it":"Il vocabolario giapponese si divide in tre strati — le parole yamato native (和語), i prestiti sino-giapponesi (漢語) e i prestiti occidentali (外来語) — ciascuno con il proprio sistema di scrittura, sistema fonologico e registro culturale.","es":"El vocabulario japonés se divide en tres capas — palabras yamato nativas (和語), préstamos sino-japoneses (漢語) y préstamos occidentales (外来語) — cada una con su propio sistema de escritura, sistema fonológico y registro cultural.","pt":"O vocabulário japonês divide-se em três camadas — palavras yamato nativas (和語), empréstimos sino-japoneses (漢語) e empréstimos ocidentais (外来語) — cada uma com seu próprio sistema de escrita, sistema fonológico e registro cultural.","ru":"Японская лексика делится на три слоя — исконные ямато-слова (和語), китайско-японские заимствования (漢語) и западные заимствования (外来語) — каждый со своей системой письма, фонологией и культурным регистром.","uk":"Японська лексика поділяється на три шари — корінні слова ямато (和語), китайсько-японські запозичення (漢語) і західні запозичення (外来語) — кожен зі своєю системою письма, фонологією та культурним регістром.","ar":"تنقسم المفردات اليابانية إلى ثلاث طبقات: الكلمات اليابانية الأصيلة (和語)، والكلمات الصينية المستعارة (漢語)، والكلمات الغربية المستعارة (外来語)؛ ولكل منها نظام كتابة وأصوات وسجل ثقافي خاص.","he":"אוצר המילים היפני מחולק לשלוש שכבות — מילים יפניות מקוריות (和語), מילים סיניות-יפניות מושאלות (漢語), ומילים מושאלות ממערב (外来語) — לכל אחת מערכת כתיב, פונולוגיה ורישום תרבותי משלה.","sw":"Msamiati wa Kijapani umegawanywa katika safu tatu — maneno ya asili ya Yamato (和語), maneno ya mkopo ya Sino-Kijapani (漢語), na maneno ya mkopo ya Kimagharibi (外来語) — kila moja na mfumo wake wa uandishi, mfumo wa sauti, na usajili wa kitamaduni."},"body":{"en":"<h3>Three Lexical Strata</h3>\n<p>Japanese vocabulary is often described in terms of three historical strata. <strong>和語</strong> (<em>wago</em>, \"Japanese-language words\") are the native core, the words Japanese already had before Chinese writing arrived. (A few very early borrowings slipped in so long ago that they now pass as wago: 梅 <em>ume</em>, \"plum\", is probably Chinese 梅 in disguise.) <strong>漢語</strong> (<em>kango</em>, \"Han-language words\") are borrowings that arrived with Buddhism, Confucianism, and literacy from China, starting roughly in the 5th century CE. <strong>外来語</strong> (<em>gairaigo</em>, \"words from outside\") form a later borrowing layer, chiefly from Portuguese, Dutch, English, German and French, beginning with European contact from 1543, when a Portuguese ship first reached Japan.</p>\n\n<div class=\"trivia-actions\">\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"山\">🏔 See 山 (yama / san) across languages</button>\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"focus\" data-code=\"ja_kun\">📍 Focus ja_kun (native readings) on map</button>\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"focus\" data-code=\"ja\">📍 Focus the Sino-Japanese readings</button>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Distribution by Domain</h3>\n<p>Each stratum dominates in different domains. Surveys find that in speech <strong>wago make up over 70%</strong> of running words (the old words for family, body, weather and feeling) while in newspaper prose <strong>kango pass 70%</strong>, partly because Meiji Japan modernised by coining kango compounds rather than borrowing Western words. The balance keeps moving: in a 1994 magazine survey by Japan's National Institute for Japanese Language and Linguistics (NINJAL), gairaigo made up 33.8% of the different words used, almost level with kango (34.2%) and well ahead of wago (25.7%). Words like 電話 (<em>denwa</em>, telephone), 経済 (<em>keizai</em>, economy, an old Chinese phrase given a new meaning) and 哲学 (<em>tetsugaku</em>, philosophy) were made in Meiji Japan and later exported to China and Korea. 哲学 has a known inventor: the scholar Nishi Amane, who in 1874 settled on it after first trying 希哲学, a calque of Greek <em>philosophia</em>, \"longing for wisdom\".</p>\n\n<p>The contrast among the three strata is visible in words for \"beautiful\":</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>美しい</strong> (<em>utsukushii</em>) — wago; warm and personal. In the Man'yōshū it did not yet mean \"beautiful\" at all: the poet Okura says that seeing his wife and children he finds them <em>megushi utsukushi</em>, \"dear and beloved\". In the Heian period it came to mean \"sweet, lovely\", above all of small things, and only towards the end of the Middle Ages did it widen to the beauty of nature and art.</li>\n  <li><strong>美麗</strong> (<em>birei</em>) — kango; formal, objective, literary, used in prose and criticism</li>\n  <li><strong>ビューティフル</strong> (<em>byūtifuru</em>) — gairaigo; young, fashionable, often ironic or playful</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The three choices differ in register and association. English has its own layered triples inherited from Germanic, French, and Latin — <em>kingly, royal, regal</em>; <em>ask, question, interrogate</em>. Japanese writing often makes the stratum visible through the choice of kanji, hiragana or katakana.</p>\n\n<h3>Writing and Lexical Strata</h3>\n<p>Japanese orthography often encodes stratum identity visually. <strong>Hiragana</strong> and <strong>kanji with kun readings</strong> signal wago. <strong>Kanji with on readings</strong> signal kango. <strong>Katakana</strong> signals gairaigo (and scientific names). These conventions can give a reader information about a word's likely history before its meaning is known. A sentence like <em>私はコーヒーを飲む</em> (\"I drink coffee\") contains all three strata: 私 (wago), コーヒー (gairaigo from Dutch <em>koffie</em>), 飲む (wago).</p>\n\n<div class=\"trivia-actions\">\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"天\">☀️ See 天 (ame/sora/ten) in all layers</button>\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"panto\" data-lat=\"35.68\" data-lng=\"139.69\" data-zoom=\"5\">🗾 Pan to Japan</button>\n</div>\n\n<h3>When the Strata Collide</h3>\n<p>The three-stratum model breaks down at the edges. Many words are hybrids: <strong>頭痛持ち</strong> (<em>zutsū-mochi</em>, \"someone prone to headaches\") welds the kango 頭痛 to the wago 持ち. <strong>チョコレート工場</strong> mixes gairaigo (チョコレート, from English <em>chocolate</em>) with kango (工場 <em>kōjō</em>, factory). Some words have been borrowed so long they feel native — <em>tabako</em> (tobacco, from Portuguese <em>tabaco</em>, brought in around 1600) feels as Japanese as <em>sakura</em>.</p>\n\n<p>Mixed forms have their own label: <strong>混種語</strong> (<em>konshugo</em>, \"mixed-type words\"), which make up about 8% of the general words in one standard modern dictionary (<em>Shinsen Kokugo Jiten</em>, 2002). <strong>テレビ局</strong> (<em>terebi-kyoku</em>, TV station) fuses English gairaigo テレビ with kango 局. Such hybrids are common in modern Japanese and show that the three-way classification is permeable.</p>\n\n<p>The three-stratum model is a simplification, but a useful one. A Japanese writer who picks 美しい over 美麗 or ビューティフル is choosing a script and a tone of voice as well as a meaning.</p>"},"sources":["国立国語研究所『現代雑誌九十種の用語用字』（国立国語研究所報告 21・22・25）東京：秀英出版, 1962–1964.","Shibatani, Masayoshi (1990). The Languages of Japan. Cambridge University Press. Chapter 6: Lexicon.","Frellesvig, Bjarke (2010). A History of the Japanese Language. Cambridge University Press.","Loveday, Leo J. (1996). Language Contact in Japan: A Socio-Linguistic History. Oxford University Press.",{"text":"NHK Web Easy: 外来語・和語・漢語 (with audio)","url":"https://www3.nhk.or.jp/news/web_easy/"},{"text":"Kotobank: 和語・漢語・外来語 解説","url":"https://kotobank.jp/word/%E5%92%8C%E8%AA%9E-653004"},{"text":"Japanese Wikipedia: 語種 (NINJAL survey figures)","url":"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/語種"},{"text":"Kotobank: うつくしい (Nihon Kokugo Daijiten, Digital Daijisen)","url":"https://kotobank.jp/word/%E7%BE%8E%E3%81%97%E3%81%84-438400"}]},{"id":"tang-empire-multilingual-coins","icon":"🪙","tags":["sinosphere","kanji","history","phonology"],"title":{"en":"One Coin, Four Readings: The Tang Dynasty's Multilingual Currency","ja":"一枚の銅銭、四つの読み方：唐の開元通寶が繋いだ世界","ko":"동전 하나, 네 가지 읽기: 당나라 개원통보의 다언어 세계","zh":"一枚铜币，四种读法：唐代開元通寶的多语言旅程","yue":"一個銅錢，四種讀法：唐朝開元通寶嘅多語言之旅","vi":"Một Đồng Tiền, Bốn Cách Đọc: Hành Trình Đa Ngôn Ngữ của Khai Nguyên Thông Bảo","th":"เหรียญเดียว สี่สำเนียง: การเดินทางข้ามภาษาของไคหยวนถงเป่า","id":"Satu Koin, Empat Bacaan: Perjalanan Multibahasa Uang Tang","hi":"एक सिक्का, चार पाठ: तांग राजवंश की बहुभाषी मुद्रा की यात्रा","de":"Eine Münze, vier Lesungen: Die mehrsprachige Reise des Tang-Kupfergeldes","fr":"Une pièce, quatre lectures : le voyage multilingue de la monnaie Tang","it":"Una moneta, quattro letture: il viaggio multilingue della moneta Tang","es":"Una moneda, cuatro lecturas: el viaje multilingüe de la moneda Tang","pt":"Uma moeda, quatro leituras: a jornada multilíngue da moeda Tang","ru":"Одна монета, четыре прочтения: многоязычное путешествие танской монеты","uk":"Одна монета, чотири прочитання: багатомовна подорож танської монети","ar":"عملة واحدة، أربع قراءات: رحلة عملة تانغ متعددة اللغات","he":"מטבע אחד, ארבע קריאות: מסעה הרב-לשוני של מטבע שושלת טאנג","sw":"Sarafu Moja, Usomaji Minne: Safari ya Lugha Nyingi ya Sarafu ya Tang"},"summary":{"en":"The Tang dynasty's 開元通寶 coin, minted from 621 CE, circulated across Asia. Its four-character inscription received different local readings in Chinese, Japanese, Korean and Vietnamese.","ja":"621年に鋳造された唐の開元通寶は、日本・朝鮮・ベトナム・中国でまったく異なる読み方をされながらアジア全域に広まり、漢字圏が文字を共有しながら音を共有しなかった証拠となっている。","ko":"621년에 주조된 당나라 개원통보는 일본·한국·베트남·중국에서 전혀 다르게 읽히면서 아시아 전역에 퍼졌으며, 한자 문화권이 문자는 공유했지만 발음은 공유하지 않았다는 살아있는 증거다.","zh":"唐代621年铸造的開元通寶流通至亚洲各地，其四个汉字在日、韩、越、中的读音各不相同，是汉字圈共享文字却不共享语音的活生生见证。","yue":"唐朝621年鑄造嘅開元通寶流通到亞洲各地，四個漢字喺日本、韓國、越南同中國讀法完全唔同，係漢字圈共享文字但唔共享發音嘅活生生證明。","vi":"Đồng tiền Khai Nguyên Thông Bảo của nhà Đường, đúc từ năm 621, lan rộng khắp châu Á với bốn chữ Hán được đọc hoàn toàn khác nhau ở Nhật, Hàn, Việt, Trung—minh chứng sống động cho việc vùng Hán tự dùng chung chữ viết nhưng không dùng chung âm thanh.","th":"เหรียญไคหยวนถงเป่าของราชวงศ์ถัง ผลิตตั้งแต่ปี ค.ศ. 621 แพร่กระจายทั่วเอเชีย โดยอักษรสี่ตัวถูกอ่านต่างกันในญี่ปุ่น เกาหลี เวียดนาม และจีน เป็นหลักฐานที่มีชีวิตว่าวงจรอักษรจีนใช้อักษรร่วมกันแต่ไม่ใช่เสียงร่วมกัน","id":"Koin 開元通寶 dinasti Tang, dicetak sejak 621 M, menyebar ke seluruh Asia dengan empat karakternya dibaca sangat berbeda dalam bahasa Jepang, Korea, Vietnam, dan Cina—bukti nyata bahwa Sinosphere berbagi aksara tetapi bukan bunyi.","hi":"621 ई. से ढाली गई तांग राजवंश की 開元通寶 मुद्रा एशिया भर में फैली, इसके चार अक्षर जापानी, कोरियाई, वियतनामी और चीनी में बिल्कुल अलग तरह से पढ़े जाते थे—यह इस बात का जीवंत प्रमाण है कि सिनोस्फीयर ने लिपि साझा की, ध्वनि नहीं।","de":"Die Tang-Münze 開元通寶, ab 621 n. Chr. geprägt, verbreitete sich in ganz Asien, wobei ihre vier Zeichen auf Japanisch, Koreanisch, Vietnamesisch und Chinesisch völlig unterschiedlich gelesen wurden—ein lebendiger Beweis dafür, dass die Sinosphäre Schriften, aber keine Laute teilte.","fr":"La pièce 開元通寶 de la dynastie Tang, frappée dès 621, s'est répandue à travers l'Asie avec ses quatre caractères lus de façon totalement différente en japonais, coréen, vietnamien et chinois—preuve vivante que la Sinosphère partageait les caractères mais pas les sons.","it":"La moneta 開元通寶 della dinastia Tang, coniata dal 621, si diffuse in tutta l'Asia con i suoi quattro caratteri letti in modo completamente diverso in giapponese, coreano, vietnamita e cinese—testimonianza viva che la Sinosfera condivideva la scrittura ma non i suoni.","es":"La moneda 開元通寶 de la dinastía Tang, acuñada desde el 621, se extendió por Asia con sus cuatro caracteres leídos de forma completamente diferente en japonés, coreano, vietnamita y chino—prueba viva de que la Sinosfera compartía escritura pero no sonidos.","pt":"A moeda 開元通寶 da dinastia Tang, cunhada a partir de 621, espalhou-se pela Ásia com seus quatro caracteres lidos de formas completamente diferentes em japonês, coreano, vietnamita e chinês—prova viva de que a Sinosfera compartilhava escrita, mas não sons.","ru":"Монета 開元通寶 династии Тан, чеканившаяся с 621 года, распространилась по всей Азии, причём её четыре иероглифа читались совершенно по-разному на японском, корейском, вьетнамском и китайском — живое свидетельство того, что Синосфера делила письменность, но не звуки.","uk":"Монета 開元通寶 династії Тан, що карбувалась з 621 року, поширилась по всій Азії, і її чотири ієрогліфи читались абсолютно по-різному японською, корейською, в'єтнамською та китайською — живе свідчення того, що Синосфера ділила письмо, але не звуки.","ar":"انتشرت عملة 開元通寶 لأسرة تانغ، المسكوكة منذ عام 621م، في أرجاء آسيا، وكانت أحرفها الأربعة تُقرأ بطرق مختلفة تماماً باليابانية والكورية والفيتنامية والصينية—دليل حي على أن الفضاء الصيني تشارك الكتابة دون الأصوات.","he":"מטבע 開元通寶 של שושלת טאנג, שנטבע משנת 621 לספירה, התפשט ברחבי אסיה כשארבעת סימניו נקראים בצורה שונה לחלוטין ביפנית, קוריאנית, וייטנאמית וסינית—עדות חיה לכך שהספֵרה הסינית שיתפה כתב אך לא צלילים.","sw":"Sarafu 開元通寶 ya nasaba ya Tang, iliyopigwa tangu mwaka 621 BK, ilienea kote Asia huku herufi zake nne zikisomwa tofauti kabisa kwa Kijapani, Kikorea, Kivietinamu na Kichina—ushahidi hai kwamba Sinosphere ilishiriki maandishi lakini si sauti."},"body":{"en":"<h3>A Coin That Crossed Civilizations</h3><p>In 621 CE, the Tang court issued a new bronze coin inscribed with four characters: <strong>開元通寶</strong>. The name means roughly \"circulating treasure of the new beginning\": 開元 hailed the new dynasty and was not a reign title (the famous Kaiyuan reign of Emperor Xuanzong began only in 713). Cast for almost three centuries, it became one of the most widely travelled coins in Asian history. Sogdian mints in Samarkand copied its design, Xinjiang has yielded hoards of thousands, and strings of Kaiyuan coins went down with a ninth-century ship bound from China for the Middle East, wrecked off Belitung in the Java Sea.</p><p>The 開元通寶 also shows how the same written inscription could circulate across communities that assigned it different local readings. Even in China people disagreed about the order: read around the square hole instead of top-bottom-right-left, the legend comes out as 開通元寶, and that misreading was common enough to inspire the \"yuanbao\" names of later coins.</p><ul><li><strong>Chinese (Mandarin):</strong> kāiyuán tōngbǎo — the modern standard reading; the Tang court itself said something closer to <em>khoj ngjwon thuwng pawX</em> (Baxter's Middle Chinese notation, where a final -X marks the rising tone)</li><li><strong>Japanese:</strong> kaigen tsūhō — a Sino-Japanese reading that is mostly 漢音, though 通 <em>tsū</em> is not the Kan-on <em>tō</em> (dictionaries file it as 呉音 or 慣用音); Japan's own earliest coins, the Fuhonsen and then the Wadō kaichin of 708, were modelled on it, calligraphy and all</li><li><strong>Korean:</strong> gaewon tongbo — the Sino-Korean reading, still used in numismatic literature today</li><li><strong>Vietnamese:</strong> khai nguyên thông bảo — the Sino-Vietnamese reading, preserving features of late-Tang (8th–9th century) Chinese phonology that Mandarin later lost</li></ul><p>The divergence is most audible in the second character, 元. In Mandarin it is <em>yuán</em>; in Japanese <em>gen</em>; in Vietnamese <em>nguyên</em>. The Tang syllable began with a velar nasal (<em>ngjwon</em>): Vietnamese keeps the ng- intact, Japanese keeps a trace of it as g-, and Mandarin has lost it entirely, because the borrowed readings froze while Mandarin went on changing.</p><p>From a temple pagoda site in Japan's Nara Prefecture to the oases of the Silk Road and the seabed of the Java Sea, the coins all bear the same inscription. The characters were the passport; the pronunciation was the local accent.</p>"},"sources":["Peng Xinwei, A Monetary History of China (中国货币史), Shanghai People's Press, 1965","Edward Schafer, The Golden Peaches of Samarkand, UC Press, 1963",{"text":"奈良国立博物館 — 開元通宝（奈良県霊安寺塔跡出土）","url":"https://www.narahaku.go.jp/collection/1059-7.html"},"Axel Schuessler, ABC Etymological Dictionary of Old Chinese, University of Hawaii Press, 2007",{"text":"Wikipedia: Kaiyuan Tongbao","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kaiyuan_Tongbao"},{"text":"Roots.sg (National Heritage Board, Singapore): Bronze coins from the Tang Shipwreck","url":"https://www.roots.gov.sg/Collection-Landing/listing/1279376"}]},{"id":"korean-eumhun-tradition","icon":"📖","tags":["sinosphere","kanji","korea","pedagogy","hanja"],"title":{"en":"물 수 (水): Why Korea Teaches Chinese Characters with Native Korean Glosses","ja":"물 수（水）：韓国が漢字を固有語の訓で教える理由","ko":"물 수(水): 한국이 한자를 고유어 훈으로 가르치는 이유","zh":"물 수（水）：韩国为何以固有语训读教授汉字","yue":"물 수（水）：韓國點解用固有語嚟教漢字","vi":"물 수(水): Tại Sao Hàn Quốc Dạy Chữ Hán Bằng Cách Chú Giải Tiếng Hàn Thuần","th":"물 수 (水): เหตุใดเกาหลีจึงสอนอักษรจีนด้วยคำอธิบายภาษาเกาหลีพื้นเมือง","id":"물 수 (水): Mengapa Korea Mengajarkan Karakter Tionghoa dengan Gloss Korea Asli","hi":"물 수 (水): कोरिया चीनी अक्षर देशज कोरियाई टिप्पणियों के साथ क्यों सिखाता है","de":"물 수 (水): Warum Korea chinesische Schriftzeichen mit koreanischen Glossen lehrt","fr":"물 수 (水) : Pourquoi la Corée enseigne les caractères chinois avec des gloses coréennes natives","it":"물 수 (水): Perché la Corea insegna i caratteri cinesi con glosse coreane native","es":"물 수 (水): Por qué Corea enseña los caracteres chinos con glosas coreanas nativas","pt":"물 수 (水): Por que a Coreia ensina caracteres chineses com glosas coreanas nativas","ru":"물 수 (水): Почему Корея преподаёт китайские иероглифы с корейскими глоссами","uk":"물 수 (水): Чому Корея викладає китайські ієрогліфи з корейськими глосами","ar":"물 수 (水): لماذا تُدرّس كوريا الأحرف الصينية بتعليقات كورية أصيلة","he":"물 수 (水): מדוע קוריאה מלמדת תווים סינים עם ביאורים קוריאניים מקוריים","sw":"물 수 (水): Kwa Nini Korea Hufundisha Herufi za Kichina na Maelezo ya Kikorea Asili"},"summary":{"en":"Korean schools teach a Hanja character with a native Korean meaning label (訓, hun) and a Sino-Korean reading (音, eum). Unlike Japanese kun-readings, the Korean gloss normally names the character rather than serving as its reading in ordinary text.","ja":"韓国の学校では各漢字を固有語の意味（訓）と朝鮮漢字音（音）の二つで教えるが、これは千字文に遡る教育伝統であり、日本が訓読みを実際の書き言葉で使うのとは根本的に異なる。","ko":"한국 학교에서는 각 한자를 고유어 의미(훈)와 한자음(음)의 두 가지로 가르치는데, 이는 천자문에 뿌리를 둔 교육 전통으로 일본이 훈독을 실제 글쓰기에 사용하는 방식과 근본적으로 다르다.","zh":"韩国学校以固有语义（训）和汉字音（音）两种方式教授每个汉字，这一源于《千字文》的教学传统揭示了与日本在日常书写中使用训读的深层结构差异。","yue":"韓國學校用固有語義（訓）同漢字音（音）兩種方式教每個漢字，呢個源自《千字文》嘅教學傳統，揭示咗同日本日常書寫中使用訓讀嘅根本結構差異。","vi":"Trường học Hàn Quốc dạy mỗi chữ Hán bằng hai nhãn — nghĩa thuần Hàn (訓, hun) và âm Hán-Hàn (音, eum) — một truyền thống sư phạm có gốc từ Thiên Tự Văn, phản ánh sự khác biệt cấu trúc sâu sắc so với cách Nhật Bản dùng âm kun trong văn viết hàng ngày.","th":"โรงเรียนเกาหลีสอนแต่ละอักษรฮันจาด้วยสองป้ายกำกับ — ความหมายภาษาเกาหลีพื้นเมือง (訓, hun) และเสียงฮัน-เกาหลี (音, eum) — ประเพณีการสอนที่มีรากจากชอนจามุน ซึ่งเผยให้เห็นความแตกต่างเชิงโครงสร้างจากวิธีที่ญี่ปุ่นใช้การอ่านคุนในการเขียนประจำวัน","id":"Sekolah Korea mengajarkan setiap karakter Hanja dengan dua label — makna Korea asli (訓, hun) dan bunyi Hanja-Korea (音, eum) — tradisi pedagogis yang berakar pada 千字文, mengungkapkan perbedaan struktural mendalam dari cara Jepang menggunakan bacaan kun dalam tulisan sehari-hari.","hi":"कोरियाई स्कूल प्रत्येक हांजा अक्षर को दो लेबल के साथ सिखाते हैं — देशज कोरियाई अर्थ (訓, हुन) और सिनो-कोरियाई ध्वनि (音, ईउम) — 千字文 में निहित यह शैक्षणिक परंपरा जापान द्वारा दैनिक लेखन में कुन-पठन के उपयोग से गहरे संरचनात्मक अंतर को दर्शाती है।","de":"Koreanische Schulen unterrichten jedes Hanja-Zeichen mit zwei Bezeichnungen — einer koreanischen Bedeutungsangabe (訓, hun) und einem sino-koreanischen Laut (音, eum) — eine in der 千字文 verwurzelte pädagogische Tradition, die einen tiefen strukturellen Unterschied dazu offenbart, wie Japan Kun-Lesungen im täglichen Schreiben einsetzt.","fr":"Les écoles coréennes enseignent chaque caractère Hanja avec deux étiquettes — un sens coréen natif (訓, hun) et un son sino-coréen (音, eum) — une tradition pédagogique enracinée dans le 千字文, révélant une différence structurelle profonde par rapport à la manière dont le Japon utilise les lectures kun dans l'écriture quotidienne.","it":"Le scuole coreane insegnano ogni carattere Hanja con due etichette — un significato coreano nativo (訓, hun) e un suono sino-coreano (音, eum) — una tradizione pedagogica radicata nel 千字文 che rivela una profonda differenza strutturale rispetto a come il Giappone usa le letture kun nella scrittura quotidiana.","es":"Las escuelas coreanas enseñan cada carácter Hanja con dos etiquetas — un significado coreano nativo (訓, hun) y un sonido sino-coreano (音, eum) — una tradición pedagógica enraizada en el 千字文 que revela una profunda diferencia estructural respecto a cómo Japón usa las lecturas kun en la escritura cotidiana.","pt":"As escolas coreanas ensinam cada caractere Hanja com dois rótulos — um significado coreano nativo (訓, hun) e um som sino-coreano (音, eum) — uma tradição pedagógica enraizada no 千字文 que revela uma profunda diferença estrutural em relação a como o Japão usa as leituras kun na escrita cotidiana.","ru":"Корейские школы преподают каждый иероглиф Ханджа с двумя пометками — корейским значением (訓, хун) и сино-корейским звуком (音, ым) — педагогическая традиция, уходящая корнями в 千字文 и обнаруживающая глубокое структурное отличие от того, как Япония использует кун-чтения в повседневном письме.","uk":"Корейські школи навчають кожному ієрогліфу Ханджа з двома позначками — корейським значенням (訓, хун) і сино-корейським звуком (音, им) — педагогічна традиція, що коренями сягає 千字文 і виявляє глибоку структурну відмінність від того, як Японія використовує кун-читання у щоденному письмі.","ar":"تُدرّس المدارس الكورية كل حرف هانجا بعلامتين — معنى كوري أصيل (訓، هون) وصوت صيني-كوري (音، ايوم) — تقليد تربوي متجذّر في 千字文 يكشف عن فارق بنيوي عميق عن الطريقة التي تستخدم بها اليابان قراءات كون في الكتابة اليومية.","he":"בתי הספר הקוריאניים מלמדים כל תו הַנְג'ה עם שתי תוויות — משמעות קוריאנית מקורית (訓, הוּן) וצליל סינו-קוריאני (音, אֶאוּם) — מסורת פדגוגית השורשת ב-千字文 המגלה הבדל מבני עמוק מהאופן שבו יפן משתמשת בקריאות קוּן בכתיבה יומיומית.","sw":"Shule za Korea hufundisha kila herufi ya Hanja na lebo mbili — maana ya Kikorea asilia (訓, hun) na sauti ya Sino-Korea (音, eum) — mila ya kufundisha yenye mizizi katika 千字文 inayoonyesha tofauti ya kina ya kimuundo kutoka jinsi Japani inavyotumia usomaji wa kun katika uandishi wa kila siku."},"body":{"en":"<h3>The Two-Label System</h3><p>Open any Korean Hanja textbook and you will find a distinctive pattern: each character is presented with two pieces of information. The character 水 appears as <strong>물 수 (水)</strong> — first the native Korean word <em>mul</em> (water), then the Sino-Korean syllable <em>su</em>. This pairing is called <strong>音訓</strong> (음훈, eum-hun): sound plus meaning.</p><p>The habit is centuries old. Korean children learned characters from the <strong>千字文</strong> (Thousand Character Classic), a 6th-century Chinese primer, and once the Korean alphabet existed (1446) primers could spell out the gloss and the sound of every single character. The <em>Hunmong chahoe</em> of 1527 does this for 3,360 characters; the oldest surviving glossed Thousand Character Classic was printed in Gwangju in 1575, and the most famous, written out by the calligrapher Han Seok-bong, in 1583. The format has barely changed since.</p><h3>More Pairs</h3><ul><li>山 = <strong>메 산</strong> (me san) — mountain. <em>Me</em> is an old word that modern Korean has otherwise dropped in favour of 산 itself; it survives mainly here and in compounds like 멧돼지 <em>metdwaeji</em>, \"mountain pig\", the wild boar</li><li>人 = <strong>사람 인</strong> (saram in) — person</li><li>木 = <strong>나무 목</strong> (namu mok) — tree</li><li>日 = <strong>날 일</strong> (nal il) — day (the gloss picks the \"day\" sense; \"sun\" in Korean is 해 <em>hae</em>)</li></ul><h3>Why This Is Not the Same as Japanese 訓読み</h3><p>A common mistake is to assume Korean 훈 (hun) functions like Japanese 訓読み (kun-yomi). The difference is fundamental: in Japanese, kun-yomi are <em>active reading strategies</em> used every day in writing — a text about water will actually use the word <em>mizu</em> (訓 of 水) written with the character. Korean hun, by contrast, is a <em>pedagogical memory aid</em>. Today nobody writes 水 and expects it to be read as <em>mul</em>; in modern Korean text, 水 is read as Sino-Korean <em>su</em>.</p><p>It was not always so. Old Korean scribes writing their own language in characters (the <em>idu</em> and <em>hyangchal</em> systems) did read some characters by their native meaning, much as Japanese still does. A trace even survives in the names of the Korean letters, first recorded in that 1527 primer. Spelling the name of ㄷ as 池末 and ㅅ as 時衣, Ch'oe Sejin could find no Chinese character pronounced <em>-geut</em> or <em>-ot</em>, so he marked 末 \"end\" and 衣 \"clothes\" to be read by their native glosses, 귿 and 옷. That is why the letters are still called 디귿 <em>digeut</em> and 시옷 <em>siot</em>.</p><p>The hun is a mnemonic anchor, not a live reading. It tells the learner: this abstract character means the same thing as the native word you already know. Once learned, the character is used only with its Sino-Korean sound in actual writing.</p><p>This makes Korean Hanja usage structurally closer to how European learners approach Greek or Latin roots — you know that <em>aqua</em> means water, which helps you decode <em>aquifer</em>, but you never write <em>aqua</em> in an English sentence.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"focus\" data-code=\"ko\">Focus Korean on map</button></div>"},"sources":["이기문 (Lee Ki-Moon), 『訓蒙字會 硏究』 (Hunmong chahoe yŏn'gu), Seoul National University Press, 1993","Iksop Lee & S. Robert Ramsey, The Korean Language, State University of New York Press, 2000",{"text":"National Institute of Korean Language — Hanja education overview","url":"https://www.korean.go.kr/"},"Gari Ledyard, The Korean Language Reform of 1446, UC Berkeley dissertation, 1966",{"text":"Korean Wikipedia: 훈몽자회 (Hunmong chahoe; letter names 池末, 時衣)","url":"https://ko.wikipedia.org/wiki/훈몽자회"},{"text":"Korean Wikipedia: 천자문 (Gwangju 1575 and Seokbong 1583 editions)","url":"https://ko.wikipedia.org/wiki/천자문"}]},{"id":"cantonese-script-uniquely","icon":"🀄","tags":["cantonese","writing","sinosphere"],"title":{"en":"Cantonese Characters in Written 粵語白話文","ja":"普通話にない文字：粤語だけの漢字","ko":"표준어에 없는 글자: 광둥어만의 한자","zh":"普通话没有的字：粤语专用汉字","yue":"普通話冇嘅字：粵語獨有嘅漢字","vi":"Chữ Hán không có trong tiếng Phổ Thông: Chữ riêng của tiếng Quảng Đông","th":"อักษรที่ภาษาจีนกลางไม่มี: อักษรฮั่นเฉพาะของภาษากวางตุ้ง","id":"Karakter yang Tidak Ada di Mandarin: Hanzi Eksklusif Kanton","hi":"मंदारिन में न होने वाले अक्षर: कैंटोनीज़ के अपने हान्ज़ी","de":"Zeichen, die Mandarin nicht hat: Kantonesische Exklusiv-Hanzi","fr":"Des caractères absents du mandarin : les hanzi exclusifs du cantonais","it":"Caratteri assenti dal mandarino: i sinogrammi esclusivi del cantonese","es":"Caracteres que el mandarín no tiene: los hanzi exclusivos del cantonés","pt":"Caracteres que o mandarim não tem: os hanzi exclusivos do cantonês","ru":"Знаки, которых нет в мандаринском: исключительные ханьзи кантонского","uk":"Знаки, яких немає в мандаринській: виключні ханьзі кантонської","ar":"حروف لا وجود لها في الماندرين: الهانزي الخاصة بالكانتونية","he":"תווים שאינם קיימים במנדרינית: ה-Hanzi הייחודיים לקנטונזית","sw":"Herufi zisizo katika Mandari: Hanzi za kipekee za Kikantoni"},"summary":{"en":"Written Cantonese (粵語白話文) uses characters such as 嘅, 喺, 唔, 咁, and 啲 to represent Cantonese grammar. These characters appear in Hong Kong newspapers and social media but are generally absent from standard written Mandarin.","ja":"粵語白話文は、嘅・喺・唔・咁・啲など粤語固有の文法を表すために新たに造られた漢字を使用し、香港の新聞やSNSに毎日登場するが、普通話の書き言葉では使われない。","ko":"광둥어 백화문(粵語白話文)은 嘅·喺·唔·咁·啲 등 광둥어 문법을 표현하기 위해 만들어진 한자를 사용하며, 홍콩 신문과 소셜 미디어에서 매일 쓰이지만 표준 중국어 표기에서는 쓰이지 않는다.","zh":"粤语白话文使用嘅、喺、唔、咁、啲等专为粤语语法而创造的汉字，每天出现在香港报纸和社交媒体上，但在普通话书面语中并不使用。","yue":"粵語白話文用嘅、喺、唔、咁、啲呢啲專門為粵語文法而造嘅字，每日喺香港報紙同社交媒體出現，但係普通話書面語唔會用呢啲字。","vi":"Văn viết tiếng Quảng Đông (粵語白話文) sử dụng các chữ Hán được tạo ra riêng cho ngữ pháp Quảng Đông — 嘅, 喺, 唔, 咁, 啲 — xuất hiện hàng ngày trên báo chí và mạng xã hội Hồng Kông nhưng không được dùng trong văn viết tiếng Phổ Thông chuẩn.","th":"ภาษากวางตุ้งเขียน (粵語白話文) ใช้อักษรฮั่นที่ถูกประดิษฐ์ขึ้นเพื่อไวยากรณ์กวางตุ้งโดยเฉพาะ ได้แก่ 嘅 喺 唔 咁 啲 ปรากฏในหนังสือพิมพ์และโซเชียลมีเดียฮ่องกงทุกวัน แต่ไม่ใช้ในการเขียนภาษาจีนกลางมาตรฐาน","id":"Bahasa Kanton tertulis (粵語白話文) menggunakan karakter Han yang diciptakan khusus untuk tata bahasa Kanton — 嘅, 喺, 唔, 咁, 啲 — yang muncul setiap hari di koran dan media sosial Hong Kong tetapi tidak dipakai dalam penulisan Mandarin standar.","hi":"कैंटोनीज़ लिखित भाषा (粵語白話文) में कैंटोनीज़ व्याकरण के लिए विशेष रूप से बनाए गए हान अक्षरों का उपयोग होता है — 嘅, 喺, 唔, 咁, 啲 — जो हांगकांग के समाचार पत्रों और सोशल मीडिया में प्रतिदिन दिखते हैं लेकिन मानक मंदारिन लेखन में इनका कोई स्थान नहीं है।","de":"Das schriftliche Kantonesisch (粵語白話文) verwendet Han-Zeichen, die speziell für die kantonesische Grammatik erfunden wurden — 嘅, 喺, 唔, 咁, 啲 — die täglich in Hongkonger Zeitungen und sozialen Medien erscheinen, im schriftlichen Mandarin jedoch keinen Platz haben.","fr":"Le cantonais écrit (粵語白話文) utilise des caractères han inventés spécifiquement pour la grammaire cantonaise — 嘅, 喺, 唔, 咁, 啲 — qui apparaissent quotidiennement dans les journaux et les réseaux sociaux de Hong Kong, mais n’ont aucune place dans l'écriture mandarine standard.","it":"Il cantonese scritto (粵語白話文) usa caratteri han inventati specificamente per la grammatica cantonese — 嘅, 喺, 唔, 咁, 啲 — che compaiono ogni giorno sui giornali e sui social media di Hong Kong, ma non hanno alcuno spazio nella scrittura mandarino standard.","es":"El cantonés escrito (粵語白話文) utiliza caracteres han inventados específicamente para la gramática cantonesa — 嘅, 喺, 唔, 咁, 啲 — que aparecen a diario en periódicos y redes sociales de Hong Kong, pero no tienen cabida en la escritura mandarín estándar.","pt":"O cantonês escrito (粵語白話文) usa caracteres han inventados especificamente para a gramática cantonesa — 嘅, 喺, 唔, 咁, 啲 — que aparecem diariamente em jornais e redes sociais de Hong Kong, mas não têm lugar na escrita mandarim padrão.","ru":"Письменный кантонский (粵語白話文) использует иероглифы хань, специально созданные для кантонской грамматики — 嘅, 喺, 唔, 咁, 啲 — которые ежедневно появляются в гонконгских газетах и социальных сетях, но не используются в стандартном письменном мандаринском.","uk":"Письмова кантонська (粵語白話文) використовує ієрогліфи хань, спеціально створені для кантонської граматики — 嘅, 喺, 唔, 咁, 啲 — які щодня з'являються в гонконгських газетах і соціальних мережах, але не вживаються в стандартному мандаринському письмі.","ar":"تستخدم اللغة الكانتونية المكتوبة (粵語白話文) حروف هانزي اخترعت خصيصاً لقواعد الكانتونية — 嘅 و喺 و唔 و咁 و啲 — تظهر يومياً في صحف هونغ كونغ ووسائل التواصل الاجتماعي لكن لا مكان لها في الكتابة الصينية المعيارية.","he":"הקנטונזית הכתובה (粵語白話文) משתמשת בתווי האן שהומצאו במיוחד לדקדוק הקנטונזי — 嘅, 喺, 唔, 咁, 啲 — המופיעים מדי יום בעיתוני הונג קונג וברשתות החברתיות, אך אין להם מקום בכתיבה הסינית הסטנדרטית.","sw":"Kikatoni kilichoandikwa (粵語白話文) kinatumia herufi za Han zilizovumbuliwa mahususi kwa sarufi ya Kikantoni — 嘅, 喺, 唔, 咁, 啲 — zinazoonekana kila siku katika magazeti na mitandao ya kijamii ya Hong Kong lakini hazitumiki katika uandishi wa Mandari."},"body":{"en":"<h3>A Writing System Within a Writing System</h3><p>When Hong Kongers type on their phones or read a tabloid, they encounter characters that standard written Chinese rarely if ever uses. These are Cantonese dialect characters (方言字), written for Cantonese words and grammar that have no direct equivalent in Mandarin. Many are built by adding the \"mouth\" radical 口 to a character with a similar sound, flagging it as a spoken, colloquial word. Some come with a story: 咩 <em>me1</em> \"what?\" is written with a character that originally stood for a sheep's bleat (口 \"mouth\" + 羊 \"sheep\").</p><p>The five workhorses are:</p><ul><li><strong>嘅 ge3</strong> — genitive/attributive particle (Mandarin uses 的 de)</li><li><strong>喺 hai2</strong> — locative copula 'to be at' (Mandarin: 在 zài)</li><li><strong>唔 m4</strong> — negation prefix (Mandarin: 不 bù / 沒 méi)</li><li><strong>咁 gam3</strong> — 'so / such / like this' (Mandarin: 這樣 zhèyàng)</li><li><strong>啲 di1</strong> — plural/partitive 'some / a bit' (Mandarin: 一些 yīxiē)</li></ul><p>These characters are not typos or slang abbreviations; all five are encoded in Unicode's basic CJK Unified Ideographs block and appear in Hong Kong print every day. Apple Daily (until it was forced to close in 2021) and Oriental Daily carried 粵語白話文 (Cantonese vernacular writing) for years in their columns, entertainment and gossip pages and racing sections — the news pages themselves stayed in Standard Written Chinese, with Cantonese surfacing there mainly in headlines and quoted speech — and they are everywhere in Hong Kongers' social-media posts and messages.</p><p>The mainland PRC, however, leaves most of them out of its standard character lists: 嘅, 喺, 咁 and 啲 are all absent from the 通用规范汉字表 (2013). 唔 is the exception — it sits at no. 4500 of that list's second-level table, but as a Mandarin interjection ('hmm'), not as the negator Cantonese uses it for. Some see a broader political and linguistic tension here: writing Cantonese in Cantonese quietly challenges the implicit assumption that Mandarin is the universal form of Chinese.</p><p>Cantonese also preserves the final stops -p, -t and -k that Mandarin lost, and it split its tones into upper and lower registers when the Middle Chinese voiced initials devoiced. Mandarin went through a similar split but then merged much of it back down to four tones, while Cantonese kept six (nine if the short checked syllables are counted separately), which is part of why even shared vocabulary is unrecognisable across the two. 嘅 most likely developed from the classifier 個 (Mandarin gè), and 唔 belongs to an ancient family of m- negatives that includes 無 and 毋, the same family as the syllabic negator <em>m</em> of Hokkien and Hakka. These are not modern inventions but centuries-old oral forms finally given their own graphic home.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"focus\" data-code=\"yue\">Show Cantonese on map</button></div>"},"sources":["Snow, Don. Cantonese as Written Language: The Growth of a Written Chinese Vernacular. Hong Kong University Press, 2004.","Bauer, Robert S. & Benedict, Paul K. Modern Cantonese Phonology. Mouton de Gruyter, 1997.",{"text":"Unicode Chart for Cantonese-specific characters (CJK Unified Ideographs)","url":"https://www.unicode.org/charts/PDF/U4E00.pdf"},"Cheung, Kwan-hin & Bauer, Robert S. The Representation of Cantonese with Chinese Characters. Journal of Chinese Linguistics Monograph Series No. 18, 2002.",{"text":"Wikipedia: Written Cantonese","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Written_Cantonese"}]},{"id":"shanghainese-ipa-wugniu","icon":"🔊","tags":["wu","phonology","middle-chinese","sinosphere"],"title":{"en":"Wugniu: Romanisation for Shanghainese Three-Way Stop Contrasts","ja":"呉語拼音（吳語拼音）：上海語の三者対立を救うローマ字","ko":"우어닙 (吳語拼音): 상하이어의 3중 대립을 살린 로마자","zh":"吴语拼音：保存上海话三组声母对立的拼音系统","yue":"吳語拼音：保住上海話三組聲母對立嘅拼音系統","vi":"Wugniu: Hệ thống Latinh hóa bảo tồn sự đối lập ba chiều trong tiếng Thượng Hải","th":"อักษรโรมันวูนีว: ระบบถอดเสียงที่ช่วยรักษาการแยกเสียงสามชั้นของภาษาเซี่ยงไฮ้","id":"Wugniu: Romanisasi yang Menyelamatkan Kontras Tiga Cara Bahasa Shanghainese","hi":"वुग्नियु: रोमनीकरण जिसने शंघाईनीज़ की तीन-तरफ़ा ध्वनि विपरीतता को बचाया","de":"Wugniu: die Romanisierung, die den Drei-Wege-Kontrast des Shanghainesen rettete","fr":"Wugniu : la romanisation qui a sauvé le triple contraste phonémique du shanghaïen","it":"Wugniu: la romanizzazione che ha salvato il contrasto a tre vie dello shanghainese","es":"Wugniu: la romanización que salvó el contraste de tres vías del shanghainés","pt":"Wugniu: a romanização que salvou o contraste tripartido do shangainês","ru":"Wugniu: романизация, сохранившая трёхсторонний фонемный контраст шанхайского","uk":"Wugniu: романізація, що зберегла тристоронній фонемний контраст шанхайської","ar":"Wugniu: النظام الروماني الذي أنقذ التمييز الثلاثي في لغة شنغهاي","he":"Wugniu: התעתיק הלטיני ששמר על הניגוד התלת-כיווני של השנגחאית","sw":"Wugniu: Utaratibu wa Kilatini Ulioihifadhi Tofauti ya Sauti Tatu za Kishanghai"},"summary":{"en":"Shanghainese (Wu) preserves a three-way distinction among stop consonants — voiced, voiceless aspirated, and voiceless unaspirated — inherited from Middle Chinese. The Wugniu romanisation represents all three contrasts in plain ASCII.","ja":"上海語（呉語）は、中古漢語から受け継いだ閉鎖音の有声・無声帯気・無声無気の三者対立を——シナ語の他のほとんどが失ったこの対立を——今も保存しており、吳語拼音（Wugniu）はこの三者すべてを標準ASCIIで書き分ける、現在もっとも広く使われているローマ字体系だ。","ko":"상하이어(우어)는 중고 중국어에서 물려받은 파열음의 유성·무기무성·유기무성 3중 대립을 — 다른 한어 계통에서는 거의 사라진 대립을 — 지금도 보존하고 있으며, 우어닙(吳語拼音)은 이 세 가지 대립을 표준 ASCII만으로 구분해 적도록 설계된, 현재 가장 널리 쓰이는 로마자 체계다.","zh":"上海话（吴语）保留了来自中古汉语的塞音三组对立——有声、清送气、清不送气——而这套对立在汉语其余方言中几乎已消失殆尽；吴语拼音（Wugniu）则是目前使用最广的现代拼音方案，设计上就是要用标准ASCII把这三组对立全部写得出来。","yue":"上海話（吳語）保留咗源自中古漢語嘅塞音三組對立——有聲、清送氣、清不送氣——而呢套對立喺漢語其餘方言入面幾乎已經消失晒；吳語拼音（Wugniu）就係而家用得最廣嘅現代拼音方案，設計上就係要用標準ASCII將三組對立全部寫得出嚟。","vi":"Tiếng Thượng Hải (Ngô) vẫn bảo tồn sự đối lập ba chiều của phụ âm tắc — hữu thanh, vô thanh có bật hơi, và vô thanh không bật hơi — thừa hưởng từ Trung cổ Hán ngữ và đã mất ở hầu hết các phương ngữ Hán khác, còn Wugniu là hệ thống Latinh hóa hiện đại được dùng rộng rãi nhất, được thiết kế để giữ cả ba sự đối lập hiện rõ bằng ASCII thuần.","th":"ภาษาเซี่ยงไฮ้ (ภาษาอู๋) ยังคงรักษาการแยกเสียงสามชั้นของพยัญชนะหยุดไว้ได้ — มีเสียง ไม่มีเสียงมีลม และไม่มีเสียงไม่มีลม — สืบทอดจากภาษาจีนกลางยุคกลางและสูญหายไปแล้วแทบทุกแห่งในตระกูลจีน ส่วน Wugniu คือระบบถอดเสียงสมัยใหม่ที่ใช้กันแพร่หลายที่สุด ออกแบบมาเพื่อคงการแยกเสียงทั้งสามไว้ให้เห็นได้ด้วย ASCII ล้วน","id":"Shanghainese (Wu) masih mempertahankan perbedaan tiga arah antara konsonan henti — bersuara, tak bersuara beraspirat, dan tak bersuara tak beraspirat — yang diwarisi dari Cina Pertengahan dan nyaris hilang di seluruh rumpun Sinitik lainnya, dan Wugniu adalah sistem romanisasi modern yang paling luas dipakai, dirancang untuk menjaga ketiga kontras itu tetap terlihat dalam ASCII biasa.","hi":"शंघाईनीज़ (वू) स्पर्श व्यंजनों की तीन-तरफ़ा विपरीतता को आज भी संरक्षित रखता है — सघोष, अघोष महाप्राण, और अघोष अल्पप्राण — जो मध्य चीनी से विरासत में मिली है और शेष लगभग पूरे सिनिटिक परिवार से लुप्त हो चुकी है, और वुग्नियु आज सबसे व्यापक रूप से प्रयुक्त आधुनिक रोमनीकरण प्रणाली है, जिसे तीनों विपरीतताओं को सादे ASCII में दिखाए रखने के लिए डिज़ाइन किया गया है।","de":"Shanghainesi (Wu) bewahrt bis heute einen Drei-Wege-Kontrast bei Verschlusslauten — stimmhaft, stimmlos aspiriert und stimmlos nicht-aspiriert — aus dem Mittelchinesischen, der im übrigen Sinitischen fast überall verloren ging, und Wugniu ist das heute meistgenutzte Romanisierungssystem, das alle drei Kontraste in reinem ASCII sichtbar hält.","fr":"Le shanghaïen (wu) préserve encore un triple contraste parmi les consonnes occlusives — sonore, sourd aspiré et sourd non-aspiré — hérité du moyen chinois et perdu presque partout ailleurs dans le domaine sinitique, et le Wugniu est le système de romanisation moderne le plus répandu, conçu pour garder ces trois contrastes visibles en ASCII pur.","it":"Lo shanghainese (wu) preserva ancora un contrasto a tre vie tra le consonanti occlusive — sonora, sorda aspirata e sorda non aspirata — ereditato dal cinese medio e perduto quasi ovunque nel resto del sinitico, e il Wugniu è il sistema di romanizzazione moderno più diffuso, progettato per mantenere visibili tutti e tre i contrasti in puro ASCII.","es":"El shanghainés (wu) sigue preservando un contraste de tres vías entre consonantes oclusivas — sonora, sorda aspirada y sorda no aspirada — heredado del chino medio y perdido en casi todo el resto del sinítico, y el Wugniu es el sistema de romanización moderno más extendido, diseñado para mantener visibles los tres contrastes en ASCII simple.","pt":"O shanghainês (wu) continua a preservar um contraste tripartido entre consoantes oclusivas — sonora, surda aspirada e surda não-aspirada — herdado do chinês médio e perdido em quase todo o resto do sinítico, e o Wugniu é o sistema de romanização moderno mais usado, concebido para manter os três contrastes visíveis em ASCII simples.","ru":"Шанхайский (ву) до сих пор сохраняет трёхсторонний контраст среди взрывных согласных — звонкий, глухой придыхательный и глухой непридыхательный — унаследованный из средневекового китайского и утраченный почти везде в остальных китайских диалектах, а Wugniu — наиболее широко используемая современная система романизации, разработанная так, чтобы все три контраста были видны в обычном ASCII.","uk":"Шанхайська (у) досі зберігає тристоронній контраст між зімкненими приголосними — дзвінкими, глухими придиховими та глухими непридиховими — успадкований із середньокитайської та втрачений майже всюди в решті китайських діалектів, а Wugniu — найпоширеніша сучасна система романізації, розроблена так, щоб усі три контрасти було видно у звичайному ASCII.","ar":"لا تزال لغة شنغهاي (الووية) تحتفظ بتمييز ثلاثي بين الأصوات الانفجارية — مجهورة، مهموسة مطبوعة، ومهموسة غير مطبوعة — ورثته من الصينية الوسطى وفقدته تقريبًا كل بقية اللهجات الصينية، وWugniu هو أوسع الأنظمة الرومانية الحديثة انتشارًا، وقد صُمم ليُبقي التمييزات الثلاثة ظاهرة بحروف ASCII وحدها.","he":"השנגחאית (וו) עדיין שומרת על ניגוד תלת-כיווני בין עיצורי הפסקה — קולי, חרש מושאב וחרש לא-מושאב — שהתקבל מהסינית התיכונה ואבד כמעט בכל שאר הניבים הסיניטיים, ו-Wugniu היא מערכת התעתיק הלטיני המודרנית הנפוצה ביותר, שתוכננה לשמור על שלושת הניגודים גלויים ב-ASCII פשוט.","sw":"Kishanghai (Wu) bado kinahifadhi tofauti ya sauti tatu kati ya konsonanti za kuzuia — zenye sauti, bila sauti zenye pumzi, na bila sauti zisizo na pumzi — zilizoibuliwa kutoka Kichina cha Kati na ambazo zimetoweka karibu kila mahali pengine katika lugha za Kisinitek, na Wugniu ndio mfumo wa Kilatini wa kisasa unaotumika zaidi kuliko yote, uliobuniwa kuweka utofauti wote mitatu ukionekana kwa ASCII ya kawaida."},"body":{"en":"<h3>When Three Sounds Become One, or Stay Three</h3><p>In Mandarin, the old Middle Chinese (中古漢語) three-way stop system collapsed long ago: voiced initials like <em>b-</em> merged into the voiceless categories. Shanghainese — the prestige dialect of Wu (吳語) — never completed that merger. It retains a three-way phonemic contrast that Mandarin lost centuries ago:</p><ul><li><strong>Voiceless unaspirated</strong>: 搭 <em>taq</em> (to put up, to build)</li><li><strong>Voiced</strong>: 踏 <em>daq</em> (to tread)</li><li><strong>Voiceless aspirated</strong>: 塔 <em>thaq</em> (pagoda)</li></ul><p>One caution: at the start of a word, the \"voiced\" stops are not voiced in the English sense. They sound voiceless but carry a breathy, murmured quality and always come with a low (\"light\") tone; they become fully voiced only between vowels.</p><p>The Wugniu romanisation (吳語拼音), built by Wu-language enthusiasts on the Wu Chinese Society scheme of the mid-2000s and settled in its present form in the 2010s, keeps all three series apart in plain ASCII: <strong>b- / d- / g-</strong> for the voiced initials, <em>p- / t- / k-</em> for the voiceless unaspirated, and <em>ph- / th- / kh-</em> for the voiceless aspirated. One spelling per series, no diacritics.</p><p>Why does this matter for historical linguistics? Middle Chinese had exactly this three-way system — it is reconstructed partly <em>because</em> languages like Shanghainese preserve it. Wu bears on Old Chinese too, but only at one remove: its voiced series continues the Middle Chinese voiced obstruents, and reconstructions such as Baxter and Sagart's start from that Middle Chinese system before working further back through Shijing rhymes and phonetic series.</p><p>Voiced obstruents are rare in Sinitic outside Wu and Xiang. Cantonese kept only two (voiceless unaspirated vs. aspirated; the old voiced series devoiced, coming out aspirated in the level and rising tones and unaspirated elsewhere). Hokkien does have voiced <em>b-</em> and <em>g-</em>, but they come from old nasals (文 \"writing\" is <em>bûn</em>, from Middle Chinese <em>m-</em>), not from the Middle Chinese voiced stops, which devoiced there too. The other big holdout is Old Xiang, the conservative Lou–Shao dialects of Hunan around Shuangfeng (some 11 million speakers), which keep the voiced series where New Xiang, as in Changsha, devoiced it. Wu and Old Xiang are the main living witnesses.</p><p>Shanghainese is under severe pressure from Mandarin. Only about half of Shanghai's population can now hold a conversation in it, and in a 2010 survey 62.6% of primary-school pupils said Mandarin was their first language at home. There are signs of a fightback, though: buses have announced stops in Shanghainese since 2013, and Apple's Siri learned it in 2017.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"focus\" data-code=\"wuu\">Show Wu region on map</button></div>"},"sources":["Kurpaska, Maria. Chinese Language(s): A Look Through the Prism of 'The Great Dictionary of Modern Chinese Dialects'. De Gruyter Mouton, 2010.","Norman, Jerry. Chinese. Cambridge University Press, 1988.",{"text":"吳語學堂 (Wugniu) — 吳語學堂拼音方案","url":"https://www.wugniu.com/"},"Baxter, William H. & Sagart, Laurent. Old Chinese: A New Reconstruction. Oxford University Press, 2014.",{"text":"Wikipedia: Shanghainese (phonology, sociolinguistic situation)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shanghainese"},{"text":"Wikipedia: Xiang Chinese","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xiang_Chinese"}]},{"id":"pan-sinosphere-counting","icon":"🔢","tags":["sinosphere","numbers","phonology","history"],"title":{"en":"Counting from 1 to 10 Across the Sinosphere","ja":"「一」の百通りの読み方――漢字圏の数詞を徹底比較","ko":"하나를 말하는 백 가지 방법: 한자문화권의 1~10","zh":"「一」的百种读法——汉字圈数词全比较","yue":"一百種講法講「一」——漢字圈數詞大比較","vi":"Trăm cách nói số Một: Đếm 1–10 khắp vùng Hán tự","th":"ร้อยวิธีพูดว่าหนึ่ง: การนับ 1–10 ทั่วโลกฮั่น","id":"Seratus Cara Mengucapkan Satu: Menghitung 1–10 di Dunia Sinosfer","hi":"एक कहने के सौ तरीके: Sinosphere में 1–10 की गिनती","de":"Hundert Arten, Eins zu sagen: Zahlen 1–10 im Sinosphären-Vergleich","fr":"Cent façons de dire Un : compter de 1 à 10 dans la sinosphère","it":"Cento modi per dire Uno: contare da 1 a 10 nella sinosfera","es":"Cien formas de decir Uno: contar del 1 al 10 en la sinosfera","pt":"Cem formas de dizer Um: contando de 1 a 10 na sinosfera","ru":"Сто способов сказать «Один»: счёт от 1 до 10 в синосфере","uk":"Сто способів сказати «Один»: лічба від 1 до 10 у синосфері","ar":"مئة طريقة لقول واحد: العدّ من 1 إلى 10 عبر الصينوسفير","he":"מאה דרכים לומר אחד: ספירה 1–10 ברחבי הסינוספרה","sw":"Njia Mia za Kusema Moja: Kuhesabu 1–10 katika Sinosphere"},"summary":{"en":"The same character 一 is read in a dozen different ways across East Asia. Counting from one to ten shows 1,500 years of sound change, layers of borrowing, and why four is a number people avoid.","ja":"「一」という漢字は、東アジア全域で十数通りに読まれる。1から10まで数えてみると、1500年にわたる音変化と借用の層、そして「四」が避けられる理由が見えてくる。","ko":"같은 한자 一이 동아시아에서 열 가지 남짓한 방식으로 읽힌다. 하나부터 열까지 세어 보면 1,500년의 음운 변화와 켜켜이 쌓인 차용, 그리고 사람들이 왜 4를 피하는지가 보인다.","zh":"同一个汉字「一」在东亚有十几种读法。从一数到十，就能看出1500年的音变、层层叠叠的借用，以及人们为什么要躲着数字四。","yue":"同一個漢字「一」喺東亞有十幾種唔同讀法。由一數到十，就見到1500年嘅音變、一層層嘅借用，同埋點解大家咁避忌數字四。","vi":"Cùng một chữ Hán 一 được đọc theo cả chục cách khác nhau khắp Đông Á. Đếm từ một đến mười là thấy 1.500 năm biến đổi ngữ âm, những lớp vay mượn chồng lên nhau, và lý do người ta né số bốn.","th":"อักษรตัวเดียวกัน 一 ถูกอ่านต่างกันนับสิบแบบทั่วเอเชียตะวันออก การนับตั้งแต่หนึ่งถึงสิบเผยให้เห็นการเปลี่ยนแปลงเสียง 1,500 ปี ชั้นของการยืม และเหตุที่ผู้คนพากันเลี่ยงเลขสี่","id":"Karakter yang sama, 一, dibaca dengan selusin cara berbeda di Asia Timur. Berhitung dari satu sampai sepuluh memperlihatkan 1.500 tahun perubahan bunyi, lapisan-lapisan peminjaman, dan alasan orang menghindari angka empat.","hi":"एक ही चीनी वर्ण 一 पूर्वी एशिया में दर्जन भर तरीक़ों से पढ़ा जाता है। एक से दस तक की गिनती 1,500 वर्षों के ध्वनि-परिवर्तन, उधार की परतों, और इस राज़ को खोल देती है कि लोग चार की संख्या से क्यों बचते हैं।","de":"Das gleiche Zeichen 一 wird in ganz Ostasien auf ein Dutzend verschiedene Arten gelesen. Zählt man von eins bis zehn, zeigen sich 1.500 Jahre Lautwandel, Schichten von Entlehnungen — und warum man der Vier lieber aus dem Weg geht.","fr":"Le même caractère 一 se lit d'une douzaine de façons différentes en Asie de l'Est. Compter de un à dix, c'est voir défiler 1 500 ans d'évolution phonétique, des couches d'emprunts, et la raison pour laquelle on évite le chiffre quatre.","it":"Lo stesso carattere 一 si legge in una dozzina di modi diversi in tutta l'Asia orientale. Contare da uno a dieci rivela 1.500 anni di mutamenti fonetici, strati di prestiti e il motivo per cui il quattro è un numero da evitare.","es":"El mismo carácter 一 se lee de una docena de maneras distintas en el este de Asia. Contar del uno al diez revela 1.500 años de cambios fonéticos, capas de préstamos y por qué el cuatro es un número que la gente evita.","pt":"O mesmo carácter 一 lê-se de uma dúzia de maneiras diferentes na Ásia Oriental. Contar de um a dez revela 1.500 anos de mudança sonora, camadas de empréstimo — e porque é que o quatro é um número que as pessoas evitam.","ru":"Один и тот же иероглиф 一 читается по всей Восточной Азии на дюжину ладов. Счёт от одного до десяти показывает 1500 лет звуковых изменений, слои заимствований — и то, почему числа четыре люди избегают.","uk":"Той самий ієрогліф 一 по всій Східній Азії читають на дюжину ладів. Лічба від одного до десяти відкриває 1500 років звукових змін, шари запозичень — і те, чому числа чотири люди уникають.","ar":"يُقرأ الحرف نفسه 一 بنحو اثنتي عشرة طريقة مختلفة في شرق آسيا. والعدّ من واحد إلى عشرة يكشف 1500 عام من التحول الصوتي، وطبقات من الاقتراض، وسرّ تجنّب الناس للرقم أربعة.","he":"אותו סימן 一 נקרא בתריסר דרכים שונות במזרח אסיה. ספירה מאחת עד עשר חושפת 1,500 שנה של שינויי הגה, רבדים של שאילה, ואת הסיבה שאנשים מתרחקים מהמספר ארבע.","sw":"Herufi ileile 一 inasomwa kwa njia kumi na mbili tofauti kote Asia Mashariki. Kuhesabu kutoka moja hadi kumi kunaonyesha miaka 1,500 ya mabadiliko ya sauti, matabaka ya ukopaji, na sababu ya watu kuikwepa namba nne."},"body":{"en":"<h3>One Character, Many Voices</h3><p>The numeral <strong>一</strong> (one) is the simplest Chinese character — a single horizontal stroke. Yet across the Sinosphere it is pronounced in strikingly different ways: <em>yī</em> in Mandarin, <em>yat</em> in Cantonese, <em>it</em> in Hokkien/Min, <em>yit</em> in Hakka, <em>iq</em> in Wu (Shanghainese), <em>ichi/itsu</em> in Japanese, <em>il</em> in Korean, <em>nhất</em> in Vietnamese. The Chinese varieties mostly inherited their readings as descendants of Middle Chinese, the language of the Sui and Tang dynasties (Min split off earlier, and its literary readings, such as Hokkien <em>it</em>, are themselves a later borrowed layer); Japanese, Korean and Vietnamese borrowed theirs, and each borrowed reading is a fossil of the Chinese being spoken when it was taken over.</p><div class='trivia-actions'><button type='button' class='trivia-action' data-action='setchar' data-char='一'>🔢 Show 一 on map</button></div><h3>Comparative Table: 1–10</h3><ul><li><strong>Mandarin (zh):</strong> yī, èr, sān, sì, wǔ, liù, qī, bā, jiǔ, shí</li><li><strong>Cantonese (yue):</strong> yat, yi, saam, sei, ng, luk, chat, baat, gau, sap</li><li><strong>Min Nan (nan):</strong> it/tsi̍t, jī/nn̄g, sam/sann, sì, gōo, la̍k, tshit, peh, káu, si̍p/tsa̍p</li><li><strong>Hakka (hak):</strong> yit, ngi, sam, si, ng, liuk, chhit, pat, kiu, sip</li><li><strong>Wu / Shanghainese (wuu):</strong> iq, gni, se, sy, ng, loq, chiq, paq, cieu, zeq</li><li><strong>Japanese Go-on 呉音:</strong> ichi, ni, san, shi, go, roku, shichi, hachi, ku, jū</li><li><strong>Japanese Kan-on 漢音:</strong> itsu, ji, san, shi, go, riku, shitsu, hatsu, kyū, shū</li><li><strong>Korean (ko):</strong> il, i, sam, sa, o, yuk, chil, pal, gu, sip</li><li><strong>Sino-Vietnamese (vi):</strong> nhất, nhị, tam, tứ, ngũ, lục, thất, bát, cửu, thập</li></ul><h3>When One Is Not <em>yī</em></h3><p>Read a phone number aloud in Mandarin and the 1 comes out <em>yāo</em>, not <em>yī</em>. This is a substitution rather than a reading: the character is 幺, literally 'the smallest', and dictionaries give 一 the single reading <em>yī</em>. The motive is audibility: over a noisy line <em>yī</em> is easily confused with <em>qī</em> (7). Chinese military radio takes the idea all the way. Its digits 0–9 are 洞 幺 两 三 四 五 六 拐 八 勾 (<em>dòng, yāo, liǎng, sān, sì, wǔ, liù, guǎi, bā, gōu</em>), where 0 is <em>dòng</em> \"hole\" and 7 is <em>guǎi</em> \"bend\", after the digits' shapes. The habit has spread to civilians: the emergency numbers 110 and 119 are normally read <em>yāo-yāo-líng</em> and <em>yāo-yāo-jiǔ</em>. Cantonese does not do this; its one everyday 幺 is 十三幺, a mahjong hand.</p><h3>Tang–Cantonese–Vietnamese Alignment</h3><p>The most striking pattern is how closely <strong>Cantonese</strong> and <strong>Sino-Vietnamese</strong> preserve Tang Dynasty (618–907 CE) phonology. Both retain final stop consonants (-t, -k, -p) that Mandarin has shed. Compare: Cantonese <em>yat</em> / Sino-Vietnamese <em>nhất</em> vs Mandarin <em>yī</em>. Cantonese inherited the stops directly, and the Sino-Vietnamese readings were borrowed mainly from the Chinese of the late Tang, so both kept what Middle Chinese had. The northern dialects that became Mandarin later lost those stops through their own sound changes — by the time of the <em>Zhongyuan Yinyun</em> rhyme book of 1324 the old -p/-t/-k contrast had already collapsed and the checked-tone syllables were being redistributed among the other tones.</p><h3>Japan's Two-Layer System</h3><p><strong>呉音 (Go-on)</strong> — Wu pronunciations — arrived via the Korean Peninsula in the 5th–6th centuries, reflecting Southern Chinese phonology. <strong>漢音 (Kan-on)</strong> — Han pronunciations — were imported directly from Tang Chang'an by Japanese embassies in the 7th–9th centuries. A third layer, <strong>唐音 (Tō-on)</strong>, came via Zen Buddhism from Song/Ming China. Japanese counting mostly uses Go-on (<em>ichi, ni, san, shi, go, roku, shichi, hachi, ku, jū</em>), with two exceptions in everyday speech: 9 is usually <em>kyū</em>, the Kan-on reading, and 4 and 7 are often replaced by the native <em>yon</em> and <em>nana</em>.</p><h3>The Death Number: 四 = 死</h3><p>In Japanese (and across the Sinosphere), <strong>4</strong> carries a shadow. In Go-on, 四 is read <em>shi</em> — identical to 死 (<em>shi</em>, death). This drives widespread <strong>tetraphobia</strong>: some hospitals and apartment blocks in Japan skip the number 4, and 49 is avoided too, because <em>shi-ku</em> sounds like \"suffer and die\". Korean 사 <em>sa</em> is also a homophone of \"death\", and South Korean lifts sometimes label the fourth floor \"F\" instead of 4. Nokia never released a phone in a 4000 series. The native Japanese reading <em>yon</em> (a later expansion of the native <em>yo</em>) is often preferred to dodge the omen.</p><div class='trivia-actions'><button type='button' class='trivia-action' data-action='setchar' data-char='四'>💀 Show 四 (the death number) on map</button><button type='button' class='trivia-action' data-action='focus' data-code='ja'>🗾 See Japanese Go-on readings</button></div><h3>Cultural Numerology</h3><p><strong>Eight</strong> (八, <em>bā</em>) is the luckiest number in Chinese culture because it sounds like 發 (<em>fā</em>, prosperity). <strong>Six</strong> symbolises smooth flow (六六大順), and <strong>nine</strong> sounds like 久 (<em>jiǔ</em>, longevity). Meanwhile <strong>four</strong> is avoided everywhere — in Mandarin 14 (<em>shísì</em>) sounds like \"is dead\" (是死 <em>shì sǐ</em>) and is considered even unluckier than 4, and 74 is feared as \"actually dead\".</p>"},"sources":["Pulleyblank, E.G. (1984). Middle Chinese: A Study in Historical Phonology. UBC Press.","Baxter, W.H. & Sagart, L. (2014). Old Chinese: A New Reconstruction. Oxford University Press.",{"text":"Sino-Vietnamese vocabulary (Wikipedia)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sino-Vietnamese_vocabulary"},{"text":"On'yomi: Go-on, Kan-on, To-on (Wikipedia)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/On%27yomi"},{"text":"Tetraphobia (Wikipedia)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tetraphobia"},{"text":"幺 yāo — 汉典 (Zdic): 数目一（称呼电话号码等）","url":"https://www.zdic.net/hans/%E5%B9%BA"},"Ferlus, M. (1992). Histoire abrégée de l'évolution des consonnes initiales du vietnamien et du sino-vietnamien. Mon-Khmer Studies 20, 111–125."]},{"id":"tea-tea-cha-cha","icon":"🍵","tags":["sinosphere","loanwords","trade-routes","phonology"],"title":{"en":"Tea and Chai: Two Transmission Pathways for 茶","ja":"「茶」の二つの語形——海路と陸路に沿った伝播","ko":"茶의 두 어형: 해로와 육로를 따른 전파","zh":"「茶」的两种词形——沿海路与陆路传播","yue":"一個「茶」字點樣將世界嘅茶詞彙一分為二——海路對陸路嘅語言地圖","vi":"Hai dạng từ của 茶: lan truyền theo đường biển và đường bộ","th":"ทีหรือชา: อักษรเดียวแบ่งโลกคำว่าชาออกเป็นสองค่าย","id":"Teh vs. Cha: Bagaimana Satu Karakter Membagi Dua Kosakata Teh Dunia","hi":"Tea बनाम Cha: एक अक्षर ने दुनिया की चाय-शब्दावली को दो भागों में बाँट दिया","de":"Tea gegen Cha: Wie ein Zeichen das Tee-Vokabular der Welt in zwei Lager spaltete","fr":"Tea contre Cha : comment un seul caractère a divisé le vocabulaire mondial du thé en deux","it":"Tea contro Cha: come un carattere ha diviso in due il vocabolario mondiale del tè","es":"Tea contra Cha: cómo un carácter dividió en dos el vocabulario mundial del té","pt":"Tea versus Cha: como um caractere dividiu o vocabulário mundial do chá em dois","ru":"Tea против Cha: как один иероглиф разделил словарь чая на два лагеря","uk":"Tea проти Cha: як один ієрогліф розколов словник чаю на два табори","ar":"تي مقابل شاي: كيف قسّم حرف واحد مفردات الشاي في العالم إلى معسكرين","he":"Tea מול Cha: כיצד סימן אחד חצה את אוצר המילים של התה לשניים","sw":"Tea dhidi ya Cha: Jinsi Herufi Moja Ilivyogawanya Msamiati wa Chai Duniani"},"summary":{"en":"The character 茶 has two historical pronunciation pathways: a southern sea-route form behind English 'tea' and a northern land-route form behind Russian 'chai'.","ja":"中国語の地域的な二つの語形は、海路と陸路を通じて各地へ伝わった。英語 tea とロシア語 чай はその代表例である。","ko":"중국어의 두 지역적 어형은 해로와 육로를 통해 여러 언어로 전해졌다. 영어 'tea'와 러시아어 'чай'가 대표적인 예다.","zh":"汉语的两种地域词形沿海路和陆路传入多种语言，英语 tea 与俄语 чай 是其中的代表。","yue":"「茶」自古有兩種讀音——南方海路形產生咗英文嘅「tea」，北方陸路形產生咗俄文嘅「чай」——由此可以解釋地球上幾乎所有嘅茶詞彙。","vi":"Hai dạng từ theo vùng của chữ 茶 đã đi vào nhiều ngôn ngữ theo đường biển và đường bộ; tiếng Anh 'tea' và tiếng Nga 'чай' là hai ví dụ.","th":"อักษร 茶 มีการออกเสียงโบราณสองแบบ — รูปแบบเส้นทางทะเลทางใต้ให้คำว่า tea ในภาษาอังกฤษ และรูปแบบเส้นทางบกทางเหนือให้คำว่า chai ในภาษารัสเซีย","id":"Karakter 茶 memiliki dua pengucapan kuno — bentuk jalur laut selatan melahirkan 'teh', dan bentuk jalur darat utara melahirkan 'chai' — bersama menjelaskan hampir setiap kata teh di Bumi.","hi":"अक्षर 茶 की दो प्राचीन उच्चारण हैं — दक्षिणी समुद्री मार्ग का रूप जिसने 'tea' को जन्म दिया, और उत्तरी भूमि मार्ग का रूप जिसने 'chai' को जन्म दिया।","de":"Das Zeichen 茶 hat zwei uralte Aussprachen — eine südliche Seewegform, die 'tea' hervorbrachte, und eine nördliche Landwegform, die 'chai' hervorbrachte — gemeinsam erklären sie fast jedes Tee-Wort der Welt.","fr":"Le caractère 茶 possède deux prononciations anciennes — une forme maritime méridionale donnant 'tea', et une forme terrestre nordique donnant 'chai' — expliquant ensemble presque tous les mots du thé.","it":"Il carattere 茶 ha due antiche pronunce — una forma marittima meridionale che ha originato 'tea', e una forma terrestre settentrionale che ha originato 'chai' — spiegando quasi ogni parola del tè nel mondo.","es":"El carácter 茶 tiene dos pronunciaciones antiguas — una forma marítima meridional que originó 'tea', y una forma terrestre norteña que originó 'chai' — explicando juntas casi todas las palabras del té en el mundo.","pt":"O caractere 茶 tem duas pronúncias antigas — uma forma marítima meridional que originou 'tea', e uma forma terrestre nortenha que originou 'chai' — explicando juntas quase todas as palavras de chá no mundo.","ru":"Иероглиф 茶 имеет два древних произношения — южная морская форма дала 'tea', а северная сухопутная форма дала 'chai' — вместе они объясняют почти каждое чайное слово на Земле.","uk":"Ієрогліф 茶 має два давніх вимови — південна морська форма дала 'tea', а північна сухопутна форма дала 'chai' — разом вони пояснюють майже кожне чайне слово на Землі.","ar":"للحرف 茶 نطقان قديمان — شكل بحري جنوبي أعطى 'tea'، وشكل بري شمالي أعطى 'chai' — وهما معاً يفسران تقريباً كل كلمة شاي في العالم.","he":"לסימן 茶 שתי הגיות עתיקות — צורת נתיב הים הדרומי שנתנה 'tea', וצורת נתיב היבשה הצפוני שנתנה 'chai' — יחד מסבירות כמעט כל מילת תה בעולם.","sw":"Herufi 茶 ina matamshi mawili ya kale — umbo la njia ya bahari ya kusini lililozaa 'tea', na umbo la njia ya nchi kavu ya kaskazini lililozaa 'chai' — pamoja yanafafanua karibu kila neno la chai duniani."},"body":{"en":"<h3>Two Pronunciation Pathways</h3><p>The Chinese character <strong>茶</strong> means tea. Two regional pronunciations of the word entered different trade networks, producing the main <em>tea</em>-type and <em>cha/chai</em>-type forms found in other languages. In the <em>World Atlas of Language Structures</em> sample of 230 languages, 110 use a <em>cha</em>-type word, 84 a <em>te</em>-type word, and only 36 something else entirely.</p><p>Both go back to one and the same word. In Middle Chinese 茶 began with a stop-like retroflex <em>ɖ-</em>. Most Chinese varieties later turned that sound into an affricate, giving <em>ch-</em>; the Min dialects of Fujian kept a plain <em>t-</em>, as they do in many words of this class (Hokkien 豬 <em>ti</em> \"pig\", 竹 <em>tek</em> \"bamboo\", against Mandarin <em>zhū</em>, <em>zhú</em>). So \"tea\" is the more old-fashioned consonant, not a different word.</p><h3>The Sea Route: t&ecirc; &rarr; thee &rarr; tea</h3><p>In the <strong>Min dialect group</strong> of coastal Fujian province &mdash; including the port of Amoy (Xiamen) &mdash; the character 茶 was pronounced approximately <em>t&ecirc;</em>. When Dutch VOC merchants began trading tea in the early 17th century, through Hokkien traders and the Malay-speaking ports of their empire, they took the word as <strong>thee</strong> (whether directly from Hokkien <em>tê</em> or via Malay <em>teh</em> is still debated). From Dutch it spread rapidly:</p><ul><li><strong>English:</strong> tea (via Dutch <em>thee</em>, ~1650s)</li><li><strong>French:</strong> th&eacute;</li><li><strong>German:</strong> Tee</li><li><strong>Spanish:</strong> t&eacute;</li><li><strong>Italian:</strong> t&egrave;</li><li><strong>Malay / Indonesian:</strong> teh (direct from Hokkien ports)</li><li><strong>Tamil:</strong> t&#275;n&#299;r (literally \"tea-water\")</li><li><strong>Afrikaans:</strong> tee</li></ul><p>These are the <strong>sea-route nations</strong> &mdash; countries whose tea word came by ship from the Min-speaking coast of southern China.</p><h3>The Land Route: ch&aacute; &rarr; &ccedil;ay &rarr; chai</h3><p>In Mandarin and most other Chinese varieties the character carries a <em>ch</em>-type initial &mdash; Mandarin <em>ch&aacute;</em>, Cantonese <em>caa4</em>. It was the northern, Mandarin-speaking end of China that sent this form overland along the <strong>Silk Road</strong> and the tea-horse routes, and from there it reached the world through caravan merchants and Russian traders. Along the way, in Persian, it picked up an ending <em>-y</em>, which is why so many of these forms are <em>chai</em> rather than plain <em>cha</em>:</p><ul><li><strong>Russian:</strong> &#1095;&#1072;&#1081; (chay)</li><li><strong>Turkish:</strong> &ccedil;ay</li><li><strong>Persian:</strong> &#1670;&#1575;&#1740; (ch&#257;y)</li><li><strong>Arabic:</strong> &#1588;&#1575;&#1610; (sh&#257;y)</li><li><strong>Hindi / Urdu:</strong> &#2330;&#2366;&#2351; (ch&#257;y)</li><li><strong>Swahili:</strong> chai</li><li><strong>Greek:</strong> &#964;&#963;&#940;&#953; (tsai)</li><li><strong>Japanese:</strong> &#12362;&#33590; (o-cha), direct from China; a later reading <em>sa</em> survives in 喫茶店 <em>kissaten</em> \"café\"</li><li><strong>Korean:</strong> 차 (cha), direct from China</li><li><strong>Portuguese:</strong> ch&aacute; (via Macau &#8212; the famous sea-nation exception)</li></ul><p><strong>Portuguese</strong> is an often-cited exception &mdash; a seafaring nation with <em>ch&aacute;</em> because its first tea contact was via Macau (a Cantonese-speaking port), not the Dutch-Hokkien network. In fact the Portuguese form reached English first: <em>cha</em> is recorded in the 1590s, decades before <em>tea</em>, and survives in the British slang \"a cup of char\".</p><div class='trivia-actions'><button type='button' class='trivia-action' data-action='panto' data-lat='24.5' data-lng='118.1' data-zoom='5'>🚢 Pan to Fujian coast (sea-route origin)</button><button type='button' class='trivia-action' data-action='focus' data-code='nan'>📍 See Min Nan on map</button></div><h3>Geographic Distribution</h3><p>The distribution broadly separates <em>tea / th&eacute; / Tee / t&eacute;</em> forms associated with maritime trade from <em>chai / chay / cha / &ccedil;ay</em> forms associated with overland routes and other Chinese contact zones. It reflects several early-modern routes by which tea and its name spread.</p><h3>What the Split Records</h3><p>The split is a favourite example of how a dialect difference can travel with trade. In most languages, the first consonant of the word for tea tells you, quite reliably, whether the word came down the trade chain that began with Hokkien speakers on the Fujian coast or by some other road out of China.</p>"},"sources":["Mair, V. H. & Hoh, E. (2009). The True History of Tea. Thames & Hudson.",{"text":"Tea etymology (Etymonline)","url":"https://www.etymonline.com/word/tea"},{"text":"Tea — Etymology section (Wikipedia)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tea#Etymology"},{"text":"Min Chinese phonology (Wikipedia)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Min_Chinese"},"Forrest, R.A.D. (1948). The Chinese Language. Faber & Faber.","Norman, J. (1988). Chinese. Cambridge University Press.",{"text":"Dahl, Östen (2013). Tea. WALS Online, Feature 138A.","url":"https://wals.info/feature/138A"}]}];
